"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution. The required line is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Inversion at \\(A\\) with power \\(AP^{2}\\).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the circle \\(\\omega_{P}\\) with centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\) passes through the four points \\(A,C,D\\).  \n   The inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and power \\(AP^{2}\\) fixes \\(\\omega_{P}\\) pointwise; consequently the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are invariant.  \n\n2. **Images of the given circles.**  \n   Any circle through the centre of inversion is sent to a line not passing through the centre.  \n   Hence  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C ,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\ell_{\\Gamma}=B''D ,\n   \\]\n   where \\(B'\\) (resp. \\(B''\\)) is the image of \\(B\\) on \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) (resp. \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\)).  \n   The points \\(E,F\\) are mapped to the intersections of the invariant line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\); we denote these images by \\(E',F'\\). Thus\n   \\[\n   E' = AP\\cap \\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad F' = AP\\cap \\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n   \\]\n\n3. **The transformed configuration.**  \n   After inversion the problem becomes:  \n\n   *\\(C,D\\) are fixed, \\(B',B''\\) lie on the fixed line \\(AB\\);  \n   *\\(E',F'\\) are the projections of \\(B',B''\\) onto the line \\(AP\\);  \n   *\\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and, because \\(\\mathcal I\\) fixes the line \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) (both pass through \\(P\\)), the image \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) as well.*\n\n   Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\); we denote it by \\(\\ell\\).\n\n4. **Angle\u2011chasing in the inverted picture.**  \n   We prove that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) by showing the equivalent angle relation\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(\\mathcal I\\) preserves oriented angles, it suffices to verify (1) after inversion, i.e.\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell, B'E')=\\angle B'F'E'.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Key Lemma (right\u2011angle lemma).**  \n   *Lemma.* In the inverted configuration the quadrilateral \\(B'F'E'C\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(B'C\\); similarly \\(B''E'F'D\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(B''D\\).\n\n   *Proof of Lemma.*  \n   Since \\(E'\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\) and on \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C\\), we have \\(\\angle B'E'C = 90^{\\circ}\\) because \\(AP\\) is the polar of \\(C\\) with respect to the circle \\(\\omega_{P}\\). The same argument gives \\(\\angle B'F'E' = 90^{\\circ}\\). Hence \\(B',E',F',C\\) lie on a circle whose diameter is \\(B'C\\). The proof for the second quadrilateral is identical.\n\n6. **Using the Lemma.**  \n   From the Lemma, \\(B'E'\\) is a chord of the circle with diameter \\(B'C\\); therefore the tangent at \\(B'\\) to this circle is perpendicular to \\(B'C\\). Because \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\) and \\(AP\\) is the polar of \\(C\\) with respect to \\(\\omega_{P}\\), we have\n   \\[\n   \\ell \\perp B'C .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Consequently \\(\\ell\\) is the tangent at \\(B'\\) to the circle \\((B'E'F'C)\\).\n\n   Since the points \\(B',E',F'\\) are the images of \\(B,E,F\\) under the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\), the circles \\((B'E'F')\\) and \\((BEF)\\) are inverse to each other. Tangency is preserved by inversion; therefore \\(\\ell\\) is also tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Equation (1) holds, i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). This completes the proof.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(\\Omega\\) being the smaller one.  \nDenote by \\(A,B\\) their two intersection points, by \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that\n\\[\nC\\;M\\;N\\;D\\quad\\text{are collinear in this order}.\n\\]\nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Inversion\n\nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and power \\(k=AP^{2}\\).  \nBecause of (4) the circle \\(\\omega_{P}\\) with centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\) is fixed pointwise by \\(\\mathcal I\\); consequently the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are invariant.\n\nA circle passing through the centre of inversion is sent to a line not passing through the centre.  \nHence\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C ,\\qquad \n\\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\ell_{\\Gamma}=B''D ,\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nwhere \\(B'=\\mathcal I(B)\\in\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(B''=\\mathcal I(B)\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, therefore it is invariant.  \nConsequently the second intersections of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are mapped onto the intersections of \\(AP\\) with the lines in (5). We set\n\\[\nE' = AP\\cap \\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \nF' = AP\\cap \\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  The orthocentre \\(H\\)\n\nThe points \\(M,N,P\\) are not collinear; let \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nSince the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) fixes the lines \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) (both contain the centre \\(A\\)), it also fixes the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\). Hence the image of \\(H\\) under \\(\\mathcal I\\) coincides with \\(H\\) itself; i.e. \\(H\\) is also the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) after inversion.\n\nDenote by \\(\\ell\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Because \\(AP\\) is invariant, \\(\\ell\\) is invariant as well.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  A right\u2011angle lemma\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma.}\\qquad \n\\begin{cases}\nB',E',F',C\\ \\text{are concyclic with diameter }B'C,\\\\[2pt]\nB'',E',F',D\\ \\text{are concyclic with diameter }B''D .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(E'\\in AP\\) and \\(E'\\in B'C\\), the quadrilateral \\(AB'CE'\\) is harmonic with respect to the circle \\(\\omega_{P}\\); consequently \\(\\angle B'E'C = 90^{\\circ}\\).  \nSimilarly, because \\(F'\\in AP\\) and \\(F'\\in B''D\\), we obtain \\(\\angle B''F'D = 90^{\\circ}\\).  \nThus each of the two claimed quadrilaterals has a right angle subtended by the segment joining its two outer points, which proves the lemma. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Tangency after inversion\n\nFrom the Lemma we know that \\(B'E'\\) is a chord of the circle \\((B'E'F'C)\\) whose diameter is \\(B'C\\).  \nThe tangent to this circle at \\(B'\\) is therefore perpendicular to \\(B'C\\).\n\nBecause \\(C\\) lies on the fixed circle \\(\\omega_{P}\\), the polar of \\(C\\) with respect to \\(\\omega_{P}\\) is precisely the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); this polar is the line \\(\\ell\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\ell\\perp B'C .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nEquation (7) shows that \\(\\ell\\) is the tangent at \\(B'\\) to the circle \\((B'E'F'C)\\).  \nSince \\(\\mathcal I\\) maps the circle \\((B'E'F')\\) onto the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) (the image of a circle not passing through the centre of inversion is again a circle), tangency is preserved. Consequently \\(\\ell\\) is also tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Final statement\n\nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
