f of Lemma.*  \n   The line \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C\\) is the polar of the fixed point \\(C\\) with respect to the circle \\(\\omega_{P}\\) (a standard fact of inversion: the image of a circle through the centre of inversion is the polar of the other intersection point).  \n   By definition of a polar, the polar of \\(C\\) is perpendicular to the line \\(CP\\).  \n   Because \\(AP\\) is a diameter of \\(\\omega_{P}\\), we have \\(AP\\perp CP\\).  \n   Hence \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to the polar of \\(C\\), i.e. \\(AP\\perp B'C\\).  \n   Consequently \\(\\angle B'E'C = 90^{\\circ}\\) (the angle between \\(B'C\\) and \\(AP\\) at their intersection \\(E'\\)).  \n   The same reasoning applied to the point \\(D\\) gives \\(\\angle B''F'D = 90^{\\circ}\\).  \n   A right angle subtended by a segment is the angle in a semicircle, therefore the four points \\(B',E',F',C\\) lie on a circle whose diameter is \\(B'C\\); analogously for \\(B'',E',F',D\\). \u220e\n\n5. **Tangency after inversion.**  \n   From the Lemma, \\(B'E'\\) is a chord of the circle \\((B'E'F'C)\\) whose diameter is \\(B'C\\).  \n   The tangent to this circle at \\(B'\\) is perpendicular to the diameter \\(B'C\\).  \n   By the observation in the Lemma we already have \\(AP\\perp B'C\\); because \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\), we obtain  \n   \\[\n   \\ell\\perp B'C .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(\\ell\\) is exactly the tangent at \\(B'\\) to the circle \\((B'E'F'C)\\).\n\n6. **Returning to the original figure.**  \n   The inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) maps the circle \\((B'E'F')\\) onto the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) (a circle not passing through the centre of inversion is sent to another circle).  \n   Tangency is preserved by inversion: if a line is tangent to a circle, its image is tangent to the image of that circle.  \n   Because \\(\\ell\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\), (3) shows that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((B'E'F')\\); therefore \\(\\ell\\) is also tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(\\Omega\\) being the smaller one;  \n* \\(A,B\\) their two intersection points;  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that \\(C,M,N,D\\) appear in this order;  \n* \\(P\\) the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nDenote by \\(\\omega_{P}\\) the circle with centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\); by (4) the points \\(A,C,D,P\\) lie on \\(\\omega_{P}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Inversion with centre \\(A\\)\n\nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and power  \n\n\\[\nk=AP^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause every point of \\(\\omega_{P}\\) is at distance \\(PA\\) from \\(P\\), the circle \\(\\omega_{P}\\) is fixed pointwise by \\(\\mathcal I\\); consequently the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are invariant:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(C)=C,\\qquad \\mathcal I(D)=D . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA circle passing through the centre of inversion is transformed into a line not passing through the centre.  \nBoth \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) contain the centre \\(A\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\ell_{\\Gamma},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) (resp. \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\)) is the line that is the image of \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \nSince \\(B\\in\\Omega\\cap\\Gamma\\), its image \\(B'=\\mathcal I(B)\\) belongs to both \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \nBecause \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) is fixed and lies on \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) pass through \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) respectively. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C,\\qquad \\ell_{\\Gamma}=B''D , \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwhere we write \\(B''=\\mathcal I(B)\\) for the image of \\(B\\) on \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) (the two images coincide as points, but we keep different symbols to stress the two lines).\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) contains the centre of inversion, therefore it is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\).  \nConsequently the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (the point \\(E\\)) is mapped onto the intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\); we denote this image by \\(E'\\).  Similarly we set  \n\n\\[\nE' = AP\\cap \\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \nF' = AP\\cap \\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus the inversion sends the quadruple \\((B,E,F)\\) to \\((B',E',F')\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nThe altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nBoth lines \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) contain the centre \\(A\\) of the inversion, so they are fixed by \\(\\mathcal I\\).  \nTherefore the image of the altitude from \\(P\\) is again the same line, and the image of the intersection of the three altitudes (i.e. \\(H\\)) is \\(H\\) itself.  Hence \\(H\\) is also the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) after inversion.\n\nDefine \\(\\ell\\) to be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  Because \\(AP\\) is invariant, \\(\\ell\\) is invariant as well.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Right\u2011angle Lemma\n\nWe now prove the crucial geometric property of the inverted picture.\n\n**Lemma.**  \nThe quadrilateral \\(B'E'F'C\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(B'C\\); similarly \\(B''F'E'D\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(B''D\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nRecall a basic fact of inversion: if a circle \\(\\gamma\\) passes through the centre of inversion \\(A\\) and meets the fixed circle \\(\\omega_{P}\\) again at a point \\(X\\neq A\\), then the image of \\(\\gamma\\) is the polar of \\(X\\) with respect to \\(\\omega_{P}\\).  \nApplying this to \\(\\Omega\\), whose second intersection with \\(\\omega_{P}\\) is the point \\(C\\), we obtain that \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C\\) is precisely the polar of \\(C\\) with respect to \\(\\omega_{P}\\).  \nBy the definition of a polar, the polar of a point is perpendicular to the line joining the point with the centre of the reference circle; therefore  \n\n\\[\nB'C\\perp CP . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(AP\\) is a diameter of \\(\\omega_{P}\\), we have \\(AP\\perp CP\\).  Combining this with (8) yields  \n\n\\[\nAP\\perp B'C . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe point \\(E'\\) is the intersection of the two lines \\(AP\\) and \\(B'C\\); consequently \\(\\angle B'E'C = 90^{\\circ}\\).  An angle of \\(90^{\\circ}\\) subtended by a segment is an angle in a semicircle, so the four points \\(B',E',F',C\\) lie on a circle whose diameter is \\(B'C\\).  The same argument applied to the second circle \\(\\Gamma\\) (whose second intersection with \\(\\omega_{P}\\) is \\(D\\)) gives that \\(B''F'E'D\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(B''D\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Tangency in the inverted configuration\n\nFrom the Lemma we know that \\(B'E'\\) is a chord of the circle \\((B'E'F'C)\\) whose diameter is \\(B'C\\).  \nThe tangent to a circle at an endpoint of a diameter is perpendicular to that diameter; therefore the tangent to \\((B'E'F'C)\\) at \\(B'\\) is perpendicular to \\(B'C\\).\n\nEquation (9) tells us that \\(AP\\) (and hence the parallel line \\(\\ell\\)) is also perpendicular to \\(B'C\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\ \\text{is the tangent at }B'\\text{ to the circle }(B'E'F'C). \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Returning to the original figure\n\nThe inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) maps the circle \\((B'E'F')\\) onto the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) because \\((B'E'F')\\) does not pass through the centre \\(A\\).  \nA line that is tangent to a circle is sent to a line tangent to the image circle; moreover a line that is invariant under the inversion remains the same after the transformation.  \nSince \\(\\ell\\) is invariant and, by (10), tangent to \\((B'E'F')\\), it follows that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the image circle \\((BEF)\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\u220e"
