            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(\\Omega\\) being the smaller one;  \n* \\(A,B\\) their two intersection points;  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that \\(C,M,N,D\\) appear in this order;  \n* \\(P\\) the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nDenote by \\(\\omega_{P}\\) the circle with centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\); by (4) the points \\(A,C,D,P\\) lie on \\(\\omega_{P}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Inversion with centre \\(A\\)\n\nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and power  \n\n\\[\nk=AP^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause every point of \\(\\omega_{P}\\) is at distance \\(PA\\) from \\(P\\), the circle \\(\\omega_{P}\\) is fixed pointwise by \\(\\mathcal I\\); consequently the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are invariant:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(C)=C,\\qquad \\mathcal I(D)=D . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA circle passing through the centre of inversion is transformed into a line not passing through the centre.  \nBoth \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) contain the centre \\(A\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\ell_{\\Gamma},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) (resp. \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\)) is the line that is the image of \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \nSince \\(B\\in\\Omega\\cap\\Gamma\\), its image \\(B'=\\mathcal I(B)\\) belongs to both \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \nBecause \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) is fixed and lies on \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) pass through \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) respectively. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C,\\qquad \\ell_{\\Gamma}=B''D , \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwhere we write \\(B''=\\mathcal I(B)\\) for the image of \\(B\\) on \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) (the two images coincide as points, but we keep different symbols to stress the two lines).\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) contains the centre of inversion, therefore it is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\).  \nConsequently the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (the point \\(E\\)) is mapped onto the intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\); we denote this image by \\(E'\\).  Similarly we set  \n\n\\[\nE' = AP\\cap \\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \nF' = AP\\cap \\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus the inversion sends the quadruple \\((B,E,F)\\) to \\((B',E',F')\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nThe altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nBoth lines \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) contain the centre \\(A\\) of the inversion, so they are fixed by \\(\\mathcal I\\).  \nTherefore the image of the altitude from