220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Tangency in the inverted configuration\n\nFrom the Lemma we know that \\(B'E'\\) is a chord of the circle \\((B'E'F'C)\\) whose diameter is \\(B'C\\).  \nThe tangent to a circle at an endpoint of a diameter is perpendicular to that diameter; therefore the tangent to \\((B'E'F'C)\\) at \\(B'\\) is perpendicular to \\(B'C\\).\n\nEquation (9) tells us that \\(AP\\) (and hence the parallel line \\(\\ell\\)) is also perpendicular to \\(B'C\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\ \\text{is the tangent at }B'\\text{ to the circle }(B'E'F'C). \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Returning to the original figure\n\nThe inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) maps the circle \\((B'E'F')\\) onto the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) because \\((B'E'F')\\) does not pass through the centre \\(A\\).  \nA line that is tangent to a circle is sent to a line tangent to the image circle; moreover a line that is invariant under the inversion remains the same after the transformation.  \nSince \\(\\ell\\) is invariant and, by (10), tangent to \\((B'E'F')\\), it follows that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the image circle \\((BEF)\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
