he circle \\(\\omega_{P}\\) (standard property of an inversion: a circle through the centre of inversion is sent to the polar of the other intersection point).  \n   By definition of a polar, \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) is perpendicular to the radius \\(CP\\); hence\n   \\[\n   BC\\perp CP . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(AP\\) is a diameter of \\(\\omega_{P}\\), we also have \\(AP\\perp CP\\).  Combining with (4) gives\n   \\[\n   AP\\perp BC . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   The point \\(E'\\) is the intersection of the two perpendicular lines \\(AP\\) and \\(BC\\); therefore \\(\\angle B'E'C=90^{\\circ}\\).  A right angle subtended by a segment is an angle in a semicircle, so the four points \\(B',E',F',C\\) lie on a circle whose diameter is \\(BC\\).  The same argument applied to the second circle \\(\\Gamma\\) (whose second intersection with \\(\\omega_{P}\\) is \\(D\\)) yields that \\(B',F',E',D\\) are concyclic with diameter \\(BD\\). \u220e\n\n4. **Tangency after inversion.**  \n   From the Lemma, the line \\(BC\\) is a diameter of the circle \\((B'E'F'C)\\).  Consequently the tangent to this circle at the endpoint \\(B'\\) is perpendicular to \\(BC\\).  \n   Equation (5) tells us that the line \\(AP\\) (and any line parallel to it) is also perpendicular to \\(BC\\).  Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is, after inversion, exactly the tangent at \\(B'\\) to the circle \\((B'E'F'C)\\).\n\n5. **Returning to the original figure.**  \n   The inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) maps the circle \\((B'E'F')\\) onto the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) (a circle not passing through the centre of inversion is sent to another circle).  Tangency is preserved by inversion: the image of a tangent line is tangent to the image circle.  \n   Because the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is invariant under the inversion (it passes through the centre \\(P\\) and is parallel to the invariant line \\(AP\\)), the line is tangent to \\((B'E'F')\\) and therefore also tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(\\Omega\\) being the smaller one;  \n* \\(A,B\\) their two intersection points;  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that \\(C,M,N,D\\) appear in this order;  \n* \\(P\\) the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nDenote by \\(\\omega_{P}\\) the circle with centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\); by (6) the points \\(A,C,D,P\\) lie on \\(\\omega_{P}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Inversion with centre \\(P\\)\n\nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(P\\) and power  \n\n\\[\nk=PA^{2}=PC^{2}=PD^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the four points \\(A,C,D,P\\) belong to \\(\\omega_{P}\\), the circle \\(\\omega_{P}\\) is fixed pointwise by \\(\\mathcal I\\); consequently\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(A)=A,\\qquad \\mathcal I(C)=C,\\qquad \\mathcal I(D)=D . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nA circle passing through the centre of inversion is transformed into a straight line that does **not** pass through the centre.  \nBoth \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) contain the centre \\(P\\) (they pass through \\(A\\) and \\(P\\) lies on the circum\u2011circle of \\(ACD\\)), therefore their images are lines.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}= \\mathcal I(\\Omega),\\qquad \n\\ell_{\\Gamma}= \\mathcal I(\\Gamma).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(B\\in\\Omega\\cap\\Gamma\\), its image \\(B'=\\mathcal I(B)\\) belongs to both \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \nBecause \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) is fixed and lies on \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) pass through \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) respectively. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C,\\qquad \n\\ell_{\\Gamma}=B'D . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, therefore it is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\).  \nConsequently the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (the point \\(E\\)) is mapped onto the intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\); we denote this image by \\(E'\\).  Similarly we set  \n\n\\[\nE' = AP\\cap \\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \nF' = AP\\cap \\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus the inversion sends the triple \\((B,E,F)\\) to \\((B',E',F')\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nThe altitude from \\(P\\) to the side \\(MN\\) is the line \\(PH\\); because this line passes through the centre of inversion, it is invariant:\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(PH)=PH .\n\\]\n\nThe other two altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) are the lines through \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) perpendicular respectively to \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\).  \nUnder the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) a line not passing through the centre is sent to a circle passing through the centre.  \nHence the images of the two altitudes are the circles \\((MPN')\\) and \\((NPM')\\) where \\(M'=\\mathcal I(M)\\) and \\(N'=\\mathcal I(N)\\).  \nThe three altitudes intersect at a single point; therefore their images intersect at a single point as well, namely the image \\(H'=\\mathcal I(H)\\).  \nSince the altitude through \\(P\\) is fixed, the three images are concurrent on the line \\(PH\\); consequently\n\n\\[\nH',\\,H,\\,P\\ \\text{are collinear}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Right\u2011angle Lemma\n\n**Lemma.** In the configuration after inversion the quadrilateral \\(B'E'F'C\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(B'C\\); similarly \\(B'F'E'D\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(B'D\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe line \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C\\) is the polar of the fixed point \\(C\\) with respect to the circle \\(\\omega_{P}\\) (a standard property of an inversion: the image of a circle through the centre of inversion is the polar of the other intersection point).  \nBy definition of a polar, \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) is perpendicular to the radius \\(CP\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nB'C\\perp CP . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(AP\\) is a diameter of \\(\\omega_{P}\\), we also have \\(AP\\perp CP\\).  Combining with (11) yields\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp B'C . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThe point \\(E'\\) is the intersection of the two perpendicular lines \\(AP\\) and \\(B'C\\); consequently \\(\\angle B'E'C=90^{\\circ}\\).  An angle of \\(90^{\\circ}\\) subtended by a segment is an angle in a semicircle, so the four points \\(B',E',F',C\\) lie on a circle whose diameter is \\(B'C\\).  The same reasoning applied to the second circle \\(\\Gamma\\) (whose second intersection with \\(\\omega_{P}\\) is \\(D\\)) gives that \\(B',F',E',D\\) are concyclic with diameter \\(B'D\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Tangency in the inverted configuration\n\nFrom the Lemma, the line \\(B'C\\) is a diameter of the circle \\((B'E'F'C)\\).  Hence the tangent to this circle at the endpoint \\(B'\\) is perpendicular to \\(B'C\\).  \nEquation (12) tells us that the line \\(AP\\) (and any line parallel to it) is also perpendicular to \\(B'C\\).  \nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is, after inversion, exactly the tangent at \\(B'\\) to the circle \\((B'E'F'C)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Returning to the original figure\n\nThe inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) maps the circle \\((B'E'F')\\) onto the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) because \\((B'E'F')\\) does not pass through the centre \\(P\\).  \nA line that is tangent to a circle