I(D)=D . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nA circle passing through the centre of inversion is transformed into a straight line that does **not** pass through the centre.  \nBoth \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) contain the centre \\(P\\) (they pass through \\(A\\) and \\(P\\) lies on the circum\u2011circle of \\(ACD\\)), therefore their images are lines.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}= \\mathcal I(\\Omega),\\qquad \n\\ell_{\\Gamma}= \\mathcal I(\\Gamma).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(B\\in\\Omega\\cap\\Gamma\\), its image \\(B'=\\mathcal I(B)\\) belongs to both \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \nBecause \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) is fixed and lies on \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) pass through \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) respectively. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C,\\qquad \n\\ell_{\\Gamma}=B'D . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, therefore it is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\).  \nConsequently the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (the point \\(E\\)) is mapped onto the intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\); we denote this image by \\(E'\\).  Similarly we set  \n\n\\[\nE' = AP\\cap \\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \nF' = AP\\cap \\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus the inversion sends the triple \\((B,E,F)\\) to \\((B',E',F')\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nThe altitude from \\(P\\) to the side \\(MN\\) is the line \\(PH\\); because this line passes through the centre of inversion, it is invariant:\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(PH)=PH .\n\\]\n\nThe other two altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) are the lines through \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) perpendicular respectively to \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\).  \nUnder the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) a line not passing through the centre is sent to a circle passing through the centre