eter of the circle \\((B'E'F'C)\\).  Hence the tangent to this circle at the endpoint \\(B'\\) is perpendicular to \\(B'C\\).  \nEquation (12) tells us that the line \\(AP\\) (and any line parallel to it) is also perpendicular to \\(B'C\\).  \nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is, after inversion, exactly the tangent at \\(B'\\) to the circle \\((B'E'F'C)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Returning to the original figure\n\nThe inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) maps the circle \\((B'E'F')\\) onto the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) because \\((B'E'F')\\) does not pass through the centre \\(P\\).  \nA line that is tangent to a circle is sent by an inversion to a line tangent to the image circle; moreover a line that passes through the centre of inversion is invariant.  \nThe line we are interested in passes through \\(P\\) (it is parallel to \\(AP\\) and contains \\(H\\), and \\(AP\\) itself passes through \\(P\\)); consequently it is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\).  \nSince we have shown that, after inversion, this line is tangent to \\((B'E'F')\\), it follows that the same line is tangent to the original circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
