lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) pass through \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) respectively. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C,\\qquad \n\\ell_{\\Gamma}=B'D . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, therefore it is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\).  \nConsequently the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (the point \\(E\\)) is mapped onto the intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\); we denote this image by \\(E'\\).  Similarly we set  \n\n\\[\nE' = AP\\cap \\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \nF' = AP\\cap \\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus the inversion sends the triple \\((B,E,F)\\) to \\((B',E',F')\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nThe altitude from \\(P\\) to the side \\(MN\\) is the line \\(PH\\); because this line passes through the centre of inversion, it is invariant:\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(PH)=PH .\n\\]\n\nThe other two altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) are the lines through \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) perpendicular respectively to \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\).  \nUnder the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) a line not passing through the centre is sent to a circle passing through the centre.  \nHence the images of the two altitudes are the circles \\((MPN')\\) and \\((NPM')\\) where \\(M'=\\mathcal I(M)\\) and \\(N'=\\mathcal I(N)\\).  \nThe three altitudes intersect at a single point; therefore their images intersect at a single point as well, namely the image \\(H'=\\mathcal I(H)\\).  \nSince the altitude through \\(P\\) is fixed, the three images are concurrent on the line \\(PH\\); consequently\n\n\\[\nH',\\,H,\\,P\\ \\text{are collinear}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Right\u2011angle Lemma\n\n**Lemma.** In the configuration after inversion the quadrilateral \\(B'E'F'C\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(B'C\\); similarly \\(B'F'E'D\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(B'D\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe line \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C\\) is the polar of the fixed point \\(C\\) with respect to the circle \\(\\omega_{P}\\) (a standard property of an inversion: the image of a circle through the centre of inversion is the polar of the other intersection point).  \nBy definition of a polar, \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) is perpendicular to the radius \\(CP\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nB'C\\perp CP . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(AP\\) is a diameter of \\(\\omega_{P}\\), we also have \\(AP\\perp CP\\).  Combining with (11) yields\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp B'C . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThe point \\(E'\\) is the intersection of the two perpendicular lines \\(AP\\) and \\(B'C\\); consequently \\(\\angle B'E'C=90^{\\circ}\\).  An angle of \\(90^{\\circ}\\) subtended by a segment is an angle in a semicircle, so the four points \\(B',E',F',C\\) lie on a circle whose diameter is \\(B'C\\).  The same reasoning applied to the second circle \\(\\Gamma\\) (whose second intersection with \\(\\omega_{P}\\) is \\(D\\)) gives that \\(B',F',E',D\\) are concyclic with diameter \\(B'D\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Tangency in the inverted configuration\n\nFrom the Lemma, the line \\(B'C\\) is a diameter of the circle \\((B'E'F'C)\\).  Hence the tangent to this circle at the endpoint \\(B'\\) is perpendicular to \\(B'C\\).  \nEquation (12) tells us that the line \\(AP\\) (and any line parallel to it) is also perpendicular to \\(B'C\\).  \nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is, after inversion, exactly the tangent at \\(B'\\) to the circle \\((B'E'F'C)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Returning to the original figure\n\nThe inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) maps the circle \\((B'E'F')\\) onto the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) because \\((B'E'F')\\) does not pass through the centre \\(P\\).  \nA line that