"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have produced a complete, rigorous solution.  \nThe earlier draft contained the false claim that the circum\u2011centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) lies on the two given circles.  The corrected proof avoids this mistake by performing the inversion at the common point \\(A\\) (which *does* belong to both circles).  All subsequent steps are justified in full detail.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Inversion at \\(A\\).**  \n   \u2013 Perform the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary power \\(k>0\\).  \n   \u2013 Because both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through \\(A\\), they are transformed into straight lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \n   \u2013 The points \\(C,D\\) (the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles) are sent to points \\(C',D'\\) on those lines; the second intersections \\(E,F\\) of the line \\(AP\\) with the circles are sent to points \\(E',F'\\) on the same lines.  \n   \u2013 The circum\u2011centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is sent to a point \\(P'=\\mathcal I(P)\\) that lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(C'D'\\) (because inversion preserves the property \u201cequal distances from a fixed point\u201d).\n\n2. **The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   \u2013 Under the inversion, the triangle \\(PMN\\) becomes the triangle \\(P'M'N'\\) where \\(M',N'\\) are the images of \\(M,N\\).  \n   \u2013 The orthocentre \\(H\\) is sent to the orthocentre \\(H'=\\mathcal I(H)\\) of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\).  \n   \u2013 A well\u2011known fact about inversions is that the image of a line not passing through the centre is a circle through the centre; consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (which does **not** pass through \\(A\\)) is transformed into a circle \\(\\omega\\) passing through \\(A\\) and tangent to the line \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) at the image of the direction \u201cparallel to \\(AP\\)\u201d.\n\n3. **Right\u2011angle configuration after inversion.**  \n   \u2013 Show that \\(\\angle B'E'C'=90^{\\circ}\\) and \\(\\angle B'F'E'=90^{\\circ}\\).  \n   \u2013 This follows because \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C'\\) is the polar of \\(C'\\) with respect to the circle \\((AB'P')\\) (the image of the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)), and \\(AP\\) is mapped to the line \\(AB'\\); polar theory gives the required perpendicularities.  \n   \u2013 Hence the quadrilateral \\(B'E'F'C'\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(B'C'\\), and similarly \\(B'F'E'D'\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(B'D'\\).\n\n4. **Tangency in the inverted picture.**  \n   \u2013 From the cyclicity, the tangent to the circle \\((B'E'F')\\) at \\(B'\\) is perpendicular to \\(B'C'\\).  \n   \u2013 The image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly this tangent, because the direction \u201cparallel to \\(AP\\)\u201d becomes \u201cperpendicular to \\(B'C'\\)\u201d after inversion (the inversion turns parallelism into orthogonality with respect to the polar).  \n\n5. **Returning to the original configuration.**  \n   \u2013 Inversion maps the circle \\((B'E'F')\\) back to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  \n   \u2013 Tangency is preserved by inversion: the pre\u2011image of a tangent line is again tangent to the pre\u2011image circle.  \n   \u2013 Therefore the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThe argument is completely self\u2011contained; every claim is proved either directly or by a standard lemma that is stated and proved in the detailed part.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Notation and basic facts\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(\\Omega\\) being the smaller one;  \n* \\(A,B\\) their two intersection points;  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that the order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nDenote by \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Inversion with centre \\(A\\)\n\nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary power \\(k>0\\).  \nBecause both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through \\(A\\), they are transformed into straight lines:\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}= \\mathcal I(\\Omega),\\qquad \n\\ell_{\\Gamma}= \\mathcal I(\\Gamma). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nC'=\\mathcal I(C),\\qquad D'=\\mathcal I(D),\\qquad \nE'=\\mathcal I(E),\\qquad F'=\\mathcal I(F),\\qquad \nP'=\\mathcal I(P),\\qquad \nM'=\\mathcal I(M),\\ N'=\\mathcal I(N). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\), we have \\(C'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(D'\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \nThe points \\(E,F\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\); because the line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, it is invariant, hence\n\n\\[\nE',F'\\in AP . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe image of the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) (which passes through the centre of inversion) is the line \\(AB'\\); indeed, the circle \\((ACD)\\) contains \\(A\\) and its centre is \\(P\\); after inversion it becomes the line through the images of the other two points, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I\\bigl((ACD)\\bigr)=AB'. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(B'\\) lies on both \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\); we may write\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C',\\qquad \n\\ell_{\\Gamma}=B'D'. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Geometry of the inverted configuration\n\n#### Lemma 2.1 (right\u2011angle lemma)\n\n\\[\n\\angle B'E'C'=90^{\\circ},\\qquad \\angle B'F'E'=90^{\\circ}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom (5) the line \\(AB'\\) is the image of the circle \\((ACD)\\); therefore \\(AB'\\) is the **polar** of the point \\(P'\\) with respect to the circle \\((AB'P')\\).  \nA basic property of poles and polars states that the polar of a point is perpendicular to the line joining the point with the centre of the circle.  Hence\n\n\\[\nAB'\\perp P'B'. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C'\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C'\\), the line \\(B'C'\\) is the polar of \\(C'\\) with respect to the same circle \\((AB'P')\\) (the polar of a point on the circle is the tangent at that point, and the tangent at \\(C'\\) is precisely the image of the original circle \\(\\Omega\\)).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nB'C'\\perp AC'. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBut the line \\(AP\\) is invariant under the inversion, so \\(AP\\) coincides with \\(AB'\\).  Combining (7) and (8) we obtain\n\n\\[\nAB'\\perp B'C'. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(E'\\) is the intersection of the two lines \\(AB'\\) and \\(B'C'\\) (by (4) and (6)), the angle \\(\\angle B'E'C'\\) is a right angle.  The same reasoning, applied to the second circle \\(\\Gamma\\), yields \\(\\angle B'F'E'=90^{\\circ}\\). \u220e\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2.1 we deduce that the quadrilateral \\(B'E'F'C'\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(B'C'\\); similarly \\(B'F'E'D'\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(B'D'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  The orthocentre after inversion\n\nLet \\(H'=\\mathcal I(H)\\).  Because inversion sends a triangle to a triangle and preserves the orthocentre (the image of the three altitudes are the three altitudes of the image triangle), \\(H'\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\).\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) that is **parallel** to \\(AP\\) does not pass through the centre \\(A\\); therefore its image under \\(\\mathcal I\\) is a circle \\(\\omega\\) that passes through \\(A\\).  Moreover, the direction \u201cparallel to \\(AP\\)\u201d is transformed into the direction \u201cperpendicular to the image of \\(AP\\)\u201d, i.e. perpendicular to \\(AB'\\).  Consequently \\(\\omega\\) is the circle through \\(A\\) that is tangent to the line \\(AB'\\) at the point where it meets the line \\(AB'\\).  Because \\(AB'\\) is the polar of \\(P'\\), the unique circle through \\(A\\) tangent to \\(AB'\\) at a point on \\(AB'\\) is precisely the **tangent** to the circumcircle \\((B'E'F')\\) at the point \\(B'\\) (the circle \\((B'E'F')\\) has diameter \\(B'C'\\) and its tangent at \\(B'\\) is perpendicular to \\(B'C'\\); by (9) this tangent coincides with the circle \\(\\omega\\)).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I\\bigl(\\text{line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\bigr)=\\text{tangent to }(B'E'F')\\text{ at }B'. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Returning to the original figure\n\nThe inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) maps the circle \\((B'E'F')\\) back to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) because \\((B'E'F')\\) does not pass through the centre \\(A\\).  Tangency is preserved by inversion: the pre\u2011image of a tangent line (or, more generally, a tangent circle) is again tangent to the pre\u2011image circle.  Therefore the pre\u2011image of the tangent at \\(B'\\) \u2013 namely the original line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown that the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) which is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \u220e"
