ne not passing through the centre of inversion is **another line**, not a circle passing through the centre. The statement confuses the two possible cases of inversion (line \u2194 circle) and is incorrect.\n* **Location:** \u201cConsequently \\(\\omega\\) is the circle through \\(A\\) that is tangent to the line \\(AB'\\) at the point where it meets the line \\(AB'\\).\u201d\n  * **Issue:** Critical Error \u2013 follows from the previous false claim; the existence and description of \\(\\omega\\) are unfounded.\n* **Location:** \u201cBecause \\(AB'\\) is the polar of \\(P'\\), the unique circle through \\(A\\) tangent to \\(AB'\\) at a point on \\(AB'\\) is precisely the **tangent** to the circumcircle \\((B'E'F')\\) at the point \\(B'\\).\u201d\n  * **Issue:** Critical Error \u2013 the link between the polar statement and the claimed tangent circle is not justified; the argument rests on earlier false premises.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Verification Log\n\n#### 2.1 Notation and basic facts\n- The definitions of the circles, points, and the statement \\(PA=PC=PD\\) (since \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)) are correct and need no further justification.\n\n#### 2.2 Inversion with centre \\(A\\)\n- The claim that both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through \\(A\\) and therefore are transformed into straight lines under inversion is correct.\n- The notation for the images \\(C',D',E',F',P',M',N'\\) is clear.\n\n#### **Critical Error at (5)**\n- The solution states: \u201cThe image of the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) (which passes through the centre of inversion) is the line \\(AB'\\).\u201d\n- **Why this is wrong:** For an inversion centred at \\(A\\), any circle passing through \\(A\\) is mapped to a line **through the images of the other two points** on the circle. The circle \\((ACD)\\) passes through \\(A\\); its other points are \\(C\\) and \\(D\\). Hence the image must be the line \\(C'D'\\). The line \\(AB'\\) is the image of the line \\(AP\\) (which passes through the centre), not of the circle \\((ACD)\\). This misidentification invalidates every subsequent argument that relies on the line \\(AB'\\) being the image of \\((ACD)\\).\n\n#### 2.3 Geometry of the inverted configuration\n- Lemma\u202f2.1 claims right angles at \\(B'E'C'\\) and \\(B'F'E'\\). The proof uses the false polar relationship from (5) and (7)\u2013(9). Because the identification of the image of \\((ACD)\\) is incorrect, the derived orthogonality \\(AB'\\perp B'C'\\) does not follow. Hence the conclusion that \\(\\angle B'E'C'=90^\\circ\\) is unsupported.\n\n#### **Critical Error at polar statements (7)\u2013(9)**\n- The argument that \u201c\\(AB'\\) is the polar of \\(P'\\) with respect to the circle \\((AB'P')\\)\u201d is unfounded. The circle \\((AB'P')\\) is not a naturally defined object in the inverted configuration, and the polar concept is misapplied. Consequently, the perpendicularities claimed in (7) and (8) are not justified, and the deduction of a right angle in Lemma\u202f2.1 collapses.\n\n#### 2.4 The orthocentre after inversion\n- The solution asserts that inversion preserves orthocentres: \u201cBecause inversion sends a triangle to a triangle and preserves the orthocentre \u2026 \\(H'\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\).\u201d\n- **Why this is false:** Inversion maps lines to circles (or lines) and does not, in general, map altitudes to altitudes. The image of an altitude (a line through a vertex perpendicular to the opposite side) becomes a circle through the image of the vertex and orthogonal to the image of the opposite side, which is not an altitude of the image triangle. Therefore the claim that \\(H'\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\) is a critical error.\n\n#### **Critical Error at (10) \u2013 image of a parallel line**\n- The solution says: \u201cThe line through \\(H\\) that is **parallel** to \\(AP\\) does not pass through the centre \\(A\\); therefore its image under \\(\\mathcal I\\) is a circle \\(\\omega\\) that passes through \\(A\\).\u201d\n- **Why this is wrong:** Under inversion centred at \\(A\\), any line **not** passing through \\(A\\) is mapped to a **circle passing through \\(A\\)**. However, the line in question *does* pass through \\(A\\) because it is parallel to \\(AP\\) and \\(AP\\) itself passes through \\(A\\). A line parallel to a line through the centre of inversion must also pass through the centre (otherwise it would intersect the centre line at infinity, which is not a point in Euclidean geometry). Hence the premise \u201cdoes not pass through the centre \\(A\\)\u201d is false, and the subsequent description of its image is invalid.\n\n#### 2.5 Returning to the original figure\n- The claim that \u201ctangency is preserved by inversion\u201d is true in general, but it can be applied only when the objects involved are correctly identified. Since the preceding steps misidentified the images of the relevant circles and lines, the conclusion that the original line is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) does not follow.\n\n#### Overall assessment\n- The proof hinges on a chain of geometric transformations that are incorrectly described: the image of \\((ACD)\\) is misidentified, polar relationships are incorrectly invoked, the preservation of orthocentre under inversion is falsely asserted, and the image of the parallel line is mishandled. Because these errors occur early and are essential for the later deductions, the entire argument collapses.\n\n**Conclusion:** The solution is invalid due to multiple critical errors that break the logical flow of the proof."
