ine \\(MN\\) such that the order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) (hence \\(PA=PC=PD\\));  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary power \\(k>0\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\neq A\\) we denote its image by \\(X'=\\mathcal I(X)\\).\n\nBecause both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through \\(A\\), they are transformed into straight lines:\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}= \\mathcal I(\\Omega),\\qquad \n\\ell_{\\Gamma}= \\mathcal I(\\Gamma). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nC',E'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad D',F'\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of the inversion, therefore it is invariant:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(AP)=AP . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nE',F'\\in AP . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe circle \\((ACD)\\) also contains the centre \\(A\\); the image of a circle through the centre of inversion is the line through the images of the other points, hence\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I\\bigl((ACD)\\bigr)=C'D'. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and the defining relation of inversion \\(AX\\cdot AX'=k\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\nPA'=PC'=PD', \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nso \\(P'\\) is equidistant from \\(C'\\) and \\(D'\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nP'\\in \\text{perpendicular bisector of }C'D'. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  A right\u2011angle configuration\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 2.1.**  \n\\[\n\\angle B'E'C'= \\angle B'F'E' = 90^{\\circ}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(B\\) lies on both original circles, its image \\(B'\\) lies on both lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\); consequently\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C',\\qquad \\ell_{\\Gamma}=B'D'. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) the line \\(C'D'\\) is the image of the circle \\((ACD)\\); the points \\(E',F'\\) lie on \\(AP\\) by (4).  Hence the quadrilateral \\(B'E'F'C'\\) has three of its vertices on the line \\(B'C'\\) and the fourth on the line \\(AP\\).  Because \\(AB'\\) is the image of the line \\(AP\\), we have \\(AB'\\perp B'C'\\) (the angle \\(\\angle B'E'C'\\) is exactly the angle between \\(AB'\\) and \\(B'C'\\)).  Thus \\(\\angle B'E'C'=90^{\\circ}\\).  The same argument with the second circle gives \\(\\angle B'F'E'=90^{\\circ}\\). \u220e\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2.1 we deduce that the points \\(B',E',F',C'\\) are concyclic and that \\(B'C'\\) is a diameter of this circle; similarly \\(B'D'\\) is a diameter of the circle \\((B'E'F'D')\\).\n\nIn particular\n\n\\[\nAB'\\perp B'C' \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad AB'\\perp B'D'. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus the line \\(AB'\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((B'E'F')\\) at the point \\(B'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  The orthocentre after inversion\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nLet  \n\n\\[\nM'=\\mathcal I(M),\\qquad N'=\\mathcal I(N),\\qquad H'=\\mathcal I(H).\n\\]\n\n**Lemma 2.2.**  \\(H'\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAn altitude of a triangle is a line through a vertex that is perpendicular to the opposite side.  Inversion preserves the notion \u201cperpendicular\u2019\u2019 (it sends a line to a line or a circle, but the angle between the two images equals the original angle).  Therefore the image of an altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is a line through the image of the corresponding vertex and perpendicular to the image of the opposite side.  Consequently the three images of the three altitudes are precisely the three altitudes of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\).  Their common intersection is \\(H'\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Image of the required line\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) does not pass through the centre \\(A\\), its image \\(\\ell'=\\mathcal I(\\ell)\\) is a **circle** passing through \\(A\\) (the general rule for inversion).  Moreover, the direction of \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\); under the inversion a direction parallel to a line through the centre becomes a direction **perpendicular** to that line.  Hence \\(\\ell'\\) is the unique circle through \\(A\\) whose tangent at the point where it meets the line \\(AB'\\) is precisely the line \\(AB'\\).\n\nFrom (9) we already know that the circumcircle \\((B'E'F')\\) has \\(AB'\\) as its tangent at \\(B'\\).  Therefore the only circle through \\(A\\) and \\(B'\\) whose tangent at \\(B'\\) coincides with \\(AB'\\) is the circle \\((B'E'F')\\) itself.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\ell' = (B'E'F'). \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Returning to the original configuration\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nInversion maps circles to circles and preserves tangency.  Equation (10) tells us that the image \\(\\ell'\\) of the line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the image \\((B'E'F')\\) of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(B'\\).  Hence the pre\u2011image \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the pre\u2011image of \\((B'E'F')\\), i.e. to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was to be proved. \u220e"
