tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Right\u2011angle configuration after inversion.**  \n   *The line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, therefore it is invariant: \\(\\mathcal I(AP)=AP\\).  \n   *Consequently \\(E',F'\\in AP\\).  \n   *In the original figure the points \\(A,E,C\\) are collinear on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(AE=AC\\) (both are radii of \\(\\Omega\\)). Hence \\(A\\) is the midpoint of the segment \\(EC\\); after inversion the image of the midpoint of a chord becomes the foot of the perpendicular from the centre to the image line. Therefore \\(AB'\\perp B'C'\\). An identical argument with the larger circle gives \\(AB'\\perp B'D'\\).  \n   *Thus  \n   \\[\n   \\angle B'E'C'=\\angle B'F'E'=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]  \n   Hence the four points \\(B',E',F',C'\\) are concyclic and \\(B'C'\\) is a diameter of the circle \\((B'E'F')\\).\n\n4. **Behaviour of the orthocentre under inversion.**  \n   *Inversion is a conformal map; it preserves oriented angles, and therefore it sends a line perpendicular to another line (or circle) to a line (or circle) perpendicular to the image of the latter.  \n   *Consequently the image of each altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is an altitude of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\). Their common intersection is \\(H'=\\mathcal I(H)\\); thus  \n\n   \\[\n   H' \\text{ is the orthocentre of } \\triangle P'M'N'. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Image of the required line.**  \n   *Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Because \\(\\ell\\) does not contain the centre \\(A\\), its image \\(\\ell'=\\mathcal I(\\ell)\\) is a circle passing through \\(A\\).  \n   *Since \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) is invariant, the direction of \\(\\ell\\) is mapped to the direction **perpendicular** to \\(AP\\); therefore \\(\\ell'\\) is the unique circle through \\(A\\) whose tangent at the point where it meets the line \\(AB'\\) is precisely the line \\(AB'\\).  \n\n   *From (2) we already know that the circum\u2011circle \\((B'E'F')\\) has \\(AB'\\) as a tangent at \\(B'\\) (the tangent to a circle at a point is perpendicular to the radius, and the radius \\(AB'\\) is perpendicular to the diameter \\(B'C'\\)).  \n   *A circle through two distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B'\\) is uniquely determined by the direction of its tangent at \\(B'\\). Hence the only circle through \\(A\\) whose tangent at \\(B'\\) is \\(AB'\\) is \\((B'E'F')\\). Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell'=(B'E'F'). \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Returning to the original configuration.**  \n   *Inversion maps circles to circles and preserves tangency (a direct consequence of conformality). Because \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to \\((B'E'F')\\) at \\(B'\\), the pre\u2011image \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the pre\u2011image of \\((B'E'F')\\). The pre\u2011image of \\((B'E'F')\\) is exactly the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (the three points \\(B,E,F\\) lie on the original circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) and are mapped to \\(B',E',F'\\)).  \n   *Thus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which is the statement to be proved. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Inversion\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal I\\) be the inversion with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary power \\(k>0\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\neq A\\) denote its image by \\(X'=\\mathcal I(X)\\).\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\)) both pass through \\(A\\), the well\u2011known property of inversion gives\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \n\\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\ell_{\\Gamma},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) are straight lines.  \nSimilarly, the circle \\((ACD)\\) contains \\(A\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I\\bigl((ACD)\\bigr)=C'D', \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nthe line through the images of