ngle B'F'E'=90^{\\circ}\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\angle B'E'C'=\\angle B'F'E'=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the four points \\(B',E',F',C'\\) are concyclic and \\(B'C'\\) is a diameter of the circle \\((B'E'F')\\). In particular the line \\(AB'\\) is tangent to \\((B'E'F')\\) at the point \\(B'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Orthocentre under inversion\n\nInversion is a M\u00f6bius transformation; it is conformal and therefore preserves oriented angles. In particular, if a line (or circle) \\(\\ell\\) is perpendicular to a line (or circle) \\(m\\), then their images \\(\\ell',m'\\) are also perpendicular.\n\nAn altitude of a triangle is a line through a vertex perpendicular to the opposite side. Applying the above preservation property to each altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) we see that the images of the three altitudes are precisely the three altitudes of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\). Their common intersection is the image of the orthocentre; therefore\n\n\\[\nH'=\\mathcal I(H) \\text{ is the orthocentre of } \\triangle P'M'N'. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Image of the required line\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Because \\(\\ell\\) does **not** contain the centre \\(A\\), the general rule for inversion tells us that \\(\\ell'=\\mathcal I(\\ell)\\) is a circle passing through \\(A\\).\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) is invariant, the direction of \\(\\ell\\) is mapped to the direction **perpendicular** to \\(AP\\). Consequently the circle \\(\\ell'\\) meets the line \\(AB'\\) at the point \\(B'\\) and has \\(AB'\\) as its tangent there.\n\nFrom (12) we already know that the circum\u2011circle \\((B'E'F')\\) has the same property: it passes through \\(A\\) and \\(B'\\) and its tangent at \\(B'\\) is the line \\(AB'\\) (because \\(AB'\\) is perpendicular to the diameter \\(B'C'\\)).  \n\nA circle is uniquely determined by two distinct points together with the direction of its tangent at one of them. Hence the only circle through \\(A\\) whose tangent at \\(B'\\) is \\(AB'\\) is \\((B'E'F')\\). Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\ell'=(B'E'F'). \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Back to the original figure\n\nInversion maps circles to circles and, by conformality, preserves tangency: if two circles are tangent at a point, their images are tangent at the image of that point.  \nEquation (14) shows that \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to \\((B'E'F')\\) at \\(B'\\). Taking the inverse image under \\(\\mathcal I\\) we obtain that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the inverse image of \\((B'E'F')\\). The inverse image of \\((B'E'F')\\) is precisely the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (the three points \\(B,E,F\\) lie on the original circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) and are mapped to \\(B',E',F'\\)).  \n\nHence the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which completes the proof. \u220e"
