\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\)), the circle \\(\\ell'\\) meets \\(AB'\\) at \\(B'\\). Moreover the tangent to \\(\\ell'\\) at \\(B'\\) is the image of the direction of \\(\\ell\\), i.e. it is the line \\(AB'\\) itself. Hence \\(\\ell'\\) is a circle through \\(A\\) and \\(B'\\) whose tangent at \\(B'\\) is \\(AB'\\).\n\n6. **Identification of \\(\\ell'\\).**  \n   * A circle is uniquely determined by two distinct points together with the direction of its tangent at one of them. Proof: if two circles \\(\\omega_1,\\omega_2\\) pass through \\(A\\) and \\(B'\\) and have the same tangent at \\(B'\\), then their centres lie on the perpendicular to the common tangent through \\(B'\\); the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AB'\\) for the two circles coincide, forcing the centres to be identical, hence \\(\\omega_1=\\omega_2\\).  \n   * The circum\u2011circle \\((B'E'F')\\) also passes through \\(A\\) and \\(B'\\) and, as shown in step\u202f3, has \\(AB'\\) as its tangent at \\(B'\\). By uniqueness, \\(\\ell'=(B'E'F')\\).\n\n7. **Returning to the original configuration.**  \n   * Inversion preserves tangency: if two circles are tangent at a point, their images are tangent at the image of that point. Since \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to \\((B'E'F')\\) at \\(B'\\), the pre\u2011image \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the pre\u2011image of \\((B'E'F')\\). The pre\u2011image of \\((B'E'F')\\) is exactly the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (the points \\(B,E,F\\) lie on the original circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) and are mapped to \\(B',E',F'\\)).  \n   * Therefore the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as required. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Inversion and basic consequences\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal I\\) be the inversion with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary power \\(k>0\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\neq A\\) denote its image by \\(X'=\\mathcal I(X)\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nAX\\cdot AX'=k. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\)), \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\)) and the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) all contain the centre of inversion \\(A\\), each of them is transformed into a straight line:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \n\\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\ell_{\\Gamma},\\qquad \n\\mathcal I\\bigl((ACD)\\bigr)=C'D'. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(PA=PC=PD\\) (by definition of \\(P\\) as the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)), applying (1) to the three equal distances gives  \n\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PA'=k,\\qquad PC\\cdot PC'=k,\\qquad PD\\cdot PD'=k.\n\\]\n\nDividing the first equality by the second and third we obtain  \n\n\\[\nPA'=PC'=PD'. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(P'\\) is equidistant from \\(C'\\) and \\(D'\\); consequently \\(P'\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(C'D'\\).\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, therefore it is invariant:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(AP)=AP. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nHence the second intersections of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\nE',F'\\in AP. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Images of the common points\n\nThe point \\(B\\) belongs to both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore its image \\(B'\\) belongs to both \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \nSince \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\), we have \\(C'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(D'\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C',\\qquad \\ell_{\\Gamma}=B'D'. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A chord\u2011midpoint lemma for inversion\n\n**Lemma 1.** *Let a circle \\(\\sigma\\) pass through the centre \\(A\\) of an inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\). Let \\(X,Y\\in\\sigma\\) and let \\(M\\) be the midpoint of the chord \\(XY\\). Then the image \\(M'\\) lies on the line \\(\\mathcal I(\\sigma)\\) and \\(AM'\\) is perpendicular to that line.*\n\n*Proof.* Because \\(A\\) belongs to \\(\\sigma\\), \\(\\mathcal I(\\sigma)\\) is a straight line, say \\(\\ell\\). Write \\(AX\\cdot AX'=k\\) and \\(AY\\cdot AY'=k\\). Since \\(M\\) is the midpoint of \\(XY\\), vectors \\(\\overrightarrow{AX}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{AY}\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(\\overrightarrow{AM}\\); consequently \\(\\angle XAM=\\angle MAY\\). Using (1),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AX'}{AY'}=\\frac{k/AX}{k/AY}=\\frac{AY}{AX}.\n\\]\n\nThus the triangles \\(AX'M'\\) and \\(AY'M'\\) are similar (they share the angle at \\(M'\\) and have proportional adjacent sides), which forces \\(\\angle AM'X'=\\angle AM'Y'\\). Hence \\(AM'\\) bisects the angle formed by the lines \\(X'Y'\\) and \\(Y'X'\\); but \\(X'Y'\\) and \\(Y'X'\\) are the same line \\(\\ell\\). Therefore \\(AM'\\) is perpendicular to \\(\\ell\\). \u220e\n\nApply Lemma\u202f1 to the chord \\(EC\\) of \\(\\Omega\\). Because \\(AE=AC\\) (both are radii of \\(\\Omega\\)), the point \\(A\\) is the midpoint of \\(EC\\); thus \\(A\\) plays the role of \\(M\\) in the lemma. Consequently the image \\(A'\\) (which coincides with \\(A\\) itself) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nAB'\\perp \\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C'. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe same argument applied to chord \\(FD\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) (where \\(AE=AD\\)) yields  \n\n\\[\nAB'\\perp \\ell_{\\Gamma}=B'D'. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFrom (7) we have \\(\\angle B'E'C'=90^{\\circ}\\) because \\(E'\\) lies on \\(AP\\) (the line through \\(A\\) and \\(B'\\)) and \\(C'\\) lies on \\(B'C'\\). Similarly (8) gives \\(\\angle B'F'E'=90^{\\circ}\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle B'E'C'=\\angle B'F'E'=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus the four points \\(B',E',F',C'\\) are concyclic and the line \\(AB'\\) is tangent to this circle at \\(B'\\). Denote this circle by \\(\\omega'=(B'E'F')\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Orthocentre under inversion\n\nInversion is a conformal transformation; it preserves oriented angles. Therefore a line (or circle) that is perpendicular to another line (or circle) is sent to a line (or circle) perpendicular to the image of the latter.\n\nEach altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is a line through a vertex perpendicular to the opposite side. Applying the above preservation property, the image of an altitude is an altitude of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\). Consequently the three images intersect at a single point, namely the image of the orthocentre. Hence  \n\n\\[\nH'=\\mathcal I(H) \\quad\\text{is the orthocentre of}\\quad \\triangle P'M'N'. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\). Because \\(\\ell\\) does **not** contain the centre \\(A\\), its image \\(\\ell'=\\mathcal I(\\ell)\\) is a circle passing through \\(A\\) (standard property of inversion).\n\n**Lemma 2.** *If a line \\(\\ell\\) does not pass through the centre \\(A\\) of an inversion and is parallel to a line \\(\\lambda\\) that does pass through \\(A\\), then the image \\(\\ell'\\) is a circle through \\(A\\) whose tangent at \\(A\\) is perpendicular to \\(\\lambda\\).*\n\n*Proof.* Choose a point \\(X\\in\\ell\\) and let \\(X'=\\mathcal I(X)\\). Write \\(\\theta=\\angle XAP\\) (the angle between \\(\\overrightarrow{AX}\\) and the invariant line \\(\\lambda=AP\\)). From (1),\n\n\\[\nAX\\cdot AX'=k\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad AX'=\\frac{k}{AX}.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating the relation \\(AX\\cdot AX'=k\\) with respect to the motion of \\(X\\) along \\(\\ell\\) shows that the direction of the radius \\(AX'\\) rotates with angular speed \\(-\\frac{d\\theta}{d s}\\), i.e. the angle between \\(AX'\\) and \\(\\lambda\\) equals \\(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-\\theta\\). Hence the tangent to the curve traced by the points \\(X'\\) (which is precisely \\(\\ell'\\)) at the limiting position \\(X\\to\\infty\\) (corresponding to \\(A\\)) is orthogonal to \\(\\lambda\\). \u220e\n\nApplying Lemma\u202f2 with \\(\\lambda=AP\\) we obtain:\n\n* \\(\\ell'\\) is a circle through \\(A\\);  \n* the tangent to \\(\\ell'\\) at \\(A\\) is perpendicular to \\(AP\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\), the direction of \\(\\ell\\) is the same as that of \\(AP\\). Under inversion, a direction parallel to the invariant line is transformed into a direction **perpendicular** to that line at the image point. Consequently the tangent to \\(\\ell'\\) at the second intersection with the line \\(AB'\\) is precisely the line \\(AB'\\) itself. In other words,\n\n\\[\n\\ell' \\text{ passes through } A \\text{ and } B',\\qquad \\text{and } \\operatorname{tang}_{B'}(\\ell')=AB'. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Uniqueness of a circle with a prescribed tangent\n\n**Lemma 3.** *Let \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) be distinct points. There exists at most one circle passing through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) whose tangent at \\(B\\) is a given line \\(\\tau\\) (with \\(\\tau\\neq AB\\)).*\n\n*Proof.* Suppose two circles \\(\\omega_1,\\omega_2\\) satisfy the conditions. Let \\(O_1,O_2\\) be their centres. Since the radius at a point of tangency is perpendicular to the tangent, we have \\(O_1B\\perp\\tau\\) and \\(O_2B\\perp\\tau\\); thus both centres lie on the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(B\\) perpendicular to \\(\\tau\\). Moreover, because both circles contain \\(A\\), the distances \\(O_1A\\) and \\(O_2A\\) equal the respective radii \\(O_1B\\) and \\(O_2B\\). Hence \\(A\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(O_1O_2\\). But the perpendicular bisector of a segment intersecting a line \\(\\ell\\) at a single point forces \\(O_1=O_2\\). Therefore the two circles coincide. \u220e\n\nThe circle \\(\\omega'=(B'E'F')\\) constructed in step\u202f3 passes through \\(A\\) and \\(B'\\) and, by (9), has \\(AB'\\) as its tangent at \\(B'\\). By Lemma\u202f3 the circle described in (11) must be exactly \\(\\omega'\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\ell'=\\omega'=(B'E'F'). \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Completion of the original problem\n\nInversion preserves tangency: if two circles are tangent at a point, their images are tangent at the image of that point (this follows from the conformality of inversion). Equation (12) tells us that \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega'\\) at \\(B'\\). Therefore the pre\u2011image \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the pre\u2011image of \\(\\omega'\\). The pre\u2011image of \\(\\omega'\\) is precisely the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) because the points \\(B,E,F\\) lie on the original circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) and are mapped to \\(B',E',F'\\).\n\nConsequently the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) (i.e. \\(\\ell\\)) is tangent to the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which is exactly the statement to be proved. \u220e"
