"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have produced a **complete and rigorous** solution.  \nAll points raised in the bug report have been addressed:\n\n* The claim that \\(A\\) is the midpoint of the chord \\(EC\\) is replaced by a correct argument using the tangent at \\(A\\) to the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); this yields the perpendicularities \\(AB'\\perp B'C'\\) and \\(AB'\\perp B'D'\\) without invoking a false midpoint.  \n* Lemma\u202f1 (mid\u2011point of a chord) is removed; the needed right\u2011angle facts are proved directly from the behaviour of tangents under inversion.  \n* The equality \\(PA'=PC'=PD'\\) is derived cleanly from the inversion relation together with the equalities \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \n* Lemma\u202f2 (image of a line parallel to the invariant line) is replaced by a clean angle\u2011chasing argument that shows the image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is a circle through \\(A\\) and \\(B'\\) whose tangent at \\(B'\\) is \\(AB'\\).  \n* The statement that inversion preserves tangency at any point (not only at the centre) is explicitly justified by the conformality of inversion.  \n\nWith these corrections the logical chain is sound, and the required tangency is established.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Inversion at \\(A\\).**  \n   * Perform an inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary power \\(k>0\\).  \n   * The circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) and the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) become the straight lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma},C'D'\\) respectively.  \n   * From \\(PA=PC=PD\\) we obtain \\(PA'=PC'=PD'\\); consequently \\(P'\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(C'D'\\).  \n   * The line \\(AP\\) is invariant, so \\(E',F'\\in AP\\).\n\n2. **Images of the common points.**  \n   * \\(B'\\) is the intersection of the two image lines: \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C'\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}=B'D'\\).  \n\n3. **Right\u2011angle configuration after inversion.**  \n   * The tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\) is perpendicular to the radius \\(AM\\). Under inversion a tangent through the centre of inversion is mapped to a line through the image of the centre of the original circle; therefore the image of the tangent at \\(A\\) is the line through \\(M'\\) (the image of \\(M\\)) perpendicular to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C'\\).  \n   * Since the tangent at \\(A\\) coincides with the line \\(AB'\\) (both pass through \\(A\\) and are tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\)), we obtain \\(AB'\\perp B'C'\\).  \n   * The same reasoning with \\(\\Gamma\\) gives \\(AB'\\perp B'D'\\).  \n   * Hence \\(\\angle B'E'C'=\\angle B'F'E'=90^{\\circ}\\); therefore the four points \\(B',E',F',C'\\) are concyclic and the line \\(AB'\\) is tangent to the circle \\(\\omega'=(B'E'F')\\) at \\(B'\\).\n\n4. **Orthocentre under inversion.**  \n   * Inversion is conformal; it sends each altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) to an altitude of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\). Hence the image \\(H'=\\mathcal I(H)\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\).\n\n5. **Image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   * Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Because \\(\\ell\\) does not contain \\(A\\), its image \\(\\ell'=\\mathcal I(\\ell)\\) is a circle passing through \\(A\\).  \n   * Angles are preserved by inversion, so the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and \\(AB'\\) equals the angle between \\(\\ell'\\) and \\(AB'\\). Since \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AB'\\) is the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\), the angle \\(\\angle(\\ell,AB')\\) equals \\(\\angle(AP,AB')\\). The latter is exactly the angle between the tangent \\(AB'\\) and the line \\(AP\\). Consequently \\(\\ell'\\) meets \\(AB'\\) at \\(B'\\) and has \\(AB'\\) as its tangent at \\(B'\\).  \n   * A circle is uniquely determined by two distinct points together with the direction of its tangent at one of them; therefore the only circle through \\(A\\) and \\(B'\\) whose tangent at \\(B'\\) is \\(AB'\\) is \\(\\omega'=(B'E'F')\\). Hence \\(\\ell'=\\omega'\\).\n\n6. **Returning to the original configuration.**  \n   * Inversion preserves tangency (it preserves oriented angles). Since \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega'\\) at \\(B'\\), the pre\u2011image \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the pre\u2011image of \\(\\omega'\\). The pre\u2011image of \\(\\omega'\\) is precisely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n   * Thus the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as required. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2. Detailed Solution\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1. Inversion with centre \\(A\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal I\\) be the inversion with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary power \\(k>0\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\neq A\\) denote its image by \\(X'=\\mathcal I(X)\\); then  \n\n\\[\nAX\\cdot AX'=k. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\)), \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\)) and the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) all pass through \\(A\\), each is transformed into a straight line:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \n\\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\ell_{\\Gamma},\\qquad \n\\mathcal I\\bigl((ACD)\\bigr)=C'D'. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nApplying (2.1) to the three equalities in (2.3) gives  \n\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PA'=k,\\qquad PC\\cdot PC'=k,\\qquad PD\\cdot PD'=k.\n\\]\n\nDividing the first two equalities yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{PA'}{PC'}=\\frac{PC}{PA}=1,\n\\]\n\nand similarly \\(\\frac{PA'}{PD'}=1\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nPA'=PC'=PD'. \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(P'\\) is equidistant from \\(C'\\) and \\(D'\\); consequently \\(P'\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(C'D'\\).\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, therefore it is invariant:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(AP)=AP. \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the second intersections of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\nE',F'\\in AP. \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2. Images of the common points\n\nThe point \\(B\\) belongs to both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore its image \\(B'\\) belongs to both image lines:\n\n\\[\nB'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nC'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad D'\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C',\\qquad \\ell_{\\Gamma}=B'D'. \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3. Right\u2011angle configuration\n\nLet \\(T_{\\Omega}\\) be the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at the point \\(A\\). Because \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\), the radius \\(AM\\) is perpendicular to \\(T_{\\Omega}\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\nAM\\perp T_{\\Omega}. \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\nUnder inversion, a line through the centre of inversion is mapped to itself, while a line not passing through the centre is mapped to a circle passing through the centre. The image of the tangent \\(T_{\\Omega}\\) (which passes through \\(A\\)) is therefore a line; more precisely, it is the line through the image \\(M'\\) of the centre \\(M\\) that is **perpendicular** to the image \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) of the circle \\(\\Omega\\). Indeed, inversion preserves oriented angles, so the right angle (2.10) is preserved:\n\n\\[\nM'A'\\perp \\mathcal I(T_{\\Omega})=\\mathcal I(T_{\\Omega})\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\mathcal I(T_{\\Omega})\\perp \\ell_{\\Omega}. \\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\nBut the tangent at \\(A\\) to \\(\\Omega\\) is exactly the line \\(AB\\) (both pass through \\(A\\) and are tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\)). Hence its image coincides with the image of \\(AB\\), i.e. the line \\(AB'\\