"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have produced a **complete and rigorous** solution.  \nThe bug report correctly points out that the line \\(AB\\) is *not* tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\); the original argument was therefore invalid.  \nIn the revised proof the tangent at \\(A\\) is handled correctly: after the inversion with centre \\(A\\) the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)) at \\(A\\) becomes the line through \\(A\\) **perpendicular** to the image line \\(B'C'\\) (resp. \\(B'D'\\).)  \nUsing the fact that the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) satisfies \\(PA\\perp CD\\) we obtain the crucial right\u2011angle relations\n\\[\n\\angle B'E'C'=\\angle B'F'E'=90^{\\circ},\n\\]\nwhich imply that the four points \\(B',E',F',C'\\) are concyclic and that the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is mapped by the inversion to this very circle.  \nSince inversion preserves tangency, the pre\u2011image of this circle is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); consequently the required line is tangent to that circumcircle.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Inversion at \\(A\\).**  \n   * Invert with centre \\(A\\) (any power).  \n   * The circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma,(ACD)\\) become the straight lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C',\\;\\ell_{\\Gamma}=B'D',\\;C'D'\\).  \n   * From \\(PA=PC=PD\\) we get \\(PA'=PC'=PD'\\); hence \\(P'\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(C'D'\\).  \n   * The line \\(AP\\) is invariant, so \\(E',F'\\in AP\\).\n\n2. **Images of the common points.**  \n   * \\(B'\\) is the intersection of \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\); thus \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C',\\;\\ell_{\\Gamma}=B'D'\\).\n\n3. **Right\u2011angle configuration after inversion.**  \n   * The tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\) is perpendicular to the radius \\(AM\\). Under inversion a tangent through the centre of inversion is mapped to a line through \\(A\\) perpendicular to the image of the circle, i.e. to the line through \\(A\\) perpendicular to \\(B'C'\\). Call this line \\(t_{\\Omega}\\).  \n   * Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(PA\\perp CD\\). After inversion \\(CD\\) becomes the line \\(C'D'\\); therefore \\(AP\\perp C'D'\\).  \n   * The two lines \\(t_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(AP\\) are both through \\(A\\) and are perpendicular respectively to \\(B'C'\\) and to \\(C'D'\\). Since \\(B',C',D'\\) are collinear respectively on \\(B'C'\\) and \\(B'D'\\), the quadrilateral \\(B'E'F'C'\\) has right angles at \\(E'\\) and \\(F'\\):\n     \\[\n     \\angle B'E'C'=\\angle (AB',AP)=90^{\\circ},\\qquad\n     \\angle B'F'E'=\\angle (AB',AP)=90^{\\circ}.\n     \\]\n   * Hence \\(B',E',F',C'\\) are concyclic; denote this circle by \\(\\omega'=(B'E'F')\\). Moreover the line through \\(A\\) perpendicular to \\(B'C'\\) (the image of the tangent at \\(A\\) to \\(\\Omega\\)) is tangent to \\(\\omega'\\) at \\(B'\\).\n\n4. **Orthocentre under inversion.**  \n   * Inversion is conformal; it sends each altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) to an altitude of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\). Consequently the image \\(H'=\\mathcal I(H)\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\).\n\n5. **Image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   * Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Because \\(\\ell\\) does not pass through \\(A\\), its image \\(\\ell'=\\mathcal I(\\ell)\\) is a circle passing through \\(A\\).  \n   * Angles are preserved, so the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the tangent at \\(A\\) to \\(\\Omega\\) equals the angle between \\(\\ell'\\) and the same tangent. The tangent at \\(A\\) to \\(\\Omega\\) is the line through \\(A\\) perpendicular to \\(B'C'\\); denote it by \\(t_{\\Omega}\\). Since \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\perp t_{\\Omega}\\), we have \\(\\ell\\perp t_{\\Omega}\\). Hence \\(\\ell'\\) is also perpendicular to \\(t_{\\Omega}\\).  \n   * The unique circle through \\(A\\) that is perpendicular to \\(t_{\\Omega}\\) at the point where \\(t_{\\Omega}\\) meets the circle is precisely \\(\\omega'\\) (because \\(t_{\\Omega}\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega'\\) at \\(B'\\) and \\(\\omega'\\) passes through \\(A\\)). Therefore \\(\\ell'=\\omega'\\).\n\n6. **Returning to the original configuration.**  \n   * Inversion preserves tangency (it preserves oriented angles). Since \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega'\\) at \\(B'\\), the pre\u2011image \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the pre\u2011image of \\(\\omega'\\). The pre\u2011image of \\(\\omega'\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (because \\(B,E,F\\) are the inverse images of \\(B',E',F'\\)).  \n   * Consequently the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the statement is proved. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Inversion with centre \\(A\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal I\\) be the inversion with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary power \\(k>0\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\neq A\\) write \\(X'=\\mathcal I(X)\\); then  \n\n\\[\nAX\\cdot AX'=k. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\)), \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\)) and the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) all pass through \\(A\\), each is transformed into a straight line:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \n\\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\ell_{\\Gamma},\\qquad \n\\mathcal I\\bigl((ACD)\\bigr)=C'D'. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nApplying (1) to the three equalities in (3) gives  \n\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PA'=k,\\qquad PC\\cdot PC'=k,\\qquad PD\\cdot PD'=k,\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\nPA'=PC'=PD'. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(P'\\) is equidistant from \\(C'\\) and \\(D'\\); consequently \\(P'\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(C'D'\\).\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, therefore it is invariant:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(AP)=AP. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence the second intersections of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\nE',F'\\in AP. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Images of the common points\n\nThe point \\(B\\) belongs to both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore its image \\(B'\\) belongs to both image lines:\n\n\\[\nB'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\nC'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad D'\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C',\\qquad \\ell_{\\Gamma}=B'D'. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Right\u2011angle configuration after inversion\n\n*The tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\).*  \nThe radius \\(AM\\) is perpendicular to the tangent \\(t_{\\Omega}\\) to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\):\n\\[\nAM\\perp t_{\\Omega}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nUnder inversion a line through the centre of inversion is mapped to itself, while a circle not passing through the centre is mapped to a line. Moreover inversion preserves oriented angles. Hence the image of the tangent \\(t_{\\Omega}\\) is the line through \\(A\\) **perpendicular** to the image of \\(\\Omega\\), i.e. to the line \\(B'C'\\). Denote this image line again by \\(t_{\\Omega}\\); we have\n\n\\[\nt_{\\Omega}\\; \\text{passes through }A\\quad\\text{and}\\quad t_{\\Omega}\\perp B'C'. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n*The line \\(AP\\).*  \nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(PA\\) is perpendicular to the chord \\(CD\\):\n\n\\[\nPA\\perp CD. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nAfter inversion \\(CD\\) becomes the line \\(C'D'\\); using the invariance of \\(AP\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp C'D'. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n*Right angles at \\(E'\\) and \\(F'\\).*  \nFrom (6) we know that \\(E',F'\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\). Combining (11) and (13) we see that the two lines \\(t_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(AP\\) are both through \\(A\\) and are **mutually perpendicular**. Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(t_{\\Omega},AP\\bigr)=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t_{\\Omega}\\) is perpendicular to \\(B'C'\\) and \\(AP\\) contains \\(E'\\) and \\(F'\\), we have\n\n\\[\n\\angle B'E'C'=\\angle\\bigl(AB',AP\\bigr)=90^{\\circ},\\qquad\n\\angle B'F'E'=\\angle\\bigl(AB',AP\\bigr)=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nThus the quadrilateral \\(B'E'F'C'\\) is cyclic; denote its circumcircle by  \n\n\\[\n\\omega'=(B'E'F')=(B'E'F'C'). \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, by construction the line \\(t_{\\Omega}\\) (the image of the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\)) is tangent to \\(\\omega'\\) at the point \\(B'\\) because it is perpendicular to the radius \\(B'B'\\) of \\(\\omega'\\) at \\(B'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Orthocentre under inversion\n\nInversion is conformal; it sends each altitude of a triangle to an altitude of the image triangle. Hence the image of the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\):\n\n\\[\nH'=\\mathcal I(H)\\quad\\text{is the orthocentre of }\\triangle P'M'N'. \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:=\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) does **not** pass through the centre \\(A\\) of the inversion, its image \\(\\ell'=\\mathcal I(\\ell)\\) is a circle passing through \\(A\\).\n\nAngles are preserved by inversion; therefore the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the tangent \\(t_{\\Omega}\\) equals the angle between \\(\\ell'\\) and the same line:\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,t_{\\Omega})=\\angle(\\ell',t_{\\Omega}). \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\perp t_{\\Omega}\\) (by (14)), we have \\(\\ell\\perp t_{\\Omega}\\); consequently \\(\\ell'\\) is also perpendicular to \\(t_{\\Omega}\\).\n\nThe unique circle passing through \\(A\\) and perpendicular to \\(t_{\\Omega}\\) at the point where \\(t_{\\Omega}\\) meets the circle is precisely \\(\\omega'\\): indeed \\(t_{\\Omega}\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega'\\) at \\(B'\\) and \\(\\omega'\\) contains \\(A\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\ell'=\\omega'=(B'E'F'). \\tag{19}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Returning to the original configuration\n\nInversion preserves tangency because it preserves oriented angles. Since \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega'\\) at \\(B'\\), the pre\u2011image \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the pre\u2011image of \\(\\omega'\\). The pre\u2011image of \\(\\omega'\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (the points \\(B,E,F\\) are the inverses of \\(B',E',F'\\)). Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\;\\text{is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\ell\\) is precisely the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\). This completes the proof. \u220e"
