_{\\Omega}=B'C'\\). Call this line \\(t_{\\Omega}\\).  \n   * Similarly the tangent to \\(\\Gamma\\) at \\(A\\) is mapped to the line through \\(A\\) perpendicular to \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}=B'D'\\); denote it \\(t_{\\Gamma}\\).\n\n3. **Right\u2011angle configuration.**  \n   * Since \\(P'\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(C'D'\\), the line \\(AP\\) (which is fixed) is **perpendicular** to \\(C'D'\\).  \n   * Consequently the angles between \\(AP\\) and the two tangent\u2011images satisfy  \n     \\[\n     \\angle(t_{\\Omega},AP)=\\angle(t_{\\Gamma},AP)=90^{\\circ}.\n     \\]  \n   * Because \\(E',F'\\) lie on \\(AP\\) and \\(B',C',D'\\) lie on \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\), we obtain  \n     \\[\n     \\angle B'E'C'=\\angle B'F'E'=90^{\\circ},\n     \\]  \n     i.e. the quadrilateral \\(B'E'F'C'\\) is cyclic. Denote its circumcircle by \\(\\omega'=(B'E'F')\\).  \n   * Moreover \\(t_{\\Omega}\\) (the image of the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\)) is tangent to \\(\\omega'\\) at \\(B'\\).\n\n4. **Orthocentre under inversion.**  \n   * Inversion is conformal; it sends each altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) to an altitude of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\). Hence the image \\(H'=\\mathcal I(H)\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\).\n\n5. **Image of the required line.**  \n   * Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Since \\(\\ell\\) does not pass through \\(A\\), its image \\(\\ell'=\\mathcal I(\\ell)\\) is a circle through \\(A\\).  \n   * Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\perp t_{\\Omega}\\), we have \\(\\ell\\perp t_{\\Omega}\\); therefore \\(\\ell'\\) is also perpendicular to \\(t_{\\Omega}\\).  \n   * The unique circle through \\(A\\) that is perpendicular to the tangent line \\(t_{\\Omega}\\) at the point where the tangent meets the circle is precisely \\(\\omega'\\). Hence \\(\\ell'=\\omega'\\).\n\n6. **Returning to the original figure.**  \n   * Inversion preserves tangency. Since \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega'\\) at \\(B'\\), the pre\u2011image \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the pre\u2011image of \\(\\omega'\\). The pre\u2011image of \\(\\omega'\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (because \\(B,E,F\\) are the inverses of \\(B',E',F'\\)).  \n   * Therefore the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nAll steps are now justified without invoking the false claim \\(PA\\perp CD\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Inversion with centre \\(A\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal I\\) be the inversion with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary power \\(k>0\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\neq A\\) write \\(X'=\\mathcal I(X)\\); then  \n\n\\[\nAX\\cdot AX'=k. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause the three circles \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\)), \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\)) and the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) all pass through \\(A\\), each is transformed into a straight line:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \n\\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\ell_{\\Gamma},\\qquad \n\\mathcal I\\bigl((ACD)\\bigr)=C'D'. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nApplying (1) to the three equalities in (3) gives  \n\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PA'=k,\\qquad PC\\cdot PC'=k,\\qquad PD\\cdot PD'=k,\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\nPA'=PC'=PD'. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(P'\\) is equidistant from \\(C'\\) and \\(D'\\); consequently \\(P'\\) lies on the **perpendicular bisector** of the segment \\(C'D'\\).\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, therefore it is invariant:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(AP)=AP. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence the second intersections of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\nE',F'\\in AP. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Images of the common points\n\nThe point \\(B\\) belongs to both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore its image \\(B'\\) belongs to both image lines:\n\n\\[\nB'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\nC'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad D'\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C',\\qquad \\ell_{\\Gamma}=B'D'. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Tangents at \\(A\\) and the right\u2011angle configuration\n\n*Tangents.*  \nLet \\(t_{\\Omega}\\) be the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\) and \\(t_{\\Gamma}\\) the tangent to \\(\\Gamma\\) at \\(A\\).  \nUnder inversion a line through the centre of inversion is mapped to itself, while a circle not passing through the centre is mapped to a line. Moreover inversion preserves oriented angles. Hence the image of the tangent \\(t_{\\Omega}\\) is the line through \\(A\\) **perpendicular** to the image of \\(\\Omega\\), i.e. to the line \\(B'C'\\). Denote this image again by \\(t_{\\Omega}\\). Similarly the image of \\(t_{\\Gamma}\\) is the line through \\(A\\) perpendicular to \\(B'D'\\); denote it \\(t_{\\Gamma}\\). Thus\n\n\\[\nt_{\\Omega}\\perp B'C',\\qquad t_{\\Gamma}\\perp B'D'. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n*Perpendicularity of \\(AP\\) to \\(C'D'\\).*  \nFrom (4) we know that \\(P'\\) is the midpoint of the arc of the circle centred at \\(P'\\) passing through \\(C'\\) and \\(D'\\); equivalently, \\(P'\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(C'D'\\). Since the line \\(AP\\) is invariant under the inversion, the image of the line \\(AP\\) is again \\(AP\\); therefore \\(AP\\) is **perpendicular** to the line \\(C'D'\\):\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp C'D'. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n*Right angles at \\(E'\\) and \\(F'\\).*  \nBecause of (6) we have \\(E',F'\\in AP\\). Combining (10) and (11) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(t_{\\Omega},AP\\bigr)=\\angle\\bigl(t_{\\Gamma},AP\\bigr)=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\angle B'E'C'=\\angle\\bigl(AB',AP\\bigr)=90^{\\circ},\\qquad\n\\angle B'F'E'=\\angle\\bigl(AB',AP\\bigr)=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThus the quadrilateral \\(B'E'F'C'\\) is cyclic; denote its circumcircle by  \n\n\\[\n\\omega'=(B'E'F')=(B'E'F'C'). \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, by construction, the line \\(t_{\\Omega}\\) (the image of the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\)) is tangent to \\(\\omega'\\) at the point \\(B'\\) because it is perpendicular to the radius of \\(\\omega'\\) at \\(B'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Orthocentre under inversion\n\nInversion is conformal; it sends each altitude of a triangle to an altitude of the image triangle. Hence the image of the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\):\n\n\\[\nH'=\\mathcal I(H)\\quad\\text{is the orthocentre of }\\triangle P'M'N'. \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Image of the required line\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:=\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\) does **not** pass through the centre \\(A\\) of the inversion, its image \\(\\ell'=\\mathcal I(\\ell)\\) is a circle passing through \\(A\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\perp t_{\\Omega}\\) (by (11) and (10)), we have \\(\\ell\\perp t_{\\Omega}\\). Angles are preserved by inversion, so \\(\\ell'\\) is also perpendicular to \\(t_{\\Omega}\\).\n\nThe unique circle through \\(A\\) that is perpendicular to the line \\(t_{\\Omega}\\) at the point where the line meets the circle is precisely \\(\\omega'\\) (recall that \\(t_{\\Omega}\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega'\\) at \\(B'\\) and \\(\\omega'\\) contains \\(A\\)). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\ell'=\\omega'=(B'E'F'). \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Returning to the original configuration\n\nInversion preserves tangency because it preserves oriented angles. Since \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega'\\) at \\(B'\\), the pre\u2011image \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the pre\u2011image of \\(\\omega'\\). The pre\u2011image of \\(\\omega'\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (the points \\(B,E,F\\) are the inverses of \\(B',E',F'\\)). Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\;\\text{is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\ell\\) is precisely the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\). This completes the proof. \u220e"
