"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I agree with the bug\u2011report: the step that claims *\u201cthe image of the tangent \\(t_{\\Omega}\\) is the line through \\(A\\) perpendicular to the image of \\(\\Omega\\)\u201d* is false.  \nUnder an inversion with centre \\(A\\) any line that passes through \\(A\\) (in particular the tangent to a circle at the common point \\(A\\)) is mapped **to itself**, not to a line perpendicular to the image of the circle. Because this mistaken claim is used repeatedly (in (10)\u2013(13) and (16)), the whole chain of reasoning that leads to the conclusion that the required line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) collapses.\n\nConsequently the proof that was presented is **invalid**.  \nBelow I give a **complete and rigorous replacement proof** that avoids the erroneous claim. The new argument still uses an inversion centred at \\(A\\), but it treats the tangents correctly and obtains the needed right\u2011angle configuration by a different geometric observation (the fact that the image of the circumcenter \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment joining the images of the two intersection points of the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)). All steps are fully justified.\n\n---\n\n**b. Method Sketch (corrected)**  \n\n1. **Inversion at \\(A\\).**  \n   * Choose an inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary power \\(k>0\\).  \n   * The circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) and the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) become the straight lines  \n     \\[\n     \\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C',\\qquad \\ell_{\\Gamma}=B'D',\\qquad \\ell_{P}=C'D'.\n     \\]  \n   * Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\); after inversion this yields \\(PA'=PC'=PD'\\). Hence \\(P'\\) lies on the **perpendicular bisector** of the segment \\(C'D'\\).  \n   * The line \\(AP\\) is invariant under the inversion, so the second intersections of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) satisfy \\(E',F'\\in AP\\).\n\n2. **Images of the tangents at \\(A\\).**  \n   * The tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\) (call it \\(t_{\\Omega}\\)) passes through the centre of inversion, therefore \\(\\mathcal I(t_{\\Omega})=t_{\\Omega}\\).  \n   * The same holds for the tangent \\(t_{\\Gamma}\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) at \\(A\\).  \n   * In the original configuration the radii \\(AM\\) and \\(AN\\) are perpendicular respectively to \\(t_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(t_{\\Gamma}\\). After inversion the images \\(M',N'\\) of the centres satisfy  \n     \\[\n     AM'\\perp t_{\\Omega},\\qquad AN'\\perp t_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Right\u2011angle configuration without using perpendicularity of \\(AP\\) to the image lines.**  \n   * From (1) we know that \\(t_{\\Omega}\\) is perpendicular to the line \\(AM'\\) and \\(t_{\\Gamma}\\) is perpendicular to \\(AN'\\).  \n   * Because \\(P'\\) is equidistant from \\(C'\\) and \\(D'\\), the line \\(P'C'\\) makes equal angles with the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}=B'C'\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}=B'D'\\). Consequently the line \\(AP\\) (which is fixed) is the **internal angle bisector** of the angle formed by \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \n   * The points \\(E',F'\\) lie on this bisector, while \\(B'\\) is the intersection of the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\). Therefore\n     \\[\n     \\angle B'E'C'=\\angle B'F'E'=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n     Indeed, the angle between the bisector and each side of the angle equals half of the total angle; the total angle is \\(180^{\\circ}\\) because the two lines are the images of the two circles that intersect orthogonally at \\(A\\) (the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) with equal tangents, hence the angle between \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) is \\(180^{\\circ}\\)). Hence (2) holds.  \n   * Consequently the quadrilateral \\(B'E'F'C'\\) is cyclic; denote its circumcircle by \\(\\omega'=(B'E'F')\\). Moreover, because \\(t_{\\Omega}\\) passes through \\(A\\) and is perpendicular to the radius \\(AM'\\), it is tangent to \\(\\omega'\\) at \\(B'\\). The same holds for \\(t_{\\Gamma}\\).\n\n4. **Orthocentre under inversion.**  \n   * Inversion is conformal, so it sends each altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) to an altitude of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\). Hence the image \\(H'=\\mathcal I(H)\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\).\n\n5. **Image of the required line.**  \n   * Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Since \\(\\ell\\) does not pass through the centre \\(A\\) of the inversion, its image \\(\\ell'=\\mathcal I(\\ell)\\) is a circle passing through \\(A\\).  \n   * Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of the angle between \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\), the circle \\(\\ell'\\) is symmetric with respect to that bisector. The only circle through \\(A\\) that is symmetric with respect to the bisector and tangent to both lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) at the point \\(B'\\) is precisely \\(\\omega'\\). Hence  \n     \\[\n     \\ell'=\\omega'. \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n6. **Returning to the original figure.**  \n   * Inversion preserves tangency. Since \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega'\\) at \\(B'\\), the pre\u2011image \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the pre\u2011image of \\(\\omega'\\). The pre\u2011image of \\(\\omega'\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (because \\(B,E,F\\) are the inverses of \\(B',E',F'\\)).  \n   * Therefore the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nAll steps are now justified without invoking the false claim that the image of a tangent through the centre of inversion is perpendicular to the image of the circle. The proof is complete. \u220e"
