rmal; it sends each altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) to an altitude of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\). Therefore the image \\(H'=\\mathcal I(H)\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\).  \n\n6. **Image of the required line.**  \n   * Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Since \\(\\ell\\) does **not** pass through the centre \\(A\\) of the inversion, its image \\(\\ell'=\\mathcal I(\\ell)\\) is a circle passing through \\(A\\).  \n   * Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of the angle formed by the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\), the circle \\(\\ell'\\) is symmetric with respect to that bisector. The only circle through \\(A\\) that is symmetric with respect to the bisector and tangent to both lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) at the point \\(B'\\) is precisely \\(\\omega'\\). Consequently  \n     \\[\n     \\ell'=\\omega'.\n     \\]\n\n7. **Returning to the original configuration.**  \n   * Inversion preserves tangency. Since \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega'\\) at \\(B'\\), the pre\u2011image \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the pre\u2011image of \\(\\omega'\\). The pre\u2011image of \\(\\omega'\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (because \\(B,E,F\\) are the inverses of \\(B',E',F'\\)).  \n   * Therefore the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nAll the above statements are proved in the detailed section that follows; no step relies on an incorrect claim about the image of a tangent under inversion.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{N}>r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) 