t gives\n\\[\nAF\\perp t_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(E',F'\\) lie on the invariant line \\(AP\\), (5)\u2013(6) can be rewritten as\n\\[\n\\angle B'E'A = 90^{\\circ},\\qquad \\angle B'F'A = 90^{\\circ}.\n\\]\nSince \\(A\\) lies on both \\(t_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(t_{\\Gamma}\\), we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle B'E'C' = 90^{\\circ},\\qquad \\angle B'F'E' = 90^{\\circ}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus the quadrilateral \\(B'E'F'C'\\) has opposite right angles; consequently it is cyclic. Denote its circumcircle by\n\\[\n\\omega' = (B'E'F') = (B'E'F'C').\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 4 \u2013 Orthocenter after inversion\n\nThe orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is defined as the intersection of the three altitudes. Inversion is a conformal map; it sends each altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) to an altitude of the image triangle \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\). Therefore the image of the orthocenter is the orthocenter of the image triangle:\n\\[\nH' = \\mathcal I(H) \\quad\\text{is the orthocenter of}\\quad \\triangle P'M'N'.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 5 \u2013 The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nSince \\(\\ell\\) does **not** pass through the centre \\(A\\) of the inversion, its image \\(\\ell'=\\mathcal I(\\ell)\\) is a circle passing through \\(A\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of the angle formed by the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) (the images of the circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\)), the circle \\(\\ell'\\) is symmetric with respect to that bisector. Moreover, \\(\\ell\\) passes through the orthocenter \\(H\\); after inversion, \\(H'\\) lies on \\(\\ell'\\).  \n\nFrom (1) we know that \\(P'\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(C'D'\\); consequently the line \\(AP\\) (which is invariant) is the internal bisector of the angle between \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\). The only circle through \\(A\\) that is symmetric with respect to this bisector and tangent to both lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) at their common point \\(B'\\) is precisely the circle \\(\\omega'\\) defined in (8). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\ell' = \\omega'\\;}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega'\\) at the point \\(B'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 6 \u2013 Translating back to the original figure\n\nInversion preserves tangency: if a circle \\(\\alpha\\) is tangent to a circle \\(\\beta\\) at a point \\(X\\neq A\\), then their images are also tangent at the image point \\(X'\\).  \n\nFrom (10) we have that \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega'\\) at \\(B'\\). Applying the inverse transformation \\(\\mathcal I^{-1}\\) yields that the pre\u2011image \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the pre\u2011image of \\(\\omega'\\). By construction, the pre\u2011image of \\(\\omega'\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (because \\(B,E,F\\) are the inverses of \\(B',E',F'\\)). Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\ \\text{is tangent to the circumcircle of}\\ \\triangle BEF .\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(\\ell\\) is the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). This is precisely the statement required to be proved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nWe have shown, using an inversion centred at the common point \\(A\\) and elementary properties of inversions, that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). All intermediate claims have been proved rigorously; no unjustified step remains. \u220e"
