t.)\n\n4. **Replacing \\(\\ell\\) by \\(AP\\).**  \n   Using the parallelism (3) in (4) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell\\text{ tangent to }(BEF)\n   \\iff \n   \\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the original statement is equivalent to the single angle equality (1).\n\n5. **Conclusion of the reduction.**  \n   The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) does not appear any more in (1); the only remaining geometric objects are the points \\(A,B,E,F\\) that are defined solely by the two given circles and the line \\(AP\\). Consequently, proving (1) will settle the problem.\n\nThe remainder of the answer consists of a **complete, rigorous proof of the equivalence (5)**, i.e. of the lemma stated above.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the notation be as in the sketch.  \nWe prove:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\ell\\text{ (the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{) is tangent to }(BEF)\n\\iff \\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle BFE\\;}\n\\tag{L}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1 (Tangency criterion).**  \nFor any point \\(T\\) and any chord \\(XY\\) of a circle \\(\\omega\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{the line }t\\text{ through }T\\text{ is tangent to }\\omega\n\\iff \n\\angle(t,XY)=\\angle XZY,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(Z\\) is the point of \\(\\omega\\) opposite the chord \\(XY\\).\n\n*Proof.* This is the well\u2011known \u201ctangent\u2013chord\u201d theorem; it follows directly from the equality of the angle between a tangent and a chord with the angle in the alternate segment. \u220e\n\n\n\n**Lemma 2 (Parallelism preserves oriented angles).**  \nIf two directed lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2}\\) are parallel, then for any third directed line \\(u\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell_{1},u)=\\angle(\\ell_{2},u).\n\\]\n\n*Proof.* Parallel lines have the same direction, hence the oriented angle they form with any third line coincides. \u220e\n\n\n\n**Proof of (L).**  \n\n1. By construction, \\(\\ell\\)