gle(AP,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the original statement is equivalent to proving the single angle equality (\u2605).\n\n5. **Remaining task.**  \n   The problem is therefore reduced to establishing (\u2605) for the points defined above. All geometric objects involved in (\u2605) are determined solely by the two given circles and the line \\(AP\\); the orthocenter \\(H\\) no longer appears.\n\nThe detailed proof of the reduction is given below.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the notation be as introduced in the sketch.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Tangent\u2013chord theorem)\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(\\omega\\) be a circle, \\(X,Y\\) two distinct points on \\(\\omega\\) and \\(Z\\) the third point of \\(\\omega\\) distinct from \\(X,Y\\). For any line \\(t\\) through a point \\(T\\),\n\n\\[\nt\\text{ is tangent to }\\omega\\text{ at }X\n\\iff \n\\angle(t,XY)=\\angle XZY .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe angle between a tangent at a point of a circle and a chord through that point equals the angle in the alternate segment subtended by the chord. This is a classical result and follows immediately from the inscribed\u2011angle theorem applied to the triangle formed by the centre of \\(\\omega\\), the point of tangency, and the other endpoint of the chord. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Parallel lines give equal oriented angles)\n\n*Statement.*  \nIf directed lines \\(\\ell_{1}\\) and \\(\\ell_{2}\\) are parallel, then for any directed line \\(u\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell_{1},u)=\\angle(\\ell_{2},u).\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nParallel lines have the same direction; consequently the oriented angle they make with any third line coincides. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Reduction to an angle equality)\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\). Then\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle }(BEF)\n\\iff \n\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n\\tag{\u2605}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  