ll_{1},u)=\\angle(\\ell_{2},u).\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nParallel lines have the same direction; consequently the oriented angle they make with any third line coincides. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Reduction to an angle equality)\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\). Then\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle }(BEF)\n\\iff \n\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n\\tag{\u2605}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. By construction \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\).\n\n2. Apply Lemma\u202f1 to the circle \\((BEF)\\) with chord \\(BE\\).  \n   The line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) (at the point where it meets the circle) **iff**\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), Lemma\u202f2 gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE). \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. Substituting (3) into (2) yields exactly (\u2605). \u220e\n\n\n\nThus the original problem is equivalent to establishing (\u2605). No further use of the orthocenter \\(H\\) is required; the statement depends only on the points \\(A,B,E,F\\) defined from the two given circles and the line \\(AP\\).\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe reduction above is complete and rigorous. Proving the angle equality  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle BFE\n\\]\n\nfor the configuration described in the problem will finish the proof that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). At present this final step remains unproved.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
