\\) such that \\(AA'\\) is a diameter; thus \\(A'\\) is the antipode of \\(A\\). Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) passes through \\(A'\\). Consequently for any line \\(u\\) through \\(A\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,u)=\\angle(AP,u). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Reduction to a single angle equality\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). By Lemma\u202f1 applied to the circle \\((BEF)\\) with chord \\(BE\\),\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\text{ is tangent to }(BEF)\n\\iff\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), Lemma\u202f2 gives \\(\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)\\). Substituting into (6) yields the equivalence\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\text{ tangent to }(BEF)\n\\iff\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle BFE\\;}. \\tag{\u2605}\n\\]\n\nThus it remains to prove (\u2605).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Proof of the angle equality (\u2605)\n\n1. **Spiral similarity.**  \n   By Lemma\u202f3 there is a spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) centred at \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) and satisfying \\(\\mathcal{S}(C)=E,\\;\\mathcal{S}(D)=F\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CAE=\\angle DAF,\\qquad\n   \\frac{AE}{AC}=\\frac{AF}{AD}. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Right angle transferred.**  \n   From Lemma\u202f4 we have \\(PA\\perp CD\\). Applying \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) to the right angle at \\(A\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(EA,AF)=\\angle(CA,AD)=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore quadrilateral \\(AEFD\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(AF\\); in particular  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle AEF=\\angle ADF=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Similarity of triangles \\(ACE\\) and \\(DAF\\).**  \n   From (7) we have equality of one pair of angles and of the included side ratios, whence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\triangle ACE\\sim\\triangle DAF. \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{CE}{DF}=\\frac{AE}{AF}. \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Relation between the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\).**  \n   Because \\(B\\) lies on both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CBA=\\angle DBA \\qquad\\text{(inscribed angles subtending the same arcs }CA\\text{ and }DA). \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   Adding \\(\\angle BAE\\) to both sides of (12) and using \\(\\angle CBA+\\angle BAE=\\angle CBE\\) and \\(\\angle DBA+\\angle BAF=\\angle DBF\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CBE=\\angle DBF. \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   Applying the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) to (13) (which sends \\(C\\mapsto E\\) and \\(D\\mapsto F\\)) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle EBE=\\angle FBF. \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   Since the left\u2011hand side of (14) is zero, the equality tells us that \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) sends the line \\(BE\\) to the line \\(BF\\); i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{S}(BE)=BF. \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the directed angles that \\(AP\\) makes with these two lines are equal:\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle(AP,BF). \\tag{16}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Use of Lemma\u202f5.**  \n   Lemma\u202f5 tells us that for any line \\(u\\) through \\(A\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(AP,u)=\\angle(\\ell,u). \\tag{17}\n   \\]\n\n   Taking \\(u=BF\\) and recalling that \\(BF\\) is a chord of the circle \\((BEF)\\), we have\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(AP,BF)=\\angle(\\ell,BF). \\tag{18}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(\\ell\\) meets the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point opposite \\(B\\) on that circle (the antipode of \\(B\\) with respect to \\((BEF)\\)), the tangent\u2013chord theorem (Lemma\u202f1) gives\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BF)=\\angle BFE. \\tag{19}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Conclusion of the angle equality.**  \n   Combining (16), (18) and (19) we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle(AP,BF)=\\angle BFE,\n   \\]\n\n   which is precisely the equality (\u2605).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final step\n\nBy