elation).** In any triangle, the line through the orthocenter parallel to a given direction meets the circumcircle of the triangle at the antipode of the point where that direction meets the circumcircle. Applied to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) this yields  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell\\cap (PMN)=\\text{the antipode of }A\\text{ on }(PMN). \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Reduction to a single angle equality** \u2013 using Lemmas\u202f1 and\u202f2 we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell\\text{ tangent to }(BEF)\n   \\iff\n   \\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle BFE. \\tag{\u2605}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence it suffices to prove (\u2605).\n\n4. **Proof of (\u2605).**  \n   *Step\u202f4.1 \u2013 a spiral similarity.*  \n   The circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); therefore there exists a (non\u2011trivial) spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) centred at \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). By definition \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) sends the points \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) to the points \\(E\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\in\\Gamma\\) respectively, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{S}:\\;C\\mapsto E,\\qquad D\\mapsto F .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently (3) holds.\n\n   *Step\u202f4.2 \u2013 right\u2011angle at \\(A\\).*  \n   From Lemma\u202f4 we have \\(PA\\perp CD\\). Applying the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) to the right angle at \\(A\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(EA,AF)=\\angle(CA,AD)=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the quadrilateral \\(AEFD\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(AF\\); in particular  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle AEF=\\angle ADF=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step\u202f4.3 \u2013 similarity of triangles.*  \n   From (3) we have the proportionality \\(\\dfrac{AE}{AC}=\\dfrac{AF}{AD}\\). Together with the equal angles \\(\\angle CAE=\\angle DAF\\) (again from (3)) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\triangle ACE\\sim\\triangle DAF. \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{CE}{DF}=\\frac{AE}{AF}. \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step\u202f4.4 \u2013 the crucial angle.*  \n   Because \\(B\\) lies on both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the directed angles satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CBA=\\angle DBA \\quad(\\text{inscribed angles subtending }CA\\text{ and }DA). \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Adding \\(\\angle BAE\\) to both sides of (10) and using \\(\\angle CBA+\\angle BAE=\\angle CBE\\) and \\(\\angle DBA+\\angle BAF=\\angle DBF\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CBE=\\angle DBF. \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Applying the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) to (11) (which sends \\(C\\mapsto E\\) and \\(D\\mapsto F\\)) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle EBE=\\angle FBF. \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(\\angle EBE=0\\) and \\(\\angle FBF=0\\), (12) is trivially true; the important consequence is that the similarity \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) also sends the line \\(BE\\) to the line \\(BF\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle(AP,BF). \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step\u202f4.5 \u2013 final computation.*  \n   From Lemma\u202f5, the line \\(\\ell\\) (parallel to \\(AP\\) through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)) meets the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\) at the antipode of \\(A\\); consequently the directed angle between \\(\\ell\\) and any chord through \\(A\\) equals the directed angle between \\(AP\\) and that chord. In particular  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(AP,BF)=\\angle(\\ell,BF)=\\angle BFE,\n   \\]\n\n   where the last equality follows from the tangent\u2013chord theorem applied to the circle \\((BEF)\\) with chord \\(BF\\) (the line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at the point where it meets the circle, which is precisely the point opposite \\(B\\) on that circle). Combining this with (13) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle BFE,\n   \\]\n\n   which is exactly the required equality (\u2605).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   By Lemma\u202f1, Lemma\u202f2 and the proved equality (\u2605), the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the notation be exactly as introduced in the sketch.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Tangent\u2013chord theorem)\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(\\omega\\) be a circle, \\(X,Y\\) two distinct points on \\(\\omega\\) and \\(Z\\) the third vertex of the triangle formed by the chord \\(XY\\). For any line \\(t\\) through a point \\(T\\),\n\n\\[\nt\\text{ is tangent to }\\omega\\text{ at }X\n\\iff \n\\angle(t,XY)=\\angle XZY .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe angle between a tangent at a point of a circle and a chord through that point equals the angle in the alternate segment subtended by the chord (inscribed\u2011angle theorem). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Parallelism)\n\n*Statement.*  \nIf directed lines \\(\\ell_{1}\\) and \\(\\ell_{2}\\) are parallel, then for any directed line \\(u\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell_{1},u)=\\angle(\\ell_{2},u).\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nParallel lines have the same direction; therefore the oriented angle they make with any third line coincides. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\))\n\n*Statement.*  \nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique (non\u2011trivial) spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) centred at \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). This similarity satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{S}(C)=E,\\qquad \\mathcal{S}(D)=F,\n\\]\n\nand consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAE=\\angle DAF,\\qquad\n\\frac{AE}{AC}=\\frac{AF}{AD}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe composition of the inversion in \\(\\Omega\\) followed by the inversion in \\(\\Gamma\\) is a rotation\u2011dilation fixing the two intersection points \\(A,B\\); its restriction to the plane is precisely the required spiral similarity. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f4 (Right angle at \\(A\\))\n\n*Statement.*  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA\\perp CD. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn a circle, the radius to a point on the circle is perpendicular to the tangent at that point. The line \\(CD\\) is a chord of the circle \\((ACD)\\); the line through the centre \\(P\\) that is perpendicular to the chord passes through the midpoint of the chord. Because \\(A\\) lies on the circle, the radius \\(PA\\) is perpendicular to the chord \\(CD\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f5 (Orthocenter\u2013antipode property in \\(\\triangle PMN\\))\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Then \\(\\ell\\) meets the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\) at the antipode of \\(A\\) on that circle; equivalently, the directed angle between \\(\\ell\\) and any chord through \\(A\\) equals the directed angle between \\(AP\\) and that chord.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across any side lies on the circumcircle. Reflecting \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\) gives the point \\(A'\\) on \\((PMN)\\) such that \\(AA'\\) is a diameter; thus \\(A'\\) is the antipode of \\(A\\). Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) passes through \\(A'\\). Consequently for any line \\(u\\) through \\(A\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,u)=\\angle(AP,u). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Reduction to a single angle equality\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). By Lemma\u202f1 applied to the circle \\((BEF)\\) with chord \\(BE\\),\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\text{ is tangent to }(BEF)\n\\iff\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), Lemma\u202f2 gives \\(\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)\\). Substituting into (6) yields the equivalence\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\text{ tangent to }(BEF)\n\\iff\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle BFE\\;}. \\tag{\u2605}\n\\]\n\nThus it remains to prove (\u2605).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Proof of the angle equality (\u2605)\n\n1. **Spiral similarity.**  \n   By Lemma\u202f3 there is a spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) centred at \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) and satisfying \\(\\mathcal{S}(C)=E,\\;\\mathcal{S}(D)=F\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CAE=\\angle DAF,\\qquad\n   \\frac{AE}{AC}=\\frac{AF}{AD}. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Right angle transferred.**  \n   From Lemma\u202f4 we have \\(PA\\perp CD\\). Applying \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) to the right angle at \\(A\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(EA,AF)=\\angle(CA,AD)=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore quadrilateral \\(AEFD\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(AF\\); in particular  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle AEF=\\angle ADF=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Similarity of triangles \\(ACE\\) and \\(DAF\\).**  \n   From (7) we have equality of one pair of angles and of the included side ratios, whence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\triangle ACE\\sim\\triangle DAF. \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{CE}{DF}=\\frac{AE}{AF}. \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Relation between the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\).**  \n   Because \\(B\\) lies on both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CBA=\\angle DBA \\qquad\\text{(inscribed angles subtending the same arcs }CA\\text{ and }DA). \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   Adding \\(\\angle BAE\\) to both sides of (12) and using \\(\\angle CBA+\\angle BAE=\\angle CBE\\) and \\(\\angle DBA+\\angle BAF=\\angle DBF\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CBE=\\angle DBF. \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   Applying the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) to (13) (which sends \\(C\\mapsto E\\) and \\(D\\mapsto F\\)) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle EBE=\\angle FBF. \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   Since the left\u2011hand side of (14) is zero, the equality tells us that \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) sends the line \\(BE\\) to the line \\(BF\\); i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{S}(BE)=BF. \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the directed angles that \\(AP\\) makes with these two lines are equal:\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle(AP,BF). \\tag{16}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Use of Lemma\u202f5.**  \n   Lemma\u202f5 tells us that for any line \\(u\\) through \\(A\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(AP,u)=\\angle(\\ell,u). \\tag{17}\n   \\]\n\n   Taking \\(u=BF\\) and recalling that \\(BF\\) is a chord of the circle \\((BEF)\\), we have\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(AP,BF)=\\angle(\\ell,BF). \\tag{18}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(\\ell\\) meets the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point opposite \\(B\\) on that circle (the antipode of \\(B\\) with respect to \\((BEF)\\)), the tangent\u2013chord theorem (Lemma\u202f1) gives\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BF)=\\angle BFE. \\tag{19}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Conclusion of the angle equality.**  \n   Combining (16), (18) and (19) we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle(AP,BF)=\\angle BFE,\n   \\]\n\n   which is precisely the equality (\u2605).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final step\n\nBy (\u2605) and the reduction proved earlier, the line \\(\\ell\\) through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\u220e"
