e angle equality.**  \n   By the tangent\u2011chord theorem, the line \\(\\ell\\) will be tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) iff  \n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE. \\tag{S7}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), Lemma\u202f2 (parallelism) gives \\(\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)\\).  Using (S5) we may replace \\(\\angle(AP,BE)\\) by \\(\\angle(AP,BF)\\).  Finally, by (S6) the angle \\(\\angle(AP,BF)\\) equals \\(\\angle BFE\\).  Thus (S7) holds, proving the required tangency.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The corrected Lemma\u202f4 (removing the false perpendicular claim) and the corrected Lemma\u202f5 (giving the exact relationship between the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) and the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\)) together with the spiral\u2011similarity arguments above complete the logical chain needed for the final tangency statement.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nBelow are the rigorous statements and proofs of the two lemmas that were missing or incorrect in the earlier attempt.  They constitute the essential backbone of a correct solution; the remaining angle\u2011chasing steps follow the outline given in the Method Sketch.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4 (corrected)\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  Then \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  The line \\(AP\\) is **not** generally perpendicular to the side \\(CD\\); rather, the *perpendicular bisector* of \\(CD\\) passes through \\(P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy definition of a circumcenter, \\(P\\) is equidistant from the three vertices \\(A,C,D\\); hence \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  The set of points equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) is the perpendicular bisector of segment \\(CD\\).  Since \\(P\\) belongs to this set, the line through \\(P\\) that is perpendicular to \\(CD\\) is precisely the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  There is no reason for this line to pass through the vertex \\(A\\) unless \\(A\\) i