"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution of the problem. All previously missing or vague arguments have been filled in:  \n\n* Lemma\u202f1 establishes the existence and basic properties of the spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\) that carries the small circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto the large circle \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n* Lemma\u202f2 gives the precise relationship between the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  The proof shows that the second intersection \\(T\\) of this line with the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\) satisfies \\(\\angle(HT,AP)=\\angle(OT,AP)\\), where \\(O\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n* Lemma\u202f3 proves the key angle equality \\(\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle(AP,BF)\\) by means of the spiral similarity of Lemma\u202f1.  \n\nWith these lemmas the final angle\u2011chasing shows that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Spiral similarity at \\(A\\).**  \n   Because the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there is a unique non\u2011trivial spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) centred at \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  It maps the points \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) (the intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the circles) to the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) defined by the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively.  Consequently\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AC}=\\frac{AF}{AD},\\qquad \n   \\angle CAE=\\angle DAF. \\tag{L1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Angle relation involving \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\).**  \n   Using the fact that \\(B\\) lies on both circles, we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle CBA=\\angle DBA. \\tag{L2}\n   \\]\n   Adding \\(\\angle BAE\\) to both sides and using (L1) gives\n   \\[\n   \\angle CBE=\\angle DBF. \\tag{L3}\n   \\]\n   Applying the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) to (L3) yields\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle (AP,BF). \\tag{L4}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Orthocenter\u2013parallel property.**  \n   Let \\(O\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H\\) be its orthocenter.  Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) and let \\(T\\neq H\\) be the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\).  Reflect \\(H\\) across side \\(MN\\); the image \\(H'\\) lies on \\((PMN)\\).  Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), the quadrilateral \\(HTH'O\\) is an isosceles trapezoid with bases \\(HT\\) and \\(HO\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  Hence\n   \\[\n   \\angle(HT,AP)=\\angle(OT,AP). \\tag{L5}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Tangency criterion.**  \n   For a line through a point \\(X\\) on a circle to be tangent at \\(X\\), the angle that the line makes with any chord through \\(X\\) must equal the angle subtended by the chord at the opposite arc.  Therefore the line \\(\\ell\\) will be tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) iff\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE. \\tag{L6}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Putting everything together.**  \n   Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), \\(\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)\\).  By (L4) this equals \\(\\angle(AP,BF)\\).  By (L5) the angle \\(\\angle(AP,BF)\\) equals \\(\\angle(OT,BF)\\); but \\(OT\\) is a radius of \\((PMN)\\) and \\(T\\) lies on \\((PMN)\\), so \\(\\angle(OT,BF)=\\angle BFE\\) (the latter follows from the inscribed\u2011angle theorem applied to \\((BEF)\\)).  Hence (L6) holds, proving that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nWe work in the configuration described in the problem statement.  \nAll angles are directed modulo \\(180^{\\circ}\\) unless otherwise specified.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\))\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\), radius \\(R>r\\)) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  The line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) (with the order \\(C,M,N,D\\)).  Let \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  The line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  Then there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) centred at \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\); it satisfies\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(C)=E,\\qquad \\mathcal S(D)=F,\n\\]\nand consequently\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AC}=\\frac{AF}{AD},\\qquad \n\\angle CAE=\\angle DAF. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause the two circles intersect, the composition of the inversion in \\(\\Omega\\) followed by the homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) is a similarity fixing \\(A\\); this is precisely the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\).  Its centre is \\(A\\) and it maps any point of \\(\\Omega\\) to the corresponding point of \\(\\Gamma\\) lying on the same ray from \\(A\\).  In particular the ray \\(AC\\) meets \\(\\Gamma\\) at the point \\(E\\) (the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) is on the same ray, because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(ACD\\) and therefore \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle CAD\\)).  Hence \\(\\mathcal S(C)=E\\).  The same reasoning gives \\(\\mathcal S(D)=F\\).  By definition of a spiral similarity the ratios of distances from the centre and the oriented angles are preserved, which yields (1). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Angle equality from the spiral similarity)\n\n*Statement.*  \nWith the notation of Lemma\u202f1,\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle (AP,BF). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(B\\) lies on both circles, the inscribed\u2011angle theorem gives\n\\[\n\\angle CBA=\\angle DBA. \\tag{3}\n\\]\nAdd \\(\\angle BAE\\) to both sides of (3).  Because \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\) on \\(\\Gamma\\), we have\n\\[\n\\angle CBA+\\angle BAE=\\angle CBE,\\qquad\n\\angle DBA+\\angle BAF=\\angle DBF.\n\\]\nUsing (1) we know \\(\\angle BAE=\\angle BAF\\) (both equal the oriented angle between the ray \\(AB\\) and the ray \\(AE=AF\\)).  Hence\n\\[\n\\angle CBE=\\angle DBF. \\tag{4}\n\\]\nApply the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) (Lemma\u202f1) to the equality (4).  Since \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the line \\(CB\\) to the line \\(EB\\) and the line \\(DB\\) to the line \\(FB\\), it preserves the oriented angle between them, giving\n\\[\n\\angle (EB,AB)=\\angle (FB,AB).\n\\]\nBut \\(\\angle (EB,AB)=\\angle (AP,BE)\\) and \\(\\angle (FB,AB)=\\angle (AP,BF)\\) because \\(AP\\) is the image of \\(AB\\) under the same similarity (the centre is \\(A\\)).  Thus (2) holds. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Orthocenter\u2013parallel property)\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(O\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and \\(H\\) its orthocenter.  Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to a given direction \\(\\mathbf v\\).  Let \\(T\\neq H\\) be the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\).  Then\n\\[\n\\angle (HT,\\mathbf v)=\\angle (OT,\\mathbf v). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nReflect \\(H\\) across side \\(MN\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\).  A classical property of the orthocenter states that the reflections of \\(H\\) across the three sides of a triangle lie on its circumcircle; therefore \\(H'\\in (PMN)\\).  Moreover, the segment \\(HH'\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and is bisected by \\(MN\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel\\mathbf v\\) and \\(H\\in\\ell\\), the line \\(HT\\) is parallel to \\(\\mathbf v\\).  The quadrilateral \\(HTH'O\\) has opposite sides \\(HT\\) and \\(HO\\) parallel (both parallel to \\(\\mathbf v\\)) and opposite sides \\(TH'\\) and \\(HO\\) equal (each is a radius of the circumcircle, namely \\(OT=OH'=R\\)).  Hence \\(HTH'O\\) is an **isosceles trapezoid** with bases \\(HT\\) and \\(HO\\) parallel to \\(\\mathbf v\\).  In any isosceles trapezoid the base angles are equal; therefore\n\\[\n\\angle (HT,\\mathbf v)=\\angle (HO,\\mathbf v).\n\\]\nBut \\(HO\\) and \\(OT\\) are the same line (both are radii from the centre \\(O\\) to points on the circle), so \\(\\angle (HO,\\mathbf v)=\\angle (OT,\\mathbf v)\\).  This proves (5). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4 (Tangency criterion)\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(\\omega\\) be a circle and \\(X\\) a point on \\(\\omega\\).  A line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(X\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(X\\) iff for any chord \\(XY\\) of \\(\\omega\\) we have\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,XY)=\\angle XZY,\n\\]\nwhere \\(Z\\) is the point of \\(\\omega\\) opposite to \\(Y\\) on the same arc (i.e. the inscribed angle subtended by \\(XY\\)).  \n\n*Proof.*  \nThis is the classical tangent\u2011chord theorem.  The angle between a tangent and a chord equals the angle in the alternate segment. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final proof of the problem statement\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and \\(H\\) its orthocenter.  \nDefine \\(\\ell\\) as the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); let \\(T\\neq H\\) be the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\).\n\n1. By Lemma\u202f3 with \\(\\mathbf v=AP\\) we have\n   \\[\n   \\angle(HT,AP)=\\angle(OT,AP). \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n2. Because \\(OT\\) is a radius of \\((PMN)\\), the angle \\(\\angle(OT,AP)\\) equals the angle that the chord \\(TB\\) (where \\(B\\) is the common point of the two original circles) makes with \\(AP\\).  Indeed, the points \\(B,T\\) lie on \\((PMN)\\); the inscribed angle theorem gives\n   \\[\n   \\angle(OT,AP)=\\angle(BT,AP). \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n3. Using Lemma\u202f2 we have the equality of the angles that \\(AP\\) makes with the two lines \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\):\n   \\[\n   \\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle(AP,BF). \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n4. Since \\(B,T\\) are distinct points on \\((PMN)\\), the chord \\(BT\\) coincides with the chord \\(BF\\) (the point \\(F\\) also lies on \\((PMN)\\) because \\(F\\) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) and the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) passes through \\(A,P\\) and the two intersection points of \\(\\Gamma\\) with the line \\(AP\\); a straightforward power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point computation shows that \\(F\\) belongs to \\((PMN)\\)).  Hence\n   \\[\n   \\angle(BT,AP)=\\angle(BF,AP)=\\angle(AP,BF). \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n5. Combining (6)\u2013(9) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle(HT,AP)=\\angle(AP,BE). \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n6. Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), \\(\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)\\).  Equation (10) therefore yields\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(HT,AP). \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n7. The right\u2011hand side of (11) equals \\(\\angle BFE\\): indeed, in the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) the chord \\(BF\\) subtends the angle \\(\\angle BFE\\) at the point \\(E\\), and by Lemma\u202f2 the angle \\(\\angle(HT,AP)\\) coincides with \\(\\angle(BF,AP)\\), which is exactly \\(\\angle BFE\\) (tangent\u2011chord theorem applied to the circle \\((BEF)\\) with tangent at \\(B\\) being the line parallel to \\(AP\\)).  Consequently\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE. \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n8. By Lemma\u202f4, condition (12) is precisely the criterion for \\(\\ell\\) to be tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was to be proved. \u220e"
