ine through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the required tangency is proved.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Spiral similarity at \\(A\\).**  \n   The two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) admit a unique non\u2011trivial spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that carries \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  It sends the point \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) (the second intersection of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\)) to \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) and, because the line \\(AP\\) meets the circles again at \\(E\\) and \\(F\\), it also sends \\(E\\) to \\(F\\).  This yields the length\u2011ratio and angle\u2011preserving relations (1).\n\n2. **Angle equality \\(\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle (AP,BF)\\).**  \n   From the common chord \\(AB\\) we have \\(\\angle CBA=\\angle DBA\\).  Adding \\(\\angle BAE\\) to both sides and using (1) (which tells us that the ray \\(AE\\) is taken to the ray \\(AF\\) by \\(\\mathcal S\\)) gives \\(\\angle CBE=\\angle DBF\\).  Applying \\(\\mathcal S\\) again (which maps the line \\(CB\\) to \\(EB\\) and \\(DB\\) to \\(FB\\)) yields (2).\n\n3. **Orthocenter\u2013parallel property.**  \n   Let \\(O\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Reflect the orthocenter \\(H\\) across side \\(MN\\); the image \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\).  Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) passes through \\(H\\) and meets \\((PMN)\\) again at \\(T\\), the quadrilateral \\(HTH'O\\) has opposite sides \\(HT\\) and \\(HO\\) parallel to \\(\\ell\\) and equal in length (both are chords subtended by the same central angle).  Hence it is an isosceles trapezoid, which gives (3).\n\n4. **Tangency criterion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f4 tells us that to prove \\(\\ell\\) tangent to \\((BEF)\\) it suffices to show \\(\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Using the parallelism \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and (2) we replace \\(\\angle(\\ell,BE)\\) by \\(\\angle(AP,BF)\\).  By (