 The ray \\(AE\\) meets \\(\\Gamma\\) at \\(F\\); hence \\(\\mathcal S(E)=F\\).\n\nTherefore \\(\\mathcal S\\) preserves the ratio of distances from \\(A\\) and the oriented angle between any two rays issuing from \\(A\\).  In particular\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AC}=\\frac{AF}{AD},\\qquad \n\\angle CAE=\\angle DAF . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Equality of the angles that \\(AP\\) makes with \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\))\n\nSince \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are common points of the two circles, the inscribed\u2011angle theorem gives\n\n\\[\n\\angle CBA=\\angle DBA . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAdd \\(\\angle BAE\\) to both sides of (2).  Because \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\) on \\(\\Gamma\\), the ray \\(AE\\) is taken to the ray \\(AF\\) by the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\); consequently \\(\\angle BAE=\\angle BAF\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\angle CBE=\\angle DBF . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nApply the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) to (3).  Under \\(\\mathcal S\\) the line \\(CB\\) is mapped to \\(EB\\) and the line \\(DB\\) to \\(FB\\); the oriented angle between the two lines is therefore preserved:\n\n\\[\n\\angle (EB,AB)=\\angle (FB,AB). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\angle (EB,AB)=\\angle (AP,BE)\\) and \\(\\angle (FB,AB)=\\angle (AP,BF)\\) because the ray \\(AB\\) is rotated into the ray \\(AP\\) by the same oriented angle (the rotation from \\(AB\\) to \\(AP\\) is exactly the angle between the two lines through \\(A\\)).  Thus we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle (AP,BF)\\;}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Orthocenter\u2013parallel property)\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Reflect the orthocenter \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\).  A well\u2011known property of the orthocenter states that each such reflection lies on the circumcircle, so \\(H'\\in (PMN)\\).\n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\((PMN)\\) again at a point \\(T\\neq H\\).  Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), the segment \\(HT\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\).  The quadrilateral \\(HTH'O\\) has the following properties:\n\n* \\(HT\\parallel HO\\) (both are parallel to \\(\\ell\\));\n* \\(TH'=OH\\) (both are chords subtended by the same central angle \\(\\angle TOH\\));\n* \\(HO\\) and \\(OT\\) are radii of the same circle, hence \\(HO=OT\\).\n\nThus \\(HTH'O\\) is an **isosceles trapezoid** whose two bases \\(HT\\) and \\(HO\\) are parallel to \\(AP\\).  In an isosceles trapezoid the base angles are equal, giving\n\n\\[\n\\angle(HT,AP)=\\angle(HO,AP)=\\angle(OT,AP). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4 (Tangent\u2013chord theorem)\n\nLet \\(\\omega\\) be a circle and \\(X\\in\\omega\\).  A line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(X\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(X\\) **iff** for any chord \\(XY\\) of \\(\\omega\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,XY)=\\angle XZY,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(Z\\) is the point of \\(\\omega\\) opposite to \\(Y\\) on the same arc.  This is a classical theorem; a short proof follows from the equality of the inscribed angle and the angle formed by the tangent and the chord.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final proof\n\nWe must show that the line \\(\\ell\\) (through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), for any line \\(L\\) we have \\(\\angle(\\ell,L)=\\angle(AP,L)\\).  Using Lemma\u202f2 (5) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle(AP,BF). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(T\\) be the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with \\((PMN)\\).  By Lemma\u202f3 (6),\n\n\\[\n\\angle(AP,BF)=\\angle(OT,AP). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(B,F,T\\) all lie on the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\): \\(B\\) belongs to both original circles, \\(F\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma