d preservation of directed angles). Assuming the existence of \\(\\mathcal S\\), the relations are correct.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f2 (Equality of the angles made by \\(AP\\) with \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\))\n\n- **Step (2):** \u201cSince \\(AB\\) is a common chord of the two circles, the inscribed\u2011angle theorem yields \\(\\angle CBA=\\angle DBA\\).\u201d\n\n  **Issue:** The inscribed\u2011angle theorem states that angles subtended by the same chord **in the same circle** are equal. Here \\(\\angle CBA\\) is an angle in circle \\(\\Omega\\) subtended by chord \\(CA\\); \\(\\angle DBA\\) is an angle in circle \\(\\Gamma\\) subtended by chord \\(DA\\). There is no reason for these two angles to be equal merely because the circles share chord \\(AB\\). This is a **critical error**; the equality is unsupported.\n\n- **Step (3):** \u201cFrom (1) we have \\(\\angle BAE=\\angle BAF\\) because the ray \\(AE\\) is taken to the ray \\(AF\\) by \\(\\mathcal S\\). Adding this common angle to both sides of (2) gives \\(\\angle CBE=\\angle DBF\\).\u201d\n\n  **Issues:**  \n  1. The claim \\(\\angle BAE=\\angle BAF\\) presumes that \\(\\mathcal S\\) maps the ray \\(AE\\) to \\(AF\\); this is true, but the equality of the *angles at \\(A\\)* does **not** directly imply the equality of the *angles at \\(B\\)* after adding to (2).  \n  2. Adding angles at different vertices is not a valid operation; one cannot add \\(\\angle BAE\\) to \\(\\angle CBA\\) and claim the sum equals \\(\\angle DBF\\). This step is a **critical error**.\n\n- **Step (4)\u2013(5):** The argument that the similarity fixes \\(B\\) and therefore sends line \\(CB\\) to \\(EB\\) etc., relies on the false premise that \\(\\mathcal S(B)=B\\). Since a non\u2011trivial spiral similarity cannot fix a second point, this reasoning is invalid. Hence the derived equality \\(\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle (AP,BF)\\) is unsupported. This is a **critical error**.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f3 (The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\