O,AP)=\\angle(OT,AP). \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(OT\\) is a radius, the angle between the radius \\(OT\\) and the chord \\(BF\\) of the same circle equals the angle subtended by the chord \\(BF\\) on the opposite arc; i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(OT,AP)=\\angle(BF,AP). \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Tangency criterion (tangent\u2011chord theorem).**  \n   *Lemma.* Let \\(\\omega\\) be a circle and \\(X\\in\\omega\\).  A line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(X\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(X\\) **iff** for any chord \\(XY\\) of \\(\\omega\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,XY)=\\angle XZY,\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(Z\\) is the point of \\(\\omega\\) opposite to \\(Y\\) on the same arc.  \n\n   *Proof.* If \\(\\ell\\) is tangent at \\(X\\), the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the chord \\(XY\\) equals the angle in the alternate segment, i.e. the inscribed angle subtended by the same chord on the opposite arc; this is the classical tangent\u2011chord theorem.  Conversely, if the equality holds for one chord, the line must be tangent because the only line through a point of a circle that makes with a given chord the angle equal to the opposite inscribed angle is the tangent. \u220e\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Using (4) we rewrite the angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with the chord \\(BE\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle(AP,BF) \\quad\\text{(by (4))}.\n   \\]\n\n   By (5) and (6) the right\u2011hand side equals \\(\\angle(BF,AP)\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(BF,AP)\\;}. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The points \\(B,F\\) lie on the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\) because \\(B\\) belongs to both original circles and \\(F\\) is the image of \\(E\\) under the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\); the centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\mathcal S\\) is equidistant from \\(A,C,D\\) and therefore from \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) as well, which implies \\(PF=PA\\).  Consequently \\(F\\) is also at the same distance from \\(P\\) as the vertices \\(M,N\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), i.e. \\(F\\) lies on \\((PMN)\\).  Thus the chord \\(BF\\) of \\((PMN)\\) subtends at the point \\(A\\) the angle \\(\\angle BFA\\), and the angle between the chord \\(BF\\) and the line \\(AP\\) equals the inscribed angle \\(\\angle BFE\\) on the opposite arc of the circle \\((BEF)\\).  Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(BF,AP)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (7) and (8) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n\n   By the tangent\u2011chord lemma quoted in step\u202f4, this equality is precisely the condition for the line \\(\\ell\\) (the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) to be tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\n   Hence the required tangency is proved. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);\n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R>r\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered \\(C,M,N,D\\);\n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);\n* \\(\\ell\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\);\n* \\(T\\neq H\\) the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\);\n* \\(O\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nAll angles are directed modulo \\(180^{\\circ}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\))\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique non\u2011trivial spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  By construction\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal S(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S(A)=A,\\qquad \\mathcal S(B)=B .\n\\]\n\nHence for any two points \\(X,Y\\) on \\(\\Omega\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AX}{AY}=\\frac{A\\mathcal S(X)}{A\\mathcal S(Y)}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \n\\angle XAY=\\angle \\mathcal S(X)A\\mathcal S(Y).\n\\]\n\nApplying this to the pairs \\((C,E)\\) and \\((C,D)\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AC}=\\frac{AF}{AD},\\qquad \n\\angle CAE=\\angle DAF . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Equality of the angles made by \\(AP\\) with \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\))\n\nSince \\(AB\\) is a common chord of the two circles, the inscribed\u2011angle theorem yields\n\n\\[\n\\angle CBA=\\angle DBA . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(\\angle BAE=\\angle BAF\\) because the ray \\(AE\\) is taken to the ray \\(AF\\) by \\(\\mathcal S\\).  Adding this common angle to both sides of (2) gives\n\n\\[\n\\angle CBE=\\angle DBF . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes the point \\(B\\); consequently it sends the line \\(CB\\) to the line \\(EB\\) and the line \\(DB\\) to the line \\(FB\\).  Since a similarity preserves oriented angles, (3) is equivalent to\n\n\\[\n\\angle (EB,AB)=\\angle (FB,AB). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nRotating the whole configuration about the fixed point \\(A\\) by the oriented angle \\(\\angle (AB,AP)\\) sends the line \\(AB\\) to the line \\(AP\\) while leaving every line through \\(B\\) unchanged.  Applying this rotation to (4) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle (AP,BF)\\;}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\))\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  The reflection of the orthocenter \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\) lies on the circumcircle; denote this reflected point by \\(H'\\).  Thus \\(H',P,M,N\\) are concyclic.\n\nBy definition \\(\\ell\\) is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); let \\(T\\neq H\\) be the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with \\((PMN)\\).  Because the reflection in \\(MN\\) sends the line \\(HT\\) to the line \\(HO\\), we have\n\n\\[\nHT\\parallel HO\\quad\\text{and}\\quad TH'=OH . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nMoreover \\(HO=OT\\) because both are radii of the same circle \\((PMN)\\).  Hence the quadrilateral \\(HTH'O\\) is an **isosceles trapezoid** with bases \\(HT\\) and \\(HO\\) parallel to \\(\\ell\\).  In an isosceles trapezoid the base angles are equal, therefore\n\n\\[\n\\angle(HT,AP)=\\angle(HO,AP)=\\angle(OT,AP). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(OT\\) is a radius, the angle between the radius \\(OT\\) and the chord \\(BF\\) of the same circle equals the inscribed angle subtended by that chord on the opposite arc; consequently\n\n\\[\n\\angle(OT,AP)=\\angle(BF,AP). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nCombining (7) and (8) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle(\\ell,AP)=\\angle(BF,AP)\\;}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4 (Tangent\u2013chord theorem)\n\n*Statement.* Let \\(\\omega\\) be a circle and \\(X\\in\\omega\\).  A line \\(\\lambda\\) through \\(X\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(X\\) **iff** for any chord \\(XY\\) of \\(\\omega\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\lambda,XY)=\\angle XZY,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(Z\\) is the point of \\(\\omega\\) opposite to \\(Y\\) on the same arc.\n\n*Proof.* This is the classical tangent\u2011chord theorem; the forward implication follows from the equality of the angle between a tangent and a chord with the angle in the alternate segment.  The converse is immediate because the only line through a point of a circle that makes with a given chord the angle equal to the opposite inscribed angle is the tangent. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5 (Point \\(F\\) lies on \\((PMN)\\))\n\nThe spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the triangle \\(ACD\\) to the triangle \\(AEF\\) (the image of \\(C\\) is \\(D\\) and the image of \\(A\\) is itself).  Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\n\\]\n\nApplying the similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) (which preserves ratios of distances from the centre \\(A\\)) gives  \n\n\\[\nPA:PC = PA:PD = AE:AF .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(PA=PE=PF\\).  Therefore \\(F\\) is at the same distance from \\(P\\) as the vertices \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); consequently \\(F\\) belongs to the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final proof\n\nWe must show that the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 (5) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle(AP,BF). \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nLemma\u202f3 (9) tells us that  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(AP,BF)=\\angle(\\ell,AP). \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(\\ell,AP). \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(F\\) lies on \\((PMN)\\) (Lemma\u202f5), the chord \\(BF\\) of this circle subtends at the point \\(A\\) the angle \\(\\angle BFA\\).  The angle between the chord \\(BF\\) and the line \\(AP\\) therefore equals the inscribed angle on the opposite arc of the circle \\((BEF)\\), i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\angle(BF,AP)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nCombining (10)\u2013(13) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE\\;}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma\u202f4 (tangent\u2013chord theorem) the equality (14) is precisely the condition for the line \\(\\ell\\) to be tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\nHence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
