eflection of } P' \\text{ across the line } M'N'. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) that is **parallel** to \\(AP\\) is sent by the inversion to the line through \\(H'\\) that is **collinear** with \\(AP\\). Indeed, a parallelism is preserved because both lines are mapped by the same similarity (the inversion) and one of them, \\(AP\\), is invariant. Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I\\bigl(\\text{line through }H\\parallel AP\\bigr)=AP. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus, after inversion, the line we have to study is simply the line \\(AP\\) itself.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4 Tangency in the inverted picture\n\nWe have to prove that the line \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B' E' F'\\).\n\nBy Lemma\u202fL we have the equality of directed angles\n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,BE')=\\angle (AP,BF'). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe right\u2011hand side of (9) is the angle subtended by the chord \\(B'F'\\) at the point \\(E'\\) of the circle \\((B' E' F')\\); the left\u2011hand side is the angle between the same line \\(AP\\) and the chord \\(B'E'\\). Equality of these two angles is precisely the **tangent\u2011chord theorem** for the circle \\((B' E' F')\\): a line through a point of a circle is tangent iff it makes with one chord the same angle as the chord makes with the line through the opposite point of the circle. Hence (9) implies that \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((B' E' F')\\) at the point \\(B'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5 Returning to the original configuration\n\nInversion preserves tangency: the image of a tangent line is tangent to the image of the circle.  \nThe pre\u2011image of the line \\(AP\\) is, by (8), the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nThe pre\u2011image of the circle \\((B' E' F')\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) (because inversion sends each of the three points \\(B',E',F'\\) back to \\(B,E