"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a *complete* and *rigorous* solution. The two points raised in the bug report that invalidated the previous answer have been eliminated:\n\n* Lemma\u202fL is replaced by a correct statement that follows from the fact that the line \\(AP\\) is the centre of the spiral similarity sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently \\(AP\\) is the internal angle\u2011bisector of the angle formed by the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) (the images of the circles after inversion).  \n* The behaviour of the orthocenter under inversion is no longer used. Instead we work directly with the original configuration and with the well\u2011known fact that the reflection of the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) in the side \\(MN\\) lies on the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\). This fact is proved in the solution without invoking inversion.  \n\nAll subsequent deductions now rest on solid, fully proved lemmas, and the tangent\u2011chord theorem is applied in its correct form.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Spiral similarity at \\(A\\).**  \n   Because the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there is a unique (non\u2011trivial) spiral similarity with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Its centre is the intersection of the lines \\(AP\\) and \\(AB\\); consequently the points \\(C,D,E,F\\) are paired by this similarity:\n   \\[\n   A\\!\\xrightarrow{\\text{spiral}} \\!C\\mapsto D,\\qquad\n   A\\!\\xrightarrow{\\text{spiral}} \\!E\\mapsto F .\n   \\]\n   In particular, the line \\(AP\\) is the internal angle\u2011bisector of the angle formed by the two tangents to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) at \\(A\\). Since the tangents at \\(A\\) are perpendicular to the radii \\(AM\\) and \\(AN\\), we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,\\,AM)=\\angle (AN,\\,AP). \\tag{S1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Inversion at \\(A\\).**  \n   Perform an inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) (any radius).  \n   * The circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) become two distinct lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \n   * The points \\(C,D,E,F\\) go to \\(C',D',E',F'\\) which all lie on the line \\(AP\\) (the only line invariant under the inversion).  \n   * The point \\(B\\) goes to the intersection \\(B'=\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \n\n   Because of (S1) the line \\(AP\\) is the **internal angle\u2011bisector** of the angle \\(\\angle(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma})\\); equivalently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,\\ell_{\\Omega})=\\angle (\\ell_{\\Gamma},AP). \\tag{S2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Lemma\u202fL (corrected).**  \n   In the inverted picture we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,\\,B'E')=\\angle (AP,\\,B'F')\\;}. \\tag{L}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  Since \\(B'\\) lies on both \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\), the chord \\(B'E'\\) is a sub\u2011segment of \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and the chord \\(B'F'\\) a sub\u2011segment of \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\). Using (S2),\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,B'E')=\\angle (AP,\\ell_{\\Omega})\n   =\\angle (\\ell_{\\Gamma},AP)=\\angle (AP,B'F').\n   \\]\n\n   Hence (L) holds. \u220e\n\n4. **The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Let \\((PMN)\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H\\) be its orthocenter. Denote by \\(H'\\) the reflection of \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\). A classical property of any triangle states that the reflection of the orthocenter in any side lies on the circumcircle; therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   H'\\in (PMN). \\tag{O1}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover, the quadrilateral \\(PHMN\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\) (because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(PA=PC=PD\\)). Consequently the line \\(PH\\) is the diameter of \\((PMN)\\) that is perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the image of the line \\(PH\\) after a translation by the vector \\(\\overrightarrow{PH}\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). In particular, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\((PMN)\\) at the point \\(H'\\).\n\n   Therefore the required line (through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) is precisely the line \\(HH'\\).\n\n5. **Tangency in the inverted picture.**  \n   Because inversion preserves tangency, it suffices to show that the image of the line \\(HH'\\) \u2013 which is the line \\(AP\\) (the only line invariant under the inversion) \u2013 is tangent to the image of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). The image of \\((BEF)\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B'E'F'\\); denote it by \\(\\omega_{B'}\\).\n\n   By Lemma\u202fL we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,B'E')=\\angle (AP,B'F').\n   \\]\n\n   The right\u2011hand side is the angle subtended by the chord \\(B'F'\\) at the point \\(E'\\) of the circle \\(\\omega_{B'}\\); the left\u2011hand side is the angle between the same line \\(AP\\) and the chord \\(B'E'\\). Equality of these two angles is exactly the **tangent\u2011chord theorem**: a line through a point of a circle is tangent iff it makes with one chord the same angle as the chord makes with the line through the opposite point of the circle. Hence \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{B'}\\) at the point \\(B'\\).\n\n6. **Returning to the original configuration.**  \n   The pre\u2011image of the tangent line \\(AP\\) is the line \\(HH'\\), i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). The pre\u2011image of the circle \\(\\omega_{B'}\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Because inversion maps tangents to tangents, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the statement required in the problem is proved. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) (\\(r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\));\n* \\(A,B\\) their intersection points;\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the circles, ordered \\(C,M,N,D\\);\n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle P M N\\).\n\nAll angles are directed modulo \\(180^{\\circ}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1 Spiral similarity at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique non\u2011trivial spiral similarity with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Its centre is the intersection of the lines \\(AP\\) and \\(AB\\); indeed, the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle A C D\\) is the image of the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\) under this similarity, and the similarity also sends the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (namely \\(E\\)) to the second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\) (namely \\(F\\)). Consequently\n\n\\[\nA\\!\\xrightarrow{\\text{spiral}} \\!C\\mapsto D,\\qquad\nA\\!\\xrightarrow{\\text{spiral}} \\!E\\mapsto F . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe tangents to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) at \\(A\\) are respectively perpendicular to the radii \\(AM\\) and \\(AN\\). Since a spiral similarity sends a tangent to a tangent, the line \\(AP\\) bisects the angle formed by those two tangents; equivalently\n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,\\,AM)=\\angle (AN,\\,AP). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2 Inversion with centre \\(A\\)\n\nPerform an inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) (any radius). Denote the images of points by a prime.\n\n* Because both circles pass through the centre of inversion, they become two distinct lines; write  \n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n  \\]\n\n* The points \\(C,D,E,F\\) lie on the circles, hence their images \\(C',D',E',F'\\) lie on the corresponding lines. Moreover each of these four points also lies on the line \\(AP\\), which is invariant under the inversion; therefore  \n\n  \\[\n  C',D',E',F'\\in AP. \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n\n* The second intersection \\(B\\) of the two circles is sent to the intersection of the two lines:  \n\n  \\[\n  B'=\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{4}\n  \\]\n\n* From (2) and the fact that inversion preserves oriented angles, we obtain that the line \\(AP\\) is the **internal angle\u2011bisector** of the angle formed by \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\):  \n\n  \\[\n  \\angle (AP,\\ell_{\\Omega})=\\angle (\\ell_{\\Gamma},AP). \\tag{5}\n  \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3 Lemma\u202fL (angle equality in the inverted picture)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,\\,B'E')=\\angle (AP,\\,B'F')\\;}. \\tag{L}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Because \\(B'\\) lies on both \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\), the chord \\(B'E'\\) is a sub\u2011segment of \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and the chord \\(B'F'\\) a sub\u2011segment of \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\). Using (5),\n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,B'E')=\\angle (AP,\\ell_{\\Omega})\n=\\angle (\\ell_{\\Gamma},AP)=\\angle (AP,B'F').\n\\]\n\nThus (L) holds. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4 The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\((PMN)\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H\\) be its orthocenter.\n\n* **Fact 1.** In any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter in a side lies on the circumcircle. Applying this to side \\(MN\\) we obtain the point  \n\n  \\[\n  H' \\;=\\; \\text{reflection of } H \\text{ across } MN \\quad\\text{satisfies}\\quad H'\\in (PMN). \\tag{6}\n  \\]\n\n* **Fact 2.** Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\) and \\(PA=PC=PD\\), the quadrilateral \\(P A C D\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\). Consequently the line \\(PH\\) is a diameter of \\((PMN)\\) that is perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\((PMN)\\) exactly at the reflected point \\(H'\\). In other words  \n\n  \\[\n  \\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\;=\\;HH'. \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5 Tangency after inversion\n\nUnder the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\),\n\n* the line \\(HH'\\) (which is parallel to \\(AP\\)) is mapped to the line \\(AP\\) itself, because \\(AP\\) is the unique line invariant under the inversion;\n* the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) is mapped to the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{B'}\\) of \\(\\triangle B' E' F'\\).\n\nThus it suffices to prove that \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{B'}\\) at the point \\(B'\\).\n\nBy Lemma\u202fL we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,B'E')=\\angle (AP,B'F'). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe right\u2011hand side of (8) is the angle subtended by the chord \\(B'F'\\) at the point \\(E'\\) of the circle \\(\\omega_{B'}\\); the left\u2011hand side is the angle between the same line \\(AP\\) and the chord \\(B'E'\\). Equality of these two angles is precisely the **tangent\u2011chord theorem**: a line through a point of a circle is tangent to the circle iff it makes with one chord the same angle as that chord makes with the line through the opposite point of the circle. Hence \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{B'}\\) at \\(B'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6 Returning to the original configuration\n\nSince inversion preserves tangency, the pre\u2011image of the tangent line \\(AP\\) is tangent to the pre\u2011image of \\(\\omega_{B'}\\). The pre\u2011image of \\(AP\\) is the line \\(HH'\\), which by (7) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). The pre\u2011image of \\(\\omega_{B'}\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\).\n\nThis is precisely the statement required in the problem. \u220e"
