umcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle A C D\\) is the image of the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\) under this similarity, and the similarity also sends the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (namely \\(E\\)) to the second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\) (namely \\(F\\)). Consequently\n\n\\[\nA\\!\\xrightarrow{\\text{spiral}} \\!C\\mapsto D,\\qquad\nA\\!\\xrightarrow{\\text{spiral}} \\!E\\mapsto F . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe tangents to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) at \\(A\\) are respectively perpendicular to the radii \\(AM\\) and \\(AN\\). Since a spiral similarity sends a tangent to a tangent, the line \\(AP\\) bisects the angle formed by those two tangents; equivalently\n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,\\,AM)=\\angle (AN,\\,AP). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2 Inversion with centre \\(A\\)\n\nPerform an inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) (any radius). Denote the images of points by a prime.\n\n* Because both circles pass through the centre of inversion, they become two distinct lines; write  \n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n  \\]\n\n* The points \\(C,D,E,F\\) lie on the circles, hence their images \\(C',D',E',F'\\) lie on the corresponding lines. Moreover each of these four points also lies on the line \\(AP\\), which is invariant under the inversion; therefore  \n\n  \\[\n  C',D',E',F'\\in AP. \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n\n* The second intersection \\(B\\) of the two circles is sent to the intersection of the two lines:  \n\n  \\[\n  B'=\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{4}\n  \\]\n\n* From (2) and the fact that inversion preserves oriented angles, we obtain that the line \\(AP\\) is the **internal angle\u2011bisector** of the angle formed by \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\):  \n\n  \\[\n  \\angle (AP,\\ell_{\\Omega})=\\angle (\\ell_{\\Gamma},AP). \\tag{5}\n  \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3 Lemma\u202fL (angle equality in the inverted picture)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,\\,B'E')=\\angle (AP,\\,B'F')\\;}. \\tag{L}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Because \\(B'\\) lies on both \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\), the chord \\(B'E'\\) is a sub\u2011segment of \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and the chord \\(B'F'\\) a sub\u2011segment of \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\). Using (5),\n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,B'E')=\\angle (AP,\\ell_{\\Omega})\n=\\angle (\\ell_{\\Gamma},AP)=\\angle (AP,B'F').\n\\]\n\nThus (L) holds. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4 The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\((PMN)\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H\\) be its orthocenter.\n\n* **Fact 1.** In any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter in a side lies on the circumcircle. Applying this to side \\(MN\\) we obtain the point  \n\n  \\[\n  H' \\;=\\; \\text{reflection of } H \\text{ across } MN \\quad\\text{satisfies}\\quad H'\\in (PMN). \\tag{6}\n  \\]\n\n* **Fact 2.** Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\) and \\(PA=PC=PD\\), the quadrilateral \\(P A C D\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\). Consequently the line \\(PH\\) is a diameter of \\((PMN)\\) that is perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\((PMN)\\) exactly at the reflected point \\(H'\\). In other words  \n\n  \\[\n  \\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\;=\\;HH'. \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5 Tangency after inversion\n\nUnder the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\),\n\n* the line \\(HH'\\) (which is parallel to \\(AP\\)) is mapped to the line \\(AP\\) itself, because \\(AP\\) is the unique line invariant under the inversion;\n* the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) is mapped to the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{B'}\\) of \\(\\triangle B' E' F'\\).\n\nThus it suffices to prove