errightarrow{PH}\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). In particular, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\((PMN)\\) at the point \\(H'\\).\n\n   Therefore the required line (through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) is precisely the line \\(HH'\\).\n\n5. **Tangency in the inverted picture.**  \n   Because inversion preserves tangency, it suffices to show that the image of the line \\(HH'\\) \u2013 which is the line \\(AP\\) (the only line invariant under the inversion) \u2013 is tangent to the image of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). The image of \\((BEF)\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B'E'F'\\); denote it by \\(\\omega_{B'}\\).\n\n   By Lemma\u202fL we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,B'E')=\\angle (AP,B'F').\n   \\]\n\n   The right\u2011hand side is the angle subtended by the chord \\(B'F'\\) at the point \\(E'\\) of the circle \\(\\omega_{B'}\\); the left\u2011hand side is the angle between the same line \\(AP\\) and the chord \\(B'E'\\). Equality of these two angles is exactly the **tangent\u2011chord theorem**: a line through a point of a circle is tangent iff it makes with one chord the same angle as the chord makes with the line through the opposite point of the circle. Hence \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{B'}\\) at the point \\(B'\\).\n\n6. **Returning to the original configuration.**  \n   The pre\u2011image of the tangent line \\(AP\\) is the line \\(HH'\\), i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). The pre\u2011image of the circle \\(\\omega_{B'}\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Because inversion maps tangents to tangents, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the statement required in the problem is proved. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) (\\(r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Ga