fter inversion.\n\n* **Step\u202f4 (Geometry of the orthocenter).**  \n  Let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\).  \n  - **Fact\u202f4.1.** In any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter in a side lies on the circumcircle. Hence  \n\n    \\[\n    H'\\in (PMN). \\tag{5}\n    \\]  \n  - **Fact\u202f4.2.** The quadrilateral \\(PMNH\\) is cyclic because \\(\\angle PMH=\\angle PNH=90^{\\circ}\\) (each is the complement of the corresponding angle at the orthocenter). Consequently  \n\n    \\[\n    \\angle PHM=\\angle PNM. \\tag{6}\n    \\]  \n  - From (1) we have \\(\\angle PNM=\\angle (AP,AN)=\\angle (AM,AP)\\). Using (6) we obtain  \n\n    \\[\n    \\angle PHM=\\angle (AM,AP). \\tag{7}\n    \\]  \n  - Since \\(M\\) lies on the radius \\(AM\\) of \\(\\Omega\\), the line \\(AM\\) is perpendicular to the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\); the same holds for \\(AN\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\). Combining (7) with (1) yields  \n\n    \\[\n    \\angle PHM=\\angle (AP,AM)=\\angle (AN,AP)=\\angle (AP,AN). \\tag{8}\n    \\]  \n  - Equality of the two directed angles in (8) shows that the line \\(PH\\) is symmetric to the line \\(AP\\) with respect to the line \\(MN\\). Therefore the reflection \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) satisfies  \n\n    \\[\n    HH'\\parallel AP. \\tag{9}\n    \\]  \n\n    This is Lemma\u202f4.\n\n* **Step\u202f5 (Tangency after inversion).**  \n  Under the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) the line \\(HH'\\) (which is parallel to \\(AP\\)) is sent to the line \\(AP\\) itself, because \\(AP\\) is the unique line through the centre of inversion that is invariant. The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) does **not** pass through \\(A\\); therefore its image \\(\\omega_{B'}\\) is a genuine circle passing through the points \\(B',E',F'\\).  \n\n  Lemma\u202fL states that the directed angles formed by \\(AP\\) with the chords \\(B'E'\\) and \\(B'F'\\) are equal; this is precisely the 