---------------\n### 2.2  Inversion with centre \\(A\\)\n\nPerform an inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) of arbitrary radius with centre \\(A\\).\n\n* Because both circles pass through the centre of inversion, they are transformed into two distinct lines; denote them by  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_{\\Omega}=\\mathcal I(\\Omega),\\qquad \\ell_{\\Gamma}=\\mathcal I(\\Gamma).\n  \\]\n\n* The points \\(C,D,E,F\\) lie on the circles, therefore their images \\(C',D',E',F'\\) lie on the corresponding lines. Moreover each of these points lies on the line \\(AP\\); the line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, hence it is invariant. Consequently  \n\n  \\[\n  C',D',E',F'\\in AP. \\tag{2.2}\n  \\]\n\n* The second intersection \\(B\\) of the circles is sent to the intersection of the two lines:  \n\n  \\[\n  B'=\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{2.3}\n  \\]\n\n* Directed angles are preserved by inversion, so (2.1) becomes  \n\n  \\[\n  \\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,\\ell_{\\Omega})=\\angle (\\ell_{\\Gamma},AP)\\;}. \\tag{2.4}\n  \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Lemma\u202fL (angle equality in the inverted picture)\n\nBecause \\(B'\\) belongs to both \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\), the chords \\(B'E'\\) and \\(B'F'\\) are respectively sub\u2011segments of \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\). Using (2.4) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,B'E')=\\angle (AP,B'F')\\;}. \\tag{L}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\[\n\\angle (AP,B'E')=\\angle (AP,\\ell_{\\Omega})\\overset{(2.4)}{=}\\angle (\\ell_{\\Gamma},AP)=\\angle (AP,B'F').\n\\]  \n\u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Geometry of the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\).\n\n* **Fact\u202f4.1.** In any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter in a side lies on the circumcircle. Hence