ne not passing through the centre.\n\n* \u201cThe points \\(C,D,E,F\\) lie on the circles, therefore their images \\(C',D',E',F'\\) lie on the corresponding lines. Moreover each of these points lies on the line \\(AP\\); the line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, hence it is invariant. Consequently \\(C',D',E',F'\\in AP.\\)\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** The statement that a line through the centre of inversion is invariant is correct. However, the claim that \\(C',D',E',F'\\) lie on \\(AP\\) follows only if the original points lie on \\(AP\\), which they do. So this part is fine.\n\n* \u201cDirected angles are preserved by inversion, so (2.1) becomes \u2026 \\(\\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,\\ell_{\\Omega})=\\angle (\\ell_{\\Gamma},AP)\\;}\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** Inversion preserves oriented angles **up to sign**; i.e., \\(\\angle XOY = \\pm \\angle X' O Y'\\). The solution does not address the possible sign change, nor does it justify that the sign is positive in this configuration. Moreover, because (2.1) itself is false, the derived equality (2.4) is meaningless.\n\n**Classification:** **Justification Gap** (the preservation claim is incomplete) and **propagation of the earlier critical error**.\n\n---\n\n#### Step 2.3 \u2013 Lemma\u202fL (angle equality in the inverted picture)\n\n> \u201cBecause \\(B'\\) belongs to both \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\), the chords \\(B'E'\\) and \\(B'F'\\) are respectively sub\u2011segments of \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\). Using (2.4) we obtain \\(\\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,B'E')=\\angle (AP,B'F')\\;}\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** Assuming (2.4) were valid, the algebraic manipulation is correct: both chords lie on the respective lines, so the angle between \\(AP\\) and each chord equals the angle between \\(AP\\) and the line. However, because (2.4) rests on the false (2.1), Lemma\u202fL is not established.\n\n**Classification:** **Justification Gap** (depends on a false premise).\n\n---\n\n#### Step 2.4 \u2013 Geometry of the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\n*Fact\u202f4.1* \u2013 \u201cIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter in a side lies on the circumcircle.\u201d  \n**Analysis:** This is a well\u2011known fact, but the solution does not provide a proof. For a grading perspective, a brief justification is required.\n\n**Classification:** **Justification Gap**.\n\n*Fact\u202f4.2* \u2013 \u201cBecause \\(H\\) is the orthocenter, the quadrilateral \\(PMNH\\) is cyclic: indeed \\(\\angle PMH=\\angle PNH=90^{\\circ}\\). Therefore \\(\\angle PHM=\\angle PNM\\).\u201d  \n**Analysis:** The cyclicity follows from the right angles, which is correct. However, the deduction that \\(\\angle PHM = \\angle PNM\\) is not explained; it requires identifying equal subtended arcs or using the Inscribed Angle Theorem. The step is omitted.\n\n**Classification:** **Justification Gap**.\n\n*Subsequent angle equalities (4.3)\u2013(4.5)* \u2013 These rely on the earlier **critical error** (2.1) to replace \\(\\angle PNM\\) with \\(\\angle (AP,AN)\\). Since (2.1) is false, the chain of equalities collapses. Even ignoring that, the transition from (4.4) to (4.5) is not rigorously justified.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** (propagation) and **Justification Gaps**.\n\n*Conclusion (4.6) \u2013 \u201c\\(HH'\\parallel AP\\).\u201d*  \nThe argument that the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) lies on the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is based on the (incorrect) symmetry claim derived from (4.5). No rigorous proof is given that the direction of \\(PH\\) is the mirror image of \\(PA\\) across \\(MN\\). Hence the parallelism is not established.\n\n**Classification:** **Justification Gap** (and dependent on the earlier critical error).\n\n---\n\n#### Step 2.5 \u2013 Tangency after inversion\n\nThe solution claims:\n\n* \u201cUnder the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) the line \\(HH'\\) (which, by (4.6), is parallel to \\(AP\\)) is sent to the line \\(AP\\) i