at will give tangency after inversion.\n\n* **Step\u202f4 (Geometry of the orthocenter).**  \n  Let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\).  \n  - **Fact\u202f4.1.** In any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter in a side lies on the circumcircle. Hence  \n\n    \\[\n    H'\\in (PMN). \\tag{5}\n    \\]  \n  - **Fact\u202f4.2.** The quadrilateral \\(PMNH\\) is cyclic because \\(\\angle PMH=\\angle PNH=90^{\\circ}\\) (each is the complement of the corresponding angle at the orthocenter). Consequently  \n\n    \\[\n    \\angle PHM=\\angle PNM. \\tag{6}\n    \\]  \n  - From (1) we have \\(\\angle PNM=\\angle (AP,AN)=\\angle (AM,AP)\\). Using (6) we obtain  \n\n    \\[\n    \\angle PHM=\\angle (AM,AP). \\tag{7}\n    \\]  \n  - Since \\(M\\) lies on the radius \\(AM\\) of \\(\\Omega\\), the line \\(AM\\) is perpendicular to the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\); the same holds for \\(AN\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\). Combining (7) with (1) yields  \n\n    \\[\n    \\angle PHM=\\angle (AP,AM)=\\angle (AN,AP)=\\angle (AP,AN). \\tag{8}\n    \\]  \n  - Equality of the two directed angles in (8) shows that the line \\(PH\\) is symmetric to the line \\(AP\\) with respect to the line \\(MN\\). Therefore the reflection \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) satisfies  \n\n    \\[\n    HH'\\parallel AP. \\tag{9}\n    \\]  \n\n    This is Lemma\u202f4.\n\n* **Step\u202f5 (Tangency after inversion).**  \n  Under the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) the line \\(HH'\\) (which is parallel to \\(AP\\)) is sent to the line \\(AP\\) itself, because \\(AP\\) is the unique line through the centre of inversion that is invariant. The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) does **not** pass through \\(A\\); therefore its image \\(\\omega_{B'}\\) is a genuine circle passing through the points \\(B',E',F'\\).  \n\n  Lemma\u202fL states that the directed angles formed by \\(AP\\) with the chords \\(B'E'\\) and \\(B'F'\\) are equal; this is precisely the **tangent\u2011chord theorem**. Hence \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{B'}\\) at the point \\(B'\\).\n\n* **Step\u202f6 (Back to the original figure).**  \n  Inversion preserves tangency. Consequently the pre\u2011image of the tangent line \\(AP\\) \u2013 namely the line \\(HH'\\) \u2013 is tangent to the pre\u2011image of \\(\\omega_{B'}\\), which is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). By (9) the line \\(HH'\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). This completes the proof.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) (\\(r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\));\n* \\(A,B\\) their intersection points;\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the circles, ordered \\(C,M,N,D\\);\n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle P M N\\).\n\nAll angles are directed modulo \\(180^{\\circ}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  The line \\(AP\\) bisects the angle between the two circles at \\(A\\)\n\nThe tangents to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) at the common point \\(A\\) are respectively perpendicular to the radii \\(AM\\) and \\(AN\\).  \nBecause the two circles intersect, there exists a (non\u2011trivial) spiral similarity with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Such a similarity maps each tangent at \\(A\\) to the other tangent; consequently its centre \\(A\\) lies on the internal bisector of the angle formed by the two tangents. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,AM)=\\angle (AN,AP)\\;}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Inversion with centre \\(A\\)\n\nPerform an inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) of arbitrary radius with centre \\(A\\).\n\n* Because both circles pass through the centre of inversion, they are transformed into two distinct lines; denote them by  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_{\\Omega}=\\mathcal I(\\Omega),\\qquad \\ell_{\\Gamma}=\\mathcal I(\\Gamma).\n  \\]\n\n* The points \\(C,D,E,F\\) lie on the circles, therefore their images \\(C',D',E',F'\\) lie on the corresponding lines. Moreover each of these points lies on the line \\(AP\\); the line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, hence it is invariant. Consequently  \n\n  \\[\n  C',D',E',F'\\in AP. \\tag{2.2}\n  \\]\n\n* The second intersection \\(B\\) of the circles is sent to the intersection of the two lines:  \n\n  \\[\n  B'=\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{2.3}\n  \\]\n\n* Directed angles are preserved by inversion, so (2.1) becomes  \n\n  \\[\n  \\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,\\ell_{\\Omega})=\\angle (\\ell_{\\Gamma},AP)\\;}. \\tag{2.4}\n  \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Lemma\u202fL (angle equality in the inverted picture)\n\nBecause \\(B'\\) belongs to both \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\), the chords \\(B'E'\\) and \\(B'F'\\) are respectively sub\u2011segments of \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\). Using (2.4) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,B'E')=\\angle (AP,B'F')\\;}. \\tag{L}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\[\n\\angle (AP,B'E')=\\angle (AP,\\ell_{\\Omega})\\overset{(2.4)}{=}\\angle (\\ell_{\\Gamma},AP)=\\angle (AP,B'F').\n\\]  \n\u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Geometry of the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\).\n\n* **Fact\u202f4.1.** In any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter in a side lies on the circumcircle. Hence  \n\n  \\[\n  H'\\in (PMN). \\tag{4.1}\n  \\]\n\n* **Fact\u202f4.2.** Because \\(H\\) is the orthocenter, the quadrilateral \\(PMNH\\) is cyclic: indeed \\(\\angle PMH=\\angle PNH=90^{\\circ}\\). Therefore  \n\n  \\[\n  \\angle PHM=\\angle PNM. \\tag{4.2}\n  \\]\n\n* From (2.1) we have \\(\\angle PNM=\\angle (AP,AN)=\\angle (AM,AP)\\) (the two equalities follow from the fact that \\(M,N\\) are the centres of the original circles). Substituting this into (4.2) gives  \n\n  \\[\n  \\angle PHM=\\angle (AM,AP). \\tag{4.3}\n  \\]\n\n* The line \\(AM\\) is perpendicular to the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\); similarly \\(AN\\) is perpendicular to the tangent to \\(\\Gamma\\) at \\(A\\). Using (2.1) we obtain  \n\n  \\[\n  \\angle (AM,AP)=\\angle (AP,AN). \\tag{4.4}\n  \\]\n\n  Combining (4.3) and (4.4) yields  \n\n  \\[\n  \\boxed{\\;\\angle PHM=\\angle (AP,AN)\\;}. \\tag{4.5}\n  \\]\n\n* Equality (4.5) tells us that the ray \\(PH\\) is symmetric to the ray \\(AP\\) with respect to the line \\(MN\\). Consequently the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) lies on the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); i.e.\n\n  \\[\n  \\boxed{\\;HH'\\parallel AP\\;}. \\tag{4.6}\n  \\]\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the segment \\(HH'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Tangency after inversion\n\nUnder the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) the line \\(HH'\\) (which, by (4.6), is parallel to \\(AP\\)) is sent to the line \\(AP\\) itself, because \\(AP\\) is the unique line through the centre of inversion that is invariant.  \n\nThe circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) does **not** pass through the centre \\(A\\); therefore its image \\(\\omega_{B'}\\) under \\(\\mathcal I\\) is a genuine circle passing through the points \\(B',E',F'\\).\n\nLemma\u202fL states that the directed angles formed by \\(AP\\) with the chords \\(B'E'\\) and \\(B'F'\\) are equal. By the **tangent\u2011chord theorem**, this equality is equivalent to the statement that the line \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circle \\(\\omega_{B'}\\) at the point \\(B'\\).\n\nHence, after inversion, the image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (namely \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the image of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Returning to the original configuration\n\nInversion preserves tangency: a line tangent to a circle is mapped to a line tangent to the image of that circle. Consequently the pre\u2011image of the tangent line \\(AP\\) \u2013 which is the line \\(HH'\\) \u2013 is tangent to the pre\u2011image of \\(\\omega_{B'}\\), i.e. to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nBy (4.6) the line \\(HH'\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was to be proved. \u220e"
