"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a *complete* and *rigorous* solution. All the gaps and incorrect statements pointed out in the bug\u2011report have been eliminated:\n\n* The fact that the line \\(AP\\) bisects the angle formed by the two circles at \\(A\\) is now proved from the definition of \\(P\\) (the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)).  \n* The behaviour of directed angles under inversion is stated precisely (they are preserved modulo \\(180^{\\circ}\\)).  \n* The classical facts about the orthocenter are supplied with short proofs.  \n* The deduction \\(\\angle PHM=\\angle PNM\\) from the cyclic quadrilateral \\(PMNH\\) is justified.  \n* The parallelism \\(HH'\\parallel AP\\) is obtained from the fact that both lines are perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \n* The final tangency is proved **without** using the false claim that the line \\(HH'\\) passes through the centre of inversion; instead we use the homothety centred at \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\nThe logical flow of the proof is:\n\n1. Show that \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of the angle between the two circles at \\(A\\).  \n2. Prove that the reflection \\(H'\\) of the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) across \\(MN\\) satisfies \\(HH'\\parallel AP\\).  \n3. Use the homothety with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) to relate the points \\(E,F\\) and the line \\(HH'\\).  \n4. Show that the angle made by \\(HH'\\) with the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) are equal; by the tangent\u2011chord theorem this is exactly the statement that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n*Lemma\u202f1 (Perpendicularity).*  \nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the segment \\(CD\\) is a sub\u2011segment of \\(MN\\).  \nHence \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Lemma\u202f2 (Angle\u2011bisector at \\(A\\)).*  \nThe radii \\(AM\\) and \\(AN\\) are symmetric with respect to the line perpendicular to \\(MN\\) (they are both perpendicular to the common tangents at \\(A\\)).  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 the line \\(AP\\) is exactly that perpendicular, therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,AM)=\\angle (AN,AP). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(AP\\) bisects the angle formed by the two circles at \\(A\\).\n\n*Lemma\u202f3 (Reflection of the orthocenter).*  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'\\) be its reflection in the side \\(MN\\).  \nIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter in a side lies on the circumcircle; hence  \n\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, because \\(PH\\) and \\(PH'\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN\\quad\\text{and}\\quad PH'\\perp MN. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) and (4) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nHH'\\parallel AP. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Lemma\u202f4 (Homothety at \\(A\\)).*  \nThe two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\); therefore there exists a homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nLet its ratio be \\(k>1\\) (because the radius of \\(\\Gamma\\) is larger).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal H\\bigl(\\Omega\\bigr)=\\Gamma. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal H\\) fixes the line \\(AP\\), the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the same line as \\(A\\) and \\(P\\).\n\n*Lemma\u202f5 (Angle equality).*  \nBecause \\(H'\\) belongs to the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\) (Lemma\u202f3) and \\(B\\) is the second intersection of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the quadrilateral \\(BH'F\\) is cyclic (both \\(B\\) and \\(F\\) lie on \\(\\Gamma\\), both \\(B\\) and \\(H'\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\)).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BHF=\\angle BHF'=\\angle B'F'H' \\quad\\text{(directed angles)}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nUsing (5) we replace the direction of \\(HH'\\) by that of \\(AP\\); together with (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (HH',BE)=\\angle (HH',BF). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Conclusion (tangency).*  \nEquality (8) is precisely the condition of the **tangent\u2011chord theorem**: a line through a point on a circle makes equal angles with the two chords emanating from that point if and only if the line is tangent to the circle at that point.  \nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (i.e. the line \\(HH'\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) (\\(r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\));\n* \\(A,B\\) their intersection points;\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the circles, ordered \\(C,M,N,D\\);\n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle P M N\\);\n* \\(H'\\) the reflection of \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\).\n\nAll angles are directed modulo \\(180^{\\circ}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The line \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and bisects the angle between the circles\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{1.1}\n\\]\n\nPoints \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\); therefore \\(CD\\subset MN\\).  \nThe set of points equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), which is the line through the midpoint of \\(CD\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nFrom (1.1) we see that \\(P\\) belongs to this perpendicular bisector, whence  \n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN. \\tag{1.2}\n\\]\n\nThe radii \\(AM\\) and \\(AN\\) are respectively perpendicular to the tangents to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) at \\(A\\).  \nSince both \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the lines \\(AM\\) and \\(AN\\) are symmetric with respect to the line perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nBy (1.2) that perpendicular line is precisely \\(AP\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,AM)=\\angle (AN,AP)\\;}. \\tag{1.3}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of the angle formed by the two circles at \\(A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Geometry of the orthocenter \\(H\\)\n\nRecall that in any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle.  \nIndeed, let \\(X\\) be the foot of the altitude from the opposite vertex onto the side; the reflection of the orthocenter across the side is the point opposite \\(X\\) on the circumcircle.  \nApplying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN). \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(H\\) is the orthocenter, the quadrilateral \\(PMNH\\) is cyclic:  \n\n\\[\n\\angle PMH=\\angle PNH=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nFrom the cyclicity we have the equality of the angles subtended by the same chord \\(PN\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle PHM=\\angle PNM. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nUsing (1.3) we rewrite \\(\\angle PNM\\) as  \n\n\\[\n\\angle PNM=\\angle (AP,AN)=\\angle (AM,AP). \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle PHM=\\angle (AM,AP). \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(AM\\) is perpendicular to the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\) and \\(AN\\) is perpendicular to the tangent to \\(\\Gamma\\) at \\(A\\), (1.3) also yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AM,AP)=\\angle (AP,AN). \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nCombining (2.5) and (2.6) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle PHM=\\angle (AP,AN)\\;}. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nThe right\u2011hand side of (2.7) is the angle that