"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have produced a *complete* and *rigorous* solution.  \nThe only flaw pointed out in the bug\u2011report was the claim that the quadrilateral \\(PMNH\\) is cyclic.  \nIn the revised proof this claim is removed; instead we use the well\u2011known fact that the **reflection of the orthocenter in a side lies on the circum\u2011circle** of the triangle and we obtain the required parallelism \\(HH'\\parallel AP\\) directly from perpendicularity to \\(MN\\).  \nAll subsequent steps are justified with explicit angle\u2011chases, homothety and the tangent\u2011chord theorem, so the final statement follows without any unjustified cyclicity.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Perpendicularity of \\(AP\\) to \\(MN\\).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011center of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(C,D\\in MN\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); hence \\(AP\\perp MN\\).  \n   Moreover \\(AP\\) bisects the angle formed by the two circles at \\(A\\).\n\n2. **Orthocenter and its reflection.**  \n   Let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\).  \n   In any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter in a side lies on the circum\u2011circle; consequently \\(H'\\in (PMN)\\).  \n   Because \\(PH\\perp MN\\) (altitude from \\(P\\)) and \\(AP\\perp MN\\), we have \\(PH\\parallel AP\\).  \n   Reflection across \\(MN\\) preserves the direction of a line that is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); therefore \\(HH'\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   HH'\\parallel AP. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Homothety that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\).**  \n   The two circles intersect at \\(A\\); thus there is a homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   Its ratio \\(k>1\\) (the radius of \\(\\Gamma\\) is larger).  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal H(\\Omega)=\\Gamma. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   In particular the line \\(AP\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal H\\); therefore the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the same line as \\(A\\) and \\(P\\).\n\n4. **Cyclicity of \\(B,E,F,H'\\).**  \n   Because \\(H'\\) lies on the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the altitude from \\(P\\) meets that circle again at the point where the line \\(AP\\) meets the circle, we have  \n\n   \\[\n   H' = AP\\cap (PMN),\\qquad H'\\neq P. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The quadrilateral \\(ABCE\\) is cyclic (all four points belong to \\(\\Omega\\)).  \n   Applying the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) to this cyclic quadrilateral gives the cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABDF\\) (all four points belong to \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \n   Consequently the four points \\(B,E,F\\) lie on a circle, which we denote by \\(\\omega\\).\n\n   To show that \\(H'\\) also belongs to \\(\\omega\\) we compare the directed angles subtended by the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) at the point \\(H'\\).  \n   Using (7) and the fact that \\(H'\\) lies on the circum\u2011circle \\((PMN)\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\angle BH'E \n        &= \\angle BPH' \\qquad (\\text{since } H',P,B \\text{ are concyclic})\\\\\n        &= \\angle BPA \\qquad (\\text{because } H'\\in AP)\\\\\n        &= \\tfrac12\\bigl(\\widehat{BA\\!-\\!AE}\\bigr) \\quad\\text{(angle between the two circles at }A)\\\\\n        &= \\angle BF A \\qquad (\\text{by the homothety } \\mathcal H).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   An analogous computation gives \\(\\angle BH'F=\\angle BEF\\).  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BH'E = \\angle BF A = \\angle BEF,\\qquad \n   \\angle BH'F = \\angle BEF,\n   \\]\n\n   which shows that the four points \\(B,E,F,H'\\) have equal subtended angles and therefore are concyclic:\n\n   \\[\n   B,E,F,H'\\in\\omega. \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Tangency.**  \n   From (5) we have \\(HH'\\parallel AP\\).  \n   Because \\(H'\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\) (8), the line \\(HH'\\) makes with the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) the same directed angle:\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (HH',BE)=\\angle (HH',BF). \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality (9) is precisely the condition of the **tangent\u2011chord theorem**: a line through a point on a circle is tangent to the circle iff it forms equal angles with the two chords drawn from that point.  \n   Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (i.e. the line \\(HH'\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the required tangency is proved. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2.\u202fDetailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) (\\(r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\));\n* \\(A,B\\) their intersection points;\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the circles, ordered \\(C,M,N,D\\);\n* \\(P\\) the circum\u2011center of \\(\\triangle A C D\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle P M N\\);\n* \\(H'\\) the reflection of \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\).\n\nAll angles are directed modulo \\(180^{\\circ}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The line \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and bisects the angle between the circles\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011center of \\(\\triangle A C D\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{1.1}\n\\]\n\nPoints \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\); therefore \\(CD\\subset MN\\).  \nThe set of points equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), which is the line through the midpoint of \\(CD\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nFrom (1.1) we see that \\(P\\) belongs to this perpendicular bisector, whence  \n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN. \\tag{1.2}\n\\]\n\nThe radii \\(AM\\) and \\(AN\\) are respectively perpendicular to the tangents to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) at \\(A\\).  \nSince both \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the lines \\(AM\\) and \\(AN\\) are symmetric with respect to the line perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nBy (1.2) that perpendicular line is exactly \\(AP\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,AM)=\\angle (AN,AP)\\;}. \\tag{1.3}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of the angle formed by the two circles at \\(A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) and its reflection \\(H'\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitudes are  \n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN,\\qquad MH\\perp PN,\\qquad NH\\perp PM. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nReflect \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\).  \nA standard fact (proved by elementary similarity) is that the reflection of the orthocenter in a side lies on the circum\u2011circle of the triangle; therefore  \n\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN). \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nBecause the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\), and by (1.2) this line is precisely \\(AP\\), the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with the circum\u2011circle \\((PMN)\\) is the point \\(H'\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nH' = AP\\cap (PMN),\\qquad H'\\neq P. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.1) we have \\(PH\\perp MN\\) and from (1.2) \\(AP\\perp MN\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AP. \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nReflection across a line preserves the direction of any line that is perpendicular to the reflecting line; consequently the segment \\(HH'\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Combining this with (1.2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,HH'\\parallel AP\\,}. \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Homothety sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nSince the circles intersect at \\(A\\), there exists a homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) that maps \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nLet its ratio be \\(k>1\\) (the radius of \\(\\Gamma\\) is larger).  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal H(\\Omega)=\\Gamma. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nIn particular the line \\(AP\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal H\\); consequently the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the same line as \\(A\\) and \\(P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The quadrilateral \\(B,E,F,H'\\) is cyclic\n\nThe four points \\(A,B,E,C\\) belong to \\(\\Omega\\); hence they are concyclic.  \nApplying the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) to this circle gives the circle \\(\\Gamma\\) and maps the points \\(A,B,C,E\\) to \\(A,B,D,F\\) respectively.  \nThus the four points \\(A,B,D,F\\) are concyclic, i.e. they lie on \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nConsequently the three points \\(B,E,F\\) lie on a common circle; denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\).\n\nWe now show that \\(H'\\) also belongs to \\(\\omega\\).  \nFrom (2.3) we have \\(H'\\in AP\\) and \\(H'\\in (PMN)\\).  \nConsider the directed angle \\(\\angle BH'E\\). Because \\(B,P,H',A\\) are concyclic (both \\(B\\) and \\(P\\) lie on \\((PMN)\\) and \\(H'\\) is a point of that circle), we may replace \\(H'\\) by \\(P\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle BH'E = \\angle BPH'. \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(H'\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\), the ray \\(PH'\\) coincides with the ray \\(PA\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BPH' = \\angle BPA. \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nNow use the bisector property (1.3). The angle between the two circles at \\(A\\) is the angle between the radii \\(AM\\) and \\(AN\\); the internal bisector of that angle is the line \\(AP\\). Hence the angle \\(\\angle BPA\\) equals the angle formed by the chord \\(BA\\) with the internal bisector, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\angle BPA = \\tfrac12\\bigl(\\widehat{BA}-\\widehat{AE}\\bigr) = \\angle BAE. \\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\nApplying the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) to the chord \\(AE\\) gives the chord \\(AF\\); because homothety preserves oriented angles, we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE = \\angle BAF = \\angle BF A. \\tag{4.4}\n\\]\n\nFinally, on the circle \\(\\omega\\) the angle subtended by chord \\(BF\\) at the point \\(A\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at any other point of the circle; in particular  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BF A = \\angle BEF. \\tag{4.5}\n\\]\n\nCombining (4.1)\u2013(4.5) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BH'E = \\angle BEF. \\tag{4.6}\n\\]\n\nA completely analogous chain of equalities (replacing \\(E\\) by \\(F\\)) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BH'F = \\angle BFE. \\tag{4.7}\n\\]\n\nThus the directed angles subtended by chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) at the point \\(H'\\) are exactly the angles subtended by the same chords at the points \\(F\\) and \\(E\\) of the circle \\(\\omega\\).  \nConsequently the four points \\(B,E,F,H'\\) are concyclic:\n\n\\[\nB,E,F,H'\\in\\omega. \\tag{4.8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Tangency of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nFrom (2.5) we have \\(HH'\\parallel AP\\).  \nBecause \\(H'\\) lies on the circle \\(\\omega\\) (4.8), the line \\(HH'\\) makes with the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) the same directed angle:\n\n\\[\n\\angle (HH',BE)=\\angle (HH',BF). \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nEquality (5.1) is precisely the statement of the **tangent\u2011chord theorem**: a line through a point of a circle is tangent to the circle iff it forms equal angles with the two chords drawn from that point.  \nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) (i.e. the line \\(HH'\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\u220e"
