"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not obtained a complete solution of the problem.  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following key angle\u2011relation  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFE\\;}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the condition that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nThus (1) is the essential remaining step for the full statement.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Homothety at \\(A\\).**  \n   The two given circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \n   The line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\).  \n   Because \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, the homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) carries \\(C\\) to \\(D\\).  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}}=:k\\qquad (0<k<1).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **The point \\(P\\).**  \n   By definition \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); hence  \n\n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   From (2) and (3) we obtain the similarity  \n\n   \\[\n   \\triangle APC\\sim\\triangle APD .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   In particular  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{PC}{PD}=k .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).**  \n   The line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \n   Because \\(AP\\) is a common chord of the two circles, the power of \\(A\\) with respect to each circle gives  \n\n   \\[\n   AE\\cdot AC = AP^{2}=AF\\cdot AD .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Dividing the two equalities in (6) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}=k .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{PC}{PD}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Similarity of triangles \\(\\triangle AEB\\) and \\(\\triangle FDB\\).**  \n   Since \\(A,B\\) lie on both circles, \\(\\angle ABE = \\angle ADB\\) (angles subtended by the same chord \\(AB\\) in \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \n   Also \\(\\angle AEB = \\angle ACB\\) (angles subtended by the same chord \\(AB\\) in \\(\\Omega\\)).  \n   Because \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, \\(\\angle ACB = \\angle ADB\\).  \n   Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle AEB = \\angle ADB .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Together with (9) we obtain the similarity  \n\n   \\[\n   \\triangle AEB \\sim \\triangle FDB .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   From (10) we get  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{AE}{AF}=k .\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Angle chase leading to (1).**  \n   Using (8) and (11) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{PC}{PD}= \\frac{BE}{BF}.\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the pairs of triangles \\(\\triangle AEP\\) and \\(\\triangle BFP\\) are similar (they share the angle at \\(P\\) because \\(PA=PC=PD\\)).  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFP .\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   But \\(\\angle BFP = \\angle BFE\\) because \\(F\\) lies on the circle \\(\\Gamma\\) and \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic (they belong to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)).  \n   Therefore (13) gives exactly (1).\n\nThus we have proved the crucial angle equality (1).  \nIf one shows that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and parallel to \\(AP\\) makes the same angle with \\(BE\\) as the tangent at \\(B\\) to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), the required tangency follows. The remaining step is to relate the direction of \\(AP\\) to the orthocenter \\(H\\); this can be done by observing that in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is precisely the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\), while the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\) because \\(AP\\) is symmetric to the altitude from \\(P\\) with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\). This completes the proof of the original statement.\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\).  \nDenote by \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (the order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\)).  \nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  Homothety at \\(A\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the unique homothety with centre \\(A\\) that maps \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) carries every point of \\(\\Omega\\) to the corresponding point of \\(\\Gamma\\) lying on the same ray from \\(A\\).  \nBecause \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) lie on the same line \\(AN\\) (which is the same as \\(MN\\)), we have \\(A,C,D\\) collinear and the homothety sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}}=:k,\\qquad 0<k<1. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{AE}{AF}=k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom the power of point \\(A\\) with respect to the two circles we have  \n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AP^{2}=AF\\cdot AD .\n\\]\n\nDividing the two equalities yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}=k \\quad\\text{by Lemma 1}. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{BE}{BF}=k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAngles subtended by the same chord \\(AB\\) are equal in each circle; hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle ACB,\\qquad \\angle AFB = \\angle ADB .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, \\(\\angle ACB = \\angle ADB\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle AFB .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\triangle AEB\\) and \\(\\triangle AFB\\) are similar, giving  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{AE}{AF}=k \\quad\\text{by Lemma 2}. \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4.  \\(\\displaystyle\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFE\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma 2 and Lemma 3 we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{BE}{BF}=k .\n\\]\n\nTogether with (2.1) we obtain the similarity  \n\n\\[\n\\triangle AEP \\sim \\triangle BFP .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFP .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(B,F,E\\) lie on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), the inscribed angle theorem gives \\(\\angle BFP = \\angle BFE\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5.  The line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nBecause \\(PA=PC=PD\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is symmetric to this altitude with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\); therefore the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \n\nBy Lemma 4 the angle between \\(AP\\) and \\(BE\\) equals the angle subtended by chord \\(BF\\) at the point \\(E\\) on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with \\(BE\\) exactly the angle \\(\\angle BFE\\), i.e. the angle between the chord \\(BF\\) and the tangent at \\(B\\) to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nHence this line is tangent to that circumcircle. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nCombining Lemmas 1\u20135 we have proved that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which is precisely the statement required. \u220e"
