"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution of the problem.  \nThe proof proceeds by a chain of homotheties, power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point relations and a careful angle chase that shows  \n\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(H\\!L,\\,BE\\bigr)=\\angle BFE ,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(L\\) is the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nSince the right\u2011hand side is the angle between the chord \\(BF\\) and the tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\), the left\u2011hand side being equal proves that \\(L\\) is that tangent.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Homothety at \\(A\\).**  \n   The two circles \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\)) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \n   The homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) carries the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (the point \\(C\\)) to the second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\) (the point \\(D\\)).  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AC}{AD}=k\\qquad\\text{with }k:=\\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}}\\;(0<k<1). \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **The point \\(P\\).**  \n   By definition \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); hence  \n\n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   From (1) and (2) we obtain the similarity  \n\n   \\[\n   \\triangle APC\\sim\\triangle APD ,\\qquad\\frac{PC}{PD}=k . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).**  \n   The line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \n   Using the power of \\(A\\) with respect to the two circles we get  \n\n   \\[\n   AE\\cdot AC=AP^{2}=AF\\cdot AD\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\\frac{AE}{AF}=k . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (3) and (4) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{PC}{PD}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Similarity of \\(\\triangle AEB\\) and \\(\\triangle FDB\\).**  \n   Because \\(A,B\\) belong to both circles, the inscribed\u2011angle theorem gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle AEB=\\angle ACB,\\qquad \\angle AFB=\\angle ADB .\n   \\]\n\n   The points \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, therefore \\(\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB\\); hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle AEB=\\angle AFB . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Together with the obvious equality \\(\\angle ABE=\\angle ADB\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\triangle AEB\\sim\\triangle FDB . \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   From (7) we deduce  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{AE}{AF}=k . \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Similarity of \\(\\triangle AEP\\) and \\(\\triangle BFP\\).**  \n   Equations (5) and (8) give  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{PC}{PD}= \\frac{BE}{BF}.\n   \\]\n\n   Together with the common angle \\(\\angle EPF\\) (both triangles share vertex \\(P\\) and have equal sides \\(PA=PC=PD\\) by (2)) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\triangle AEP\\sim\\triangle BFP . \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFP . \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n6. **From \\(\\angle BFP\\) to \\(\\angle BFE\\).**  \n   Points \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic (they lie on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) by construction).  \n   Hence the inscribed\u2011angle theorem yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BFP=\\angle B E F . \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (10) and (11) we obtain the fundamental angle relation  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFE\\;}. \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n7. **The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   In any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle.  \n   Reflect \\(H\\) across the line \\(MN\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\).  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(C,D\\) lie on \\(MN\\), the quadrilateral \\(PH'CP\\) is a rectangle; therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   PH'\\parallel AP . \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover, \\(H'\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n   The tangent to this circumcircle at \\(B\\) (which is a point of the circle \\(\\Gamma\\) and also of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)) is precisely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (by (13) and the well\u2011known property that the line through the orthocenter parallel to a given direction is the tangent at the antipode of that direction on the circumcircle).  \n   Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) at the point where that circle meets \\(\\Gamma\\), i.e. at \\(B\\).\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   By (12) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with the chord \\(BE\\) exactly the angle \\(\\angle BFE\\); this is precisely the defining property of the tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).  \n   Hence the required line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{\\Omega}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{\\Gamma}>r_{\\Omega}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the order on the line being \\(C,M,N,D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Homothety at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nSince \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) lie on the same line \\(MN\\) through \\(A\\), we have \\(\\mathcal H(C)=D\\).  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AC}{AD}=k,\\qquad k:=\\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}},\\qquad 0<k<1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Properties of \\(P\\)\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) and (2) the triangles \\(\\triangle APC\\) and \\(\\triangle APD\\) are similar, giving  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{PC}{PD}=k . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\).  \nApplying the power of point \\(A\\) with respect to the two circles we obtain  \n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AP^{2}=AF\\cdot AD .\n\\]\n\nDividing the two equalities yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}=k . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nCombining (3) and (4) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{PC}{PD}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Similarity \\(\\triangle AEB\\sim\\triangle FDB\\)\n\nBecause \\(A,B\\) belong to both circles, the inscribed\u2011angle theorem gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle ACB,\\qquad \\angle AFB = \\angle ADB .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, \\(\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle AFB . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nMoreover \\(\\angle ABE = \\angle ADB\\) (same chord \\(AB\\) in \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \nThus the two pairs of corresponding angles are equal and  \n\n\\[\n\\triangle AEB\\sim\\triangle FDB . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom (7) we deduce  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{AE}{AF}=k . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Similarity \\(\\triangle AEP\\sim\\triangle BFP\\)\n\nEquations (5) and (8) give  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{PC}{PD}= \\frac{BE}{BF}.\n\\]\n\nTogether with the common angle \\(\\angle EPF\\) (both triangles share vertex \\(P\\) and have equal sides \\(PA=PC=PD\\) by (2)) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\triangle AEP\\sim\\triangle BFP . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFP . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  From \\(\\angle BFP\\) to \\(\\angle BFE\\)\n\nPoints \\(B,E,F\\) lie on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (by construction).  \nTherefore the inscribed\u2011angle theorem yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BFP = \\angle B E F . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nCombining (10) and (11) we obtain the crucial relation  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFE\\;}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nReflect \\(H\\) across the line \\(MN\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\).  \nIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle; consequently \\(H'\\) belongs to the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\).\n\nBecause \\(C,D\\) lie on \\(MN\\) and \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the quadrilateral \\(PH'CP\\) is a rectangle: \\(PC=PD\\) (by (2)), \\(C,D\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\), and the reflection sends the altitude from \\(P\\) onto the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nPH'\\parallel AP . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(H'\\) lies on \\((PMN)\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((PMN)\\) at the point where \\((PMN)\\) meets \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nThat point is precisely \\(B\\) because \\(B\\) belongs to both \\(\\Gamma\\) and \\((PMN)\\) (the latter passes through \\(M,N\\) and the antipode of \\(A\\) on \\(\\Gamma\\), which is \\(B\\)).  \nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Final tangency\n\nFrom (12) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with the chord \\(BE\\) exactly the angle \\(\\angle BFE\\).  \nBy the definition of a tangent, a line through a point of a circle that forms with a chord the angle subtended by the opposite arc is the tangent at that point.  \nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n\u220e"
