s equidistant from \\(A,C,D\\), triangles \\(APC\\) and \\(APD\\) share side \\(AP\\) and have the ratio of the other two sides equal to the ratio of the corresponding chords \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\), which is \\(k\\). Hence the similarity and the ratio follow.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\n> \u201cApplying the power of point \\(A\\) with respect to the two circles we obtain \\(AE\\cdot AC = AP^{2}=AF\\cdot AD.\\) Dividing the two equalities yields \\(\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}=k.\\)\u201d\n\n**Assessment:** Correct. For a point on a circle, the power equals the product of the lengths of the two intersecting chords; here \\(A\\) lies on both circles, so the equalities hold and the ratio follows.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Similarity \\(\\triangle AEB\\sim\\triangle FDB\\)\n\n> \u201cBecause \\(A,B\\) belong to both circles, the inscribed\u2011angle theorem gives \\(\\angle AEB = \\angle ACB,\\qquad \\angle AFB = \\angle ADB.\\) Since \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, \\(\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB\\); hence \\(\\angle AEB = \\angle AFB .\\) Moreover \\(\\angle ABE = \\angle ADB\\) (same chord \\(AB\\) in \\(\\Gamma\\)). Thus the two pairs of corresponding angles are equal and \\(\\triangle AEB\\sim\\triangle FDB .\\)\u201d\n\n**Issue:** **Critical Error.**  \n\n* The claim \u201c\\(\\angle ACB = \\angle ADB\\) because \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear\u201d is false. \\(\\angle ACB\\) is the angle between \\(AC\\) and \\(CB\\); \\(\\angle ADB\\) is the angle between \\(AD\\) and \\(DB\\). Collinearity of \\(C,M,N,D\\) only tells us that \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the same line, but it does **not** make the two angles equal. In general the two angles are unrelated.  \n\n* Consequently the derived equality \\(\\angle AEB = \\angle AFB\\) is unsupported.  \n\n* The subsequent statement that \\(\\angle ABE = \\angle ADB\\) is also incorrect: \\(\\angle ABE\\) subtends chord \\(AE\\) in circle \\(\\Omega\\), not chord \\(AB\\) in \\(\\Gamma\\); the inscribed\u2011angle theorem 