ne through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with the chord \\(BE\\) exactly the angle \\(\\angle BFE\\). By the definition of a tangent, a line through a point of a circle that forms with a chord the angle subtended by the opposite arc is the tangent at that point. Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue:** **Critical Error.**  \n\n* Equation (12) states \\(\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFE\\). To infer tangency at \\(B\\), the line in question must pass through \\(B\\). The constructed line is **through \\(H\\)** and parallel to \\(AP\\); there is no proof that this line meets the circle at \\(B\\). In fact, unless \\(H\\) lies on the line \\(AB\\) (which has not been shown), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) will not pass through \\(B\\).  \n\n* Consequently the application of the tangent\u2011chord angle criterion is invalid, and the final statement is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### Overall Conclusion\n\nThe solution fails at several pivotal points:\n\n1. Incorrect angle equalities leading to a false similarity claim in Step\u202f4.\n2. Unjustified similarity in Step\u202f5 that relies on a non\u2011existent common angle.\n3. Misapplication of the inscribed\u2011angle theorem in Step\u202f6.\n4. Unfounded geometric constructions concerning the orthocenter and the parallelism in Step\u202f7.\n5. The final tangency argument in Step\u202f8 rests on a line that has not been shown to pass through the point of tangency.\n\nBecause these errors break the logical chain of the proof, the solution does **not** constitute a valid proof of the problem statement."
