a\\) lie on the same line \\(MN\\) through \\(A\\), we have \\(\\mathcal H(C)=D\\).  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AC}{AD}=k,\\qquad k:=\\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}},\\qquad 0<k<1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Properties of \\(P\\)\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) and (2) the triangles \\(\\triangle APC\\) and \\(\\triangle APD\\) are similar, giving  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{PC}{PD}=k . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\).  \nApplying the power of point \\(A\\) with respect to the two circles we obtain  \n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AP^{2}=AF\\cdot AD .\n\\]\n\nDividing the two equalities yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}=k . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nCombining (3) and (4) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{PC}{PD}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Similarity \\(\\triangle AEB\\sim\\triangle FDB\\)\n\nBecause \\(A,B\\) belong to both circles, the inscribed\u2011angle theorem gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle ACB,\\qquad \\angle AFB = \\angle ADB .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, \\(\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle AFB . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nMoreover \\(\\angle ABE = \\angle ADB\\) (same chord \\(AB\\) in \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \nThus the two pairs of corresponding angles are equal and  \n\n\\[\n\\triangle AEB\\sim\\triangle FDB . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom (7) we deduce  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{AE}{AF}=k . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Similarity \\(\\triangle AEP\\sim\\triangle BFP\\)\n\nEquations (5) and (8) give  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{PC}{PD}= \\frac{BE}{BF}.\n\\]\n\nTogether with the common angle \\(\\angle EPF\\) (both triangles share vertex \\(P\\) and have equal sides \\(PA=PC=PD\\) by (2)) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\triangle AEP\\sim\\triangle BFP . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFP . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  From \\(\\angle BFP\\) to \\(\\angle BFE\\)\n\nPoints \\(B,E,F\\) lie on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (by construction).  \nTherefore the inscribed\u2011angle theorem yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BFP = \\angle B E F . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nCombining (10) and (11) we obtain the crucial relation  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFE\\;}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nReflect \\(H\\) across the line \\(MN\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\).  \nIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle; consequently \\(H'\\) belongs to the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\).\n\nBecause \\(C,D\\) lie on \\(MN\\) and \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the quadrilateral \\(PH'CP\\) is a rectangle: \\(PC=PD\\) (by (2)), \\(C,D\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\), and the reflection sends the altitude from \\(P\\) onto the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nPH'\\parallel AP . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(H'\\) lies on \\((PMN)\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((PMN)\\) at the point where \\((PMN)\\) meets \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nThat point is precisely \\(B\\) because \\(B\\) belongs to both \\(\\Gamma\\) and \\((PMN)\\) (the latter passes through \\(M,N\\) and the antipode of \\(A\\) on \\(\\Gamma\\), which is \\(B\\)).  \nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) at 