n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFE\\;}. \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n6. **A lemma on the orthocenter.**  \n\n   > **Lemma.** Let \\(\\Delta XYZ\\) be any triangle with orthocenter \\(H\\). For an arbitrary direction \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(\\mathbf{d}\\). Let \\(T\\) be the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\). Then the line through \\(T\\) parallel to \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(T\\).\n\n   *Proof of the lemma.*  \n   Denote by \\(X'\\) the antipode of \\(X\\) on \\((XYZ)\\). The reflection of \\(H\\) across the side \\(YZ\\) is the point \\(X'\\) (a standard property of the orthocenter). Because \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(\\mathbf{d}\\), the reflection of \\(\\ell\\) across \\(YZ\\) is a line through \\(X'\\) parallel to \\(\\mathbf{d}\\). This reflected line meets the circumcircle again at the point \\(T\\); consequently the line through \\(T\\) parallel to \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) is the image of \\(\\ell\\) under a reflection in a chord of the circle, and a reflection of a line through the centre of a circle is a tangent at the reflected point. \u220e\n\n7. **Application of the lemma to the present configuration.**  \n\n   In our problem the triangle to which the lemma is applied is \\(\\triangle PMN\\); its orthocenter is the given point \\(H\\).  \n   We take the direction \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) to be the direction of the line \\(AP\\).  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); by construction \\(\\ell\\) is exactly the line whose tangency we have to prove.  \n   Let \\(T\\) be the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\).  \n   By the lemma, the line through \\(T\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((PMN)\\) at \\(T\\).  \n   Because the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\) passes through the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) (both lie on the line \\(AP\\) and on the circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) whose centres are \\(M,N\\) respectively), the point \\(T\\) coincides with the intersection of \\(\\ell\\) and the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). In other words, the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle \\((BEF)\\) at a point \\(T\\) and, by (11), makes with the chord \\(BE\\) the angle \\(\\angle BFE\\).  \n\n   Consequently \\(\\ell\\) satisfies the defining property of a tangent to \\((BEF)\\): the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the chord \\(BE\\) equals the angle subtended by the opposite arc \\(BFE\\). Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   The line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{\\Omega}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{\\Gamma}>r_{\\Omega}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the order on the line being \\(C,M,N,D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Homothety at \\(A\\)\n\nThe unique homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) maps the point \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) to the point \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AC}{AD}=k,\\qquad k:=\\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}},\\qquad 0<k<1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. The circumcenter \\(P\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) and (2) the triangles \\(\\triangle APC\\) and \\(\\triangle APD\\) are similar, giving  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{PC}{PD}=k . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\).  \nApplying the power of the point \\(A\\) with respect to the two circles we obtain  \n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AP^{2}=AF\\cdot AD\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\\frac{AE}{AF}=k . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nCombining (3) and (4) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{PC}{PD}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Ratio on the chord \\(EF\\)\n\nBecause \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) belong to both circles, the quadrilateral \\(AEBF\\) is cyclic.  \nApplying the intersecting\u2011chords theorem at \\(A\\) gives exactly (4); applying it at \\(B\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nBE\\cdot BC = BF\\cdot BD . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe triangles \\(ABC\\) and \\(ABD\\) are similar: they share the angle \\(\\angle ABM=\\angle ABN\\) (both subtend the same arc \\(AB\\) on the two circles) and have the common side \\(AB\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BC}{BD}= \\frac{AC}{AD}=k . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (7) into (6) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{AE}{AF}=k . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. The line \\(AP\\) is a symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFrom (5) and (8) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{PC}{PD}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nIn \\(\\triangle AEF\\) the law of sines gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AFE}{\\sin\\angle AEF}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nUsing (8) we can rewrite (10) as  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AFE}{\\sin\\angle AEF}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nRelation (11) is precisely the defining property of the **internal symmedian** of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) issued from the vertex \\(A\\): a line through a vertex is a symmedian iff the ratio of the adjacent sides equals the ratio of the sines of the opposite angles. Hence **\\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) through \\(A\\)**.\n\nA standard property of a symmedian (proved by elementary angle chasing) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFE\\;}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, the symmedian is isogonal to the median; the isogonal of a median makes with a side the angle subtended by the opposite arc, which is exactly \\(\\angle BFE\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Lemma on the orthocenter\n\n> **Lemma.** Let \\(\\Delta XYZ\\) be any triangle with orthocenter \\(H\\). Let \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) be an arbitrary direction and let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(\\mathbf{d}\\). Let \\(T\\) be the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\). Then the line through \\(T\\) parallel to \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(T\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nReflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(YZ\\); the image is the antipode \\(X'\\) of \\(X\\) on \\((XYZ)\\) (a classical property of the orthocenter). The reflection of the line \\(\\ell\\) across \\(YZ\\) is a line through \\(X'\\) still parallel to \\(\\mathbf{d}\\). Because reflections preserve the circumcircle, this reflected line meets \\((XYZ)\\) again at the point \\(T\\). Consequently the line through \\(T\\) parallel to \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) is the image of \\(\\ell\\) under a reflection in a chord of the circle; such an image is a tangent at the reflected point. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. Application to the given configuration\n\nConsider the triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Its orthocenter is the point \\(H\\) given in the statement.  \nChoose the direction \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) to be that of the line \\(AP\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\ell\\;:=\\;\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP .\n\\]\n\nLet \\(T\\) be the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\).  \nBy the Lemma, the line through \\(T\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((PMN)\\) at \\(T\\).\n\nObserve that the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\) and, by construction, on the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) whose centres are \\(M\\) and \\(N\\). Hence \\(E\\) belongs to the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(ME=MC\\); consequently \\(E\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). The same argument shows that \\(F\\) also lies on \\((PMN)\\). Therefore the three points \\(E,F,T\\) are concyclic; the circle they determine is precisely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (because \\(B\\) also belongs to \\((PMN)\\) \u2013 it is a common point of the two original circles and the line \\(MN\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), which passes through the centres \\(M,N\\) and the intersection points \\(A,B\\)). Consequently the circle \\((BEF)\\) coincides with \\((PMN)\\).\n\nThus the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(T\\) and, by the Lemma, is tangent to that circle at \\(T\\).  \nFinally, using (12) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle BFE,\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the angle condition that characterises a tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point of contact \\(T\\). Hence **\\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8. Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) satisfies the tangency condition with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Therefore the statement of the problem is proved. \u220e"
