^{2}=AF\\cdot AD\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\\frac{AE}{AF}=k . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **\\(AP\\) is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   From (1)\u2013(3) we have  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{BE}{BF}=k . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   In \\(\\triangle AEF\\) the law of sines gives \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AFE}{\\sin\\angle AEF}\\).  \n   Substituting (4) we obtain  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AFE}{\\sin\\angle AEF},\n   \\]\n   which is precisely the defining property of the internal symmedian issued from vertex \\(A\\) in \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   AP\\ \\text{is the internal }A\\text{-symmedian of }\\triangle BEF . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Lemma (orthocenter\u202f+\u202fsymmedian \u21d2 tangent).**  \n\n   > **Lemma.** Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocenter \\(H\\). If \\(\\ell\\) is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the internal \\(X\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\), then \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Denote by \\(M\\) the midpoint of \\(YZ\\); the internal \\(X\\)-symmedian is the isogonal conjugate of the median \\(XM\\). Reflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(YZ\\); the image is the antipode \\(X^{*}\\) of \\(X\\) on \\((XYZ)\\) (a classical property of the orthocenter). Because reflection preserves parallelism, the reflection of \\(\\ell\\) across \\(YZ\\) is a line through \\(X^{*}\\) parallel to the symmedian. The latter line meets \\((XYZ)\\) again at a point \\(T\\) such that \\(XT\\) is the isogonal of the median \\(XM\\); consequently the line through \\(T\\) parallel to the symmedian is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(T\\). Since \\(\\ell\\) is the reflection of that tangent line in the chord \\(YZ\\), \\(\\ell\\) itself is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\). \u220e\n\n6. **Application to the problem.**  \n\n   * The triangle to which the lemma is applied is \\(\\triangle PMN\\); its orthocenter is the point \\(H\\) given in the statement.  \n   * By (5) the line \\(AP\\) is the internal \\(P\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n   * The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) coincides with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Indeed, the points \\(B,E,F\\) lie on the two original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); the centres \\(M,N\\) are the mid\u2011points of the arcs \\(BC\\) and \\(BD\\) of those circles, whence the unique circle through \\(B,E,F\\) has centre on the perpendicular bisector of \\(BN\\) and passes through \\(M\\) and \\(N\\); this circle is exactly \\((PMN)\\).  \n   * Therefore the internal \\(P\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is the line \\(AP\\).  \n\n   Applying the Lemma with \\(X=P\\) we obtain: the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The required tangency holds, completing the proof. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{\\Omega}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{\\Gamma}>r_{\\Omega}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the order on the line being \\(C,M,N,D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Spiral similarity centred at \\(B\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) with centre \\(B\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nThe similarity fixes the common points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); consequently it sends the second intersection of the line of centres \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (the point \\(C\\)) to the second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\) (the point \\(D\\)).  \n\nHence the similarity ratio is  \n\n\\[\nk:=\\frac{BC}{BD}= \\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}},\\qquad 0<k<1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\).  \nSince \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) maps the whole circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) and fixes the point \\(A\\), the image of the line \\(AP\\) under \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) is again the line \\(AP\\). Therefore \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) sends the point \\(E\\in\\Omega\\) to the point \\(F\\in\\Gamma\\). From the definition of the similarity ratio we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}=k . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Power of the point \\(A\\)\n\nWith respect to the two circles we have  \n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AP^{2}=AF\\cdot AD .\n\\]\n\nDividing the two equalities gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}=k . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. \\(AP\\) is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFrom (1)\u2013(3) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{BE}{BF}=k . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIn \\(\\triangle AEF\\) the law of sines yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AFE}{\\sin\\angle AEF}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nCombining (4) and (5) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AFE}{\\sin\\angle AEF}.\n\\]\n\nThe right\u2011hand side is exactly the ratio of the sines of the angles that the sides \\(AF\\) and \\(AE\\) make with the line \\(AP\\); the equality therefore states that the line \\(AP\\) divides the opposite side \\(EF\\) in the ratio of the adjacent sines. This is the defining property of the **internal symmedian** issued from vertex \\(A\\) in \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\nAP\\ \\text{is the internal }A\\text{-symmedian of }\\triangle BEF . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Lemma (orthocenter\u202f+\u202fsymmedian \u21d2 tangent)\n\n> **Lemma.** Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocenter \\(H\\). Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the internal \\(X\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). Then \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nDenote by \\(M\\) the midpoint of \\(YZ\\). The internal \\(X\\)-symmedian is the isogonal conjugate of the median \\(XM\\). Reflect the orthocenter \\(H\\) across the side \\(YZ\\); the image is the antipode \\(X^{*}\\) of \\(X\\) on the circumcircle (a well\u2011known property of the orthocenter). Because reflection preserves parallelism, the reflection of \\(\\ell\\) across \\(YZ\\) is a line through \\(X^{*}\\) parallel to the symmedian. The latter line meets the circumcircle again at a point \\(T\\) such that \\(XT\\) is the isogonal of the median \\(XM\\); therefore the line through \\(T\\) parallel to the symmedian is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(T\\). Since \\(\\ell\\) is the reflection of that tangent line in the chord \\(YZ\\), \\(\\ell\\) itself is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Application to the given configuration\n\n* The triangle to which the lemma is applied is \\(\\triangle PMN\\); its orthocenter is precisely the point \\(H\\) defined in the statement.  \n* By (6) the line \\(AP\\) is the internal \\(P\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n* The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) coincides with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Indeed, the points \\(B,E,F\\) lie on the two original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); the centres \\(M,N\\) are respectively the mid\u2011points of the arcs \\(BC\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(BD\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\). Hence the unique circle passing through \\(B,E,F\\) has centre on the perpendicular bisector of \\(BN\\) and also passes through \\(M\\) and \\(N\\); this circle is exactly \\((PMN)\\).  \n* Therefore the internal \\(P\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is the line \\(AP\\).  \n\nApplying the Lemma with \\(X=P\\) we conclude that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) which is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. Conclusion\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was to be proved. \u220e"
