hole circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) and fixes the point \\(A\\), the image of the line \\(AP\\) under \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) is again the line \\(AP\\).\u201d  \n  **Issue:** Justification Gap \u2013 a spiral similarity fixing two points sends any line through those points to itself, but this needs explicit mention that \\(P\\) lies on the image of \\(AP\\); the argument that \\(P\\) is mapped to itself is not given.\n\n* **Location:** Step\u202f1 \u2013 \u201cThe similarity fixes the common points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); consequently it sends the second intersection of the line of centres \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (the point \\(C\\)) to the second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\) (the point \\(D\\)).\u201d  \n  **Issue:** Justification Gap \u2013 while true for the spiral similarity centred at \\(B\\), the solution does not prove that the line \\(MN\\) is invariant under this similarity; this requires showing that \\(B\\), \\(M\\), \\(N\\) are collinear, which is not addressed.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Verification Log\n\nBelow each quoted portion of the proposed solution is followed by an analysis that either confirms its correctness or explains the identified problem.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Spiral similarity centred at \\(B\\)\n\n> \u201cBecause the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) with centre \\(B\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n> The similarity fixes the common points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); consequently it sends the second intersection of the line of centres \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (the point \\(C\\)) to the second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\) (the point \\(D\\)).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe existence of a spiral similarity centred at a common point of two intersecting circles that maps one circle onto the other is standard. However, the claim that this similarity sends the line of centres \\(MN\\) to itself (hence \\(C\\) to \\(D\\)) is not justified. The line \\(