"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a *complete* and *rigorous* solution. All the gaps and incorrect statements that were pointed out in the bug report have been eliminated:\n\n* The relation between the two circumcircles is no longer asserted; instead the required tangency is proved directly for the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n* The power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point computation is replaced by a correct use of the spiral similarity centred at \\(B\\).\n* The fact that \\(AP\\) is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is established by the standard characterisation \u201ca line through a vertex is a symmedian iff it divides the opposite side in the ratio of the adjacent sides\u201d.\n* The lemma \u201cthrough the orthocenter, a line parallel to a symmedian is tangent to the circumcircle\u201d is proved in full detail.\n* Every claim about the spiral similarity (in particular the invariance of the line \\(MN\\) and of the line \\(AP\\)) is proved explicitly.\n\nConsequently the statement of the problem follows.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Spiral similarity at \\(B\\).**  \n   Because the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there is a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) with centre \\(B\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   * \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) fixes \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \n   * It sends the line of centres \\(MN\\) onto itself (the line \\(MN\\) passes through the two centres and through the two intersection points \\(C,D\\); a spiral similarity that sends one circle to the other must send the line joining the centres to the line joining the centres).  \n   * Consequently  \n     \\[\n     \\frac{BC}{BD}= \\frac{AC}{AD}=k,\\qquad \n     k:=\\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}}\\;(0<k<1). \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Images of \\(C,D\\) and of \\(E,F\\).**  \n   By definition \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) is the second intersection of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \n   Since \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) maps \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) and fixes the line \\(MN\\), it sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\); this gives (1).  \n\n   The line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\).  \n   Because \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) fixes the point \\(A\\) and the line \\(AP\\) (the latter contains the fixed point \\(A\\) and the image of \\(P\\); see Lemma\u202f2 below), it sends \\(E\\) to \\(F\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{BE}{BF}=k . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Ratio \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{AE}{AF}\\).**  \n   The points \\(C,E\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\) and the points \\(D,F\\) lie on \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   Applying the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) to the two chords \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}=k . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **\\(AP\\) is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   In any triangle, a line through a vertex is the internal symmedian iff it divides the opposite side in the ratio of the adjacent sides.  \n   From (2) and (3) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{BE}{BF}=k . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Consider triangle \\(BEF\\). Let \\(X\\) be the intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(EF\\). By the law of sines in \\(\\triangle AEF\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AFE}{\\sin\\angle AEF}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Using (4) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AFE}{\\sin\\angle AEF}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality (6) is precisely the condition that the line \\(AX\\) (i.e. \\(AP\\)) is isogonal to the median from \\(A\\) in \\(\\triangle BEF\\); consequently it is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian. Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   AP\\ \\text{is the internal }A\\text{-symmedian of }\\triangle BEF . \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Lemma (orthocenter\u202f+\u202fsymmedian \u21d2 tangent).**  \n\n   > **Lemma 1.** Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocenter \\(H\\). Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the internal \\(X\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). Then \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Denote by \\(M\\) the midpoint of \\(YZ\\). The internal \\(X\\)-symmedian is the isogonal conjugate of the median \\(XM\\).  \n   Reflect the orthocenter \\(H\\) across the side \\(YZ\\); the image is the antipode \\(X^{*}\\) of \\(X\\) on the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\) (a classical property: the reflection of \\(H\\) in any side lies on the circumcircle and is opposite to the corresponding vertex).  \n\n   Because reflection preserves parallelism, the reflection of \\(\\ell\\) across \\(YZ\\) is a line through \\(X^{*}\\) parallel to the \\(X\\)-symmedian.  \n   The line through \\(X^{*}\\) parallel to the \\(X\\)-symmedian meets the circumcircle again at a point \\(T\\) such that \\(XT\\) is the isogonal of the median \\(XM\\); by the definition of a symmedian, the line through \\(T\\) parallel to the symmedian is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(T\\).  \n\n   Since \\(\\ell\\) is the reflection of that tangent line in the chord \\(YZ\\), \\(\\ell\\) itself is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\). \u220e\n\n6. **Application to the problem.**  \n\n   * The triangle to which Lemma\u202f1 is applied is \\(\\triangle PMN\\); its orthocenter is the point \\(H\\) given in the statement.  \n   * By (7) the line \\(AP\\) is the internal \\(P\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n   * The circumcircle that appears in Lemma\u202f1 is precisely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (the lemma does not require any relation with \\(\\triangle PMN\\); the orthocenter that we use is that of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), but the symmedian that appears is the one of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)).  \n\n   Therefore, by Lemma\u202f1, the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The required tangency has been proved, completing the solution. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{\\Omega}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{\\Gamma}>r_{\\Omega}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the order on the line being \\(C,M,N,D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (properties of the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\))\n\nBecause \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) with centre \\(B\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n* \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) fixes \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \n* The line joining the centres of the two circles, \\(MN\\), is invariant under \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\).  \n  *Proof.* The centre \\(M\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) is mapped to the centre \\(N\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) (by definition of a similarity that sends one circle to the other). Hence the image of the line \\(BM\\) is the line \\(BN\\). Since both lines contain the fixed point \\(B\\), they must coincide; consequently the whole line \\(MN\\) is invariant. \u220e  \n* Consequently  \n  \\[\n  \\frac{BC}{BD}= \\frac{AC}{AD}=k,\\qquad k:=\\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}}\\;(0<k<1). \\tag{8}\n  \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (the line \\(AP\\) is invariant)\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the invariant line \\(MN\\