the previous (incorrect) claim that \\(MN\\) is invariant. Since Lemma\u202f2 is flawed, this claim is not justified.\n\n2. **Argument that \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of chord \\(CD\\).**  \n   *Correct*: Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the line \\(AP\\) indeed passes through the midpoint of \\(CD\\) and is perpendicular to \\(CD\\).\n\n3. **Conclusion that \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) fixes the whole line \\(AP\\).**  \n   *Justification Gap*: The solution asserts that because the image of chord \\(CD\\) is itself (as \\(C\\leftrightarrow D\\)), the image of its perpendicular bisector must be the same line. However, a spiral similarity that swaps the endpoints of a segment does not necessarily fix its perpendicular bisector; it could rotate the bisector unless additional symmetry is proved. The argument lacks a rigorous justification.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f1 \u2013 Ratios supplied by the spiral similarity\n\n1. **Derivation of (9) and (10).**  \n   *Correct in principle*: If \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) maps \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) and fixes \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), then it sends chord \\(AE\\) to chord \\(AF\\) and chord \\(BE\\) to chord \\(BF\\), preserving the ratio of lengths equal to the similarity factor \\(k\\). However, this relies on the correctness of Lemma\u202f2 (the existence of the similarity with factor \\(k\\)) and on the fact that \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are indeed the images of each other, which was not established because the invariance of \\(AP\\) (Lemma\u202f3) is unproved. Hence the step rests on earlier gaps.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f2 \u2013 \\(AP\\) is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\n1. **From (9) and (10) to (11).**  \n   *Algebraically correct*: Both ratios equal \\(k\\), so they are equal to each other.\n\n2. **Use of law of sines in \\(\\triangle AEF\\) to obtain (12).**  \n   *Correct*: \\(\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AFE}{\\sin\\angle AEF}\\) follows from the law of sines.\n