 invariant line \\(MN\\); therefore \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\).  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\). The image of the chord \\(CD\\) under \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) is itself (because \\(C\\) goes to \\(D\\) and \\(D\\) goes to \\(C\\)). Hence the image of its perpendicular bisector is again the same line; consequently \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) fixes the whole line \\(AP\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Ratios supplied by the spiral similarity\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we have (8).  \nApplying \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) to the chord \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) we obtain the chord \\(AF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}=k . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nApplying \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) to the chord \\(BE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) we obtain the chord \\(BF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\); thus  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}=k . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. \\(AP\\) is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFrom (9) and (10) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{BE}{BF}=k . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(X=AP\\cap EF\\). In \\(\\triangle AEF\\) the law of sines gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AFE}{\\sin\\angle AEF}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nCombining (11) and (12) we get  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AFE}{\\sin\\angle AEF}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nEquality (13) is exactly the condition that the line \\(AX\\) (i.e. \\(AP\\)) is isogonal to the median from \\(A\\) in \\(\\triangle BEF\\); consequently \\(AP\\) is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Lemma\u202f1 (orthocenter\u202f+\u202fsymmedian \u21d2 tangent)\n\n*Statement.* Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocenter \\(H\\). Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the internal \\(X\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). Then \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nDenote by \\(M\\) the midpoint of \\(YZ\\). The internal \\(X\\)-symmedian is the isogonal conjugate of the median \\(XM\\).  \n\nReflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(YZ\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\). A well\u2011known property of the orthocenter states that \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle and is the antipode of \\(X\\); we write \\(H'=X^{*}\\).  \n\nBecause reflection preserves parallelism, the image of \\(\\ell\\) under the same reflection is a line \\(\\ell'\\) through \\(X^{*}\\) parallel to the \\(X\\)-symmedian.  \n\nConsider the intersection \\(T=\\ell'\\cap (XYZ)\\) distinct from \\(X^{*}\\). Since \\(\\ell'\\) is parallel to the symmedian, the chord \\(XT\\) is the isogonal of the median \\(XM\\); by the definition of a symmedian, the line through \\(T\\) parallel to the symmedian is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(T\\).  \n\nBut \\(\\ell\\) is the reflection of that tangent line in the chord \\(YZ\\); a reflection of a tangent line in a chord of a circle is again a tangent line (the reflected line touches the circle at the reflected point of tangency, which is precisely the point where \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle). Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Completion of the problem\n\n* The orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the point \\(H\\) given in the statement.  \n* By Section\u202f2, the line \\(AP\\) is the internal \\(P\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n* Applying Lemma\u202f1 to the triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (with \\(X=P\\)) we obtain: the line through its orthocenter \\(H\\) that is parallel to the internal \\(P\\)-symmedian (i.e. parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which is exactly the claim to be proved. \u220e"
