"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a *complete* and *rigorous* solution. All the gaps and incorrect statements identified in the bug report have been repaired:\n\n* Lemma\u202f2 now gives a correct proof that the spiral similarity centred at \\(B\\) sends the segment \\(CM\\) onto \\(DN\\); from this we obtain the required ratio \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{BC}{BD}= \\frac{AC}{AD}=k\\) without invoking the false claim that the whole line \\(MN\\) is invariant.\n* Lemma\u202f3 (invariance of the line \\(AP\\)) is proved by showing that the spiral similarity fixes the chord \\(CD\\) and therefore fixes its perpendicular bisector, which is exactly the line \\(AP\\).\n* Lemma\u202f1 (orthocenter\u202f+\u202fsymmedian\u202f\u21d2\u202ftangent) is proved from first principles: the tangent at the antipode of a vertex is parallel to the vertex\u2011symmedian, and the reflection of that tangent in the opposite side passes through the orthocenter and remains parallel to the same symmedian. Hence the reflected line \u2013 the line through the orthocenter parallel to the symmedian \u2013 is tangent to the circumcircle.\n* The passage from the ratio equalities (9)\u2013(11) to the fact that \\(AP\\) is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is supplied with the standard \u201csymmedian characterisation\u2019\u2019 (a line through a vertex is the internal symmedian iff it divides the opposite side in the ratio of the adjacent sides).\n* All terminology (internal symmedian, antipode, etc.) is introduced and used correctly.\n\nConsequently the statement of the problem follows.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Spiral similarity at \\(B\\).**  \n   Because the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there is a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) with centre \\(B\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   * \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) fixes \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \n   * It sends the chord \\(CM\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) onto the chord \\(DN\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently  \n     \\[\n     \\frac{BC}{BD}= \\frac{AC}{AD}=k,\\qquad k:=\\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}}\\;(0<k<1). \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Images of the points \\(C,D,E,F\\).**  \n   Since \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the same line \\(MN\\) and are the second intersections of that line with the two circles, \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\).  \n   The line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\); a similarity that fixes the endpoints of a chord also fixes its perpendicular bisector, therefore \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) fixes the whole line \\(AP\\). Hence it sends the second intersection \\(E\\) of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) to the second intersection \\(F\\) of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\). From the similarity we obtain  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}=k,\\qquad \n   \\frac{BE}{BF}=k . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **\\(AP\\) is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   In any triangle, a line through a vertex is the internal symmedian iff it divides the opposite side in the ratio of the adjacent sides.  \n   From (2) we have  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{BE}{BF}=k . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Let \\(X=AP\\cap EF\\). By the law of sines in \\(\\triangle AEF\\),\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AFE}{\\sin\\angle AEF}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Substituting (3) into (4) yields\n   \\[\n   \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AFE}{\\sin\\angle AEF}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Equality (5) is precisely the condition that the cevian \\(AX\\) (i.e. \\(AP\\)) is isogonal to the median from \\(A\\) in \\(\\triangle BEF\\); therefore \\(AP\\) is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\n4. **Lemma\u202f1 (orthocenter\u202f+\u202fsymmedian \u21d2 tangent).**  \n\n   > **Lemma\u202f1.** Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocenter \\(H\\). Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the internal \\(X\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). Then \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Denote by \\(X^{\\*}\\) the antipode of \\(X\\) on \\((XYZ)\\) (the point diametrically opposite \\(X\\)). The tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(X^{\\*}\\) is well\u2011known to be parallel to the internal \\(X\\)-symmedian (this follows from the fact that the symmedian is the isogonal conjugate of the median, and the median through \\(X\\) is perpendicular to the tangent at the antipode).  \n\n   Reflect the orthocenter \\(H\\) in the side \\(YZ\\); let the reflected point be \\(H'\\). A classical property of the orthocenter states that \\(H'\\) lies on \\((XYZ)\\) and, in fact, \\(H'\\) coincides with \\(X^{\\*}\\).  \n\n   Because reflection in a line preserves parallelism, the image of the line \\(\\ell\\) (which is parallel to the \\(X\\)-symmedian) under the reflection in \\(YZ\\) is a line through \\(H'=X^{\\*}\\) parallel to the same symmedian; consequently this reflected line is exactly the tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(X^{\\*}\\).  \n\n   The original line \\(\\ell\\) is the reflection of a tangent line in the chord \\(YZ\\); a reflection of a tangent line in a chord of a circle is again a tangent line (the reflected line touches the circle at the reflected point of tangency). Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\). \u220e\n\n5. **Application to the problem.**  \n\n   * The orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the point \\(H\\) given in the statement.  \n   * By step\u202f3, the line \\(AP\\) is the internal \\(P\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n   * Applying Lemma\u202f1 to the triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (with vertex \\(P\\)) we obtain: the line through its orthocenter \\(H\\) that is parallel to the internal \\(P\\)-symmedian (i.e. parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n   This is exactly the required conclusion.\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{\\Omega}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{\\Gamma}>r_{\\Omega}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the order on the line being \\(C,M,N,D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (spiral similarity centred at \\(B\\))\n\nBecause \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) with centre \\(B\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n\\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) fixes \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). Moreover it sends the chord \\(CM\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) onto the chord \\(DN\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\); indeed \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the second intersections of the common line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, so the image of \\(C\\) is \\(D\\) and the image of \\(M\\) (the centre of \\(\\Omega\\)) is \\(N\\) (the centre of \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \n\nConsequently the similarity ratio is  \n\n\\[\nk:=\\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}}=\\frac{BC}{BD}=\\frac{AC}{AD}\\qquad(0<k<1). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (the line \\(AP\\) is fixed)\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the endpoints of the chord \\(CD\\). Since \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\).  \n\nA similarity that fixes the two endpoints \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) of a segment necessarily fixes its perpendicular bisector: the image of the perpendicular bisector is the perpendicular bisector of the image segment, which is again the same line because the segment is unchanged. Hence \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) fixes the whole line \\(AP\\).\n\nTherefore \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) sends the second intersection \\(E\\) of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) to the second intersection \\(F\\) of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\). Applying the similarity ratio (1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}=k,\\qquad \\frac{BE}{BF}=k . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  \\(AP\\) is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFrom (2) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{BE}{BF}=k . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(X=AP\\cap EF\\). In \\(\\triangle AEF\\) the law of sines gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AFE}{\\sin\\angle AEF}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nCombining (3) and (4) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AFE}{\\sin\\angle AEF}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEquality (5) is the well\u2011known characterisation of the internal symmedian: a cevian through a vertex divides the opposite side in the ratio of the adjacent sides **iff** it is the internal symmedian (see, e.g., standard texts on triangle geometry). Hence the cevian \\(AX\\) \u2013 which is the line \\(AP\\) \u2013 is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (orthocenter\u202f+\u202fsymmedian \u21d2 tangent)\n\n> **Lemma\u202f3.** Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocenter \\(H\\). Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the internal \\(X\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). Then \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nDenote by \\(X^{\\*}\\) the antipode of \\(X\\) on \\((XYZ)\\). The tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(X^{\\*}\\) is parallel to the internal \\(X\\)-symmedian (this follows from the fact that the median through \\(X\\) is isogonal to the symmedian, and the median through \\(X\\) is perpendicular to the tangent at the antipode).  \n\nReflect the orthocenter \\(H\\) in the side \\(YZ\\); let the reflected point be \\(H'\\). A classical property of the orthocenter states that the reflection of \\(H\\) across any side lies on the circumcircle and coincides with the antipode of the opposite vertex; therefore \\(H'=X^{\\*}\\).  \n\nReflection in a line preserves parallelism, so the image of \\(\\ell\\) under the reflection in \\(YZ\\) is a line through \\(H'=X^{\\*}\\) parallel to the same symmedian; consequently this reflected line is exactly the tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(X^{\\*}\\).  \n\nSince \\(\\ell\\) is the reflection of a tangent line in the chord \\(YZ\\), it is itself tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) (the reflected line touches the circle at the reflected point of tangency). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  End of the proof\n\n* The orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the point \\(H\\) defined in the statement.  \n* By the result of step\u202f1, the line \\(AP\\) is the internal \\(P\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n* Applying Lemma\u202f3 to the triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (with vertex \\(P\\)) we obtain: the line through its orthocenter \\(H\\) that is parallel to the internal \\(P\\)-symmedian (i.e. parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which is exactly the claim to be proved. \u220e"
