            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{\\Omega}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{\\Gamma}>r_{\\Omega}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the order on the line being \\(C,M,N,D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (spiral similarity centred at \\(B\\))\n\nBecause \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) with centre \\(B\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n\\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) fixes \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). Moreover it sends the chord \\(CM\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) onto the chord \\(DN\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\); indeed \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the second intersections of the common line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, so the image of \\(C\\) is \\(D\\) and the image of \\(M\\) (the centre of \\(\\Omega\\)) is \\(N\\) (the centre of \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \n\nConsequently the similarity ratio is  \n\n\\[\nk:=\\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}}=\\frac{BC}{BD}=\\frac{AC}{AD}\\qquad(0<k<1). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (the line \\(AP\\) is fixed)\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the endpoints of the chord \\(CD\\). Since \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\).  \n\nA similarity that fixes the two endpoints \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) of a segment necessarily fixes its perpendicular bisector: the image of the perpendicular bisector is the perpendicular bisector of the image segment, which is again the same line because the segment is unchanged. Hence \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) fixes the whole line \\(AP\\).\n\nTherefore \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) sends the second intersection \\(E\\) of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) to the second intersection \\(F\\) of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\). Applying the similarity ratio (1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}=k,\\qquad \\frac{BE}{BF}=k . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  \\(AP\\) is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFrom (2) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{BE}{BF}=k . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(X=AP\\cap EF\\). In \\(\\triangle AEF\\) the law of sines gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AFE}{\\sin\\angle AEF}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nCombining (3) and (4) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AFE}{\\sin\\angle AEF}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEquality (5) is the well\u2011known characterisation of the internal symmedian: a cevian through a vertex divides the opposite side in the ratio of the adjacent sides **iff** it is the internal symmedian (see, e.g., standard texts on triangle geometry). Hence the cevian \\(AX\\) \u2013 which is the line \\(AP\\) \u2013 is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (orthocenter\u202f+\u202fsymmedian \u21d2 tangent)\n\n> **Lemma\u202f3.** Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocenter \\(H\\). Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the internal \\(X\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). Then \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nDenote by \\(X^{\\*}\\) the antipode of \\(X\\) on \\((XYZ)\\). The tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(X^{\\*}\\) is parallel to the internal \\(X\\)-symmedian (this follows from the fact that the median through \\(X\\) is isogonal to the symmedian, and the median through \\(X\\) is perpendicular to the tangent at the antipode).  \n\nReflect the orthocenter \\(H\\) in the side \\(YZ\\); let the reflected point be \\(H'\\). A classical property of the orthocenter states that the reflection of \\(H\\) across any side lies on the circumcircle and coincides with the antipode of the opposite vertex; therefore \\(H'=X^{\\*}\\).  \n\nReflection in a line preserves parallelism, so the image of \\(\\ell\\) under the reflection in \\(YZ\\) is a line through \\(H'=X^{\\*}\\) parallel to the same symmedian; consequently this reflected line is exactly the tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(X^{\\*}\\).  \n\nSince \\(\\ell\\) is the reflection of a tangent line in the chord \\(YZ\\), it is itself tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) (the reflected line touches the circle at the reflected point of tangency). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  End of the proof\n\n* The orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the point \\(H\\) defined in the statement.  \n* By the result of step\u202f1, the line \\(AP\\) is the internal \\(P\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n* Applying Lemma\u202f3 to the triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (with vertex \\(P\\)) we obtain: the line through its orthocenter \\(H\\) that is parallel to the internal \\(P\\)-symmedian (i.e. parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which is exactly the claim to be proved. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
