s the ratio of the radii **only** when measured from the centre of the similarity to the centres of the circles, i.e. \\(\\frac{BM}{BN} = \\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}}\\). The distances \\(BC\\) and \\(BD\\) are not radii; \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), not on the line \\(BM\\). In general \\(\\frac{BC}{BD}\\neq\\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}}\\). Hence the equality claimed in (1) is false. This is a **Critical Error** because the subsequent steps rely on the incorrect ratio.\n\n### Lemma\u202f2 (the line \\(AP\\) is fixed)\n\n> \u201cSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nIn a triangle, the circumcenter is the intersection of the three perpendicular bisectors of the sides. The perpendicular bisector of side \\(CD\\) indeed passes through the circumcenter \\(P\\), but there is no reason for it to pass through vertex \\(A\\). Therefore \\(AP\\) is **not** generally the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\). This statement is false, constituting a **Critical Error**.\n\n> \u201cA similarity that fixes the two endpoints \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) of a segment necessarily fixes its perpendicular bisector\u2026 Hence \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) fixes the whole line \\(AP\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nEven if a similarity fixed \\(C\\) and \\(D\\), it would fix the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), not necessarily the line \\(AP\\). Since the previous claim that \\(AP\\) is that perpendicular bisector is false, the conclusion that \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) fixes line \\(AP\\) is unsupported. This is another **Critical Error**.\n\n### Consequence (Equation (2))\n\n> \u201cApplying the similarity ratio (1) we obtain  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{AE}{AF}=k,\\qquad \\frac{BE}{BF}=k . \\tag{2}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nEquation (2) depends on the (incorrect) claim that \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) maps the whole line \\(AP\\) onto itself, sending \\(E\\) to \\(F\\). Since Lemma\u202f2 is false, the derivation of (2) is invalid. Hence (2) is unjustified \u2013 a **Critical Error**.\n\n### Step\u202f1 \u2013 Claim that \\(AP\\) is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nThe argument proceeds:\n\n1. From (2) we have \\(\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{BE}{BF}=k\\).  \n2. Using the law of sines in \\(\\triangle AEF\\) to write \\(\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AFE}{\\sin\\angle AEF}\\).  \n3. Concluding \\(\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AFE}{\\sin\\angle AEF}\\) and invoking the characterisation of the internal symmedian.\n\n**Analysis:**  \nBecause (2) is false, step\u202f1\u2019s premise is invalid, making the whole deduction unsound \u2013 a **Critical Error**.  \nEven assuming the ratios were correct, the solution does not verify that the point \\(X = AP\\cap EF\\) indeed lies on side \\(EF\\) (it could be outside the segment), nor does it justify that the ratio of sines condition is sufficient for the symmedian in the specific configuration of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). This omission is a **Justification Gap**, but it is moot given the earlier critical failure.\n\n### Lemma\u202f3 (orthocenter\u202f+\u202fsymmedian \u21d2 tangent)\n\n> \u201cThe tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(X^{\\*}\\) is parallel to the internal \\(X\\)-symmedian\u2026 Reflect the orthocenter \\(H\\) in the side \\(YZ\\); \u2026 the reflected point is \\(X^{\\*}\\). \u2026 Hence the reflected line is exactly the tangent \u2026 Since \\(\\ell\\) is the reflection of a tangent line \u2026 it is itself tangent.\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nSeveral statements are incorrect:\n\n* The tangent at the antipode \\(X^{\\*}\\) is **not** generally parallel to the internal symmedian through \\(X\\). The symmedian is isogonal to the median, while the tangent at the antipode is perpendicular to the diameter through \\(X\\); there is no such parallelism in general.  \n* The reflection of the orthocenter across side \\(YZ\\) lies on the circumcircle, but it coincides with the point where the altitude from \\(X\\) meets the circumcircle, **not** with the antipode of \\(X\\). Thus the claim \u201cthe reflected point is \\(X^{\\*}\\)\u201d is false.  \n\nThese false statements render Lemma\u202f3 invalid \u2013 a **Critical Error**. Consequently any argument that invokes Lemma\u202f3 (the final step) is unsound.\n\n### Final step \u2013 Application to triangle \\(PMN\\)\n\nThe solution states:\n\n* \u201cBy step\u202f1, \\(AP\\) is the internal \\(P\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n* Applying Lemma\u202f3 to triangle \\(PMN\\) \u2026 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nBoth premises are false: step\u202f1\u2019s conclusion is unsupported, and Lemma\u202f3 is false. Therefore the final conclusion does not follow. This is a **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n**Overall Assessment:** The proof contains multiple foundational mistakes: an incorrect description of the spiral similarity ratio, a false claim about the relationship between the circumcenter and side \\(CD\\), misuse of similarity to assert that line \\(AP\\) is invariant, and an invalid Lemma\u202f3 about orthocenter reflections and symmedians. Because these errors occur early and propagate throughout, the solution fails to establish the required result."
