n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{\\Omega}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{\\Gamma}>r_{\\Omega}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the order on the line being \\(C,M,N,D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (spiral similarity centred at \\(B\\))\n\nBecause \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) with centre \\(B\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n\\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) fixes \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). Moreover it sends the chord \\(CM\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) onto the chord \\(DN\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\); indeed \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the second intersections of the common line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, so the image of \\(C\\) is \\(D\\) and the image of \\(M\\) (the centre of \\(\\Omega\\)) is \\(N\\) (the centre of \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \n\nConsequently the similarity ratio is  \n\n\\[\nk:=\\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}}=\\frac{BC}{BD}=\\frac{AC}{AD}\\qquad(0<k<1). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (the line \\(AP\\) is fixed)\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the endpoints of the chord \\(CD\\). Since \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\).  \n\nA similarity that fixes the two endpoints \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) of a segment necessarily fixes its 