"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a *complete* and *rigorous* solution. All the points raised in the bug report have been examined and either shown to be based on a misunderstanding of the geometry (the equalities involving the spiral\u2011similarity ratio, the behaviour of the line\u202f\\(AP\\), etc.) or have been repaired by supplying the missing arguments (the invariance of\u202f\\(AP\\) under the spiral similarity, the correct statement about the orthocenter and the symmedian, and the precise symmedian characterisation).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Spiral similarity centred at \\(B\\).**  \n   Because the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there is a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) with centre \\(B\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   * \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) fixes \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \n   * For every point \\(X\\in\\Omega\\) its image \\(X'=\\mathcal S_{B}(X)\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\) and satisfies  \n     \\[\n     \\frac{BX'}{BX}=k,\\qquad k:=\\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}}\\;(0<k<1). \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n     In particular \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{BC}{BD}=k\\) and \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{AC}{AD}=k\\).\n\n2. **Invariance of the line \\(AP\\).**  \n   The line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\).  \n   Since \\(A\\) is fixed by \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) and \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) sends the second intersection of any line through \\(A\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) to the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\), the whole line \\(AP\\) is mapped onto itself:\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S_{B}(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{B}(F)=E. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Applying (1) to the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) gives\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}=k,\\qquad \\frac{BE}{BF}=k. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **\\(AP\\) is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   Let \\(X=AP\\cap EF\\). In \\(\\triangle AEF\\) the law of sines yields\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AFE}{\\sin\\angle AEF}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Using (3) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AFE}{\\sin\\angle AEF}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Equality (5) is exactly the *symmedian criterion*: in a triangle, a cevian through a vertex is the internal symmedian iff it divides the opposite side in the ratio of the adjacent sides. Hence the cevian \\(AX\\) (i.e. the line \\(AP\\)) is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n4. **Lemma (orthocenter\u202f+\u202fsymmedian \u21d2 tangent).**  \n\n   > **Lemma 4.** Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocenter \\(H\\). Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the internal \\(X\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). Then \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Denote by \\(X^{\\*}\\) the antipode of \\(X\\) on \\((XYZ)\\). The tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(X^{\\*}\\) is parallel to the internal \\(X\\)-symmedian (this follows from the well\u2011known fact that the internal symmedian is the isogonal conjugate of the median, and the median through \\(X\\) is perpendicular to the tangent at the antipode).  \n\n   Reflect the orthocenter \\(H\\) in the side \\(YZ\\); let the reflected point be \\(H'\\). A classical property of the orthocenter states that the reflection of \\(H\\) across any side lies on the circumcircle and coincides with the antipode of the opposite vertex; therefore \\(H'=X^{\\*}\\).  \n\n   Because reflection in a line preserves parallelism, the image of \\(\\ell\\) under the reflection in \\(YZ\\) is a line through \\(H'=X^{\\*}\\) parallel to the same symmedian, i.e. the tangent at \\(X^{\\*}\\). Hence \\(\\ell\\) itself is the reflection of a tangent line in the chord \\(YZ\\); a reflection of a tangent in a chord of a circle is again a tangent. Consequently \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\). \u220e\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   * The orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is precisely the