 to the particular points \\(C,D\\) and \\(A\\) we obtain\n\\[\n\\frac{BC}{BD}=k,\\qquad \\frac{AC}{AD}=k. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (the line \\(AP\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\))\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\).  \nSince \\(A\\) is fixed by \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) and \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) maps the second intersection of any line through \\(A\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) to the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\), the whole line \\(AP\\) is mapped onto itself:\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{B}(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{B}(F)=E. \\tag{3}\n\\]\nConsequently, applying (1) to the pair \\((E,F)\\) yields\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}=k,\\qquad \\frac{BE}{BF}=k. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (\\(AP\\) is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\))\n\nLet \\(X=AP\\cap EF\\). In \\(\\triangle AEF\\) the law of sines gives\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AFE}{\\sin\\angle AEF}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\nUsing (4) we replace the left\u2011hand side of (5) by \\(\\dfrac{BE}{BF}\\) and obtain\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AFE}{\\sin\\angle AEF}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIn any triangle, a cevian through a vertex is the internal symmedian iff it divides the opposite side in the ratio of the adjacent sides (the *symmedian criterion*). Equality (6) is precisely that criterion for the cevian \\(AX\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Hence the line \\(AX\\), i.e. the line \\(AP\\), is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4 (orthocenter\u202f+\u202fsymmedian \u21d2 tangent)\n\n> **Lemma\u202f4.** Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocenter \\(H\\). Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the internal \\(X\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle XY