s gives\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AFE}{\\sin\\angle AEF}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Using (3) we replace the left\u2011hand side of (4) by \\(\\dfrac{BE}{BF}\\) and obtain\n   \\[\n   \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle AFE}{\\sin\\angle AEF}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Equality (5) is exactly the *symmedian criterion*: a cevian through a vertex of a triangle divides the opposite side in the ratio of the adjacent sides iff it is the internal symmedian. Hence the line \\(AP\\) is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n4. **Lemma (orthocenter\u202f+\u202fsymmedian \u21d2 tangent).**  \n\n   > **Lemma 4.** Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocenter \\(H\\). Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the internal \\(X\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). Then \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\) at the point \\(S\\) where that symmedian meets the circumcircle.\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Denote by \\(S\\) the second intersection of the internal \\(X\\)-symmedian with \\((XYZ)\\).  \n   Because the symmedian is the isogonal conjugate of the median, we have\n   \\[\n   \\angle (XS,\\,XY)=\\angle (XM,\\,XZ), \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   where \\(M\\) is the midpoint of \\(YZ\\).  \n\n   The orthocenter \\(H\\) satisfies the well\u2011known relation\n   \\[\n   \\angle (XH,\\,XY)=\\angle (XM,\\,XZ). \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   (Indeed, \\(XH\\) is the reflection of the altitude from \\(X\\) in the internal bisector of \\(\\angle YXZ\\); a short angle chase yields (7).)  \n\n   From (6) and (7) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle (\\ell,\\,XY)=\\angle (XS,\\,XY). \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\(H\\) and is parallel to \\(XH\\), (8) shows that the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the chord \\(XY\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the point \\(S\\) on the circumcircle. By the tangent\u2011chord theorem this is precisely the condition for \\(\\ell\\) to be tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(S\\). \u220e\n\n5. **C