\).**  \n   Let \\(X=AP\\cap EF\\). In the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) the chord \\(EF\\) subtends the same angle at \\(B\\) and at the point \\(S\\) where the line \\(BX\\) meets the circle again. By the intersecting\u2011chords theorem,\n   \\[\n   \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle BXS}{\\sin\\angle BXS}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle BFX}{\\sin\\angle BEX}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Equality (4) is precisely the *symmedian criterion*: a cevian through a vertex divides the opposite side in the ratio of the adjacent sides iff it is the internal symmedian. Because (3) gives the same ratio \\(\\dfrac{BE}{BF}\\), we conclude that the cevian \\(BX\\) \u2013 which coincides with the line \\(AP\\) \u2013 is the internal \\(B\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f4 (orthocenter\u202f+\u202fsymmedian \u21d2 tangent).**  \n\n   > **Lemma\u202f4.** Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocenter \\(H\\). Let \\(S\\neq X\\) be the second intersection of the internal \\(X\\)-symmedian with the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\). Then the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the internal \\(X\\)-symmedian is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(S\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Denote by \\(M\\) the midpoint of \\(YZ\\); the line \\(XM\\) is the internal median.  \n\n   1. The internal \\(X\\)-symmedian is the isogonal conjugate of the internal median \\(XM\\); consequently\n      \\[\n      \\angle (XS,\\,XY)=\\angle (XM,\\,XZ). \\tag{5}\n      \\]\n\n   2. In any triangle the altitude from \\(X\\) is the isogonal conjugate of the median \\(XM\\). Since the orthocenter \\(H\\) lies on that altitude, the line \\(XH\\) is isogonal to \\(XM\\); therefore\n      \\[\n      \\angle (XH,\\,XY)=\\angle (XM,\\,XZ). \\tag{6}\n      \\]\n\n   3. Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the symmedian \\(XS\\). Because \\(\\ell\\parallel XS\\), from (5) we have\n      \\[\n      \\angle (\\ell,\\,XY)=\\angle (XS,\\,XY). \\tag{7}\n      \\]\n      From (6) and the parallelism \\(\\ell\\parallel XS\\) we also obtain\n      \\[\n      \\angle (\\ell,\\,XY)=\\angle (XH,\\,XY). \\tag{8}\n      \\]\n\n   4. Equality (7) states that the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the chord \\(XY\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the point \\(S\\) on the circumcircle. By the tangent\u2011chord theorem this is exactly the condition for \\(\\ell\\) to be tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(S\\). \u220e\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n\n   * The orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the point \\(H\\) defined in the problem.  \n   * By step\u202f3, the line \\(AP\\) is the internal \\(P\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n   * Applying Lemma\u202f4 to the triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (with vertex \\(P\\)) we deduce that the line through its orthocenter \\(H\\) which is parallel to the internal \\(P\\)-symmedian (i.e. parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the required statement is proved. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{\\Omega}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{\\Gamma}>r_{\\Omega}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the order on the line being \\(C,M,N,D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (spiral similarity centred at \\(B\\))\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S