ine through \\(A\\) is mapped onto itself:\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S_{B}(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{B}(F)=E. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Consequently\n   \\[\n   \\frac{BE}{BF}=k. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **\\(AP\\) is the internal \\(B\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   Let \\(X=AP\\cap EF\\) and let \\(S\\neq B\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(BX\\) with the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\). By the intersecting\u2011chords theorem applied to chords \\(BX\\) and \\(EF\\) we have\n   \\[\n   \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle BFX}{\\sin\\angle BEX}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Equality (3) together with (4) yields\n   \\[\n   \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle BFX}{\\sin\\angle BEX}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Condition (5) is exactly the *symmedian criterion*: a cevian through a vertex divides the opposite side in the ratio of the adjacent sides iff it is the internal symmedian. Hence the cevian \\(BX\\) \u2013 which coincides with the line \\(AP\\) \u2013 is the internal \\(B\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f4 (orthocenter\u202f+\u202fsymmedian \u21d2 tangent).**  \n\n   > **Lemma\u202f4.** Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocenter \\(H\\). Let \\(S\\neq X\\) be the second intersection of the internal \\(X\\)-symmedian with the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\). Then the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the internal \\(X\\)-symmedian is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(S\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Denote by \\(M\\) the midpoint of \\(YZ\\); the line \\(XM\\) is the internal median.  \n\n   1. The internal \\(X\\)-symmedian is the isogonal conjugate of the median \\(XM\\); therefore\n      \\[\n      \\angle (XS,\\,XY)=\\angle (XM,\\,XZ). \\tag{6}\n      \\]\n\n   2. In any triangle the altitude from \\(X\\) is the isogonal conjugate of the median \\(XM\\). Since the orthocenter \\(H\\) lies on that altitude, the line \\(XH\\) is isogonal to \\(XM\\); consequently\n      \\[\n      \\angle (XH,\\,XY)=\\angle (XM,\\,XZ). \\tag{7}\n      \\]\n\n   3. Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the symmedian \\(XS\\). From (6) we obtain\n      \\[\n      \\angle (\\ell,\\,XY)=\\angle (XS,\\,XY). \\tag{8}\n      \\]\n      From (7) and the parallelism \\(\\ell\\parallel XS\\) we also have\n      \\[\n      \\angle (\\ell,\\,XY)=\\angle (XH,\\,XY). \\tag{9}\n      \\]\n\n   4. Equality (8) states that the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the chord \\(XY\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the point \\(S\\) on the circumcircle. By the tangent\u2011chord theorem this is precisely the condition for \\(\\ell\\) to be tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(S\\). \u220e\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n\n   * The orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the point \\(H\\) defined in the problem.  \n   * By step\u202f3, the line \\(AP\\) is the internal \\(P\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n   * Applying Lemma\u202f4 to the triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (with vertex \\(P\\)) we deduce that the line through its orthocenter \\(H\\) which is parallel to the internal \\(P\\)-symmedian (i.e. parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the required statement is proved. \u220e"
