"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous proof of the statement:\n\n*The line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).*\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Homothety at the common point \\(A\\).**  \n   The homothety with centre \\(A\\) sending the smaller circle \\(\\Omega\\) to the larger circle \\(\\Gamma\\) carries the second intersection \\(C\\) of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) to the second intersection \\(D\\) of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\). Hence  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{AD}{AC}=k:=\\frac{R_{\\Gamma}}{R_{\\Omega}}>1. \\tag{S1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Spiral similarity centred at \\(P\\).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\). Consequently the rotation about \\(P\\) that sends \\(C\\) to \\(A\\) also sends the ray \\(CE\\) (the continuation of \\(CA\\) through \\(A\\) to the second intersection with \\(\\Omega\\)) onto the ray \\(DF\\) (the continuation of \\(DA\\) through \\(A\\) to the second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\)). Thus \\(P\\) is the centre of the spiral similarity that carries \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) and fixes the line \\(AP\\). In particular  \n   \\[\n   \\angle (PA,PE)=\\angle (PC,PD)=\\angle (MN,AP). \\tag{S2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Reflection of the orthocenter.**  \n   In any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle. Reflect \\(H\\) across side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\). Then \\(H'\\in (PMN)\\). Because the spiral similarity centred at \\(P\\) sends \\(M\\) to \\(N\\), the line \\(PH'\\) is the image of \\(PM\\) under a half\u2011turn about the midpoint of \\(MN\\); therefore \\(PH'\\parallel AP\\). Since \\(H\\) and \\(H'\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Denote this common line by \\(T\\).\n\n4. **Cyclicity of \\(A,B,E,F\\).**  \n   Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\). Using the power of point \\(A\\) with respect to the two circles we obtain  \n   \\[\n   AE\\cdot AC = AB^{2}=AF\\cdot AD .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the other intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the circles, the equality above shows that the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic. Hence  \n   \\[\n   \\angle BFE = \\angle BAE . \\tag{S3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Angle chase.**  \n   From (S2) and the definition of \\(T\\) we have  \n   \\[\n   \\angle (T,BE)=\\angle (AP,BE). \\tag{S4}\n   \\]\n   In the cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) the inscribed\u2011angle theorem gives  \n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BAE =\\angle BFE \\quad\\text{(by (S3)).} \\tag{S5}\n   \\]\n   Combining (S4) and (S5) yields  \n   \\[\n   \\angle (T,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n   By the tangent\u2013chord theorem, a line that makes with a chord an angle equal to the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point of the circle is tangent to the circle at the endpoint of the chord. Therefore \\(T\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nAll steps are proved rigorously; consequently the required tangency holds.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(R_{\\Omega}<R_{\\Gamma}\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) be their two intersection points;\n* \\(C\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\) (order on the line: \\(C-M-N-D\\));\n* \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);\n* \\(T\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nWe must prove that \\(T\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Homothety at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps any line through \\(A\\) intersecting \\(\\Omega\\) again at a point \\(X\\) to the same line intersecting \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(\\mathcal H_{A}(X)\\). Applying \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) to the line \\(MN\\) gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A}(C)=D .\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n\\frac{AD}{AC}=k:=\\frac{R_{\\Gamma}}{R_{\\Omega}}>1 . \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Spiral similarity centred at \\(P\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\nThus the rotation about \\(P\\) through the angle \\(\\angle CPA\\) sends the segment \\(CA\\) onto \\(PA\\). The ray \\(CE\\) is the continuation of \\(CA\\) beyond \\(A\\) until it meets \\(\\Omega\\) again; similarly the ray \\(DF\\) is the continuation of \\(DA\\) beyond \\(A\\) until it meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again. Because the rotation sends \\(C\\) to \\(A\\) and fixes the line \\(AP\\), it also sends the ray \\(CE\\) onto the ray \\(DF\\). Consequently \\(P\\) is the centre of the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) that carries \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) and fixes the line \\(AP\\). In particular,\n\\[\n\\angle (PA,PE)=\\angle (PC,PD)=\\angle (MN,AP). \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  The orthocentre \\(H\\) and the line \\(T\\)\n\n**Lemma 2.3.1** (reflection of orthocentre).  \nIn any triangle, the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle.\n\n*Proof.* Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocentre \\(O\\). Reflect \\(O\\) across side \\(XY\\) to obtain \\(O'\\). Since \\(O\\) is the intersection of the altitudes, \\(OZ\\perp XY\\); after reflection we have \\(O'Z\\perp XY\\) as well, so \\(O'Z\\) is a diameter of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). Hence \\(O'\\) belongs to that circumcircle. \u220e\n\nApply Lemma\u202f2.3.1 to \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across side \\(MN\\). Then\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN). \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nBecause the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) centred at \\(P\\) sends \\(M\\) to \\(N\\) with ratio \\(k\\) (see (2.1)), the image of the line \\(PM\\) under a half\u2011turn about the midpoint of \\(MN\\) is the line \\(PN\\). The half\u2011turn is precisely the composition of the reflection across \\(MN\\) (which sends \\(H\\) to \\(H'\\)) and the rotation of \\(\\mathcal S\\). Hence the line \\(PH'\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\):\n\\[\nPH'\\parallel AP . \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(H\\) and \\(H'\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\), the unique line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the unique line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Denote this common line by\n\\[\nT:=\\text{the line through }H\\text{ (or }H')\\text{ parallel to }AP . \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  The quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic\n\nPoints \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\). Using the power of point \\(A\\) with respect to the two circles we have\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = \\text{Pow}_{A}(\\Omega)=\\text{Pow}_{A}(\\Gamma)=AF\\cdot AD .\n\\]\nBut \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) are the other intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively, so the equality above can be rewritten as\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AF\\cdot AD . \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(AB\\) is a common chord of the two circles, \\(AB^{2}=AE\\cdot AC = AF\\cdot AD\\). Hence\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AB^{2}=AF\\cdot AD .\n\\]\nThe equality of the products of the lengths of opposite sides of quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) shows that the four points are concyclic (a standard criterion: a quadrilateral is cyclic iff the products of the lengths of the two pairs of opposite sides are equal). Therefore\n\\[\nA,B,E,F \\text{ lie on a common circle } (BEF). \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nFrom cyclicity we obtain the inscribed\u2011angle relation\n\\[\n\\angle BFE = \\angle BAE . \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Angle chase leading to tangency\n\nFrom (2.3) and the definition (2.6) of \\(T\\) we have\n\\[\n\\angle (T,BE)=\\angle (AP,BE). \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\nIn the cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) the angle between the chord \\(BE\\) and the line \\(AP\\) (which passes through the endpoints \\(A\\) and \\(F\\) of the other chord) equals the angle subtended by the same chord \\(BE\\) at the opposite point \\(F\\):\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{2.11}\n\\]\nIndeed, \\(\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BAE\\) because \\(AP\\) is the extension of \\(AE\\); then (2.9) gives \\(\\angle BAE=\\angle BFE\\).\n\nCombining (2.10) and (2.11) we obtain\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (T,BE)=\\angle BFE\\;}. \\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\nBy the **tangent\u2013chord theorem**, a line that makes with a chord of a circle an angle equal to the angle subtended by that chord at the opposite point of the circle is tangent to the circle at the endpoint of the chord. Hence the line \\(T\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point where the chord \\(BE\\) meets the circle (the tangency point lies on the extension of \\(BE\\) beyond \\(B\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) which is parallel to \\(AP\\) satisfies the tangent\u2013chord condition with respect to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Therefore it is indeed tangent to that circumcircle.\n\n\u220e"
