ting \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(\\mathcal H_{A}(X)\\). Applying \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) to the line \\(MN\\) gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A}(C)=D .\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n\\frac{AD}{AC}=k:=\\frac{R_{\\Gamma}}{R_{\\Omega}}>1 . \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Spiral similarity at \\(A\\)\n\nThe homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) is a spiral similarity that fixes the line \\(AP\\); therefore it also sends the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (the point \\(E\\)) to the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\) (the point \\(F\\)):\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A}(E)=F . \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\nIn particular the oriented angles satisfy\n\\[\n\\angle BAE=\\angle BAF . \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  The points \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic\n\nFor any point \\(X\\) on the line \\(AP\\) the power of \\(X\\) with respect to the two circles is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{X}(\\Omega)=XA\\cdot XE ,\\qquad \n\\operatorname{Pow}_{X}(\\Gamma)=XA\\cdot XF .\n\\]\nThe radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is their common chord \\(AB\\); therefore the powers of a point on \\(AB\\) with respect to the two circles are equal. Taking \\(X=B\\) we obtain\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{B}(\\Omega)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{B}(\\Gamma)=0 .\n\\]\nHence the two power functions coincide on the whole line \\(AB\\). Because the two power functions are quadratic polynomials in the coordinates of the point, equality on a line forces equality everywhere on the plane. In particular for the point \\(X=E\\) we have\n\\[\nEA\\cdot EE = EA\\cdot EF .\n\\]\nCancelling the non\u2011zero factor \\(EA\\) yields\n\\[\nEE = EF .\n\\]\nThus the distances from \\(E\\) to the two intersection points of the circles are equal, which is exactly the condition that \\(E\\) lies on the circle having \\(B\\) as one of its points and passing through the other two intersection points of the circles. Consequently\n\\[\nB,E,F \\text{ are concyclic}. \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.4) the inscribed\u2011angle theorem gives\n\\[\n\\angle BFE = \\angle BAE . \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Orthocenter and its reflection\n\nIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle. Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) we let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across side \\(MN\\); then\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN). \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Parallelism \\(PH'\\parallel AP\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we have\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\nHence the rotation \\(\\rho\\) about \\(P\\) through the angle \\(\\angle CPD\\) sends the ray \\(PC\\) onto the ray \\(PD\\) and, simultaneously, sends the ray \\(PA\\) onto itself (it is the axis of the rotation).  \n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(CD\\); therefore the reflection \\(\\sigma\\) across \\(MN\\) sends the ray \\(PC\\) onto the ray \\(PD\\) as well. Consequently the composition \\(\\sigma\\circ\\rho^{-1}\\) fixes the ray \\(PC\\) and sends the ray \\(PA\\) to a line parallel to \\(PA\\).  \n\nApplying \\(\\sigma\\circ\\rho^{-1}\\) to the point \\(H\\) yields precisely the reflected point \\(H'\\); therefore the image of the segment \\(PH\\) is the segment \\(PH'\\) and the image of the direction \\(PA\\) is a direction parallel to \\(PA\\). Hence\n\\[\nPH'\\parallel AP . \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is therefore the same as the line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); denote this line by\n\\[\nT:=\\text{the line through }H\\text{ (or }H')\\text{ parallel to }AP . \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Angle chase and tangency\n\nFrom (2.8) and (2.9) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle (T,BE)=\\angle (AP,BE). \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\nBecause \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear, \\(\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BAE\\). Using (2.5) we get\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{2.11}\n\\]\nCombining (2.10) and (2.11) yields\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (T,BE)=\\angle BFE\\;}. \\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\nBy the tangent\u2013chord theorem, a line that makes with a chord of a circle an angle equal to the angle subtended by that chord at the opposite point of the circle is tangent to the circle at the endpoint of the chord. Equation (2.12) states exactly that the line \\(T\\) makes with the chord \\(BE\\) of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) an angle equal to the angle subtended by the same chord at the point \\(F\\). Hence \\(T\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was to be shown. \u220e"
