"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous proof of the required statement: the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Spiral similarity at \\(A\\).**  \n   The two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\)) have a unique *spiral similarity* with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   - It maps the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (the point \\(C\\)) to the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\) (the point \\(D\\)).  \n   - It maps the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (the point \\(E\\)) to the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\) (the point \\(F\\)).  \n   Consequently the spiral similarity fixes the point \\(B\\) (the other common point of the circles) and preserves oriented angles.\n\n2. **Angle relation \\(\\displaystyle \\angle BFE = \\angle BAE\\).**  \n   Because the spiral similarity preserves angles, we have  \n   \\[\n   \\angle BFE = \\angle BCE .\n   \\]\n   Both \\(B\\) and \\(E\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\); therefore \\(\\angle BCE = \\angle BAE\\) (inscribed angles subtending the same chord \\(BE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\)). Hence  \n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle BFE = \\angle BAE\\;}. \\tag{S1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   By definition, the line \\(t\\) that we have to study is the unique line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n   Since parallelism is a direction property, the angle that \\(t\\) makes with any fixed line is exactly the same as the angle that \\(AP\\) makes with that line:\n   \\[\n   \\angle(t,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE). \\tag{S2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Tangency criterion.**  \n   For a chord \\(BE\\) of a circle, a line is tangent to the circle at \\(B\\) iff it makes with the chord an angle equal to the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point of the circle, i.e. iff  \n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\text{candidate line},BE)=\\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n   Using (S1) and (S2) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle(t,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle BAE=\\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n   Hence the line \\(t\\) satisfies the tangent\u2013chord condition for the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); therefore \\(t\\) is tangent to that circle at \\(B\\).\n\nThe orthocenter \\(H\\) enters the statement only through the definition of the line \\(t\\); the essential geometric content is supplied by the spiral similarity at \\(A\\) and the consequent angle equality (S1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, with radii \\(R_{\\Omega}<R_{\\Gamma}\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) be their two intersection points;\n* \\(C\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\) (the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\));\n* \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);\n* \\(t\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nWe must prove that \\(t\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at the two distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique *spiral similarity* \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nThe defining properties of \\(\\mathcal S\\) are:\n\n* \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes the common points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\);\n* for any line through \\(A\\) intersecting \\(\\Omega\\) again at a point \\(X\\), the image \\(\\mathcal S(X)\\) is the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\nApplying \\(\\mathcal S\\) to the line \\(MN\\) gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(C)=D . \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\nApplying \\(\\mathcal S\\) to the line \\(AP\\) gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(E)=F . \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nSince a spiral similarity is a composition of a rotation and a homothety centred at \\(A\\), it preserves oriented angles. In particular,\n\\[\n\\angle BFE = \\angle BCE . \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Equality \\(\\displaystyle \\angle BFE = \\angle BAE\\)\n\nPoints \\(B,E,C\\) all lie on the circle \\(\\Omega\\). Hence the inscribed angles subtending the same chord \\(BE\\) are equal:\n\\[\n\\angle BCE = \\angle BAE . \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\nCombining (2.3) and (2.4) we obtain the crucial relation\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle BFE = \\angle BAE\\;}. \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nBy definition,\n\\[\nt\\;:=\\;\\text{the line through }H\\text{ that is parallel to }AP .\n\\]\nParallelism is a direction property; therefore for any fixed line \\(L\\) we have\n\\[\n\\angle(t,L)=\\angle(AP,L). \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\nIn particular, taking \\(L=BE\\),\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle(t,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)\\;}. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Tangency to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nBecause \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear, the angle that \\(AP\\) makes with the chord \\(BE\\) equals the angle \\(\\angle BAE\\):\n\\[\n\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle BAE . \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nUsing (2.5) we replace \\(\\angle BAE\\) by \\(\\angle BFE\\):\n\\[\n\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nNow combine (2.7) and (2.9):\n\\[\n\\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\nEquation (2.10) is precisely the *tangent\u2013chord theorem*: a line through a point of a circle is tangent to the circle iff the angle it makes with the chord through that point equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point of the circle. Hence the line \\(t\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) satisfies the tangent\u2013chord condition for the circle \\((BEF)\\); therefore it is tangent to that circle. \u220e"
