gle BFE = \\angle BCE . \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\u201d\n\n   *Analysis:* A spiral similarity centered at \\(A\\) indeed preserves oriented angles **with vertex at \\(A\\)**. It does **not** in general preserve angles whose vertex is elsewhere. The angles \\(\\angle BFE\\) and \\(\\angle BCE\\) have vertices at \\(F\\) and \\(C\\), respectively, not at \\(A\\). The only guaranteed relation is that the directed angle \\(\\angle BFA\\) equals \\(\\angle BCA\\) (or similar). Therefore the equality (2.3) is **false** and constitutes a **Critical Error**. Because the subsequent argument relies on (2.3), the logical chain is broken at this point.\n\n   *Result:* Critical Error \u2013 the claimed angle equality is unjustified and incorrect.\n\n---\n\n### 2.2 Equality \\(\\displaystyle \\angle BFE = \\angle BAE\\)\n\n1. **Claim (2.4):** \u201cPoints \\(B,E,C\\) all lie on the circle \\(\\Omega\\). Hence the inscribed angles subtending the same chord \\(BE\\) are equal:\n\\[\n\\angle BCE = \\angle BAE . \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\u201d\n\n   *Analysis:* The chord \\(BE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) indeed subtends \\(\\angle BCE\\) (vertex at \\(C\\)) and \\(\\angle BAE\\) (vertex at \\(A\\)). Since both vertices lie on the same circle, the inscribed\u2011angle theorem gives \\(\\angle BCE = \\angle BAE\\). This step is correct **provided** that \\(A\\) is on \\(\\Omega\\), which it is (the circles intersect at \\(A\\)). So (2.4) is valid.\n\n2. **Combination (2.5):** Using (2.3) and (2.4) to obtain \\(\\angle BFE = \\angle BAE\\).\n\n   *Analysis:* Because (2.3) is false, the derived equality (2.5) is unsupported. Hence (2.5) rests on a Critical Error.\n\n---\n\n### 2.3 The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\n1. **Definition (2.6):** \u201cParallelism is a direction property; therefore for any fixed line \\(L\\) we have\n\\[\n\\angle(t,L)=\\angle(AP,L). \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\u201d\n\n   *Justification:* If two lines are parallel, they make equal oriented angles with any third line. This is correct.\n\