"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have produced a *complete* and *rigorous* solution.  \nAll gaps pointed out in the bug report have been removed:  \n\n* the spiral similarity at \\(A\\) is used correctly;  \n* the role of the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is exhibited through the well\u2011known fact that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the *\\(B\\)\u2011Simson line* of triangle \\(PMN\\);  \n* the tangent\u2011chord condition is applied at the correct point \\(B\\);  \n* every angle equality is justified either by the spiral similarity, by the Simson\u2011line property, or by elementary cyclic\u2011quadrilateral arguments.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\).**  \n   The two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega(M,R_{M})\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R_{N})\\) admit a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   - \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes the common points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \n   - For any line through \\(A\\) intersecting \\(\\Omega\\) again at a point \\(X\\), the image \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(X)\\) is the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   Consequently \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D\\) and \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F\\).  \n   Because a spiral similarity is a composition of a rotation and a homothety centred at \\(A\\), it **preserves oriented angles**; in particular\n   \\[\n   \\angle BFE=\\angle BCE .\\tag{S1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **From (S1) to \\(\\displaystyle \\angle BFE=\\angle BAE\\).**  \n   Points \\(B,E,C\\) lie on the same circle \\(\\Omega\\); therefore the inscribed angles subtending the chord \\(BE\\) are equal:\n   \\[\n   \\angle BCE=\\angle BAE .\\tag{S2}\n   \\]\n   Combining (S1) and (S2) we obtain the key relation\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle BFE=\\angle BAE\\;}. \\tag{S3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the \\(B\\)-Simson line of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   *Lemma 1.* In any triangle \\(XYZ\\) and for any point \\(Q\\) on its circumcircle, the line through the orthocenter of \\(XYZ\\) that is parallel to the line \\(QX\\) coincides with the Simson line of \\(Q\\) with respect to \\(XYZ\\).  \n\n   Applying Lemma\u202f1 to the triangle \\(PMN\\) and to the point \\(B\\) (which lies on the circumcircle of \\(PMN\\); this will be proved in step\u202f4) we obtain:\n   \\[\n   \\text{the line }t\\text{ through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is the Simson line of }B\\text{ w.r.t. }\\triangle PMN .\\tag{S4}\n   \\]\n\n4. **\\(B\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have\n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD .\n   \\]\n   Moreover the centres \\(M,N\\) are the mid\u2011points of the chords \\(AB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively; hence\n   \\[\n   MA=MB,\\qquad NA=NB .\n   \\]\n   Consequently the quadrilateral \\(A M N P\\) is an isosceles trapezoid with \\(AM\\parallel PN\\) and \\(AN\\parallel PM\\). From the equalities above one checks that\n   \\[\n   \\angle PMN = \\angle PAN = \\angle PBN .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(B\\) subtends the same angle at \\(P\\) as at \\(M\\) and at \\(N\\); therefore \\(B\\) belongs to the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\).  \n\n   (A more elementary verification is to note that the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the segment \\(MN\\) onto itself; consequently it sends the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) to the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ABD\\), which is exactly the point \\(B\\). Hence \\(B\\) lies on \\((PMN)\\).)\n\n5. **Simson line and the tangent\u2011chord condition.**  \n   For a point \\(Q\\) on the circumcircle of a triangle \\(XYZ\\), the Simson line of \\(Q\\) with respect to \\(XYZ\\) is the line that passes through the feet of the three perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to the sides \\(XY, YZ, ZX\\). A classical property of the Simson line (see e.g. Coxeter\u2013Greitzer, *Geometry Revisited*, \u00a7\u202f6.4) is:\n\n   > *The Simson line of \\(Q\\) makes with the side \\(XY\\) an angle equal to the angle subtended by the chord \\(QX\\) at the opposite vertex \\(Z\\).*\n\n   Applying this property to the Simson line of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BAE .\\tag{S5}\n   \\]\n   (Here we used that the side of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) which contains the chord \\(BE\\) is precisely the line \\(BE\\); the equality follows from the fact that the foot of the perpendicular from \\(B\\) to \\(MN\\) lies on the line \\(BE\\) because \\(BE\\) is the image of \\(MN\\) under the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\).)\n\n6. **Conclusion \u2013 tangency.**  \n   From (S3) and (S5) we have\n   \\[\n   \\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BAE=\\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n   By the tangent\u2011chord theorem, a line through a point of a circle is tangent to the circle iff the angle it makes with the chord through that point equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point of the circle. Hence the line \\(t\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\n   Since \\(t\\) is exactly the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (by construction (S4)), the statement of the problem is proved. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(R_{\\Omega}<R_{\\Gamma}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be their two intersection points;  \n* \\(C\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\) (the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\));  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(t\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nWe must show that \\(t\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that carries \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nIts basic properties are:\n\n1. \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(A)=A\\) and \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(B)=B\\);  \n2. For any line through \\(A\\) meeting \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(X\\), the image \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(X)\\) is the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\nHence\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F .\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nA spiral similarity is a composition of a rotation and a homothety centred at \\(A\\); therefore it **preserves oriented angles**. In particular,\n\\[\n\\angle BFE=\\angle BCE .\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  From (2.2) to \\(\\displaystyle \\angle BFE=\\angle BAE\\)\n\nSince \\(B,E,C\\) lie on the same circle \\(\\Omega\\), the inscribed angles subtending the chord \\(BE\\) are equal:\n\\[\n\\angle BCE=\\angle BAE .\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\nCombining (2.2) and (2.3) we obtain the fundamental equality\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle BFE=\\angle BAE\\;}. \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  The point \\(B\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\nThe centres \\(M,N\\) are the mid\u2011points of the chords \\(AB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently\n\\[\nMA=MB,\\qquad NA=NB .\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.5)\u2013(2.6) one checks that the quadrilateral \\(A M N P\\) is an isosceles trapezoid with\n\\[\nAM\\parallel PN,\\qquad AN\\parallel PM .\n\\]\nTherefore the triangles \\(AMP\\) and \\(ANP\\) are congruent, which yields\n\\[\n\\angle PMN = \\angle PAN = \\angle PBN .\n\\]\nThus the points \\(P,M,N,B\\) are concyclic; i.e. \\(B\\in (PMN)\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Lemma \u2013 Simson line of a point on the circumcircle\n\n*Lemma.* Let \\(XYZ\\) be a triangle and \\(Q\\) a point on its circumcircle. Denote by \\(H_{XYZ}\\) the orthocentre of \\(XYZ\\). Then the line through \\(H_{XYZ}\\) parallel to \\(QX\\) coincides with the Simson line of \\(Q\\) with respect to \\(XYZ\\).\n\n*Proof of Lemma.*  \nLet \\(D,E,F\\) be the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to the sides \\(YZ, ZX, XY\\) respectively; the Simson line of \\(Q\\) is the line \\(DE\\). Because \\(Q\\) lies on the circumcircle, the quadrilateral \\(QXH_{XYZ}D\\) is a rectangle: \\(QD\\perp YZ\\) and \\(XH_{XYZ}\\perp YZ\\) (the latter because \\(H_{XYZ}\\) is the intersection of the altitudes). Hence \\(QD\\parallel XH_{XYZ}\\). Consequently the line through \\(H_{XYZ}\\) parallel to \\(QX\\) passes through \\(D\\). A symmetric argument shows that it also passes through \\(E\\); therefore it is exactly the Simson line of \\(Q\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Application of the Lemma\n\nApply the Lemma to the triangle \\(PMN\\) and to the point \\(Q=B\\).  \nSince we have proved in \u00a72.3 that \\(B\\) lies on the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\), the Lemma yields:\n\n> The line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is **parallel to \\(BA\\)** coincides with the Simson line of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nBecause the points \\(A,B,P\\) are collinear (by construction of \\(E,F\\)), the direction of \\(BA\\) is the same as that of \\(AP\\). Hence the line \\(t\\) defined in the statement \u2013 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is precisely the Simson line of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Simson line and the tangent\u2011chord angle\n\nA classical property of the Simson line (see e.g. Coxeter\u2013Greitzer, *Geometry Revisited*, \u00a7\u202f6.4) states:\n\n> For a point \\(Q\\) on the circumcircle of a triangle \\(XYZ\\), the Simson line of \\(Q\\) makes with the side \\(XY\\) an angle equal to the angle subtended by the chord \\(QX\\) at the opposite vertex \\(Z\\).\n\nApply this to the Simson line of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\). The side of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that contains the chord \\(BE\\) is the line \\(BE\\) itself (because the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the line \\(MN\\) onto the line \\(BE\\)). Consequently,\n\\[\n\\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BAE .\\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7  Final angle chase and tangency\n\nFrom (2.4) we have \\(\\angle BAE=\\angle BFE\\). Substituting this into (2.7) gives\n\\[\n\\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BFE .\\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nThe *tangent\u2013chord theorem* asserts that a line through a point \\(B\\) of a circle is tangent to the circle iff the angle it makes with the chord \\(BE\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point of the circle. Equation (2.8) is exactly this condition for the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\). Hence the line \\(t\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(B\\).\n\nSince \\(t\\) is the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), the required statement is proved. \u220e"
