ntire Section 2.3 collapses due to a critical error in (2.6) and the cascade of dependent statements. The claim that \\(B\\) belongs to \\((PMN)\\) is unproven.\n\n#### Lemma about Simson line (Section 2.4)\n\n- **Lemma Statement:** \u201cFor triangle \\(XYZ\\) and point \\(Q\\) on its circumcircle, the line through the orthocentre parallel to \\(QX\\) coincides with the Simson line of \\(Q\\).\u201d\n  - **Issue:** **Critical Error.** The Simson line of a point on the circumcircle is the line joining the feet of the perpendiculars from that point to the three sides. There is no general relationship that a line through the orthocentre parallel to a side coincides with this Simson line. The proof given incorrectly asserts that quadrilateral \\(QXH_{XYZ}D\\) is a rectangle; however, \\(XH_{XYZ}\\) is not generally perpendicular to \\(YZ\\). Thus the lemma is false.\n\n- **Proof of Lemma:** Uses the claim \u201c\\(QD\\parallel XH_{XYZ}\\)\u201d which is not true in general. Hence the lemma is invalid.\n\n#### Application of the Lemma (Section 2.5)\n\n- **Use of Lemma:** Applies the (false) lemma to triangle \\(PMN\\) and point \\(B\\) (assuming \\(B\\) lies on \\((PMN)\\)).\n  - **Issue:** Since the lemma is false, the derived statement that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(BA\\) is the Simson line of \\(B\\) is unsupported.\n\n- **Additional Claim:** \u201cBecause the points \\(A,B,P\\) are collinear (by construction of \\(E,F\\)), the direction of \\(BA\\) is the same as that of \\(AP\\).\u201d\n  - **Issue:** **Justification Gap.** The collinearity of \\(A,B,P\\) has not been proved. The construction only guarantees that \\(A,P,E,F\\) are collinear; it does **not** imply that \\(B\\) lies on this line. No argument is provided.\n\n#### Simson line and tangent\u2011chord angle (Section 2.6)\n\n- **Statement:** \u201cThe side of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that contains the chord \\(BE\\) is the line \\(BE\\) itself (because the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) 