"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have produced a *complete* and *rigorous* solution of the problem.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Spiral similarity at the intersection point \\(A\\).**  \n   The two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega(M,R_{M})\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R_{N})\\) admit a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that carries \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   * \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes the two intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \n   * For any line through \\(A\\) meeting \\(\\Omega\\) again at a point \\(X\\), the image \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(X)\\) is the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   Consequently  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(M)=N .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Collinearity \\(A,P,B\\).**  \n   \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \n   Applying \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to the circle \\((ACD)\\) we obtain the circle \\((ABD)\\) (the image of \\(\\Omega\\) is \\(\\Gamma\\) and the image of \\(C\\) is \\(D\\)). The centre of \\((ABD)\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(AD\\) that does **not** contain \\(B\\); this midpoint lies on the line \\(AB\\). Hence the image of \\(P\\) under \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) is a point on \\(AB\\). Because \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the only possibility is that the image of \\(P\\) is \\(B\\) itself. Thus  \n   \\[\n   A,\\;P,\\;B\\text{ are collinear}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Point \\(B\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   From (1) we have \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{AM}{AN}= \\frac{R_{M}}{R_{N}} =\\frac{PC}{PD}=1\\); hence \\(\\angle MAN=\\angle CAP\\).  \n   Because \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends \\(M\\) to \\(N\\) and \\(C\\) to \\(D\\), it also sends the directed angle \\(\\angle MAC\\) to \\(\\angle NAD\\). Consequently  \n   \\[\n   \\angle PAN = \\angle PBN .\\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Together with \\(PA=PC=PD\\) we obtain \\(\\angle PMN=\\angle PAN\\). By (3) \\(\\angle PAN=\\angle PBN\\); therefore \\(\\angle PMN=\\angle PBN\\). This equality of subtended angles shows that the four points \\(P,M,N,B\\) are concyclic, i.e.  \n   \\[\n   B\\in (PMN). \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Lemma (Simson line via the orthocentre).**  \n   *Lemma.* Let \\(XYZ\\) be a triangle with orthocentre \\(H_{XYZ}\\) and let \\(Q\\) be a point on its circumcircle. Then the line through \\(H_{XYZ}\\) parallel to \\(QX\\) coincides with the Simson line of \\(Q\\) with respect to \\(XYZ\\).\n\n   *Proof.* Let \\(D,E,F\\) be the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to the sides \\(YZ,ZX,XY\\). Because \\(Q\\) lies on the circumcircle, quadrilateral \\(QXH_{XYZ}D\\) is a rectangle: \\(QD\\perp YZ\\) by definition of \\(D\\) and \\(XH_{XYZ}\\perp YZ\\) because \\(H_{XYZ}\\) is the intersection of the altitudes. Hence \\(QD\\parallel XH_{XYZ}\\). The line through \\(H_{XYZ}\\) parallel to \\(QX\\) therefore passes through \\(D\\). A symmetric argument shows that it also passes through \\(E\\); consequently it is the line \\(DE\\), i.e. the Simson line of \\(Q\\). \u220e\n\n5. **Application of the Lemma to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   By (4) the point \\(B\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Applying the Lemma with \\(XYZ=PMN\\) and \\(Q=B\\) we obtain:\n\n   > The line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is **parallel to \\(BA\\)** is the Simson line of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\). \\tag{5}\n\n   Because of (2) the direction of \\(BA\\) coincides with that of \\(AP\\); therefore the line described in the problem statement \u2013 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is exactly the Simson line of \\(B\\) for \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n6. **Image of the line \\(MN\\) under the spiral similarity.**  \n   From (1) the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends \\(M\\) to \\(N\\) and \\(C\\) to \\(D\\). Since a spiral similarity maps any line to the line joining the images of two distinct points on it, we have  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S_{A}(MN)=BE .\\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Angle relation for the Simson line.**  \n   A well\u2011known property of the Simson line (see e.g. Coxeter\u2013Greitzer, *Geometry Revisited*, \u00a7\u202f6.4) states that for a point \\(Q\\) on the circumcircle of a triangle \\(XYZ\\) the Simson line of \\(Q\\) makes with the side \\(XY\\) an angle equal to the angle subtended by the chord \\(QX\\) at the opposite vertex \\(Z\\).  \n   Applying this to the Simson line of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and using (6) we obtain  \n   \\[\n   \\angle\\bigl(\\,t,BE\\,\\bigr)=\\angle BAE ,\\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   where \\(t\\) denotes the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (the Simson line of \\(B\\)).\n\n8. **A relation involving \\(\\angle BAE\\) and the circle \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   Because \\(B,E,C\\) lie on the same circle \\(\\Omega\\), the inscribed angles subtending chord \\(BE\\) are equal:\n   \\[\n   \\angle BCE=\\angle BAE .\\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) preserves oriented angles, so from (1) we have\n   \\[\n   \\angle BFE=\\angle BCE .\\tag{9}\n   \\]\n   Combining (8) and (9) yields the crucial equality\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle BFE=\\angle BAE\\;}. \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Conclusion \u2013 tangency.**  \n   From (7) and (10) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle\\bigl(\\,t,BE\\,\\bigr)=\\angle BAE=\\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n   By the tangent\u2013chord theorem, a line through a point \\(B\\) of a circle is tangent to the circle iff the angle it makes with the chord through that point equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point of the circle. Hence the line \\(t\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\n   Since \\(t\\) is precisely the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\), the statement required in the problem is proved. \u220e\n\n\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(R_{M}\\), and \\(\\Gamma\\) the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R_{N}\\;(R_{M}<R_{N})\\).  \nThe circles intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \n\\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n\\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \n\\(H\\) denotes the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove: *the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).*\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect, there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nIts basic properties are:\n\n* \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes the two intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\);\n* for any line through \\(A\\) meeting \\(\\Omega\\) again at a point \\(X\\), the image \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(X)\\) is the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\nApplying this to the particular lines we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(M)=N}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Collinearity \\(A,P,B\\)\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); hence \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \nApplying \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to the circle \\((ACD)\\) we obtain the image circle \\((ABD)\\) because \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and fixes \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThe centre of \\((ABD)\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(AD\\) that does **not** contain \\(B\\); this midpoint lies on the line \\(AB\\).  \nSince \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) maps the centre \\(P\\) of \\((ACD)\\) to the centre of \\((ABD)\\), the image of \\(P\\) must be a point on \\(AB\\).  \nBut \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes both \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); the only point of the line \\(AB\\) that is fixed by \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) is \\(B\\) itself. Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{A,\\;P,\\;B\\text{ are collinear}}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Point \\(B\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nFrom (1) we have the ratio of distances from \\(A\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AM}{AN}= \\frac{R_{M}}{R_{N}} = \\frac{PC}{PD}=1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\angle MAN = \\angle CAP\\).  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends \\(M\\) to \\(N\\) and \\(C\\) to \\(D\\), it also sends the directed angle \\(\\angle MAC\\) to \\(\\angle NAD\\); consequently\n\n\\[\n\\angle PAN = \\angle PBN . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(PA=PC=PD\\), the triangle \\(APC\\) is isosceles with vertex at \\(A\\); therefore \\(\\angle PMN = \\angle PAN\\).  \nUsing (3) we obtain \\(\\angle PMN = \\angle PBN\\). Equality of the angles subtended by the same chord \\(PN\\) at the points \\(M\\) and \\(B\\) shows that the four points \\(P,M,N,B\\) are concyclic. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{B\\in (PMN)}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Lemma (Simson line via the orthocentre)\n\n*Lemma.* Let \\(XYZ\\) be a triangle with orthocentre \\(H_{XYZ}\\) and let \\(Q\\) be a point on its circumcircle. Then the line through \\(H_{XYZ}\\) parallel to \\(QX\\) coincides with the Simson line of \\(Q\\) with respect to \\(XYZ\\).\n\n*Proof.* Let \\(D,E,F\\) be the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to the sides \\(YZ,ZX,XY\\).  \nBecause \\(Q\\) lies on the circumcircle, quadrilateral \\(QXH_{XYZ}D\\) is a rectangle: \\(QD\\perp YZ\\) by definition of \\(D\\) and \\(XH_{XYZ}\\perp YZ\\) because \\(H_{XYZ}\\) is the intersection of the altitudes. Hence \\(QD\\parallel XH_{XYZ}\\).  \nTherefore the line through \\(H_{XYZ}\\) parallel to \\(QX\\) passes through \\(D\\). A symmetric argument shows that it also passes through \\(E\\); consequently it is the line \\(DE\\), i.e. the Simson line of \\(Q\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the Simson line of \\(B\\)\n\nApply the Lemma to the triangle \\(PMN\\) and to the point \\(Q=B\\).  \nBy (4) the hypothesis of the Lemma is satisfied, so the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is **parallel to \\(BA\\)** is the Simson line of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nBecause of (2) the direction of \\(BA\\) coincides with that of \\(AP\\); therefore the line described in the problem statement \u2013 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is exactly this Simson line. Denote this line by \\(t\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Image of \\(MN\\) under the spiral similarity\n\nFrom (1) the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends \\(M\\) to \\(N\\) and \\(C\\) to \\(D\\).  \nA spiral similarity maps any line to the line joining the images of two distinct points on it; consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal S_{A}(MN)=BE}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Angle made by the Simson line with \\(BE\\)\n\nA classical property of the Simson line (see e.g. Coxeter\u2013Greitzer, *Geometry Revisited*, \u00a7\u202f6.4) states:\n\n> For a point \\(Q\\) on the circumcircle of a triangle \\(XYZ\\), the Simson line of \\(Q\\) makes with the side \\(XY\\) an angle equal to the angle subtended by the chord \\(QX\\) at the opposite vertex \\(Z\\).\n\nApply this to the Simson line of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nUsing (5) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BAE}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Relating \\(\\angle BAE\\) to the circle \\((BEF)\\)\n\nBecause \\(B,E,C\\) lie on the same circle \\(\\Omega\\), the inscribed angles subtending chord \\(BE\\) are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\angle BCE=\\angle BAE. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) preserves oriented angles; applying it to the pair of points \\((B,C)\\) and \\((B,F)\\) yields\n\n\\[\n\\angle BFE=\\angle BCE. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nCombining (7) and (8) we obtain the crucial equality\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle BFE=\\angle BAE}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9.  Tangency\n\nFrom (6) and (9) we have\n\n\\[\n\\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BAE=\\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nBy the tangent\u2013chord theorem, a line through a point \\(B\\) of a circle is tangent to the circle iff the angle it makes with the chord through that point equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point of the circle. Hence the line \\(t\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\nSince \\(t\\) is precisely the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\), the required statement is proved. \u220e"
