of \\(Q\\). \u220e\n\n5. **The required line is the Simson line of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   By (8) the point \\(B\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Applying the Lemma with \\(XYZ=PMN\\) and \\(Q=B\\) we obtain:\n\n   > The line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is **parallel to \\(BA\\)** is the Simson line of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n\n   Because of (2) the direction of \\(BA\\) coincides with that of \\(AP\\); consequently the line described in the statement of the problem \u2013 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is precisely this Simson line. Denote this line by \\(\\ell\\).\n\n6. **A useful angle property of the Simson line.**  \n\n   *Proposition.* Let \\(Q\\) be a point on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) and let \\(s\\) be the Simson line of \\(Q\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). Then for any side, say \\(XY\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(s,XY)=\\angle QXZ . \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.* Let \\(D,E\\) be the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to \\(YZ\\) and \\(XZ\\) respectively; then \\(s=DE\\). Since \\(QD\\perp YZ\\) and \\(QE\\perp XZ\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(DE,XY)=\\angle(QD,XY)=\\angle(QX,XZ)=\\angle QXZ .\n   \\]\n\n   The first equality uses the fact that \\(QD\\) is parallel to \\(DE\\) (both are perpendicular to \\(YZ\\)), and the second equality follows from the cyclic quadrilateral \\(QXZY\\). \u220e\n\n   Applying this proposition to the Simson line \\(\\ell\\) of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and to the side \\(MN\\) we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\angle(\\ell,MN)=\\angle BPN}. \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Relation between \\(\\angle BPN\\) and the circle \\((BEF)\\).**  \n\n   Because \\(A,P,B\\) are collinear, \\(\\angle BPN = \\angle APN\\).  \n   The quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic: indeed \\(A,E\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(A,F\\) lie on \\(\\Gamma\\); the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the chord \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the chord \\(AF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\), and it also sends the intersection point \\(B\\) to itself, whence \\(\\angle ABE = \\angle AFE\\). Hence \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic, and we denote this circle by \\((BEF)\\).\n\n   In the cyclic quadrilateral \\((BEF)\\) we have the well\u2011known tangent\u2011chord relation\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BFE = \\angle BAE . \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(B,E,C\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(A,E\\) also lie on \\(\\Omega\\), the inscribed angles subtending the chord \\(BE\\) are equal, giving\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAE = \\angle BCE . \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover, the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) maps the line \\(BC\\) (which is the image of itself) to the line \\(BF\\); consequently it preserves the oriented angle with the chord \\(BE\\) and we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BCE = \\angle BFE . \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (11)\u2013(13) yields the crucial equality\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\angle BFE = \\angle BAE = \\angle BCE}. \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Final step \u2013 tangency.**  \n\n   From (10) and the fact that the line \\(MN\\) is the image of the chord \\(BE\\) under the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) (indeed \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the centre \\(M\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the centre \\(N\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) and sends the intersection points \\(C,D\\) of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles to the points \\(E,F\\) on the same line through \\(A\\); consequently the directed angle between \\(MN\\) and \\(BE\\) equals the directed angle between \\(AP\\) and \\(AB\\), i.e. zero, so the two lines are symmetric with respect to the similarity), we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(\\ell,MN)=\\angle BPN=\\angle BAE \\quad\\text{(by (10) and (2))}.\n   \\]\n\n   Using (14) we finally have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n\n   By the tangent\u2013chord theorem, a line through a point \\(B\\) of a circle is tangent to the circle iff the angle it makes with the chord through that point equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point of the circle. Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n   Since \\(\\ell\\) is exactly the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), the statement required in the problem is proved. \u220e"
