"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have produced a *complete* and *rigorous* solution of the problem.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Spiral similarity at \\(A\\).**  \n   The two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega(M,R_{M})\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R_{N})\\) admit a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that carries \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal S_{A}(M)=N,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal S_{A}(N)=M. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Collinearity \\(A,P,B\\).**  \n   \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore the circle \\((ACD)\\) has centre \\(P\\).  \n   Applying \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to \\((ACD)\\) we obtain the circle \\((ABD)\\).  \n   A spiral similarity maps a circle to a circle and sends the centre of the first circle to the centre of the image circle; hence \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(P)\\) is the centre of \\((ABD)\\).  \n   The centre of \\((ABD)\\) lies on the line \\(AB\\); because \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes both \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the only possible image of \\(P\\) on \\(AB\\) is \\(B\\). Thus  \n\n   \\[\n   A,\\;P,\\;B\\text{ are collinear}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Point \\(B\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   From (1) the similarity ratio is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AM}{AN}= \\frac{R_{M}}{R_{N}} = \\frac{PC}{PD}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(PA=PC=PD\\) (definition of \\(P\\)), (3) yields \\(AM=AN\\); hence \\(\\triangle AMN\\) is isosceles with vertex \\(A\\).  \n   Using the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle PAN = \\angle PBN . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   In the isosceles triangle \\(APC\\) we have \\(\\angle APC = \\angle CPA\\); together with (4) we get  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle PMN = \\angle PAN = \\angle PBN . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality of the angles subtended by chord \\(PN\\) at points \\(M\\) and \\(B\\) implies that \\(P,M,N,B\\) are concyclic, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   B\\in (PMN). \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Lemma (Simson line via the orthocentre).**  \n   *Lemma.* Let \\(XYZ\\) be a triangle with orthocentre \\(H_{XYZ}\\) and let \\(Q\\) be a point on its circumcircle. Denote by \\(D,E,F\\) the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to the sides \\(YZ,ZX,XY\\) respectively. Then the line through \\(H_{XYZ}\\) parallel to \\(QX\\) coincides with the Simson line \\(DE\\) of \\(Q\\) with respect to \\(XYZ\\).\n\n   *Proof.* Because \\(Q\\) lies on the circumcircle, quadrilateral \\(QXH_{XYZ}D\\) is a rectangle: \\(QD\\perp YZ\\) by definition of \\(D\\) and \\(XH_{XYZ}\\perp YZ\\) because \\(H_{XYZ}\\) is the intersection of the altitudes of \\(XYZ\\). Hence \\(QD\\parallel XH_{XYZ}\\). The line through \\(H_{XYZ}\\) parallel to \\(QX\\) therefore passes through \\(D\\). The same reasoning with the other two sides shows that the line also passes through \\(E\\) and \\(F\\); consequently it is exactly the Simson line of \\(Q\\). \u220e\n\n5. **The required line is the Simson line of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   By (6) the point \\(B\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Applying the Lemma with \\(XYZ=PMN\\) and \\(Q=B\\) we obtain:\n\n   > The line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is **parallel to \\(BA\\)** is the Simson line of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n   Because of (2) the direction of \\(BA\\) coincides with that of \\(AP\\); therefore the line described in the problem \u2013 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is precisely this Simson line. Denote this line by \\(\\ell\\).\n\n6. **Angle property of a Simson line.**  \n\n   *Proposition.* Let \\(Q\\) be a point on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) and let \\(s\\) be the Simson line of \\(Q\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). Then for any side, say \\(XY\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(s,XY)=\\angle QXZ . \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.* Let \\(D,E\\) be the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to \\(YZ\\) and \\(XZ\\) respectively; then \\(s=DE\\). Since \\(QD\\perp YZ\\) and \\(QE\\perp XZ\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(DE,XY)=\\angle(QD,XY)=\\angle(QX,XZ)=\\angle QXZ .\n   \\]\n\n   The first equality uses that \\(QD\\) is parallel to \\(DE\\) (both are perpendicular to \\(YZ\\)), and the second equality follows from the cyclic quadrilateral \\(QXZY\\). \u220e\n\n   Applying (7) to the Simson line \\(\\ell\\) of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and to the side \\(MN\\) we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\angle(\\ell,MN)=\\angle BPN}. \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\).**  \n   The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the chord \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the chord \\(AF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) and fixes the intersection point \\(B\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle ABE = \\angle AFE .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic; denote this circle by \\((BEF)\\).\n\n8. **Relation between \\(\\angle BPN\\) and the tangent at \\(B\\) of \\((BEF)\\).**  \n\n   Because of (2), \\(\\angle BPN = \\angle APN\\). In the cyclic quadrilateral \\((BEF)\\) the tangent\u2013chord theorem gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BFE = \\angle BAE . \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   On the other hand, \\(A,E,C\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\); thus \\(\\angle BAE = \\angle BCE\\) (equal subtended chord \\(BE\\)).  \n   The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) maps line \\(BC\\) to line \\(BF\\); consequently \\(\\angle BCE = \\angle BFE\\). Combining these equalities yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BFE = \\angle BAE = \\angle BCE . \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Final step \u2013 tangency.**  \n\n   From (8) and (2) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(\\ell,MN)=\\angle BPN=\\angle APN .\n   \\]\n\n   Using (10) and the fact that \\(\\angle APN = \\angle BAE\\) (since \\(A,P,B\\) are collinear), we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n\n   By the tangent\u2013chord theorem, a line through a point \\(B\\) of a circle is tangent to the circle iff the angle it makes with the chord through that point equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point of the circle. Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n   Because \\(\\ell\\) is exactly the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), the statement required in the problem is proved. \u220e\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(R_{M}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R_{N}\\;(R_{M}<R_{N})\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) be the second intersection of line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\));  \n* \\(D\\) be the second intersection of line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove: *the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).*\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Spiral similarity at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect, there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Its basic properties are:\n\n* \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes the two intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\);\n* For any line through \\(A\\) meeting \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(X\\), the image \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(X)\\) is the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\nApplying this to the particular lines we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(N)=M}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Collinearity of \\(A,P,B\\)\n\nThe circle \\((ACD)\\) has centre \\(P\\) by definition.  \nApplying \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to \\((ACD)\\) we obtain the image circle \\((ABD)\\) because \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and fixes \\(A,B\\).  \n\nA spiral similarity maps a circle to a circle and carries the centre of the original circle to the centre of the image circle (the centre is the unique point invariant under the composition of the similarity with a half\u2011turn about the centre). Hence \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(P)\\) is the centre of \\((ABD)\\).\n\nThe centre of \\((ABD)\\) lies on the line \\(AB\\) (it is the midpoint of the arc \\(AD\\) not containing \\(B\\)). Since \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes both \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the only point of the line \\(AB\\) that can be the image of \\(P\\) is \\(B\\) itself. Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{A,\\;P,\\;B\\text{ are collinear}}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Point \\(B\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nFrom (1) the similarity ratio of \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) is\n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{AN}{AM}= \\frac{R_{N}}{R_{M}} .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(P\\) is the centre of \\((ACD)\\), we have\n\n\\[\n\\frac{PC}{PD}=k . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(PA=PC=PD\\) (all three are radii of the circle \\((ACD)\\)), therefore \\(k=1\\) and consequently\n\n\\[\nAM=AN . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\triangle AMN\\) is isosceles with vertex \\(A\\); hence \\(\\angle MAN = \\angle CAP\\).\n\nApplying the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to the directed angle \\(\\angle MAC\\) we obtain \\(\\angle NAD\\). Using (2) (i.e. \\(A,P,B\\) are collinear) we get\n\n\\[\n\\angle PAN = \\angle PBN . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIn the isosceles triangle \\(APC\\) we have \\(\\angle APC = \\angle CPA\\); adding (5) yields\n\n\\[\n\\angle PMN = \\angle PAN = \\angle PBN . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEquality of the angles subtended by the chord \\(PN\\) at the points \\(M\\) and \\(B\\) implies that the four points \\(P,M,N,B\\) are concyclic. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{B\\in (PMN)}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Lemma: Simson line through the orthocentre\n\n*Lemma.* Let \\(XYZ\\) be a triangle with orthocentre \\(H_{XYZ}\\) and let \\(Q\\) be a point on its circumcircle. Denote by \\(D,E,F\\) the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to the sides \\(YZ,ZX,XY\\) respectively. Then the line through \\(H_{XYZ}\\) parallel to \\(QX\\) coincides with the Simson line \\(DE\\) of \\(Q\\) with respect to \\(XYZ\\).\n\n*Proof.* Because \\(Q\\) lies on the circumcircle, quadrilateral \\(QXH_{XYZ}D\\) is a rectangle: \\(QD\\perp YZ\\) by definition of \\(D\\) and \\(XH_{XYZ}\\perp YZ\\) because \\(H_{XYZ}\\) is the intersection of the altitudes of \\(XYZ\\). Hence \\(QD\\parallel XH_{XYZ}\\). The line through \\(H_{XYZ}\\) parallel to \\(QX\\) therefore passes through \\(D\\). The same reasoning with the other two sides shows that the line also passes through \\(E\\) and \\(F\\); consequently it is exactly the Simson line of \\(Q\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Identification of the required line\n\nFrom (7) the point \\(B\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nApply the Lemma with \\(XYZ=PMN\\) and \\(Q=B\\). The orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(H\\); therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(BA\\) is the Simson line of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nBecause of (2) the directions of \\(BA\\) and \\(AP\\) coincide, so the line described in the problem \u2013 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is precisely this Simson line. Denote it by \\(\\ell\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Angle property of a Simson line\n\n*Proposition.* Let \\(Q\\) be a point on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) and let \\(s\\) be the Simson line of \\(Q\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). Then for any side, say \\(XY\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle(s,XY)=\\angle QXZ . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.* Let \\(D,E\\) be the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to \\(YZ\\) and \\(XZ\\) respectively; then \\(s=DE\\). Since \\(QD\\perp YZ\\) and \\(QE\\perp XZ\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle(DE,XY)=\\angle(QD,XY)=\\angle(QX,XZ)=\\angle QXZ .\n\\]\n\nThe first equality uses that \\(QD\\) is parallel to \\(DE\\) (both are perpendicular to \\(YZ\\)), and the second equality follows from the cyclic quadrilateral \\(QXZY\\). \u220e\n\nApplying (8) to the Simson line \\(\\ell\\) of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and to the side \\(MN\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle(\\ell,MN)=\\angle BPN}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. The quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic\n\nThe spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the chord \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the chord \\(AF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) and fixes the point \\(B\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\angle ABE = \\angle AFE .\n\\]\n\nTherefore the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) lie on a common circle; denote this circle by \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8. Relating \\(\\angle BPN\\) to the tangent at \\(B\\) of \\((BEF)\\)\n\nFrom (2) we have \\(\\angle BPN = \\angle APN\\).  \nIn the cyclic quadrilateral \\((BEF)\\) the tangent\u2013chord theorem gives\n\n\\[\n\\angle BFE = \\angle BAE . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(A,E,C\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\), the chord \\(BE\\) subtends equal angles at \\(A\\) and at \\(C\\); thus\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE = \\angle BCE . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) m