); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle MAN = \\angle NAC . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Applying \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to \\(\\angle MAC\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle MAN = \\angle NBD . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Using Lemma\u202f1 (\\(A,P,B\\) collinear) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle PAN = \\angle PBN . \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   In the isosceles triangle \\(APC\\) we have \\(\\angle APC = \\angle CPA\\); adding (7) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle PMN = \\angle PAN = \\angle PBN . \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality of the angles subtended by the chord \\(PN\\) at the points \\(M\\) and \\(B\\) implies that the four points \\(P,M,N,B\\) are concyclic; i.e. \\(B\\in (PMN)\\). \u220e\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f3 (Simson\u2013orthocentre Lemma).**  \n   Let \\(XYZ\\) be a triangle with orthocentre \\(H_{XYZ}\\) and let \\(Q\\) be a point on its circumcircle. Denote by \\(D,E,F\\) the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to the sides \\(YZ,ZX,XY\\) respectively. Then the line through \\(H_{XYZ}\\) parallel to \\(QX\\) coincides with the Simson line \\(DE\\) of \\(Q\\) with respect to \\(XYZ\\).\n\n   *Proof.* Because \\(Q\\) lies on the circumcircle, quadrilateral \\(QXH_{XYZ}D\\) is a rectangle: \\(QD\\perp YZ\\) by definition of \\(D\\) and \\(XH_{XYZ}\\perp YZ\\) because \\(H_{XYZ}\\) is the intersection of the altitudes of \\(XYZ\\). Hence \\(QD\\parallel XH_{XYZ}\\). The line through \\(H_{XYZ}\\) parallel to \\(QX\\) therefore passes through \\(D\\). The same reasoning with the other two sides shows that the line also passes through \\(E\\) and \\(F\\). The three points \\(D,E,F\\) are known to be collinear (the Simson line property); consequently the line through \\(H_{XYZ}\\) parallel to \\(QX\\) is exactly the Simson line of \\(Q\\). \u220e\n\n5. **Application of Lemma\u202f3.**  \n   By Lemma\u202f2, \\(B\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Applying Lemma\u202f3 with \\(XYZ=PMN\\) and \\(Q=B\\) we obtain:\n\n   > The line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is **parallel to \\(BA\\)** is the Simson line of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n   Lemma\u202f1 tells us that \\(A,B,P\\) are collinear, hence the direction of \\(BA\\) coincides with that of \\(AP\\). Therefore the line described in the problem \u2013 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is precisely this Simson line. Denote it by \\(\\ell\\).\n\n6. **Angle made by a Simson line.**  \n\n   *Proposition\u202f4.* Let \\(Q\\) be a point on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) and let \\(s\\) be the Simson line of \\(Q\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). Then for any side, say \\(XY\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(s,XY)=\\angle QXZ . \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.* Let \\(D,E\\) be the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to \\(YZ\\) and \\(XZ\\) respectively; then \\(s=DE\\). Because \\(QD\\perp YZ\\) and \\(QE\\perp XZ\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(DE,XY)=\\angle(QD,XY) .\n   \\]\n\n   In the cyclic quadrilateral \\(QXZY\\) the angle between \\(QD\\) (which is parallel to \\(QE\\)) and \\(XY\\) equals \\(\\angle QXZ\\). Hence (9) holds. \u220e\n\n   Applying (9) to the Simson line \\(\\ell\\) of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and to the side \\(MN\\) we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\angle(\\ell,MN)=\\angle BPN}. \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Cyclicity of \\(A,B,E,F\\).**  \n   The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the chord \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the chord \\(AF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) and fixes the point \\(B\\). Since a spiral similarity preserves oriented angles,\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle ABE = \\angle AFE . \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) lie on a common circle; denote this circle by \\((BEF)\\).\n\n8. **Relation between \\(\\angle BPN\\) and the tangent at \\(B\\) of \\((BEF)\\).**  \n\n   Because of Lemma\u202f1, \\(A,P,B\\) are collinear, so \\(\\angle BPN = \\angle APN\\).  \n   In the cyclic quadrilateral \\((BEF)\\) the tangent\u2013chord theorem gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BFE = \\angle BAE . \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   Points \\(A,E,C\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\); therefore the chord \\(BE\\) subtends equal angles at \\(A\\) and at \\(C\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAE = \\angle BCE . \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) maps line \\(BC\\) to line \\(BF\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BCE = \\angle BFE . \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (12)\u2013(14) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\angle BFE = \\angle BAE = \\angle BCE}. \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Final step \u2013 tangency.**  \n\n   From (10) and Lemma\u202f1 we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(\\ell,MN)=\\angle BPN=\\angle APN .\n   \\]\n\n   Using (15) and the collinearity \\(A,P,B\\) we get \\(\\angle APN = \\angle BAE\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BAE = \\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n\n   By the tangent\u2013chord theorem, a line through a point \\(B\\) of a circle is tangent to the circle iff the angle it makes with the chord through that point equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point of the circle. Therefore the line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n   Since \\(\\ell\\) is exactly the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), the statement required in the problem is proved. \u220e\n\n\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(R_{M}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R_{N}>R_{M}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Spiral similarity at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect, there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Its basic properties are\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \n\\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F,\\qquad \n\\mathcal S_{A}(M)=N,\\qquad \n\\mathcal S_{A}(N)=M . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe similarity ratio is  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{AN}{AM}= \\frac{R_{N}}{R_{M}} . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 \\(A,P,B\\) are collinear\n\nThe circle \\((ACD)\\) has centre \\(P\\). Applying \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to this circle we obtain the image circle \\((ABD)\\); the centre of the image is the circum\u2011centre \\(O_{ABD}\\) of \\(\\triangle ABD\\). A spiral similarity sends the centre of a circle to the centre of its image, therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(P)=O_{ABD}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe point \\(O_{ABD}\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\) and also on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\); consequently it is the midpoint of the arc \\(BD\\) of \\((ABD)\\) that does **not** contain \\(A\\). In particular \\(O_{ABD}\\) lies on the line \\(AB\\).\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the only point of the line \\(AB\\) that can be the image of \\(P\\) is \\(B\\) itself. Hence \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(P)=B\\) and (3) yields  \n\n\\[\nA,P,B\\ \\text{are collinear}. \\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 \\(B\\) lies on \\((PMN)\\)\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D\\). Because \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) i