ion that \\(\\angle PMN = \\angle PBN\\) is not established.\n\n> \u201cEquality of the angles subtended by the chord \\(PN\\) at the points \\(M\\) and \\(B\\) implies that the four points \\(P,M,N,B\\) are concyclic; i.e. \\(B\\in (PMN)\\).\u201d\n\nBecause the angle equality was never proved, the cyclicity claim is unsupported. **Critical Error** \u2013 Lemma\u202f2 fails.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Lemma\u202f3 (Simson\u2013orthocentre Lemma)\n\n> *Statement.* \u201cLet \\(XYZ\\) be a triangle with orthocentre \\(H_{XYZ}\\) and let \\(Q\\) be a point on its circumcircle. \u2026 Then the line through \\(H_{XYZ}\\) parallel to \\(QX\\) coincides with the Simson line \\(DE\\) of \\(Q\\) with respect to \\(XYZ\\).\u201d\n\n**Critical Error.**  \nThe classical Simson line theorem states that the feet of the perpendiculars from a point \\(Q\\) on the circumcircle to the three sides are collinear; this line is the Simson line. There is **no** general relationship between that line and a line through the orthocentre parallel to \\(QX\\). The statement is false; a counter\u2011example can be constructed with an acute triangle where the Simson line is not parallel to any line through the orthocentre. Hence the lemma is incorrect.\n\n> *Proof.* \u201cBecause \\(Q\\) lies on the circumcircle, quadrilateral \\(QXH_{XYZ}D\\) is a rectangle\u2026 Hence \\(QD\\parallel XH_{XYZ}\\).\u201d\n\nThe quadrilateral \\(QXH_{XYZ}D\\) is **not** a rectangle. While \\(QD\\perp YZ\\) and \\(XH_{XYZ}\\perp YZ\\), this only shows that \\(QD\\) and \\(XH_{XYZ}\\) are **parallel**, not that the four points form a rectangle (the other sides are not shown to be parallel). Moreover, the argument does not connect this parallelism to the required line through the orthocentre parallel to \\(QX\\). The proof is therefore invalid.\n\n*Result:* Lemma\u202f3 is false; it constitutes a **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n### 5. The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is a Simson line\n\n> \u201cFrom Lemma\u202f2, 