t \\(A\\) sends line \\(AB\\) to line \\(AF\\), one must argue that the oriented angle \\(\\angle ABE\\) (angle between \\(AB\\) and \\(BE\\)) equals the oriented angle between the images of those lines, i.e. \\(\\angle AFE\\). The solution merely states this without proof. Moreover, to deduce cyclicity from a single angle equality one needs the equality of *subtended* angles at the same segment, which is not explicitly shown. Therefore the cyclicity claim is not rigorously justified.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Relating \\(\\angle BPN\\) to the tangent at \\(B\\) of \\((BEF)\\)\n\nThe chain of equalities (13)\u2013(16) relies on:\n\n* Lemma\u202f1 (collinearity \\(A,P,B\\)) \u2013 false.\n* Lemma\u202f2 (point \\(B\\) on \\((PMN)\\)) \u2013 false.\n* The cyclicity of \\(A,B,E,F\\) \u2013 only a justification gap.\n* Various angle chase steps that assume the previous false statements.\n\nBecause the foundational premises are invalid, the derived relation \\(\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE\\) is not established. This constitutes a **Critical Error** in the final argument.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Final step \u2013 tangency\n\nThe conclusion \u201cthe line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\)\u201d follows from the previous erroneous steps. Hence the proof of tangency is not valid.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\nThe solution contains several **critical logical mistakes** (incorrect use of properties of spiral similarity, false lemmas about the Simson line, and unfounded collinearity/cyclicity claims) and **justification gaps** where statements are asserted without proper proof. Because the core lemmas are false, the entire argument collapses, and the problem remains unproved. Consequently the solution is **invalid**."
