ent\u2013chord theorem gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BFE = \\angle BAE . \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   Points \\(A,E,C\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\); therefore the chord \\(BE\\) subtends equal angles at \\(A\\) and at \\(C\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAE = \\angle BCE . \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) maps line \\(BC\\) to line \\(BF\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BCE = \\angle BFE . \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (12)\u2013(14) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\angle BFE = \\angle BAE = \\angle BCE}. \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Final step \u2013 tangency.**  \n\n   From (10) and Lemma\u202f1 we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(\\ell,MN)=\\angle BPN=\\angle APN .\n   \\]\n\n   Using (15) and the collinearity \\(A,P,B\\) we get \\(\\angle APN = \\angle BAE\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BAE = \\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n\n   By the tangent\u2013chord theorem, a line through a point \\(B\\) of a circle is tangent to the circle iff the angle it makes with the chord through that point equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point of the circle. Therefore the line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n   Since \\(\\ell\\) is exactly the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), the statement required in the problem is proved. \u220e\n\n\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(R_{M}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R_{N}>R_{M}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Spiral similarity at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect, there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Its basic properties are\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \n\\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F,\\qquad \n\\mathcal S_{A}(M)=N,\\qquad \n\\mathcal S_{A}(N)=M . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe similarity ratio is  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{AN}{AM}= \\frac{R_{N}}{R_{M}} . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 \\(A,P,B\\) are collinear\n\nThe circle \\((ACD)\\) has centre \\(P\\). Applying \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to this circle we obtain the image circle \\((ABD)\\); the centre of the image is the circum\u2011centre \\(O_{ABD}\\) of \\(\\triangle ABD\\). A spiral similarity sends the centre of a circle to the centre of its image, therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(P)=O_{ABD}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe point \\(O_{ABD}\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\) and also on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\); consequently it is the midpoint of the arc \\(BD\\) of \\((ABD)\\) that does **not** contain \\(A\\). In particular \\(O_{ABD}\\) lies on the line \\(AB\\).\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the only point of the line \\(AB\\) that can be the image of \\(P\\) is \\(B\\) itself. Hence \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(P)=B\\) and (3) yields  \n\n\\[\nA,P,B\\ \\text{are collinear}. \\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 \\(B\\) lies on \\((PMN)\\)\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D\\). Because 