the line through \\(H_{XYZ}\\) parallel to \\(QX\\) is exactly the Simson line of \\(Q\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is a Simson line\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2, \\(B\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Applying Lemma\u202f3 with  \n\n\\[\nXYZ = PMN,\\qquad Q = B,\n\\]\n\nwe obtain:\n\n> The line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is **parallel to \\(BA\\)** is the Simson line of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nLemma\u202f1 tells us that \\(A,B,P\\) are collinear, therefore the direction of \\(BA\\) coincides with that of \\(AP\\). Consequently the line required in the problem \u2013 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is precisely this Simson line. Denote it by \\(\\ell\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Angle made by a Simson line\n\n*Proposition\u202f4.*  \nLet \\(Q\\) be a point on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) and let \\(s\\) be the Simson line of \\(Q\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). Then for any side, say \\(XY\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle(s,XY)=\\angle QXZ . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(D,E\\) be the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to \\(YZ\\) and \\(XZ\\) respectively; then \\(s=DE\\). Because \\(QD\\perp YZ\\) and \\(QE\\perp XZ\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle(DE,XY)=\\angle(QD,XY).\n\\]\n\nIn the cyclic quadrilateral \\(QXZY\\) the angle between \\(QD\\) (which is parallel to \\(QE\\)) and \\(XY\\) equals \\(\\angle QXZ\\). Hence (10) holds. \u220e\n\nApplying (10) to the Simson line \\(\\ell\\) of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and to the side \\(MN\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle(\\ell,MN)=\\angle BPN}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. The quadrilateral \\(A,B,E,F\\) is cyclic\n\nThe spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the chord \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the chord \\(AF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) and fixes \\(B\\). Since a spiral similarity preserves oriented angles,\n\n\\[\n\\angle ABE = \\angle AFE . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThus the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) lie on a common circle; denote this circle by \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8. Relating \\(\\angle BPN\\) to the tangent at \\(B\\) of \\((BEF)\\)\n\nBecause of Lemma\u202f1, \\(A,P,B\\) are collinear, so \\(\\angle BPN = \\angle APN\\).\n\nIn the cyclic quadrilateral \\((BEF)\\) the tangent\u2013chord theorem gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BFE = \\angle BAE . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nPoints \\(A,E,C\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\); therefore the chord \\(BE\\) subtends equal angles at \\(A\\) and at \\(C\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE = \\angle BCE . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThe spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) maps line \\(BC\\) to line \\(BF\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BCE = \\angle BFE . \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nFrom (13)\u2013(15) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle BFE = \\angle BAE = \\angle BCE}. \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9. Final step \u2013 tangency\n\nFrom (11) and Lemma\u202f1 we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(\\ell,MN)=\\angle BPN=\\angle APN .\n\\]\n\nUsing (16) and the collinearity \\(A,P,B\\) we get \\(\\angle APN = \\angle BAE\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BAE = \\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nBy the tangent\u2013chord theorem, a line through a point \\(B\\) of a circle is tangent to the circle iff the angle it makes with the chord through that point equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point of the circle. Therefore the line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\) is exactly the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), the statement required in the problem is proved. \u220e"
