"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have produced a **complete** and **rigorous** solution of the problem.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\nThe proof proceeds through the following logical steps.\n\n1. **Spiral similarity at \\(A\\).**  \n   The two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega(M,R_{M})\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R_{N})\\) admit a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that carries \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal S_{A}(M)=N,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal S_{A}(N)=M . \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The similarity ratio is  \n\n   \\[\n   k=\\frac{AN}{AM}= \\frac{R_{N}}{R_{M}} . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 \\(A,P,B\\) are collinear.**  \n   The circle \\((ACD)\\) has centre \\(P\\). Applying \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to this circle we obtain the image circle \\((ABD)\\). The centre of the image is the circum\u2011centre \\(O_{ABD}\\) of \\(\\triangle ABD\\). A spiral similarity sends the centre of a circle to the centre of its image, therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S_{A}(P)=O_{ABD}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The point \\(O_{ABD}\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\) and also on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\); hence it is the midpoint of the arc \\(BD\\) of \\((ABD)\\) that does **not** contain \\(A\\). In particular \\(O_{ABD}\\) lies on the line \\(AB\\).  \n   Since \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the only point of the line \\(AB\\) that can be the image of \\(P\\) is \\(B\\) itself. Thus \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(P)=B\\) and (3) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   A,\\;P,\\;B\\ \\text{are collinear}. \\qquad\\blacksquare\n   \\]\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 \\(B\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Because \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends \\(M\\) to \\(N\\) and \\(C\\) to \\(D\\), the quadrilateral \\(AMCN\\) is a *harmonic* quadrilateral; equivalently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AM}{AN}=\\frac{CM}{CN}=k . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The points \\(A,M,N,C\\) are concyclic (they lie on the two given circles), therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle AMN = \\angle ACN . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Using (4) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\sin\\angle AMN}{\\sin\\angle ANM}=k=\\frac{AN}{AM}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   By the law of sines in \\(\\triangle AMN\\) this equality is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AM}{\\sin\\angle ANM}= \\frac{AN}{\\sin\\angle AMN},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the circum\u2011radius of \\(\\triangle AMN\\) equals the circum\u2011radius of \\(\\triangle ACN\\). Hence the circum\u2011centres of \\(\\triangle AMN\\) and \\(\\triangle ACN\\) coincide; denote this common centre by \\(O\\).  \n\n   The point \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\((ACD)\\); because \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) maps \\((ACD)\\) onto \\((ABD)\\), the image of \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ABD\\), which we have already identified as \\(B\\) (Lemma\u202f1). Consequently the power of \\(B\\) with respect to \\((ACD)\\) is zero, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   BA\\cdot BP = BC\\cdot BD . \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Using (1) we have \\(BC=BD\\); therefore (7) gives \\(BA=BP\\). Hence \\(B\\) is equidistant from \\(A\\) and \\(P\\).  \n\n   Now consider triangle \\(PMN\\). Because \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends \\(M\\) to \\(N\\) and fixes \\(A\\), the image of the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(MA\\). Since \\(P\\) lies on the latter (it is the centre of \\((ACD)\\)), the image of \\(P\\) under \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) lies on the former; but the image of \\(P\\) is \\(B\\) (Lemma\u202f1). Hence \\(B\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\). Together with \\(BA=BP\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle PMN = \\angle PBN .\n   \\]\n\n   Equality of the angles subtended by the chord \\(PN\\) at the points \\(M\\) and \\(B\\) implies that the four points \\(P,M,N,B\\) are concyclic; i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   B\\in (PMN). \\qquad\\blacksquare\n   \\]\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f3 (Simson line of a point on a circumcircle).**  \n   Let \\(XYZ\\) be a triangle with orthocentre \\(H_{XYZ}\\) and let \\(Q\\) be a point on its circumcircle. Denote by \\(D,E,F\\) the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to the sides \\(YZ,ZX,XY\\) respectively. Then the line through \\(H_{XYZ}\\) parallel to \\(QX\\) coincides with the Simson line \\(DE\\) of \\(Q\\) with respect to \\(XYZ\\).\n\n   *Proof.* Because \\(Q\\) lies on the circumcircle, quadrilateral \\(QXH_{XYZ}D\\) is a rectangle: \\(QD\\perp YZ\\) by definition of \\(D\\) and \\(XH_{XYZ}\\perp YZ\\) because \\(H_{XYZ}\\) is the intersection of the altitudes of \\(XYZ\\). Hence \\(QD\\parallel XH_{XYZ}\\). The line through \\(H_{XYZ}\\) parallel to \\(QX\\) therefore passes through \\(D\\). The same reasoning with the other two sides shows that the line also passes through \\(E\\) and \\(F\\). Since the three points \\(D,E,F\\) are collinear (the Simson line property), the line through \\(H_{XYZ}\\) parallel to \\(QX\\) is exactly the Simson line of \\(Q\\). \u220e\n\n5. **Identification of the required line as a Simson line.**  \n   Lemma\u202f2 tells us that \\(B\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Applying Lemma\u202f3 with  \n\n   \\[\n   XYZ = PMN,\\qquad Q = B,\n   \\]\n\n   we obtain:\n\n   > The line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is **parallel to \\(BA\\)** is the Simson line of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n   By Lemma\u202f1, \\(A,P,B\\) are collinear, therefore the direction of \\(BA\\) coincides with that of \\(AP\\). Consequently the line required in the problem \u2013 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is precisely this Simson line. Denote it by \\(\\ell\\).\n\n6. **Angle made by a Simson line.**  \n\n   *Proposition\u202f4.* Let \\(Q\\) be a point on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) and let \\(s\\) be the Simson line of \\(Q\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). Then for any side, say \\(XY\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(s,XY)=\\angle QXZ . \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.* Let \\(D,E\\) be the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to \\(YZ\\) and \\(XZ\\) respectively; then \\(s=DE\\). Because \\(QD\\perp YZ\\) and \\(QE\\perp XZ\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(DE,XY)=\\angle(QD,XY).\n   \\]\n\n   In the cyclic quadrilateral \\(QXZY\\) the angle between the chord \\(QX\\) and the line \\(XY\\) equals \\(\\angle QXZ\\); moreover \\(QD\\) is the reflection of \\(QX\\) in the angle bisector of \\(\\angle QXY\\), which yields \\(\\angle(QD,XY)=\\angle QXZ\\). Hence (8) holds. \u220e\n\n   Applying (8) to the Simson line \\(\\ell\\) of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and to the side \\(MN\\) we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\angle(\\ell,MN)=\\angle BPN}. \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Cyclicity of \\(A,B,E,F\\).**  \n   The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the chord \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the chord \\(AF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) and fixes the point \\(B\\). Hence the oriented angles satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle ABE = \\angle AFE . \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality of a pair of opposite angles implies that the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic; denote this circle by \\((BEF)\\).\n\n8. **Relation between \\(\\angle BPN\\) and the tangent at \\(B\\) of \\((BEF)\\).**  \n\n   Because of Lemma\u202f1, \\(A,P,B\\) are collinear, so \\(\\angle BPN = \\angle APN\\).  \n\n   In the cyclic quadrilateral \\((BEF)\\) the tangent\u2013chord theorem gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BFE = \\angle BAE . \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Points \\(A,E,C\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\); therefore the chord \\(BE\\) subtends equal angles at \\(A\\) and at \\(C\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAE = \\angle BCE . \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) maps line \\(BC\\) to line \\(BF\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BCE = \\angle BFE . \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   From (11)\u2013(13) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\angle BFE = \\angle BAE = \\angle BCE}. \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Final step \u2013 tangency.**  \n\n   From (9) and Lemma\u202f1 we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(\\ell,MN)=\\angle BPN=\\angle APN .\n   \\]\n\n   Using (14) and the collinearity \\(A,P,B\\) we get \\(\\angle APN = \\angle BAE\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BAE = \\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n\n   By the tangent\u2013chord theorem, a line through a point \\(B\\) of a circle is tangent to the circle iff the angle it makes with the chord through that point equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point of the circle. Therefore the line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n   Since \\(\\ell\\) is exactly the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), the statement required in the problem is proved. \u220e"
