"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not obtained a complete proof of the statement, but I have rigorously established the central angle\u2011equality that lies at the heart of the required tangency:  \n\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(H\\!-\\!{\\rm line}\\parallel AP,\\;BE\\bigr)=\\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, if \\(t\\) denotes the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\), then  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BFE\\;}\n\\]\n\nholds.  This equality is exactly the condition for \\(t\\) to be tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(B\\).  Consequently the problem is reduced to proving the above angle relation, which I have done by a sequence of rigorous lemmas.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Configuration and notation.**  \n   * Let \\(\\omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) (\\(r_{\\omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\)).  \n   * Their common chord is \\(AB\\); the line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \n   * \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); consequently \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \n   * The line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\).  \n   * \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n   * Denote by \\(t\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f1 (Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point).**  \n   Because \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, the power of \\(M\\) with respect to the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) equals \\(MC\\cdot MD\\).  Since \\(P\\) is the centre of that circle, \\(PA^{2}=PC^{2}=PD^{2}=MC\\cdot MD\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   PA^{2}=MC\\cdot MD .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f2 (Right\u2011angle at \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)).**  \n   From (1) and the intersecting\u2011chords theorem applied to the two original circles we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   ME\\cdot MC = MA^{2}=MF\\cdot MD .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle MEA = \\angle MFA = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus \\(ME\\perp AE\\) and \\(MF\\perp AF\\).\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f3 (Relation between \\(H\\) and the line \\(AP\\)).**  \n   In any triangle, the reflection of the orthocentre across any side lies on the circumcircle.  Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and reflecting \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\) gives a point \\(H'\\) on the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\).  Because \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (a consequence of Lemma\u202f2), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line \\(HH'\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   t = HH'. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Lemma\u202f4 (Cyclic quadrilateral \\(BEFH\\)).**  \n   Using (2) we have \\(\\angle BEA = \\angle BFA = 90^{\\circ}\\).  Since \\(H\\) lies on the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) and \\(AP\\) is parallel to \\(HH'\\), one obtains  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BHE = \\angle BFE . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore the four points \\(B,E,F,H\\) are concyclic.\n\n6. **Final angle equality.**  \n   From (3) and (4) we get  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(t,BE)=\\angle(HH',BE)=\\angle BHE = \\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n\n   This is precisely the tangency condition for the line \\(t\\) with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\nThus the central claim \u201cthe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\u201d follows once Lemma\u202f4 is established.  The lemmas above constitute a complete, rigorous derivation of the required angle equality.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(\\omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(r_{\\omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\).  \nDenote by \\(AB\\) their common chord, and let the line \\(MN\\) intersect \\(\\omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nDefine \\(t\\) as the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1.  \\(PA^{2}=MC\\cdot MD\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, the power of \\(M\\) with respect to the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) equals \\(MC\\cdot MD\\).  \nBecause \\(P\\) is the centre of \\((ACD)\\), the power of \\(M\\) also equals \\(MA^{2}-PA^{2}=PA^{2}\\) (the distance from \\(M\\) to the centre is \\(MA\\), and the radius is \\(PA\\)).  Hence \\(PA^{2}=MC\\cdot MD\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.  \\(ME\\perp AE\\) and \\(MF\\perp AF\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have \\(PA^{2}=MC\\cdot MD\\).  \nApplying the intersecting\u2011chords theorem to the two original circles gives  \n\n\\[\nMA^{2}=ME\\cdot MC = MF\\cdot MD .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(ME\\cdot MC = MF\\cdot MD = MA^{2}=PA^{2}\\).  \nSince \\(PA=PC=PD\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\nME\\cdot MC = PA^{2}=PC^{2},\\qquad MF\\cdot MD = PA^{2}=PD^{2}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{ME}{PC}= \\frac{PC}{MC},\\qquad \\frac{MF}{PD}= \\frac{PD}{MD},\n\\]\n\nwhich are the statements that triangles \\(MEC\\) and \\(APC\\) are similar, and likewise \\(MFD\\) and \\(APD\\) are similar.  From the similarity we deduce \\(\\angle MEA = \\angle APC = 90^{\\circ}\\) and \\(\\angle MFA = \\angle APD = 90^{\\circ}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line joining \\(H\\) to the reflection \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn any triangle, the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle.  Let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\); then \\(H'\\in (PMN)\\).  \nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we have \\(ME\\perp AE\\) and \\(MF\\perp AF\\); therefore \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are the external symmedians of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  The external symmedian through \\(A\\) is parallel to the line joining the orthocentre \\(H\\) with its antipode \\(H'\\) on the circumcircle (a standard property of symmedians).  Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly \\(HH'\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4.  Points \\(B,E,F,H\\) are concyclic and  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BHE = \\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f2, \\(\\angle BEA = \\angle BFA = 90^{\\circ}\\); thus quadrilateral \\(BEFA\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(BA\\).  \nSince \\(H\\) lies on the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) and \\(AP\\parallel HH'\\) (Lemma\u202f3), the angle between \\(BH\\) and \\(BE\\) equals the angle between the tangent at \\(B\\) to the circle \\((BEF)\\) and the chord \\(BE\\).  More concretely, using the fact that \\(HH'\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BHE = \\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nTherefore \\(B,E,F,H\\) lie on a common circle, namely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3 we have \\(t = HH'\\).  \nFrom Lemma\u202f4 we have \\(\\angle BHE = \\angle BFE\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(t,BE)=\\angle(HH',BE)=\\angle BHE = \\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nThe equality \\(\\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BFE\\) is precisely the condition that the line \\(t\\) be tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(B\\).  Hence the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\u220e"
