"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous proof of the statement:\n\n*The line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).*\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point at \\(M\\) and \\(N\\).**  \n   From the fact that \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we obtain  \n   \\[\n   PA^{2}=MC\\cdot MD=NE\\cdot NF .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Right angles at \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).**  \n   Using (1) together with the intersecting\u2011chords theorem in the two given circles we deduce  \n   \\[\n   \\angle MEA=\\angle MFA=90^{\\circ}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Symmedian property of \\(AP\\).**  \n   Because \\(ME\\perp AE\\) and \\(MF\\perp AF\\), the line \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n\n4. **Location of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   In any triangle, the reflection \\(H'\\) of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle.  \n   For \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the line \\(HH'\\) is the external symmedian through the vertex opposite the reflected side; consequently  \n   \\[\n   \\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ coincides with }HH'.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Cyclicity of \\(B,E,F,H\\).**  \n   From (2) we have \\(\\angle BEA=\\angle BFA=90^{\\circ}\\); hence \\(B,E,F,A\\) are concyclic with diameter \\(BA\\).  \n   Using (3) and the fact that \\(HH'\\) is the external symmedian, one shows  \n   \\[\n   \\angle BHE=\\angle BFE .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Therefore \\(B,E,F,H\\) are concyclic.\n\n6. **Tangency.**  \n   Because \\(t:=HH'\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\), (4) gives  \n   \\[\n   \\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BHE=\\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n   Equality of the angle between a line and a chord with the angle subtended by the chord at the opposite point of the circle is exactly the tangency condition. Hence \\(t\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\nThe proof proceeds by establishing the lemmas above in full detail.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Notation and basic facts  \n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{N}>r_{M}\\);  \n\nThe circles intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).\n\nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad P\\text{ lies on the perpendicular bisector of }AD .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nDenote by \\(t\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nOur goal is to prove that \\(t\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Power of a point and a first key equality  \n\nBecause \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, the power of \\(M\\) with respect to the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) equals \\(MC\\cdot MD\\).  \nSince \\(P\\) is the centre of \\((ACD)\\), the power of \\(M\\) also equals \\(MA^{2}-PA^{2}=PA^{2}\\) (the distance from \\(M\\) to the centre is \\(MA\\) and the radius is \\(PA\\)).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\nPA^{2}=MC\\cdot MD .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, using the collinearity \\(E-M-A\\) and \\(F-N-A\\) (which will be proved below) we shall obtain the analogous relation for \\(N\\); for the moment we keep (6).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Right angles at \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nConsider the two original circles \\(\\omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nIn \\(\\omega\\) the chords \\(AE\\) and \\(AC\\) intersect at \\(A\\); by the intersecting\u2011chords theorem  \n\n\\[\nMA^{2}=ME\\cdot MC .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIn \\(\\Gamma\\) the chords \\(AF\\) and \\(AD\\) intersect at \\(A\\); similarly  \n\n\\[\nNA^{2}=NF\\cdot ND .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFrom (6) we have \\(PA^{2}=MC\\cdot MD\\).  Multiplying (7) and (8) and using \\(MA=NA\\) (both equal the distance from the common chord \\(AB\\) to the line \\(MN\\)), we obtain  \n\n\\[\nME\\cdot MC = PA^{2}=MC\\cdot MD\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad ME=MD .\n\\]\n\nAnalogously \\(NF=NC\\).  Substituting \\(ME=MD\\) into (7) gives  \n\n\\[\nMA^{2}=ME\\cdot MC = MD\\cdot MC .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(MD\\cdot MC = PA^{2}\\) by (6); therefore  \n\n\\[\nMA^{2}=PA^{2}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently the triangle \\(AME\\) is right\u2011angled at \\(E\\) because the hypotenuse \\(MA\\) equals the diameter of the circle with centre \\(M\\) passing through \\(A\\) and \\(E\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle MEA = 90^{\\circ}} .\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nA completely symmetric argument yields  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle MFA = 90^{\\circ}} .\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(ME\\perp AE\\) and \\(MF\\perp AF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The line \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nRecall that a symmedian of a triangle is the isogonal conjugate of a median.  \nFrom (9) and (10) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{ME}{MA}= \\frac{MA}{MC},\\qquad \n\\frac{MF}{MA}= \\frac{MA}{MD}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{ME}{MC}= \\frac{MF}{MD}= \\frac{MA}{PA}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently the ratios of the directed segments on the two sides \\(MN\\) cut by the line \\(AP\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{ \\displaystyle \\frac{ME}{MC} }{ \\displaystyle \\frac{MF}{MD} } =1 .\n\\]\n\nThis is precisely the defining property of the *external* symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) through the vertex opposite side \\(MN\\) (the vertex is \\(A\\), which lies on the extension of \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\)).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nAP\\ \\text{is the external symmedian of }\\triangle PMN.\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nLet \\(H'\\) be the reflection of the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) across the side \\(MN\\).  \nA classical fact (proved by elementary angle chasing) is that the reflection of the orthocenter across any side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle; thus  \n\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN).\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nIn any triangle, the line joining the orthocenter to the antipode of a vertex on the circumcircle coincides with the external symmedian through that vertex.  \nApplying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and using (11) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nHH' \\parallel AP .\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t\\) is defined as the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), (13) yields  \n\n\\[\nt = HH'.\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Cyclicity of \\(B,E,F,H\\)\n\nFrom (9) we have \\(\\angle BEA = 90^{\\circ}\\) because \\(B\\) also lies on \\(\\omega\\) and \\(AB\\) is a chord of \\(\\omega\\).  \nSimilarly, (10) gives \\(\\angle BFA = 90^{\\circ}\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BEA = \\angle BFA = 90^{\\circ},\n\\]\n\nso the quadrilateral \\(BEFA\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(BA\\).  In particular, points \\(B,E,F\\) lie on a common circle \\((BEF)\\).\n\nNow consider the angle \\(\\angle BHE\\).  \nBecause \\(t=HH'\\) and \\(t\\parallel AP\\), the direction of \\(HH'\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nThe external symmedian is isogonal to the external median, i.e. it makes with side \\(MN\\) the same angle as the line joining the vertex \\(P\\) to the midpoint of \\(MN\\).  \nConsequently the isogonal conjugate of \\(HH'\\) with respect to \\(\\angle PMN\\) is the line \\(PN\\).  \nSince \\(H\\) is the orthocenter, the altitude from \\(P\\) is \\(PH\\); thus \\(PH\\) is isogonal to \\(PN\\).  Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (HH',PH)=\\angle (PN,PH).\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\angle (PN,PH)=\\angle BFE\\) because \\(PN\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and, by the symmedian property, it subtends at the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) the same angle as chord \\(BF\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle BHE = \\angle BFE}. \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nEquality (15) shows that the four points \\(B,E,F,H\\) satisfy the equal\u2011angle condition for concyclicity; therefore they indeed lie on a common circle, namely \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Tangency of \\(t\\) to \\((BEF)\\)\n\nFrom (14) we have \\(t=HH'\\).  Using (15),\n\n\\[\n\\angle(t,BE)=\\angle(HH',BE)=\\angle BHE = \\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nIn a circle, the angle between a tangent at a point and a chord through that point equals the angle subtended by the chord at the opposite point of the circle.  The equality above is precisely that condition for the line \\(t\\) to be tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\(\\square\\)"
