A^{2}-PA^{2}=PA^{2}\\) (the distance from \\(M\\) to the centre is \\(MA\\) and the radius is \\(PA\\)).  \n> Hence \\(PA^{2}=MC\\cdot MD\\).\u201d\n\n**Critical Error:**  \nThe power of a point \\(X\\) with respect to a circle centred at \\(O\\) and radius \\(R\\) is \\(XO^{2}-R^{2}\\). Here the centre is \\(P\\), not \\(M\\). Therefore the power of \\(M\\) is \\(MP^{2}-PA^{2}\\), **not** \\(MA^{2}-PA^{2}\\). The claim that the power equals \\(PA^{2}\\) is false, and the resulting equality (6) is unjustified. This error invalidates the entire subsequent chain that depends on (6).\n\nBecause this is a critical error, any later step that uses (6) cannot be trusted. Nevertheless, we continue scanning for other independent issues.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Right angles at \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\n> \u201cIn \\(\\omega\\) the chords \\(AE\\) and \\(AC\\) intersect at \\(A\\); by the intersecting\u2011chords theorem \\(MA^{2}=ME\\cdot MC\\).  \n> In \\(\\Gamma\\) the chords \\(AF\\) and \\(AD\\) intersect at \\(A\\); similarly \\(NA^{2}=NF\\cdot ND\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** The intersecting\u2011chords theorem states that for two chords intersecting **inside** a circle, the products of the segments are equal: \\(AE\\cdot EC = AC\\cdot CD\\). Here the chords intersect at \\(A\\), which is an endpoint, not an interior point, so the theorem does **not** apply. Hence the equalities (7) and (8) are false. This is a **Critical Error**.\n\n> \u201cFrom (6) we have \\(PA^{2}=MC\\cdot MD\\). Multiplying (7) and (8) and using \\(MA=NA\\) \u2026 we obtain \\(ME=MD\\).\u201d\n\n**Critical Error:** Since (7) and (8) are false, the derived equality \\(ME=MD\\) is baseless. Moreover, the statement \u201c\\(MA=NA\\) (both equal the distance from the common chord \\(AB\\) to the line \\(MN\\))\u201d is unproved and generally false; the distances from the two circle centres to the chord \\(AB\\) are not necessarily equal.\n\n> \u201cConsequently the triangle \\(AME\\) is right\u2011angled at \\(E\\