s tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\nThe proof proceeds by establishing the lemmas above in full detail.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Notation and basic facts  \n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{N}>r_{M}\\);  \n\nThe circles intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).\n\nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad P\\text{ lies on the perpendicular bisector of }AD .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nDenote by \\(t\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nOur goal is to prove that \\(t\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Power of a point and a first key equality  \n\nBecause \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, the power of \\(M\\) with respect to the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) equals \\(MC\\cdot MD\\).  \nSince \\(P\\) is the centre of \\((ACD)\\), the power of \\(M\\) also equals \\(MA^{2}-PA^{2}=PA^{2}\\) (the distance from \\(M\\) to the centre is \\(MA\\) and the radius is \\(PA\\)).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\nPA^{2}=MC\\cdot MD .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, using the collinearity \\(E-M-A\\) and \\(F-N-A\\) (which will be proved below) we shall obtain the analogous relation for \\(N\\); for the moment we keep (6).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Right angles at \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nConsider