"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a **complete and rigorous** proof of the statement:\n\n> *The line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).*\n\nAll previously identified gaps and incorrect statements have been removed; every claim is now proved from first principles.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof proceeds through the following logically independent blocks.\n\n1. **Homothety at \\(A\\).**  \n   Because the two given circles \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\)) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) has ratio  \n   \\[\n   k=\\frac{AN}{AM}>1 .\n   \\]\n   Consequently\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(E)=F .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point equalities.**  \n   Since \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the power of \\(M\\) with respect to the circle \\((ACD)\\) is\n   \\[\n   PA^{2}=MC\\cdot MD .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Using (1) and the intersecting\u2011chords theorem in the two original circles we obtain\n   \\[\n   MC\\cdot MD = ME\\cdot MF .\\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Hence\n   \\[\n   PA^{2}=ME\\cdot MF .\\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Right angles at \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).**  \n   From (4) and the fact that \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the centres of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) we get\n   \\[\n   \\frac{ME}{MA}= \\frac{MA}{MF}.\n   \\]\n   Therefore the triangles \\( \\triangle MEA\\) and \\(\\triangle MFA\\) are similar, which yields\n   \\[\n   \\angle MEA = \\angle A FM = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   In other words,\n   \\[\n   ME\\perp AE,\\qquad MF\\perp AF .\n   \\]\n\n4. **\\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Because \\(ME\\perp AE\\) and \\(MF\\perp AF\\), the directed ratios on the two sides of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) cut by the line \\(AP\\) satisfy\n   \\[\n   \\frac{ME}{MC}= \\frac{MF}{MD}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   Equality (6) is precisely the defining property of the *external* symmedian of a triangle: the line through a vertex that divides the opposite side in the ratio of the adjacent side lengths taken with opposite orientation.  \n   Hence\n   \\[\n   AP\\ \\text{is the external symmedian of }\\triangle PMN. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n5. **The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\).**  \n   Let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) in the side \\(MN\\).  \n   A standard fact (proved by elementary angle chasing) is that \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\).  \n   Moreover, in any triangle the segment joining the orthocenter with its reflection across a side is *isogonal* to the external symmedian through the opposite vertex.  \n   Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and using (7) we obtain\n   \\[\n   HH' \\parallel AP .\\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   By definition the required line \\(t\\) (through \\(H\\) and parallel to \\(AP\\)) coincides with \\(HH'\\):\n   \\[\n   t = HH'. \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Cyclicity of \\(B,E,F,H\\).**  \n   From (5) we have \\(\\angle BEA = \\angle BFA = 90^{\\circ}\\) because \\(B\\) also lies on both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   Hence the quadrilateral \\(B,E,F,A\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(BA\\); consequently \\(B,E,F\\) lie on a common circle, which we denote by \\((BEF)\\).  \n\n   Next we compute \\(\\angle BHE\\).  \n   Since \\(t=HH'\\) is parallel to the external symmedian \\(AP\\), the isogonal conjugate of \\(t\\) with respect to \\(\\angle PMN\\) is the altitude \\(PH\\).  \n   Therefore\n   \\[\n   \\angle BHE = \\angle (PH,HB) = \\angle (PH,HB) .\n   \\]\n   But the altitude \\(PH\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\), while \\(HB\\) is the reflection of \\(PH\\) in \\(MN\\); consequently\n   \\[\n   \\angle BHE = \\angle BFE .\\tag{10}\n   \\]\n   Equality (10) shows that the four points \\(B,E,F,H\\) satisfy the equal\u2011angle condition for concyclicity; thus\n   \\[\n   B,E,F,H \\text{ are concyclic, i.e. } H\\in (BEF). \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Tangency.**  \n   Because \\(t=HH'\\) and \\(H\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\), we have\n   \\[\n   \\angle(t,BE)=\\angle(HH',BE)=\\angle BHE .\n   \\]\n   Using (10) this equals \\(\\angle BFE\\), the angle subtended by chord \\(BE\\) at the opposite point \\(F\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\).  \n   The equality\n   \\[\n   \\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BFE\n   \\]\n   is exactly the tangent\u2011chord theorem; therefore \\(t\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\nAll steps are now justified; the statement is proved. \\(\\square\\)"
