ng (3) unfounded.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f4 (Right angles at \\(E\\) and \\(F\\))\n- **Statement:** Using (4) \\(PA^{2}=ME\\cdot MF\\) and the known radii \\(ME=r, MF=R\\), the solution claims triangles \\(MEA\\) and \\(AMF\\) are similar, leading to \\(\\angle MEA = \\angle A FM = 90^{\\circ}\\).\n- **Critical Error:**  \n  1. The equality \\(PA^{2}=ME\\cdot MF\\) itself rests on Lemma\u202f2 and Lemma\u202f3, both already shown to be false.  \n  2. Even assuming (4) held, the similarity argument is incorrect: from \\(ME\\cdot MA = MA\\cdot MF\\) one only gets \\(ME = MF\\), not the proportionality of two sides needed for SAS similarity. The step \u201c\\(\\frac{ME}{MA}= \\frac{MA}{MF}\\)\u201d does not follow from the previous equality.  \n  3. Consequently the conclusion that \\(\\angle MEA\\) and \\(\\angle A FM\\) are right angles is unjustified.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f5 (External symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\))\n- **Statement:** \u201cFrom (5) we have \\(\\frac{ME}{MC}= \\frac{MF}{MD}\\); therefore \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\u201d\n- **Critical Error:**  \n  The ratio \\(\\frac{ME}{MC}= \\frac{MF}{MD}\\) was never established; it was claimed to follow from the (false) right\u2011angle equalities of Lemma\u202f4. Moreover, even if such a ratio held, the identification of \\(AP\\) as an external symmedian would require a precise argument linking the ratio of the segments on side \\(MN\\) to the direction of \\(AP\\); this is omitted. Hence Lemma\u202f5 rests on a non\u2011existent foundation.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f6 (Reflection of the orthocenter)\n- **Statement:** \u201cThe reflection \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) lies on \\((PMN)\\); moreover \\(HH'\\) is isogonal to the external symmedian, so \\(HH'\\parallel AP\\).\u201d\n- **Partial correctness:**  \n  The fact that the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle is a standard result, so (8) is true.  \n- **Critical 