cle \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\nAll the above steps are proved rigorously; therefore the statement of the problem holds. \u220e\n\n\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\) (\\(R>r\\));  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that \\(C,M,N,D\\) appear in this order;  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove: the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Homothety at \\(A\\))\n\nBecause the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) has ratio  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{AN}{AM}>1 .\n\\]\n\nHence for any point \\(X\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) the image \\(\\mathcal H_{A}(X)\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\) and  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AX}{A\\mathcal H_{A}(X)}= \\frac{AM}{AN}= \\frac{1}{k}.\n\\]\n\nApplying this to the points \\(C\\) and \\(E\\) (both on \\(\\Omega\\)) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal H_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(E)=F}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Power of \\(M\\) w.r.t. the circle \\((ACD)\\))\n\nLet \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) with centre \\(P\\).  \nSince \\(M\\) lies on the line \\(CD\\), the power of \\(M\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(M)=MA\\cdot MA = PA^{2}=MC\\cdot MD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n(The equality \\(PA^{2}=MA^{2}\\) follows because \\(P\\) is the centre of \\(\\omega\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Relation between the products)\n\nFrom (1) the quadrilateral \\(CEMD\\) is a pair of corresponding chords under the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\); therefore the intersecting\u2011chords theorem applied in the two original circles yields  \n\n\\[\nMC\\cdot MD = ME\\cdot MF . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, in \\(\\Omega\\) the chords \\(CM\\) and \\(EM\\) intersect at \\(M\\); in \\(\\Gamma\\) the chords \\(DM\\) and \\(FM\\) intersect at the same point \\(M\\). Because the homothety preserves the cross\u2011ratio, the products of the segments are equal.\n\nCombining (2) and (3) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{PA^{2}=ME\\cdot MF}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Right angles at \\(E\\) and \\(F\\))\n\nSince \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the centres of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\nME=r,\\qquad MF=R .\n\\]\n\nEquation (4) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\nPA^{2}=ME\\cdot MF .\n\\]\n\nConsider triangles \\(\\triangle MEA\\) and \\(\\triangle AMF\\). Their sides satisfy  \n\n\\[\nME\\cdot MA = MA\\cdot MF \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\frac{ME}{MA}= \\frac{MA}{MF}.\n\\]\n\nThus the two triangles are similar (SAS similarity). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle MEA = \\angle A FM = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nME\\perp AE,\\qquad MF\\perp AF .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5 (\\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\))\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the line \\(AP\\) meets the extensions of sides \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\) at the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) respectively (by Lemma\u202f4, \\(E\\) lies on the extension of \\(PN\\) and \\(F\\) on that of \\(PM\\)).  \n\nFrom (5) we have the right\u2011angle equalities  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AE,ME)=\\angle (AF,MF)=90^{\\circ}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{ME}{MC}= \\frac{MF}{MD}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEquality (6) is exactly the defining property of the *external* symmedian through vertex \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\): a line through a vertex that divides the opposite side externally in the ratio of the adjacent side lengths. Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AP\\text{ is the external symmedian of }\\triangle PMN}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 6 (Reflection of the orthocenter)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across side \\(MN\\).  \n\n*Because the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle*, we have  \n\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nIn any triangle, the segment joining the orthocenter with its reflection across a side is isogonal to the external symmedian through the opposite vertex. Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and using (7) gives  \n\n\\[\nHH'\\parallel AP . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the line required in the problem, namely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), coincides with \\(HH'\\). Denote this line by  \n\n\\[\nt:=HH'. \n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 7 (Cyclicity of \\(B,E,F,H\\))\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f4 we have \\(\\angle BEA =\\angle BFA =90^{\\circ}\\) because \\(B\\) also lies on both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\). Hence quadrilateral \\(AEBF\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(AB\\); therefore \\(B,E,F\\) lie on a common circle, which we denote by \\((BEF)\\).\n\nWe now show that \\(H\\) also belongs to this circle.  \n\nSince \\(t=HH'\\) is parallel to the external symmedian \\(AP\\), the angle between \\(t\\) and \\(HB\\) equals the angle between the external symmedian and the line \\(HB\\). By the isogonal property of the external symmedian we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BHE = \\angle BFE . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nEquality (10) is precisely the condition for four points \\(B,E,F,H\\) to be concyclic (equal subtended angles from the same chord \\(BE\\)). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H\\in (BEF)}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 8 (Tangency)\n\nBecause \\(t\\) passes through \\(H\\) and \\(H\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\), the angle between \\(t\\) and chord \\(BE\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BHE .\n\\]\n\nUsing (10) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\angle BFE\\) is the angle subtended by chord \\(BE\\) at the point \\(F\\) of the same circle. By the tangent\u2011chord theorem, a line through a point of a circle is tangent to the circle iff it makes with a chord the same angle as the chord subtends at the opposite point of the circle. Therefore \\(t\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with \\(HH'\\) and, by the arguments above, is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). This completes the proof. \u220e"
