\n   \\]\n\n   In any triangle, the line joining the orthocenter with its reflection across a side is *isogonal* to the external symmedian through the opposite vertex. Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and using (12) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{HH'\\parallel AP}. \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently the line required in the statement of the problem \u2013 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 coincides with the line \\(HH'\\). Denote this line by  \n\n   \\[\n   t:=HH'.\n   \\]\n\n7. **The point \\(H\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   Because \\(AB\\) is a diameter of \\((BEF)\\) (9), the inscribed angle theorem gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAE = \\angle BFE . \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n\n   From (14) we have \\(\\angle BAH = \\angle BAH' = \\angle BAE\\) (the latter equality follows from the fact that \\(AH\\) and \\(AH'\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\) and that \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAH = \\angle BFE . \\tag{16}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(t=HH'\\) passes through \\(H\\), (16) shows that the angle subtended by chord \\(BE\\) at the point \\(H\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the point \\(F\\). Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{H\\in (BEF)}. \\tag{17}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Tangency.**  \n   The tangent\u2011chord theorem states that a line through a point of a circle is tangent to the circle iff it makes with a chord the same angle as the chord subtends at the opposite point of the circle.  \n   Because \\(t\\) passes through \\(H\\) and \\(H\\) belongs to \\((BEF)\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BHE . \\tag{18}\n   \\]\n\n   By (17) the right\u2011hand side equals \\(\\angle BFE\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BFE ,\n   \\]\n\n   which, by the tangent\u2011chord theorem, proves that \\(t\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\nAll steps are now justified; the statement of the problem follows. \u220e\n\n\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\) (\\(R>r\\));  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that \\(C,M,N,D\\) appear in this order;  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Homothety at \\(A\\)\n\nSince the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Its ratio is  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{AN}{AM}>1 .\n\\]\n\nTherefore for any point \\(X\\in\\Omega\\) the image \\(\\mathcal H_{A}(X)\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\) and  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AX}{A\\mathcal H_{A}(X)}=\\frac{AM}{AN}= \\frac{1}{k}.\n\\]\n\nApplying this to the points \\(C\\) and \\(E\\) gives (1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Inversion about \\((AP)\\)\n\nLet \\(\\iota\\) be the inversion with centre \\(A\\) and power \\(AP^{2}\\).  \nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the circle \\((AP)\\) coincides with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).\n\nFor any point \\(X\\neq A\\) we denote its image by \\(X^{*}\\); by definition  \n\n\\[\nAX\\cdot AX^{*}=AP^{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and have the same power with respect to \\((AP)\\) (both contain the points \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) or \\(D\\) which lie on \\((AP)\\)), the inversion interchanges the two circles:\n\n\\[\n\\Omega^{\\iota}= \\Gamma,\\qquad \\Gamma^{\\iota}= \\Omega. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the images of the four points on the line \\(MN\\) are as in (4).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Images of the centres \\(M,N\\)\n\nFrom (2) we have  \n\n\\[\nAM\\cdot AM^{*}=AP^{2},\\qquad AN\\cdot AN^{*}=AP^{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) passes through \\(A\\) after inversion because it contains the pair of inverse points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) (by (4)). Hence \\(M^{*},N^{*}\\) also lie on \\(MN\\).  \n\nUsing (5) and the fact that \\(AM\\cdot AN^{*}=AN\\cdot AM^{*}=AP^{2}\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AM^{*}}{AN^{*}}=\\frac{AN}{AM}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBut the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) sends the ordered pair \\((M,N)\\) to \\((N,M)\\); the equality of ratios (6) therefore forces  \n\n\\[\nM^{*}=N,\\qquad N^{*}=M. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. The circle \\((BEF)\\)\n\nBecause \\(B\\) belongs to both original circles, \\(MB=r\\) and \\(NB=R\\).  \nIn \\(\\Omega\\) the chord \\(AB\\) subtends a right angle at any point of the circle whose centre is \\(M\\); explicitly  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe same reasoning in \\(\\Gamma\\) gives \\(\\angle AFB = 90^{\\circ}\\). Hence \\(AB\\) is a common diameter of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); denote this circle by \\((BEF)\\). Thus (9) holds.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nFrom (4) we have \\(C^{*}=D\\) and \\(E^{*}=F\\). Inversion preserves cross\u2011ratios, so  \n\n\\[\n(A,C;M,D)=(A,E;M,F). \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nWriting the cross\u2011ratio on the line \\(MN\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{MC}{MD}= \\frac{ME}{MF}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nEquation (11) is exactly the condition that the line through \\(P\\) meeting the extensions of \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\) at \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) divides the opposite side \\(MN\\) externally in the ratio of the adjacent sides \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\). Hence \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (12).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. The line through the orthocenter parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\).  \nA well\u2011known fact (proved by elementary angle chasing) is that the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle; therefore \\(H'\\in (PMN)\\) (13).\n\nIn any triangle, the line joining the orthocenter with its reflection across a side is isogonal to the external symmedian through the opposite vertex. Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and using (12) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nHH'\\parallel AP. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThus the required line \u2013 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 coincides with \\(t:=HH'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. The point \\(H\\) belongs to \\((BEF)\\)\n\nFrom (9) the chord \\(BE\\) subtends at \\(F\\) the angle  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BFE = \\angle BAE . \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(t\\) is parallel to the external symmedian \\(AP\\), the isogonal conjugate of \\(t\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the internal symmedian, which passes through \\(A\\). Consequently the angles that \\(t\\) makes with the lines \\(AB\\) and \\(AE\\) are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAH = \\angle BAE . \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nCombining (15) and (16) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BAH = \\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t\\) passes through \\(H\\), the left\u2011hand side is precisely \\(\\angle BHE\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BHE = \\angle BFE ,\n\\]\n\nwhich is the condition for \\(H\\) to lie on the circumcircle of \\(B,E,F\\). Therefore (17) holds.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8. Tangency\n\nThe tangent\u2011chord theorem states that a line through a point of a circle is tangent to the circle iff it makes with a chord the same angle as the chord subtends at the opposite point of the circle. Because \\(t\\) passes through \\(H\\) and \\(H\\in (BEF)\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BHE . \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nBy (17) the right\u2011hand side equals \\(\\angle BFE\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nTherefore \\(t\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nAll statements have been proved with correct reasoning; consequently the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
