n sends \\(M\\) to \\(N\\). The argument conflates a homothety with an inversion and lacks any justification.\n* **Location:** \u201cIn \\(\\Omega\\) the centre \\(M\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of chord \\(AB\\); therefore the angle subtended by \\(AB\\) at any point of \\(\\Omega\\) is a right angle: \\(\\angle AEB = 90^{\\circ}\\).\u201d\n  * **Issue:** Critical Error \u2013 the fact that \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\) only guarantees that \\(\\angle A B\\) subtended at the *centre* is \\(2\\angle AEB\\); it does **not** imply that \\(\\angle AEB = 90^{\\circ}\\). The right\u2011angle property holds only for points on the *circle with diameter \\(AB\\)*, not for arbitrary points on \\(\\Omega\\). Hence the claim that \\(AB\\) is a diameter of the circumcircle of \\(BEF\\) is false.\n* **Location:** \u201cInversion preserves cross\u2011ratios, so \\((A,C;M,D)=(A,E;M,F)\\).\u201d\n  * **Issue:** Critical Error \u2013 cross\u2011ratio preservation under inversion requires the four points to be *concyclic* (or collinear) **and** the inversion centre to lie on the circle (or line). Here the quadruples \\((A,C,M,D)\\) and \\((A,E,M,F)\\) are not all on a common circle, so the equality of cross\u2011ratios is not guaranteed.\n* **Location:** \u201cEquality (11) is precisely the condition that the line through \\(P\\) meeting the extensions of the two sides divides the opposite side \\(MN\\) externally in the ratio of the adjacent sides \u2026 This is the definition of the external symmedian through vertex \\(P\\).\u201d\n  * **Issue:** Justification Gap \u2013 even if (11) were correct, the step that identifies the line through \\(P\\) (i.e. \\(AP\\)) as the *external* symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is not proved; the relationship between the ratio \\(\\frac{MD}{MC}\\) and the side lengths \\(PN,PM\\) is asserted without derivation.\n* **Location:** \u201cLemma. In any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle