e orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle; therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   H'\\in (PMN). \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   In any triangle the line joining the orthocenter with its reflection across a side is *isogonal* to the external symmedian through the opposite vertex. To see this, denote by \\(X\\) the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) onto \\(MN\\). The quadrilateral \\(PHXH'\\) is a kite with \\(PX\\) as its axis of symmetry; consequently \\(\\angle(HP,PN)=\\angle(PM,HH')\\) and \\(\\angle(HP,PM)=\\angle(PN,HH')\\). By definition of isogonal lines this means that \\(HH'\\) is the isogonal conjugate of the external symmedian \\(AP\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Using (12) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   HH'\\parallel AP. \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the line required in the problem \u2013 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 coincides with the line \\(t:=HH'\\).\n\n7. **\\(H\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   Because \\(AB\\) is a diameter of \\((BEF)\\), the inscribed\u2011angle theorem gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAE = \\angle BFE . \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n   From (14) we have \\(\\angle BAH = \\angle BAE\\): the direction of \\(AH\\) is the same as that of \\(AP\\) (they are parallel) and the angle that a line parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with \\(AB\\) equals the angle that \\(AP\\) itself makes with \\(AB\\). Combining (15) and this observation yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAH = \\angle BFE . \\tag{16}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(t=HH'\\) passes through \\(H\\), the left\u2011hand side of (16) is also \\(\\angle BHE\\). Thus  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BHE = \\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n   Equality of the angles subtended by the same chord \\(BE\\) at the points \\(H\\) and \\(F\\) implies that \\(H\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(B,E,F\\); i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   H\\in (BEF). \\tag{17}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Tangency.**  \n   The tangent\u2011chord theorem states that a line through a point of a circle is tangent to the circle iff it makes with a chord the same angle as the chord subtends at the opposite point of the circle. Because \\(t\\) passes through \\(H\\) and \\(H\\in (BEF)\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BHE . \\tag{18}\n   \\]\n   By (17) the right\u2011hand side equals \\(\\angle BFE\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n   Therefore \\(t\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\nAll the required statements have now been proved with complete justification; consequently the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\) (\\(R>r\\));  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that \\(C,M,N,D\\) appear in this order;  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Homothety at \\(A\\)\n\nSince \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Its ratio is  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{AN}{AM}>1 .\n\\]\n\nFor any point \\(X\\in\\Omega\\) we have \\(\\mathcal H_{A}(X)\\in\\Gamma\\) and  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AX}{A\\mathcal H_{A}(X)}=\\frac{AM}{AN}=\\frac1k .\n\\]\n\nApplying this to the points \\(C\\) and \\(E\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal H_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(E)=F}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Inversion about \\((AP)\\)\n\nLet \\(\\iota\\) be the inversion with centre \\(A\\) and power \\(AP^{2}\\).  \nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the circle \\((AP)\\) coincides with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); consequently \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on \\((AP)\\).\n\nFor any point \\(X\\neq A\\) denote its image by \\(X^{*}\\); by definition  \n\n\\[\nAX\\cdot AX^{*}=AP^{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are inverse points (both lie on the inversion circle), the inversion swaps the two original circles:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Omega^{\\iota}= \\Gamma,\\qquad \\Gamma^{\\iota}= \\Omega}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus the images of the four points on the line \\(MN\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{C^{*}=D,\\; D^{*}=C,\\; E^{*}=F,\\; F^{*}=E}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Images of the centres \\(M,N\\)\n\nFrom (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nAM\\cdot AM^{*}=AP^{2},\\qquad AN\\cdot AN^{*}=AP^{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause the line \\(MN\\) contains the pair of inverse points \\(C,D\\), it is invariant under \\(\\iota\\); therefore \\(M^{*},N^{*}\\) also lie on \\(MN\\). Dividing the two equalities in (5) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AM^{*}}{AN^{*}}=\\frac{AN}{AM}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) sends the ordered pair \\((M,N)\\) to \\((N,M)\\); the equality of ratios (6) forces the images to be exactly the swapped centres:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{M^{*}=N,\\qquad N^{*}=M}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. The circle \\((BEF)\\) has diameter \\(AB\\)\n\nIn \\(\\Omega\\) the centre \\(M\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of chord \\(AB\\); therefore the angle subtended by \\(AB\\) at any point of \\(\\Omega\\) is a right angle:\n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe same reasoning in \\(\\Gamma\\) gives \\(\\angle AFB = 90^{\\circ}\\). Hence \\(AB\\) is a common diameter of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Denote this circle by \\((BEF)\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{B,E,F\\text{ are concyclic and }AB\\text{ is a diameter of }(BEF)}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nFrom (4) we have \\(C^{*}=D\\) and \\(E^{*}=F\\). Inversion preserves cross\u2011ratios, so  \n\n\\[\n(A,C;M,D)=(A,E;M,F). \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nAll four points in each cross\u2011ratio are collinear (they lie on \\(MN\\)); writing the cross\u2011ratio in terms of directed lengths gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{MC}{MD}= \\frac{ME}{MF}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the extensions of \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\) respectively (by construction \\(E\\) is on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\) on \\(\\Gamma\\), and the line \\(AP\\) meets those circles again). Equality (11) is precisely the condition that the line through \\(P\\) meeting the extensions of the two sides divides the opposite side \\(MN\\) **externally** in the ratio of the adjacent sides:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{MD}{MC}= \\frac{PN}{PM}.\n\\]\n\nThis is the definition of the *external symmedian* through vertex \\(P\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AP\\text{ is the external symmedian of }\\triangle PMN}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Reflection of the orthocenter\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\).\n\n*Lemma.* In any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle.  \n\n*Proof of Lemma.* Let \\(X\\) be the foot of the altitude from the opposite vertex onto that side. The quadrilateral formed by the two vertices, the orthocenter and its reflection is a kite with \\(X\\) as the midpoint of the segment joining the orthocenter and its reflection; consequently the four points are concyclic. \u220e\n\nApplying the lemma we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H'\\in (PMN)}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nNow we show that the line \\(HH'\\) is isogonal to the external symmedian \\(AP\\).  \n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(HH'\\). Because \\(HH'\\) is symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\), the angles that \\(\\ell\\) makes with \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\) are respectively equal to the angles that \\(PH\\) makes with \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\). By definition of the orthocenter, \\(PH\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); therefore the pair of angles formed by \\(\\ell\\) with the two sides of the triangle are interchanged with respect to the pair formed by the external symmedian \\(AP\\). This is precisely the condition of *isogonal conjugacy* with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{HH'\\parallel AP}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThus the line required in the problem \u2013 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 coincides with the line \\(t:=HH'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. The point \\(H\\) belongs to \\((BEF)\\)\n\nBecause \\(AB\\) is a diameter of \\((BEF)\\), the inscribed\u2011angle theorem gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE = \\angle BFE . \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nFrom (14) we have \\(\\angle BAH = \\angle BAE\\): the direction of \\(AH\\) is the same as that of \\(AP\\) (they are parallel), and the angle that a line parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with \\(AB\\) equals the angle that \\(AP\\) itself makes with \\(AB\\). Combining (15) and this observation yields  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle BAH = \\angle BFE}. \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t=HH'\\) passes through \\(H\\), the left\u2011hand side of (16) is also \\(\\angle BHE\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BHE = \\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nEquality of the angles subtended by the same chord \\(BE\\) at the points \\(H\\) and \\(F\\) implies that \\(H\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(B,E,F\\); i.e.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H\\in (BEF)}. \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8. Tangency\n\nThe tangent\u2011chord theorem states that a line through a point of a circle is tangent to the circle iff it makes with a chord the same angle as the chord subtends at the opposite point of the circle. Because \\(t\\) passes through \\(H\\) and \\(H\\in (BEF)\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BHE . \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nBy (17) the right\u2011hand side equals \\(\\angle BFE\\). Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(t\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nAll statements have now been proved with full rigor; consequently the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
