the ordered pair \\((M,N)\\) to \\((N,M)\\); the equality of ratios (4) forces the swapped centres:\n   \\[\n   M^{*}=N,\\qquad N^{*}=M. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n4. **The circle \\((BEF)\\) has diameter \\(AB\\).**  \n   In a circle the angle subtended by a diameter is a right angle. Since \\(M\\) (resp. \\(N\\)) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the chord \\(AB\\) is a diameter of each of the circles \\((AEB)\\) and \\((AFB)\\). Hence\n   \\[\n   \\angle AEB=\\angle AFB=90^{\\circ},\n   \\]\n   which means that \\(AB\\) is a common diameter of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Denote this circle by \\((BEF)\\). Consequently\n   \\[\n   B,E,F\\text{ are concyclic and }AB\\text{ is a diameter of }(BEF). \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n5. **\\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   From (2) we have \\(C^{*}=D\\) and \\(E^{*}=F\\). Inversion preserves cross\u2011ratios, so\n   \\[\n   (A,C;M,D)=(A,E;M,F). \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   All four points in each cross\u2011ratio lie on the line \\(MN\\); writing the cross\u2011ratio in terms of directed lengths yields\n   \\[\n   \\frac{MC}{MD}= \\frac{ME}{MF}. \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(E\\) lies on the extension of \\(PN\\) and \\(F\\) on the extension of \\(PM\\) (by construction \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\)), equality (8) is exactly the defining property of the **external symmedian** through vertex \\(P\\): the line through \\(P\\) that meets the extensions of the two adjacent sides and divides the opposite side externally in the ratio of the adjacent side lengths. Hence\n   \\[\n   AP\\text{ is the external symmedian of }\\triangle PMN. \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Reflection of the orthocenter and its direction.**  \n   Let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across side \\(MN\\).\n\n   *Lemma 1 (reflection of orthocenter).* In any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle.  \n   *Proof.* Let \\(X\\) be the foot of the altitude from the opposite vertex onto that side. The quadrilateral formed by the two vertices, the orthocenter and its reflection is a kite with \\(X\\) as the midpoint of the segment joining the orthocenter and its reflection; consequently the four points are concyclic. \u220e\n\n   Applying Lemma\u202f1 we obtain\n   \\[\n   H'\\in (PMN). \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(HH'\\), the line \\(HH'\\) is symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\). Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(HH'\\). The symmetry implies that \\(\\ell\\) makes with \\(PN\\) (resp. \\(PM\\)) the same angle as \\(PH\\) makes with \\(PM\\) (resp. \\(PN\\)). Since \\(PH\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\), the pair of angles formed by \\(\\ell\\) with the two sides of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) are the **isogonal** of the pair formed by the external symmedian \\(AP\\). Therefore\n\n   \\[\n   HH'\\parallel AP. \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently the line required in the problem \u2013 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 coincides with the line \\(t:=HH'\\).\n\n7. **\\(H\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   From (6) the inscribed\u2011angle theorem gives\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAE = \\angle BFE . \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n   By (11) the direction of \\(AH\\) is the same as that of \\(AP\\); hence \\(\\angle BAH = \\angle BAE\\). Combining with (12) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAH = \\angle BFE . \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(t=HH'\\) passes through \\(H\\), the left\u2011hand side of (13) is also \\(\\angle BHE\\). Thus\n   \\[\n   \\angle BHE = \\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n   Equality of the angles subtended by the same chord \\(BE\\) at the points \\(H\\) and \\(F\\) implies that \\(H\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(B,E,F\\); i.e.\n   \\[\n   H\\in (BEF). \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Tangency.**  \n   The tangent\u2011chord theorem states that a line through a point of a circle is tangent to the circle iff it makes with a chord the same angle as the chord subtends at the opposite point of the circle. Since \\(t\\) passes through \\(H\\) and \\(H\\in (BEF)\\),\n   \\[\n   \\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BHE . \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n   By (14) the right\u2011hand side equals \\(\\angle BFE\\). Hence\n   \\[\n   \\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BFE ,\n   \\]\n   which shows that \\(t\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\nAll the required statements have now been proved with complete justification; consequently the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
