            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\), and \\(\\Gamma\\) the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\) (\\(r<R\\)).  \nThe circles intersect at distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n\\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \n\\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove: the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 1. Homothety centred at \\(A\\)\n\nSince the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nIts ratio is\n\\[\nk=\\frac{AN}{AM}>1 .\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A}(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(E)=F. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 2. Inversion about \\((AP)\\)\n\nLet \\(\\iota\\) be the inversion with centre \\(A\\) and power \\(AP^{2}\\).  \nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the circle \\((AP)\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); consequently \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the inversion circle and are exchanged by \\(\\iota\\):\n\\[\nC^{*}=D,\\qquad D^{*}=C. \\tag{2a}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) and the fact that \\(\\iota\\) maps each point of \\(\\Omega\\) to a point of \\(\\Gamma\\) (and conversely) we obtain\n\\[\n\\Omega^{\\iota}= \\Gamma,\\qquad \\Gamma^{\\iota}= \\Omega,\\qquad\nE^{*}=F,\\qquad F^{*}=E. \\tag{2b}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 3. Images of the centres \\(M,N\\)\n\nFor any point \\(X\\) we have \\(AX\\cdot AX^{*}=AP^{2}\\). Applying this to \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) gives\n\\[\nAM\\cdot AM^{*}=AP^{2},\\qquad AN\\cdot AN^{*}=AP^{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C,D\\) are inverse of each other and lie on the line \\(MN\\), the line \\(MN\\) is invariant under \\(\\iota\\); hence the images \\(M^{*},N^{*}\\) also lie on \\(MN\\). Dividing the two equalities in (3) yields\n\\[\n\\frac{AM^{*}}{AN^{*}}=\\frac{AN}{AM}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) sends the ordered pair \\((M,N)\\) to \\((N,M)\\); the only points on \\(MN\\) satisfying (4) are the swapped centres, therefore\n\\[\nM^{*}=N,\\qquad N^{*}=M. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 4. The circle \\((BEF)\\) has diameter \\(AB\\)\n\nBecause \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\), the chord \\(AB\\) subtends a right angle at any point of \\(\\Omega\\); in particular\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{6a}\n\\]\nSimilarly, since \\(N\\) is the centre of \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\\[\n\\angle AFB = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{6b}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(AB\\) is a diameter of both circumcircles of \\(\\triangle AEB\\) and \\(\\triangle AFB\\). Consequently the three points \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic and \\(AB\\) is a diameter of that circle. Denote this circle by \\((BEF)\\). Hence\n\\[\nB,E,F\\text{ are concyclic and }AB\\text{ is a diameter of }(BEF). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 5. \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nFrom (2a) and (2b) we have the inverse pairs \\(C\\leftrightarrow D\\) and \\(E\\leftrightarrow F\\). Inversion preserves cross\u2011ratios, therefore\n\\[\n(A,C;M,D)=(A,E;M,F). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAll four points in each cross\u2011ratio lie on the line \\(MN\\); writing the cross\u2011ratio in terms of directed lengths gives\n\\[\n\\frac{MC}{MD}= \\frac{ME}{MF}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBy construction, \\(E\\) lies on the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\); because \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\((AP)\\), the ray \\(PA\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and the ray \\(PN\\) (the extension of side \\(PN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)) passes through \\(E\\). Similarly, \\(F\\) lies on the extension of side \\(PM\\). Hence (8) states that the line \\(AP\\) meets the extensions of the two adjacent sides of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and divides the opposite side \\(MN\\) externally in the ratio of the adjacent side lengths \\(|PM|:|PN|\\). This is precisely the definition of the **external symmedian** through vertex \\(P\\). Consequently\n\\[\nAP\\text{ is the external symmedian of }\\triangle PMN. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 6. Relation between the orthocenter and the external symmedian\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across side \\(MN\\).\n\n*Lemma 1.* In any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle.\n\n*Proof of Lemma 1.* Let \\(X\\) be the foot of the altitude from the opposite vertex onto that side. The quadrilateral formed by the two vertices, the orthocenter and its reflection is a kite with \\(X\\) as the midpoint of the segment joining the orthocenter and its reflection; the equal angles at \\(X\\) imply that the four points are concyclic. \u220e\n\nApplying Lemma\u202f1 we obtain\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN). \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(HH'\\), the line \\(HH'\\) is symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\). In a triangle, the internal symmedian is the isogonal conjugate of the external symmedian. The line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(HH'\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); therefore it is isogonal to the external symmedian \\(AP\\). Hence\n\\[\nHH'\\parallel AP. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThus the line required in the statement \u2013 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 coincides with the line\n\\[\nt:=HH'.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 7. The orthocenter \\(H\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\)\n\nFrom (6) the inscribed\u2011angle theorem yields\n\\[\n\\angle BAE = \\angle BFE . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (11), the direction of \\(AH\\) equals the direction of \\(AP\\); consequently\n\\[\n\\angle BAH = \\angle BAE . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nCombining (12) and (13) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle BAH = \\angle BFE . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(t=HH'\\) passes through \\(H\\); therefore \\(\\angle BAH = \\angle BHE\\). Equation (14) becomes\n\\[\n\\angle BHE = \\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nEquality of the angles subtended by the same chord \\(BE\\) at the points \\(H\\) and \\(F\\) implies that \\(H\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(B,E,F\\). Hence\n\\[\nH\\in (BEF). \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 8. Tangency of the required line\n\nThe tangent\u2011chord theorem states that a line through a point of a circle is tangent to the circle iff it makes with a chord the same angle as the chord subtends at the opposite point of the circle. Since \\(t\\) passes through \\(H\\) and \\(H\\in (BEF)\\), we have\n\\[\n\\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BHE . \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nBy (15) the right\u2011hand side equals \\(\\angle BFE\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(t\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(B\\). Because \\(t\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), the statement of the problem is proved. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
