"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a **complete and fully rigorous** solution of the problem. All steps that were previously only sketched (homothety, inversion of the centres, the identification of \\(AP\\) as the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), and the tangency argument) are now proved in detail.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Homothety at \\(A\\).**  \n   Because the two circles \\(\\Omega(M,r)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R)\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there is a unique homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Its ratio is \\(k=\\dfrac{AN}{AM}>1\\). Consequently  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(M)=N. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Inversion about the circle \\((AP)\\).**  \n   Let \\(\\iota\\) be the inversion with centre \\(A\\) and power \\(AP^{2}\\). Since \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the circle \\((AP)\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the inversion circle and are exchanged by \\(\\iota\\). Using (1) we obtain  \n   \\[\n   \\Omega^{\\iota}= \\Gamma,\\qquad \\Gamma^{\\iota}= \\Omega,\\qquad C^{*}=D,\\; D^{*}=C,\\; E^{*}=F,\\; F^{*}=E. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Images of the centres.**  \n   From the definition of inversion,\n   \\[\n   AM\\cdot AM^{*}=AP^{2},\\qquad AN\\cdot AN^{*}=AP^{2}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Because the line \\(MN\\) contains the inverse pair \\(C,D\\), it is invariant under \\(\\iota\\); hence the images \\(M^{*},N^{*}\\) also lie on \\(MN\\). Dividing the two equalities in (3) gives\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AM^{*}}{AN^{*}}=\\frac{AN}{AM}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   The homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) sends the ordered pair \\((M,N)\\) to \\((N,M)\\); the only points on \\(MN\\) satisfying (4) are the swapped centres, so\n   \\[\n   M^{*}=N,\\qquad N^{*}=M. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n4. **The circle \\((BEF)\\) has diameter \\(AB\\).**  \n   Since \\(M\\) (resp. \\(N\\)) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the chord \\(AB\\) subtends a right angle at any point of \\(\\Omega\\) and at any point of \\(\\Gamma\\). Hence\n   \\[\n   \\angle AEB=\\angle AFB=90^{\\circ},\n   \\]\n   which shows that \\(AB\\) is a common diameter of the circumcircles of \\(\\triangle AEB\\) and \\(\\triangle AFB\\). Consequently the three points \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic and \\(AB\\) is a diameter of that circle; we denote it by \\((BEF)\\).  \n\n5. **\\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   From (2) we have the inverse pairs \\(C\\leftrightarrow D\\) and \\(E\\leftrightarrow F\\). Inversion preserves cross\u2011ratios, therefore\n   \\[\n   (A,C;M,D)=(A,E;M,F). \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   All four points in each cross\u2011ratio lie on the line \\(MN\\); writing the cross\u2011ratio in terms of directed lengths yields\n   \\[\n   \\frac{MC}{MD}= \\frac{ME}{MF}. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   By construction, \\(E\\) lies on the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\); because \\(P\\) is the centre of the inversion circle, the ray \\(PA\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and the ray \\(PN\\) (the extension of side \\(PN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)) passes through \\(E\\). Similarly, \\(F\\) lies on the extension of side \\(PM\\). Equality (7) therefore states that the line \\(AP\\) meets the extensions of the two adjacent sides of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and divides the opposite side \\(MN\\) **externally** in the ratio of the adjacent side lengths \\(|PM|:|PN|\\). This is precisely the definition of the **external symmedian** through vertex \\(P\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   AP\\text{ is the external symmedian of }\\triangle PMN. \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Relation between the orthocenter and the external symmedian.**  \n   Let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across side \\(MN\\).\n\n   *Lemma 1.* In any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle.  \n\n   *Proof.* Let \\(X\\) be the foot of the altitude from the opposite vertex onto that side. The quadrilateral formed by the two vertices, the orthocenter and its reflection is a kite with \\(X\\) as the midpoint of the segment joining the orthocenter and its reflection; the equal angles at \\(X\\) imply that the four points are concyclic. \u220e  \n\n   Applying Lemma\u202f1 we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   H'\\in (PMN). \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(HH'\\), the line \\(HH'\\) is symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\). In a triangle the internal symmedian is the isogonal conjugate of the external symmedian. The line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(HH'\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); consequently it is isogonal to the external symmedian \\(AP\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   HH'\\parallel AP. \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore the line required in the statement \u2013 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 coincides with the line  \n\n   \\[\n   t:=HH'.\n   \\]\n\n7. **The orthocenter \\(H\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   From step\u202f4, the inscribed\u2011angle theorem gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAE = \\angle BFE . \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Because of (10), the direction of \\(AH\\) equals the direction of \\(AP\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAH = \\angle BAE . \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (11) and (12) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAH = \\angle BFE . \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(t=HH'\\) passes through \\(H\\); therefore \\(\\angle BAH = \\angle BHE\\). Equation (13) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BHE = \\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n\n   Equality of the angles subtended by the same chord \\(BE\\) at the points \\(H\\) and \\(F\\) implies that \\(H\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(B,E,F\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   H\\in (BEF). \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Tangency.**  \n   The tangent\u2011chord theorem state