2011chord theorem states that a line through a point of a circle is tangent to the circle iff it makes with a chord the same angle as the chord subtends at the opposite point of the circle. Since \\(t\\) passes through \\(H\\) and \\(H\\in (BEF)\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BHE . \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n\n   By (14) the right\u2011hand side equals \\(\\angle BFE\\). Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BFE ,\n   \\]\n\n   which shows that \\(t\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(B\\). Because \\(t\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), the required statement is proved. \u220e\n\n\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\) (\\(r<R\\));  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) the second intersection of line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (order \\(C-M-N-D\\));  \n* \\(D\\) the second intersection of line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Homothety centred at \\(A\\)\n\nSince the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Its ratio is  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{AN}{AM}>1 .\n\\]\n\nConsequences of \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A}(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(E)=F. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Inversion about the circle \\((AP)\\)\n\nDefine \\(\\iota\\) as the inversion with centre \\(A\\) and power \\(AP^{2}\\).  \nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the circle \\((AP)\\) is precisely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the inversion circle and are exchanged:\n\n\\[\nC^{*}=D,\\qquad D^{*}=C. \\tag{2a}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) and the fact that \\(\\iota\\) maps each point of \\(\\Omega\\) to a point of \\(\\Gamma\\) (and conversely) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\Omega^{\\iota}= \\Gamma,\\qquad \\Gamma^{\\iota}= \\Omega,\\qquad\nE^{*}=F,\\qquad F^{*}=E. \\tag{2b}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Images of the centres \\(M,N\\)\n\nFor any point \\(X\\) we have \\(AX\\cdot AX^{*}=AP^{2}\\). Applying this to \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) gives\n\n\\[\nAM\\cdot AM^{*}=AP^{2},\\qquad AN\\cdot AN^{*}=AP^{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are inverse of each other and lie on the line \\(MN\\), the line \\(MN\\) is invariant under \\(\\iota\\); consequently the images \\(M^{*},N^{*}\\) also lie on \\(MN\\). Dividing the two equalities in (3) yields\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AM^{*}}{AN^{*}}=\\frac{AN}{AM}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) sends the ordered pair \\((M,N)\\) to \\((N,M)\\); the only points on \\(MN\\) satisfying (4) are the swapped centres, therefore\n\n\\[\nM^{*}=N,\\qquad N^{*}=M. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. The circle \\((BEF)\\) has diameter \\(AB\\)\n\nBecause \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\), the chord \\(AB\\) subtends a right angle at any point of \\(\\Omega\\); in particular\n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{6a}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, \\(N\\) being the centre of \\(\\Gamma\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\angle AFB = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{6b}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(AB\\) is a diameter of both circumcircles of \\(\\triangle 