reflection is a kite with \\(X\\) as the midpoint of the segment joining the orthocenter and its reflection; the equal angles at \\(X\\) imply that the four points are concyclic. \u220e\n\nApplying Lemma\u202f1 we obtain\n\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN). \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(HH'\\), the line \\(HH'\\) is symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\). In a triangle the internal symmedian is the isogonal conjugate of the external symmedian. The line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(HH'\\) is therefore the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); consequently it is isogonal to the external symmedian \\(AP\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nHH'\\parallel AP. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThus the line required in the problem \u2013 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 coincides with the line  \n\n\\[\nt:=HH'.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. The orthocenter \\(H\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\)\n\nFrom step\u202f4, the inscribed\u2011angle theorem yields\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE = \\angle BFE . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (11), the direction of \\(AH\\) equals the direction of \\(AP\\); consequently\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAH = \\angle BAE . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nCombining (12) and (13) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAH = \\angle BFE . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(t=HH'\\) passes through \\(H\\); therefore \\(\\angle BAH = \\angle BHE\\). Equation (14) becomes\n\n\\[\n\\angle BHE = \\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nEquality of the angles subtended by the same chord \\(BE\\) at the points \\(H\\) and \\(F\\) implies that \\(H\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(B,E,F\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nH\\in (BEF). \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8. Tangency of the required line\n\nThe tangent\u2011chord theorem states that a line through a point of a circle is tangent to the circle iff it makes with a chord the same angle as the chord subtends at the opposite point of the circle. Since \\(