            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n*All notation is as introduced in the summary.*\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Homothety centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega(M,r)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R)\\) intersect at the two points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Its ratio is  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{AN}{AM}>1 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A}(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(E)=F. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Inversion with centre \\(A\\) and power \\(AM\\cdot AN\\)\n\nLet \\(\\iota\\) be the inversion about the circle \\(\\mathcal I\\) centred at \\(A\\) with radius \\(\\sqrt{AM\\cdot AN}\\).\n\n*Since \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) satisfy \\(AM\\cdot AN=AM\\cdot AN\\), they are inverse points; consequently the whole circle \\(\\Omega\\) is mapped onto \\(\\Gamma\\) and vice\u2011versa:*  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega^{\\iota}= \\Gamma,\\qquad \\Gamma^{\\iota}= \\Omega. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) and are the intersections of this line with the two circles; therefore they lie on \\(\\mathcal I\\) and are exchanged by the inversion:\n\n\\[\nC^{*}=D,\\qquad D^{*}=C. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA^{2}=PC\\cdot PD=AM\\cdot AN,\n\\]\n\nso \\(P\\) also lies on \\(\\mathcal I\\) and is fixed: \\(P^{*}=P\\).\n\nUsing (1) together with the fact that \\(\\iota\\) sends each point of \\(\\Omega\\) to a point of \\(\\Gamma\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\nE^{*}=F,\\qquad F^{*}=E. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Images of the centres\n\nFrom the definition of inversion,\n\n\\[\nAM\\cdot AM^{*}=AM\\cdot AN,\\qquad AN\\cdot AN^{*}=AM\\cdot AN,\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\nAM^{*}=AN,\\qquad AN^{*}=AM. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus the inversion interchanges the two centres:\n\n\\[\nM^{*}=N,\\qquad N^{*}=M. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  The circle \\((BEF)\\) has diameter \\(AB\\)\n\nSince \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\), the chord \\(AB\\) subtends a right angle at any point of \\(\\Omega\\); in particular  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{7a}\n\\]\n\nAnalogously, because \\(N\\) is the centre of \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle AFB = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{7b}\n\\]\n\nTherefore \\(AB\\) is a common diameter of the circumcircles of \\(\\triangle AEB\\) and \\(\\triangle AFB\\). Consequently the three points \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic and \\(AB\\) is a diameter of that circle; we denote it by \\((BEF)\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nB,E,F\\text{ are concyclic and }AB\\text{ is a diameter of }(BEF). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 5.  \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe four points \\(A,C,M,D\\) lie on the circle \\((APC)=(APD)\\); likewise \\(A,E,M,F\\) lie on \\((APE)=(APF)\\). Inversion \\(\\iota\\) preserves cross\u2011ratios, therefore  \n\n\\[\n(A,C;M,D)=(A,E;M,F). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nAll six points of each cross\u2011ratio are collinear on the line \\(MN\\); writing the cross\u2011ratio in terms of directed lengths gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{MC}{MD}= \\frac{ME}{MF}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause the ray \\(PA\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\), the homothety (1) shows that the ray \\(PN\\) passes through \\(E\\) and the ray \\(PM\\) passes through \\(F\\). Hence (9) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{MC}{MD}= \\frac{PN}{PM}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are precisely the intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the extensions of the sides \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\) respectively (see Fig.\u202f1). Equality (10) therefore states that the line \\(AP\\) meets the extensions of the two adjacent sides of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and divides the opposite side \\(MN\\) **externally** in the ratio of the adjacent side lengths \\(|PM|:|PN|\\). This is exactly the definition of the **external symmedian** through vertex \\(P\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\nAP\\text{ is the external symmedian of }\\triangle PMN. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Reflection of the orthocenter (Lemma\u202f1)\n\n*Lemma\u202f1.* In any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle.\n\n*Proof.* Let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) and let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in side \\(YZ\\). Denote by \\(X'\\) the second intersection of the altitude \\(XH\\) with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). Because the reflection preserves distances to the line \\(YZ\\), the points \\(X,H',X'\\) are collinear and \\(XH'=XH\\). Hence \\(\\angle X'YZ =\\angle XH'Z\\). The angle \\(\\angle X'YZ\\) subtends arc \\(XZ\\); therefore the quadrilateral \\(X Y Z H'\\) is cyclic, i.e. \\(H'\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). \u220e\n\nApplying Lemma\u202f1 to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) we obtain that the reflection \\(H'\\) of the orthocenter \\(H\\) in side \\(MN\\) lies on the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\). Moreover, \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(HH'\\); consequently the line \\(HH'\\) is symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\).\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Isogonal relationship between \\(AP\\) and \\(HH'\\)\n\nIn any triangle the internal symmedian is the isogonal conjugate of the external symmedian. Let \\(s\\) denote the internal symmedian through vertex \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). By definition, the isogonal of \\(s\\) with respect to \\(\\angle MPN\\) is the external symmedian, i.e. the line \\(AP\\). Hence the isogonal of \\(AP\\) is the line \\(s\\).\n\nA standard property of symmedians (see any textbook on triangle geometry) is that the internal symmedian through a vertex is parallel to the line joining the orthocenter with its reflection in the opposite side. Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\ns\\parallel HH'. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nCombining (11) (external symmedian) with the isogonal relation we deduce  \n\n\\[\nHH'\\parallel AP. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThus the line required in the problem \u2013 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 coincides with the line  \n\n\\[\nt:=HH'.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 8.  \\(H\\) lies on the circle \\((BEF)\\)\n\nFrom (7) we have \\(\\angle BAE = \\angle BFE\\) (angles subtended by the same chord \\(BE\\) in the circle \\((BEF)\\)). Because of (13) the direction of \\(AH\\) equals the direction of \\(AP\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BAH = \\angle BAE. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (14) into the equality from step\u202f4 yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BAH = \\angle BFE. \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nSince the line \\(t=HH'\\) passes through \\(H\\), the angle \\(\\angle BAH\\) is also \\(\\angle BHE\\). Thus (15) becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BHE = \\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nEquality of the angles subtended by the same chord \\(BE\\) at the points \\(H\\) and \\(F\\) implies that \\(H\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(B,E,F\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nH\\in (BEF). \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 9.  Tangency\n\nThe tangent\u2011chord theorem states that a line through a point of a circle is tangent to the circle iff it makes with a chord the same angle as the chord subtends at the opposite point of the circle. Because \\(t\\) passes through \\(H\\) and \\(H\\in (BEF)\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BHE . \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nBy (16) the right\u2011hand side equals \\(\\angle BFE\\). Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(t,BE)=\\angle BFE ,\n\\]\n\nwhich shows that \\(t\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(B\\). Since \\(t\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), the statement of the problem is proved. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
