rcle \\((APC)= (APD)\\)), and the same holds for \\(A,E,M,F\\) (they lie on the circle \\((APE)= (APF)\\)). Inversion preserves cross\u2011ratios, therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   (A,C;M,D)=(A,E;M,F). \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   All six points of each cross\u2011ratio are collinear on the line \\(MN\\); writing the cross\u2011ratio in terms of directed lengths yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{MC}{MD}= \\frac{ME}{MF}. \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   By construction (see Fig.\u202f1) the ray \\(PA\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\). Because \\(P\\) is the centre of the inversion circle, the ray \\(PN\\) (the extension of side \\(PN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)) passes through \\(E\\) and the ray \\(PM\\) passes through \\(F\\). Consequently (9) can be rewritten as  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{MC}{MD}= \\frac{PN}{PM}. \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   The points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the extensions of the sides \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\) respectively (by the homothety (1)). Equality (10) therefore states that the line \\(AP\\) meets the extensions of the two adjacent sides of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and divides the opposite side \\(MN\\) **externally** in the ratio of the adjacent side lengths \\(|PM|:|PN|\\). This is precisely the definition of the **external symmedian** through vertex \\(P\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   AP\\text{ is the external symmedian of }\\triangle PMN. \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Reflection of the orthocenter.**  \n\n   *Lemma\u202f1.* In any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle.  \n\n   *Proof.* Let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) and let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in side \\(YZ\\). Denote by \\(X'\\) the point where the altitude from \\(X\\) meets the circumcircle again. Because \\(XH\\perp YZ\\) and the reflection preserves distances to the line \\(YZ\\), we have \\(XH'=XH\\) and \\(XH'\\perp YZ\\) as well; thus \\(X,H',X'\\) are col