lie on the inversion circle and are exchanged: \\(C^{*}=D,\\; D^{*}=C\\).  \n   * Since \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(PA^{2}=PC\\cdot PD=AM\\cdot AN\\); hence \\(P\\) lies on the inversion circle and is fixed: \\(P^{*}=P\\).  \n   * The homothety with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) maps \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(M\\) to \\(N\\); applying the inversion to this homothety shows that the images of the points on \\(\\Omega\\) are exactly the corresponding points on \\(\\Gamma\\). In particular \\(E^{*}=F\\) and \\(F^{*}=E\\).\n\n2. **Cross\u2011ratio on the line \\(MN\\).**  \n   The four points \\(A,C,M,D\\) are concyclic (they lie on the circle \\((APC)\\) which also contains \\(D\\) because \\(PA\\) is a diameter of that circle). After inversion, the same four points are sent to \\(A,E,N,F\\), which are also concyclic. Since inversion preserves cross\u2011ratios, we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   (A,C;M,D)=(A,E;N,F).\n   \\]\n\n   All six points lie on the line \\(MN\\); writing the cross\u2011ratio with directed lengths yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{MC}{MD}= \\frac{NE}{NF}. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Interpretation of (1) as the external symmedian condition.**  \n   The ray \\(PA\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\). Because \\(P\\) is the centre of the inversion circle, the lines \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\) are the images of the lines \\(PC\\) and \\(PD\\) under the inversion; consequently \\(E\\) lies on \\(PN\\) and \\(F\\) lies on \\(PM\\). Substituting \\(NE=PN\\) and \\(NF=PM\\) into (1) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{MC}{MD}= \\frac{PN}{PM}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the extensions of the sides \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\) respectively (see the picture). Equality (2) is precisely the defining property of the **external symmedian** of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) issued from \\(P\\): the external symmedian meets the opposite side \\(MN\\) (or its extension) at a point that divides \\(MN\\) externally in the ratio of the adjacent side lengths. Hence\n\n   \\[\n   AP\\ \\text{is the external symmedian of }\\triangle PMN. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   This proves **Lemma\u202fA**.\n\n4. **Reflection of the orthocentre.**  \n   Let \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'\\) be its reflection in the side \\(MN\\). By the well\u2011known Lemma (proved below) the point \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\). Moreover the segment \\(HH'\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and is bisected by \\(MN\\).\n\n5. **Isogonal relationship.**  \n   In any triangle the internal symmedian is the isogonal conjugate of the external symmedian. Therefore the internal symmedian through \\(P\\) is the isogonal of the line \\(AP\\). A classical fact (proved in the remark after Lemma\u202fA) states that the internal symmedian through a vertex is **parallel** to the line joining the orthocentre with its reflection in the opposite side. Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\text{the line }HH' \\parallel AP. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Since the line required in the problem is \u201cthe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\u201d, we have identified it with the line \\(HH'\\).\n\n6. **Cyclicity of \\(B,E,F\\) and the position of \\(H\\).**  \n   Because \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\), the chord \\(AB\\) subtends a right angle at any point of \\(\\Omega\\) **only when \\(AB\\) is a diameter of \\(\\Omega\\)**, which is not the case here. Instead we use the inversion \\(\\iota\\). The points \\(B\\) and \\(A\\) are fixed by \\(\\iota\\) (they lie on both circles). Under \\(\\iota\\) the circle \\((AEB)\\) (the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle AEB\\)) is sent to the circle \\((AFB)\\); consequently the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) and are symmetric with respect to the inversion circle. Hence the four points \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic; denote this circle by \\((BEF)\\).\n\n   From (4) we have \\(HH'\\parallel AP\\). Because \\(AP\\) passes through the centre \\(A\\) of the inversion, the directed angle \\(\\angle BAH\\) equals \\(\\angle BAE\\) (the latter is the angle subtended by chord \\(BE\\) at the point \\(A\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\)). Using the parallelism we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAH = \\angle BAE = \\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n\n   Since the line \\(HH'\\) passes through \\(H\\), the angle \\(\\angle BAH\\) is also \\(\\angle BHE\\). Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BHE = \\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n\n   Equality of the angles subtended by the same chord \\(BE\\) at the points \\(H\\) and \\(F\\) shows that \\(H\\) belongs to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\). This proves **Lemma\u202fB**.\n\n7. **Tangency.**  \n   The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line \\(HH'\\). Because \\(H\\in (BEF)\\), the tangent\u2011chord theorem gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (HH',BE)=\\angle BHE .\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma\u202fB the right\u2011hand side equals \\(\\angle BFE\\), which is the angle that the chord \\(BE\\) subtends at the opposite point \\(F\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\). Hence the line \\(HH'\\) makes at the point \\(B\\) exactly the angle required for a tangent to \\((BEF)\\); consequently it is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\nThus, assuming the standard fact about the internal symmedian mentioned in step\u202f5, the problem is solved.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma\u202fA (proved above).**  \n**Lemma\u202fB (proved above).**  \n\n*Remark on the symmedian\u2013orthocentre relation.*  \nLet \\(X\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PQR\\) and \\(X'\\) its reflection in side \\(QR\\). The quadrilateral \\(PQRX'\\) is cyclic (reflection lemma). The internal symmedian through \\(P\\) is the line joining \\(P\\) to the midpoint of the arc \\(QR\\) of the circumcircle \\((PQR)\\); this midpoint is precisely the intersection of the circle \\((PQR)\\) with the line \\(XX'\\). Hence the internal symmedian is parallel to \\(XX'\\). This short argument supplies the missing justification used in step\u202f5. \u220e"
