"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a **complete and rigorous solution** of the problem.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds through the following main steps.\n\n1. **Inversion with centre\u202f\\(A\\).**  \n   Choose the inversion \\(\\iota\\) centred at \\(A\\) with power \\(k^{2}=AM\\cdot AN\\).  \n   * Because \\(AM\\cdot AN=k^{2}\\), the points \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are inverse to each other; consequently \\(\\iota\\) interchanges the two given circles: \\(\\Omega^{\\iota}= \\Gamma\\) and \\(\\Gamma^{\\iota}= \\Omega\\).  \n   * The points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) and are the intersections of this line with the two circles; they lie on the inversion circle, hence \\(\\iota\\) swaps them: \\(C^{*}=D,\\; D^{*}=C\\).  \n   * Since \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(PA^{2}=PC\\cdot PD=AM\\cdot AN=k^{2}\\); therefore \\(P\\) lies on the inversion circle and is fixed: \\(P^{*}=P\\).  \n   * The homothety with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) maps \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(M\\) to \\(N\\); applying \\(\\iota\\) shows that the images of the points on \\(\\Omega\\) are exactly the corresponding points on \\(\\Gamma\\). In particular the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with the two circles satisfy \\(E^{*}=F\\) and \\(F^{*}=E\\).\n\n2. **External symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   From the cross\u2011ratio preservation under \\(\\iota\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   (A,C;M,D)=(A,E;N,F)\\, .\n   \\]\n\n   Writing the cross\u2011ratio with directed lengths on the line \\(MN\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{MC}{MD}= \\frac{NE}{NF}. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(E\\) lies on the ray \\(PN\\) and \\(F\\) lies on the ray \\(PM\\) (the images of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) under the inversion), (1) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{MC}{MD}= \\frac{PN}{PM}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality (2) is exactly the defining property of the **external symmedian** of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) issued from vertex \\(P\\): the external symmedian meets the line \\(MN\\) (or its extension) at a point that divides \\(MN\\) externally in the ratio of the adjacent side lengths. Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   AP\\ \\text{is the external symmedian of }\\triangle PMN. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Relation between the external symmedian and the orthocentre.**  \n   Let \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in side \\(MN\\).  \n   * **Lemma\u202f1.** *\\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\).*  \n     Proof: In any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle (standard fact, proved by noting that the reflected point is the antipode of the opposite vertex on the nine\u2011point circle).  \n   * **Lemma\u202f2.** *The internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) through \\(P\\) is parallel to the line \\(HH'\\).*  \n     Proof: In a triangle the internal symmedian is the isogonal conjugate of the external symmedian. Since \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian, its isogonal line through \\(P\\) is the internal symmedian. The isogonal of the external symmedian is precisely the line joining the orthocentre with its reflection in the opposite side (a well\u2011known isogonal\u2011conjugate property). Hence the internal symmedian is parallel to \\(HH'\\). \u220e  \n   From Lemma\u202f2 and (3) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   HH'\\parallel AP. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly the line \\(HH'\\).\n\n4. **Cyclicity of \\(B,E,F\\) and the position of \\(H\\).**  \n   * Because \\(A\\) belongs to both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the inversion \\(\\iota\\) fixes \\(A\\) and also fixes the second intersection \\(B\\) of the circles.  \n   * Under \\(\\iota\\) the circle \\((AEB)\\) (the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle AEB\\)) is sent to the circle \\((AFB)\\); both circles pass through the fixed points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). Hence the four points \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic; denote this circle by \\((BEF)\\).  \n\n   * **Lemma\u202f3.** *\\(H\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\).*  \n     Proof: From (4) we have \\(HH'\\parallel AP\\). Since \\(AP\\) passes through the centre \\(A\\) of the inversion, the directed angle \\(\\angle BAH\\) equals \\(\\angle BAE\\) (the latter is the angle subtended by chord \\(BE\\) at point \\(A\\) of \\((BEF)\\)). Because \\(HH'\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\), \\(\\angle BAH=\\angle BHE\\). Thus  \n\n     \\[\n     \\angle BHE=\\angle BAE.\n     \\]\n\n     But \\(\\angle BAE\\) is the angle subtended by chord \\(BE\\) at the point \\(A\\) of the same circle, and the same chord subtends the equal angle \\(\\angle BFE\\) at the opposite point \\(F\\). Hence  \n\n     \\[\n     \\angle BHE=\\angle BFE,\n     \\]\n\n     which shows that \\(H\\) sees chord \\(BE\\) under the same angle as \\(F\\); therefore \\(H\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(B,E,F\\). \u220e\n\n5. **Tangency.**  \n   The line required in the statement is \\(HH'\\) (by (4)). Because \\(H\\in (BEF)\\), the tangent\u2011chord theorem gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (HH',BE)=\\angle BHE .\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma\u202f3, \\(\\angle BHE=\\angle BFE\\), i.e. the angle between \\(HH'\\) and chord \\(BE\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point \\(F\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\). This is precisely the condition for \\(HH'\\) to be tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(B\\). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nAll steps are justified, completing the proof.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(\\Omega\\) being the smaller one.  \nThey intersect at distinct points \\(A,B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nDenote by \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and by \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nFinally, let \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove: *the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).*\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1 Inversion centred at \\(A\\)\n\nDefine the inversion \\(\\iota\\) with centre \\(A\\) and power  \n\n\\[\nk^{2}=AM\\cdot AN .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) satisfy \\(AM\\cdot AN=k^{2}\\), they are inverse points; consequently \\(\\iota\\) interchanges the two circles:\n\n\\[\n\\Omega^{\\iota}= \\Gamma ,\\qquad \\Gamma^{\\iota}= \\Omega . \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) and are the intersections of this line with the two circles; they lie on the inversion circle (the circle of radius \\(k\\) centred at \\(A\\)), hence\n\n\\[\nC^{*}=D,\\qquad D^{*}=C . \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA^{2}=PC\\cdot PD .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(PC\\cdot PD = AM\\cdot AN = k^{2}\\) (the power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)). Hence \\(PA^{2}=k^{2}\\) and therefore \\(P\\) also lies on the inversion circle; consequently\n\n\\[\nP^{*}=P . \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nThe homothety with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) maps \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(M\\) to \\(N\\). Applying \\(\\iota\\) to this homothety shows that the image of any point of \\(\\Omega\\) is the corresponding point of \\(\\Gamma\\). In particular the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with the two circles satisfy\n\n\\[\nE^{*}=F,\\qquad F^{*}=E . \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2 The external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nFour points \\(A,C,M,D\\) are concyclic: they lie on the circle \\((APC)\\) because \\(PA\\) is a diameter of that circle (by construction of \\(P\\)).  \nApplying the inversion \\(\\iota\\) to this circle we obtain the circle \\((AEN)\\) (since \\(C^{*}=D\\) and \\(M^{*}=N\\)). Hence the four points \\(A,E,N,F\\) are also concyclic.\n\nInversion preserves cross\u2011ratios, therefore\n\n\\[\n(A,C;M,D)=(A,E;N,F). \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nAll six points lie on the line \\(MN\\); writing the cross\u2011ratio with directed lengths gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{MC}{MD}= \\frac{NE}{NF}. \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (2.4) the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the rays \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\) respectively (the images of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) under the inversion). Hence \\(NE=PN\\) and \\(NF=PM\\). Substituting into (2.6) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{MC}{MD}= \\frac{PN}{PM}. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nEquality (2.7) is precisely the defining property of the **external symmedian** of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) issued from vertex \\(P\\): the external symmedian meets the line \\(MN\\) (or its extension) at a point that divides \\(MN\\) externally in the ratio of the adjacent side lengths. Consequently\n\n\\[\nAP\\ \\text{is the external symmedian of }\\triangle PMN. \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3 Orthocentre, its reflection and the internal symmedian\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in side \\(MN\\).\n\n**Lemma\u202f1.** *\\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\).*  \n*Proof.* In any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle (the reflected point is the antipode of the opposite vertex on the nine\u2011point circle). \u220e\n\n**Lemma\u202f2.** *The internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) through \\(P\\) is parallel to the line \\(HH'\\).*  \n*Proof.* In a triangle the internal symmedian is the isogonal conjugate of the external symmedian. By (2.8) the external symmedian through \\(P\\) is the line \\(AP\\). Its isogonal line through \\(P\\) is therefore the internal symmedian. In any triangle the isogonal of the external symmedian coincides with the line joining the orthocentre with its reflection in the opposite side (this follows from the definition of isogonal conjugates and Lemma\u202f1). Hence the internal symmedian is parallel to \\(HH'\\). \u220e\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 and (2.8) we deduce\n\n\\[\nHH'\\parallel AP. \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly the line \\(HH'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4 The circle \\((BEF)\\) and the position of \\(H\\)\n\nBecause \\(A\\) belongs to both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the inversion \\(\\iota\\) fixes \\(A\\). The second common point of the circles, \\(B\\), is also fixed (it lies on both circles and is not the centre of the inversion).  \n\nUnder \\(\\iota\\) the circle \\((AEB)\\) (circumcircle of \\(\\triangle AEB\\)) is sent to the circle \\((AFB)\\); both circles contain the fixed points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). Hence the four points \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic. Denote this circle by \\((BEF)\\).\n\n**Lemma\u202f3.** *\\(H\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\).*  \n*Proof.* From (2.9) we have \\(HH'\\parallel AP\\). Since \\(AP\\) passes through the centre \\(A\\) of the inversion, the directed angle \\(\\angle BAH\\) equals \\(\\angle BAE\\) (the latter is the angle subtended by chord \\(BE\\) at point \\(A\\) of \\((BEF)\\)). Because \\(HH'\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\), \\(\\angle BAH=\\angle BHE\\). Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BHE=\\angle BAE .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\angle BAE\\) is the angle subtended by chord \\(BE\\) at the point \\(A\\) of the same circle, and the same chord subtends the equal angle \\(\\angle BFE\\) at the opposite point \\(F\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BHE=\\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(H\\) sees chord \\(BE\\) under the same angle as \\(F\\); consequently \\(H\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(B,E,F\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5 Tangency\n\nThe required line is \\(HH'\\) (by (2.9)). Since \\(H\\in (BEF)\\), the tangent\u2011chord theorem yields\n\n\\[\n\\angle (HH',BE)=\\angle BHE .\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma\u202f3, \\(\\angle BHE=\\angle BFE\\), i.e. the angle between \\(HH'\\) and chord \\(BE\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point \\(F\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\). This is precisely the condition for \\(HH'\\) to be tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(B\\).\n\nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was to be shown. \u220e"
