mega^{\\iota}= \\Gamma ,\\qquad \\Gamma^{\\iota}= \\Omega .\\)\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe statement that two points are inverse with respect to an inversion centred at \\(A\\) requires that the product of their distances from \\(A\\) equals the square of the inversion radius. Since the previous definition of the radius is false, the conclusion that \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are inverse points is unfounded. Moreover, even if \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) were inverse, it does **not** automatically follow that the whole circles are interchanged; one would need to verify that each point of \\(\\Omega\\) maps to a point of \\(\\Gamma\\). No such verification is provided.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 relies on the false definition of the inversion.\n\n---\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n\u201cThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) and are the intersections of this line with the two circles; they lie on the inversion circle (the circle of radius \\(k\\) centred at \\(A\\)), hence\n\\(C^{*}=D,\\qquad D^{*}=C .\\)\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nEven assuming the inversion were correctly defined, there is no reason for \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) to lie on the inversion circle. The inversion circle consists of points at distance \\(k\\) from \\(A\\); the distances \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) are not shown to equal \\(k\\). The claim is therefore unsupported.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 unjustified claim about the images of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).\n\n---\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n\u201cSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\\(PA^{2}=PC\\cdot PD .\\)\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nFor a point \\(P\\) that is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\). The relation \\(PA^{2}=PC\\cdot PD\\) is a mis\u2011application of the power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point theorem, which would require \\(A\\) to be *outside* the circle \\((ACD)\\). Here \\(A\\) lies on that circle, so its power is zero, not \\(PA^{2}\\). Hence the equality is false.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 incorrect use of power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point.\n\n---\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n\u201cBut \\(PC\\cdot PD = AM\\cdot AN = k^{2}\\) (the power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nEven if we accepted the previous (false) equality, the product \\(PC\\cdot PD\\) equals \\(PA^{2}\\) (since all three distances are equal), not \\(AM\\cdot AN\\). Moreover, the power of \\(A\\) with respect to either circle is zero, not \\(AM\\cdot AN\\). This statement conflates several unrelated quantities.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 false equality and misuse of power.\n\n---\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n\u201cHence \\(PA^{2}=k^{2}\\) and therefore \\(P\\) also lies on the inversion circle; consequently\n\\(P^{*}=P .\\)\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe conclusion that \\(P\\) lies on the inversion circle follows from the previous (incorrect) equality \\(PA^{2}=k^{2}\\). Since that equality is invalid, the claim that \\(P\\) is fixed by the inversion is unsupported.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 depends on earlier false statements.\n\n---\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n\u201cThe homothety with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) maps \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(M\\) to \\(N\\). Applying \\(\\iota\\) to this homothety shows that the image of any point of \\(\\Omega\\) is the corresponding point of \\(\\Gamma\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nTwo issues: (1) The existence of a homothety centred at \\(A\\) mapping \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) would require \\(A\\) to be the external centre of similarity of the two circles, which is true, but the author does not justify it. (2) The statement that applying the inversion to this homothety yields a point\u2011wise correspondence between the circles is not proved; it is a non\u2011trivial claim that needs a clear argument. Since the inversion itself is incorrectly defined, any derived correspondence is unreliable.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 relies on the flawed inversion and lacks justification.\n\n---\n\n### 2.2 The external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n\u201cFour points \\(A,C,M,D\\) are concyclic: they lie on the circle \\((APC)\\) because \\(PA\\) is a diameter of that circle (by construction of \\(P\\)).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nFrom the definition, \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus \\(PA=PC=PD\\). This makes \\(P\\) the centre of the circumcircle of \\(A,C,D\\). There is no reason for \\(PA\\) to be a *diameter* of that circle, nor for \\(A,C,M,D\\) to be concyclic (note that \\(M\\) is not generally on the circumcircle of \\(A,C,D\\)). The claim is false.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 incorrect statement about concyclicity.\n\n---\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n\u201cApplying the inversion \\(\\iota\\) to this circle we obtain the circle \\((AEN)\\) (since \\(C^{*}=D\\) and \\(M^{*}=N\\)). Hence the four points \\(A,E,N,F\\) are also concyclic.\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nBecause the previous claim that \\(A,C,M,D\\) are concyclic is false, the inversion of that (non\u2011existent) circle is meaningless. Moreover, the images \\(C^{*}=D\\) and \\(M^{*}=N\\) were themselves unjustified. Hence the derived concyclicity of \\(A,E,N,F\\) is baseless.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 depends on earlier false statements.\n\n---\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n\u201cInversion preserves cross\u2011ratios, therefore\n\\((A,C;M,D)=(A,E;N,F).\\)\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nEven if the inversion were correctly defined, the cross\u2011ratio equality would involve the images of the four points under the inversion. However, the points \\(C,D,M,N\\) are not all on a single line; they are on line \\(MN\\) but \\(A\\) is not. The cross\u2011ratio \\((A,C;M,D)\\) is not defined in the usual sense for four points not collinear (it would be a cross\u2011ratio of four lines through \\(A\\) or of four 