ts \\(M,N\\) on the extension of a side; the argument does not correctly connect the ratio to the symmedian property.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 conclusion rests on invalid earlier steps.\n\n---\n\n### 2.3 Orthocentre, its reflection and the internal symmedian\n\n**Quoted text (Lemma\u202f1):**  \n\u201c*\\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\).*  \n*Proof.* In any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle (the reflected point is the antipode of the opposite vertex on the nine\u2011point circle).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe statement is true, but the proof is only sketched. For a rigorous IMO\u2011level solution, a complete argument (e.g., using the fact that the reflection of \\(H\\) across a side lies on the circumcircle because the side is the perpendicular bisector of the segment joining the vertex and its reflection) should be provided. The omission constitutes a **Justification Gap**.\n\n**Classification:** **Justification Gap** \u2013 proof is incomplete.\n\n---\n\n**Quoted text (Lemma\u202f2):**  \n\u201cThe internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) through \\(P\\) is parallel to the line \\(HH'\\).  \n*Proof.* In a triangle the internal symmedian is the isogonal conjugate of the external symmedian. By (2.8) the external symmedian through \\(P\\) is the line \\(AP\\). Its isogonal line through \\(P\\) is therefore the internal symmedian. In any triangle the isogonal of the external symmedian coincides with the line joining the orthocentre with its reflection in the opposite side (this follows from the definition of isogonal conjugates and Lemma\u202f1). Hence the internal symmedian is parallel to \\(HH'\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe relationship \u201cthe isogonal of the external symmedian coincides with the line joining the orthocentre with its reflection\u201d is a non\u2011trivial geometric fact that requires proof. Moreover, the identification of the external symmedian with \\(AP\\