lies on earlier false inversion properties.\n\n---\n\n**Quoted text (Lemma\u202f3):**  \n\u201c\\(H\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\).  \n*Proof.* From (2.9) we have \\(HH'\\parallel AP\\). Since \\(AP\\) passes through the centre \\(A\\) of the inversion, the directed angle \\(\\angle BAH\\) equals \\(\\angle BAE\\) (the latter is the angle subtended by chord \\(BE\\) at point \\(A\\) of \\((BEF)\\)). Because \\(HH'\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\), \\(\\angle BAH=\\angle BHE\\). Therefore  \n\\(\\angle BHE=\\angle BAE\\). \u2026 Hence \\(H\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(B,E,F\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe proof uses the previously unproved parallelism \\(HH'\\parallel AP\\) (which itself is false). It also assumes that the angle \\(\\angle BAH\\) equals \\(\\angle BAE\\) because \\(A\\) is the centre of the inversion\u2014a statement that has no basis. Consequently the lemma is unsupported.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 depends on multiple false statements.\n\n---\n\n### 2.5 Tangency\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n\u201cThe required line is \\(HH'\\) (by (2.9)). Since \\(H\\in (BEF)\\), the tangent\u2011chord theorem yields\n\\(\\angle (HH',BE)=\\angle BHE\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f3, \\(\\angle BHE=\\angle BFE\\), i.e. the angle between \\(HH'\\) and chord \\(BE\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point \\(F\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\). This is precisely the condition for \\(HH'\\) to be tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(B\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nBoth the identification of the required line as \\(HH'\\) and the membership of \\(H\\) in \\((BEF)\\) have been shown to be invalid. Therefore the final tangency claim is not established.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 conclusion rests on earlier invalid steps.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\nThe solution contains numerous foundational mistakes: the inversion is incorrectly defined, several geometric relations (concyclicities, cross\u2011ratios, symmedian identification) a