nt \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\); hence\n   \\[\n   \\angle CAP = \\angle PAD . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Cyclic quadrilateral \\(AEBF\\).**  \n   Using (1) and (2) we compare the directed angles \\(\\angle AEB\\) and \\(\\angle AFB\\).  \n   The homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) preserves oriented angles, therefore\n   \\[\n   \\angle AEB = \\angle AFB .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Since both angles subtend the same chord \\(AB\\), (3) is precisely the condition for the four points \\(A,E,B,F\\) to lie on a common circle. Hence the lemma is proved.\n\nThe lemma reduces the original statement to a tangency problem involving the circle \\((BEF)=(AEBF)\\) and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). The next natural step would be to relate the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) to the direction of \\(AP\\) and to the circle \\((BEF)\\); this part of the argument remains to be completed."
