e \\((ACD)\\). Hence\n   \\[\n   \\angle CAP = \\angle PAD .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Equality of the directed angles \\(\\angle AEB\\) and \\(\\angle AFB\\).**  \n   Since \\(\\mathcal H\\) is a similarity with centre \\(A\\), it preserves oriented angles. Applying \\(\\mathcal H\\) to the ray \\(AE\\) we obtain the ray \\(AF\\); applying it to the ray \\(AB\\) we obtain the same ray \\(AB\\) (because \\(B\\) lies on both circles). Therefore\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AE,AB)=\\angle (AF,AB).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   In terms of points this reads \\(\\angle AEB = \\angle AFB\\).\n\n5. **Cyclicity of \\(A,E,B,F\\).**  \n   Equality (3) says that the two angles subtended by the chord \\(AB\\) at the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are equal. This is precisely the criterion for the four points \\(A,E,B,F\\) to lie on a common circle. Hence the lemma is proved.\n\nThe lemma reduces the original problem to the following equivalent formulation:\n\n> Show that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) which is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circle \\((AEBF)\\).\n\nAt present I have not succeeded in linking the direction of \\(AP\\) (or its parallel through \\(H\\)) with the tangent at \\(A\\) (or at another point) of the circle \\((AEBF)\\). A plausible route would be to express \\(H\\) in terms of the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and to use the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) together with the fact that \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); however, a complete, gap\u2011free derivation of the tangency has not been achieved."
