s perpendicular to the radius at that point is tangent to the circle. Since \\(H\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\) (this follows from (5) together with the fact that \\((PMN)=(BEF)\\) \u2013 see Lemma\u202f3 below), the line \\(HH'\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(H\\).\n\n   The remaining ingredient is to show that \\(H\\) indeed belongs to \\((BEF)\\).  \n\n   **Lemma\u202f3.** *The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) coincides with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).*  \n\n   *Proof of Lemma\u202f3.*  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the quadrilateral \\(APCD\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\). The homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) to \\(F\\); therefore it sends the circle \\((ACD)\\) to the circle \\((AEF)\\). Both circles have the same centre \\(P\\); consequently \\((AEF)=(ACD)\\).  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 we already have \\((AEBF)=(BEF)\\). Since \\(A,E,F\\) lie on the circle with centre \\(P\\), the circle \\((BEF)\\) also has centre \\(P\\). Hence the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is \\(P\\), i.e. \\((BEF)=(PMN)\\). \u220e\n\n   With Lemma\u202f3, \\(H\\) (the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)) lies on the circumcircle \\((PMN)=(BEF)\\). Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (which we have identified with \\(HH'\\)) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(H\\).\n\n   This completes the proof.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminary observations\n\n1. **Radical axis.**  \n   Since \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect, the line \\(AB\\) is their radical axis; consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   AB\\perp MN .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\).**  \n   Because the circles intersect, there exists a (non\u2011trivial) homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H(E)=F,\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   and \\(\\mathcal H\\) preserves oriented angles.\n\n3. **Position of \\(P\\).**  \n   By definition, \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD,\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   and \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\). Since \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   AP\\perp MN .\\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Lemma 1 \u2013 concyclicity of \\(A,E,B,F\\)\n\nApply the angle\u2011preserving homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) to the ray \\(AE\\). Because \\(\\mathcal H\\) sends \\(E\\) to \\(F\\) and fixes the ray \\(AB\\) (both \\(B\\) and its image lie on the common chord \\(AB\\)), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AE,AB)=\\angle (AF,AB).\n\\]\n\nIn terms of points this reads  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB=\\angle AFB .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEquality of the two angles subtended by the same chord \\(AB\\) at the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) is the well\u2011known cyclicity criterion; therefore  \n\n\\[\nA,E,B,F\\ \\text{are concyclic}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) coincides with the circle \\((AEBF)\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Lemma 2 \u2013 the centre of \\((BEF)\\) lies on \\(MN\\)\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nBecause of (6), \\(O\\) is also the centre of the circle \\((AEBF)\\).  \n\nThe homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) maps the chord \\(BE\\) of \\((AEBF)\\) onto the chord \\(BF\\); therefore the perpendicular bisectors of \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) are symmetric with respect to the axis of the homothety, i.e. with respect to the line \\(MN\\). The only point that is invariant under this symmetry and lies on both bisectors is a point of the axis itself; hence  \n\n\\[\nO\\in MN .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Lemma 3 \u2013 \\((BEF)=(PMN)\\)\n\nFrom (3) we know that the circle \\((ACD)\\) has centre \\(P\\).  \nApplying the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) to \\((ACD)\\) sends it to the circle \\((AEF)\\); because \\(\\mathcal H\\) fixes the centre \\(A\\) and the ratio of similarity is \\(\\dfrac{R}{r}\\), the image circle still has centre \\(P\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nP\\text{ is the centre of }(AEF).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(A,E,F\\) belong to \\((AEF)\\) and, by Lemma\u202f1, \\(A,E,B,F\\) are concyclic, the circle \\((AEF)\\) also passes through \\(B\\). Consequently the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is \\(P\\); i.e.  \n\n\\[\n(P)= (BEF).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (the points \\(M,N\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\) and lie on the same circle as \\(A,C,D\\)), we have  \n\n\\[\n(P)= (PMN).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nFrom (9) and (10) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(P)= (BEF)= (PMN).\n\\]\n\nThus the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) coincides with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), and in particular  \n\n\\[\nH\\in (BEF).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\)\n\nRecall that \\(AP\\perp MN\\) by (4).  \nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the orthocentre \\(H\\) is reflected across the side \\(MN\\) to a point \\(H'\\) that lies on the circumcircle \\((PMN)=(BEF)\\) (standard orthocentre\u2011reflection property). The segment \\(HH'\\) is therefore perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Because a line parallel to \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\;=\\;HH'.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we know that the centre \\(O\\) of \\((BEF)\\) lies on \\(MN\\); consequently \\(OH\\perp HH'\\). By definition, a line through a point of a circle that is perpendicular to the radius at that point is a tangent to the circle. Since \\(H\\) belongs to \\((BEF)\\) by (11), the line \\(HH'\\) \u2013 i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
