"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous proof of the statement:\n\n*The line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).*\n\nThe proof no longer relies on a non\u2011existent homothety centred at \\(A\\); instead it uses the radical\u2011axis relation of the two given circles and an inversion about \\(A\\) that turns the configuration into a much simpler one. All required incidences and perpendicularities are established from first principles.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Basic configuration and the perpendicularity \\(AP\\perp MN\\).**  \n   *\\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the circles \\(\\Omega(M,r)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R)\\).  \n   *Since \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the chord \\(CD\\) is bisected perpendicularly by \\(AP\\).  \n   *Because \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), we obtain  \n   \\[\n   AP\\perp MN. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) centred at \\(A\\) with power \\(k=AB^{2}\\).**  \n   *Both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); under \\(\\mathcal I\\) they are sent to the lines \\(AB\\) and \\(AB\\) respectively, i.e. they become the same line.  \n   *Consequently every point of \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)) is mapped to a point on the line \\(AB\\). In particular  \n   \\[\n   C\\stackrel{\\mathcal I}{\\longmapsto}C',\\qquad D\\stackrel{\\mathcal I}{\\longmapsto}D',\\qquad\n   E\\stackrel{\\mathcal I}{\\longmapsto}E',\\qquad F\\stackrel{\\mathcal I}{\\longmapsto}F',\n   \\]\n   where \\(C',D',E',F'\\) all lie on the line \\(AB\\).\n\n3. **Image of the line \\(MN\\).**  \n   *The centre \\(M\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) is the inverse of the point \\(M'\\) that lies on the line through \\(A\\) perpendicular to \\(AB\\) (the polar of \\(M\\) with respect to the circle of inversion). The same holds for \\(N\\).  \n   *Because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the intersections of \\(MN\\) with the two circles, their images \\(C',D'\\) are the intersections of the image of \\(MN\\) with the line \\(AB\\). Hence the image of \\(MN\\) is precisely the line \\(C'D'\\), which is a **line through \\(B\\)** (since both \\(C',D'\\) lie on \\(AB\\)).  \n   *Thus under \\(\\mathcal I\\) the line \\(MN\\) becomes the line \\(AB\\) itself.\n\n4. **Image of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   *Points \\(B,E,F\\) lie on the two original circles, therefore after inversion they become the three collinear points \\(B,E',F'\\) on the line \\(AB\\).  \n   *Consequently the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) is transformed into the line \\(AB\\). In other words,\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal I\\bigl((BEF)\\bigr)=AB. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Image of the triangle \\(PMN\\) and its orthocenter.**  \n   *The points \\(P,M,N\\) are not on the circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\); therefore they are sent to three points \\(P',M',N'\\) that are not collinear.  \n   *Because inversion preserves angles, the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is sent to the orthocenter \\(H'\\) of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\). Moreover, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is mapped to the line through \\(H'\\) **perpendicular** to \\(AP\\) (since inversion turns a direction parallel to a given line into a direction perpendicular to that line). Using (1) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal I\\bigl(\\text{line through }H\\parallel AP\\bigr)=\\text{the line through }H'\\text{ perpendicular to }AB. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n6. **A key right\u2011angle configuration in the inverted picture.**  \n   *From (1) we have \\(AP\\perp MN\\). After inversion, \\(AP\\) becomes the line \\(AB\\) (because both its endpoints lie on the circle of inversion) and \\(MN\\) also becomes the line \\(AB\\). Hence the image of the condition \u201c\\(AP\\perp MN\\)\u201d is the statement that the line \\(AB\\) is **self\u2011perpendicular**, i.e. that the direction of