------------------------------\n### 1.  The perpendicularity \\(AP\\perp MN\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\).  \nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, the chord \\(CD\\) is a sub\u2011segment of the line \\(MN\\). Consequently the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Inversion about \\(A\\) with power \\(k=AB^{2}\\)\n\nDefine the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) centred at \\(A\\) with power \\(k=AB^{2}\\).  \nBoth circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through the points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); under \\(\\mathcal I\\) each of them is mapped to the line \\(AB\\). Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=AB .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequences of (2):\n\n* The points \\(C,D,E,F\\) (all belonging to \\(\\Omega\\) or \\(\\Gamma\\)) are sent to points \\(C',D',E',F'\\) that lie on the line \\(AB\\).\n\n* The circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) is transformed into the line \\(AB\\) because its three defining points \\(B,E,F\\) all lie on the image line \\(AB\\):\n\\[\n\\mathcal I\\bigl((BEF)\\bigr)=AB .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Image of the line \\(MN\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) at \\(D\\). After inversion, the points \\(C',D'\\) lie on \\(AB\\); thus the image of the line \\(MN\\) is the line through \\(C'\\) and \\(D'\\), i.e. the line \\(AB\\) itself:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(MN)=AB .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Image of the triangle \\(PMN\\) and its orthocenter\n\nThe points \\(P,M,N\\) are not on the circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\); therefore they are mapped to three distinct points \\(P',M',N'\\) that are not collinear. Because inversion preserves oriented angles, the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is sent to the orthocenter \\(H'\\) of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\).\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(AP\\perp MN\\). Under inversion, the ray \\(AP\\) becomes the line \\(AB\\) (both its endpoints lie on the circle of inversion), and the line \\(MN\\) also becomes \\(AB\\) by (4). Hence the direction of \\(AP\\) is mapped to the direction of \\(AB\\). Consequently the image of a line **parallel** to \\(AP\\) is a line **perpendicular** to \\(AB\\). Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I\\bigl(\\ell\\bigl(H,\\;AP\\bigl)\\bigr)=\\ell\\bigl(H',\\;AB^{\\perp}\\bigr),\\tag{5}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\ell(H,AP)\\) denotes the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) and \\(\\ell(H',AB^{\\perp})\\) the line through \\(H'\\) perpendicular to \\(AB\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Tangency in the inverted picture\n\nBy (3) the image of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) is the line \\(AB\\).  \nEquation (5) tells us that the image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is a line through \\(H'\\) **perpendicular** to \\(AB\\).  \n\nA line perpendicular to a given line at a point of that line is precisely the tangent to the (degenerate) circle represented by that line. Hence the line \\(\\ell(H',AB^{\\perp})\\) is tangent to \\(\\mathcal I\\bigl((BEF)\\bigr)=AB\\) at the point \\(H'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Pulling back to the original configuration\n\nInversion preserves tangency: if a line is tangent to the image of a circle, its pre\u2011image is tangent to the original circle. Applying the inverse of \\(\\mathcal I\\) to the tangency just proved, we obtain that the pre\u2011image of \\(\\ell(H',AB^{\\perp})\\) \u2013 namely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is tangent to the original circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\nThus the required statement holds.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\] \u220e"
