tical Error** \u2013 Because the previous steps are flawed, the conclusion that the original line is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) does not follow.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Verification Log\n\n### Step\u202f1 \u2013 Perpendicularity \\(AP\\perp MN\\)\n\n> \u201cSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\). Hence \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\). Because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, the chord \\(CD\\) is a sub\u2011segment of the line \\(MN\\). Consequently the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); i.e. \\(AP\\perp MN\\).\u201d\n\n**Verification:** Correct. In a circle the line joining the centre to the midpoint of a chord is perpendicular to the chord. Since \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\), it is the midpoint of the arc \\(CD\\) on the circumcircle of \\(ACD\\); thus \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) and is perpendicular to the line containing \\(CD\\), which is \\(MN\\). No gap.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f2 \u2013 Inversion about \\(A\\) with power \\(k=AB^{2}\\)\n\n> \u201cBoth circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through the points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); under \\(\\mathcal I\\) each of them is mapped to the line \\(AB\\).\u201d\n\n**Verification:** Correct. An inversion with centre \\(A\\) and power \\(AB^{2}\\) sends any circle passing through \\(A\\) to a line not passing through \\(A\\); the image line is the line through the other intersection point(s) with the circle of inversion, here \\(B\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=AB\\). No gap.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f3 \u2013 Image of the line \\(MN\\)\n\n> \u201cThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) at \\(D\\). After inversion, the points \\(C',D'\\) lie on \\(AB\\); thus the image of the line \\(MN\\) is the line through \\(C'\\) and \\(D'\\), i.e. the line \\(AB\\) itself: \\(\\mathcal I(MN)=AB\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error:**  \nInversion sends a line **not passing through the centre** to a **circle** that passes through the centre. The line \\(MN\\) does **not** pass through \\(A\\); therefore \\(\\mathcal I(MN)\\) must be a circle through \\(A\\). While the images \\(C',D'\\) indeed lie on \\(AB\\), the unique circle through the three points \\(A, C', D'\\) is the line \\(AB\\) **only if** those three points are collinear, which would make the \u201ccircle\u2019\u2019 degenerate. In the context of inversion, the image cannot be a degenerate line; it must be a genuine circle. Consequently the claim \\(\\mathcal I(MN)=AB\\) is false. The correct statement is that \\(\\mathcal I(MN)\\) is the circle through \\(A, C', D'\\), which is not the line \\(AB\\) in general.\n\nBecause this step is false, any later reasoning that relies on \\(\\mathcal I(MN)=AB\\) is invalid.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f4 \u2013 Image of triangle \\(PMN\\) and its orthocenter\n\n> \u201cBecause inversion preserves oriented angles, the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is sent to the orthocenter \\(H'\\) of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error:**  \nInversion does **not** preserve orthocenters. While it preserves angles, the orthocenter is defined via perpendicularity to the sides, and perpendicularity is not generally preserved under inversion (a line may become a circle). Hence there is no reason for the image of the orthocenter to be the orthocenter of the image triangle. This statement is unfounded and invalidates the subsequent use of \\(H'\\).\n\n> \u201cFrom (1) we have \\(AP\\perp MN\\). Under inversion, the ray \\(AP\\) becomes the line \\(AB\\) \u2026 and the line \\(MN\\) also becomes \\(AB\\) by (4). Hence the direction of \\(AP\\) is mapped to the direction of \\(AB\\). Consequently the image of a line **parallel** to \\(AP\\) is a line **perpendicular** to \\(AB\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Justification Gap:**  \nEven assuming the (incorrect) claim that both \\(AP\\) and \\(MN\\) map to the line \\(AB\\), the inference that a line parallel to \\(AP\\) maps to a line perpendicular to \\(AB\\) is not justified. Inversion does not convert parallelism into perpendicularity in a systematic way; the relationship depends on the specific positions of the lines relative to the centre of inversion. No argument is provided, so this is a gap.\n\nBecause the orthocenter mapping is false and the parallel\u2011to\u2011perpendicular claim is unsupported, the whole \u201cimage picture\u2019\u2019 constructed in Steps\u202f4\u20135 collapses.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f5 \u2013 Tangency in the inverted picture\n\n> \u201cA line perpendicular to a given line at a point of that line is precisely the tangent to the (degenerate) circle represented by that line.\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Justification Gap:**  \nThe notion of a \u201ctangent to a degenerate circle\u2019\u2019 (i.e., a line) is not a standard geometric concept. Moreover, the argument that the line through \\(H'\\) perpendicular to \\(AB\\) is tangent to the image of \\((BEF)\\) (which was claimed to be the line \\(AB\\)) relies on the false premises from Steps\u202f3 and\u202f4. Hence this step lacks rigorous justification.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f6 \u2013 Pulling back to the original configuration\n\n> \u201cInversion preserves tangency \u2026 the pre\u2011image of the line \u2026 is tangent to the original circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Consequence of Earlier Errors:**  \nEven if inversion does preserve tangency, the tangency established in the inverted picture is based on the erroneous statements identified above. Therefore this concluding step does not follow from a valid argument.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\nThe solution hinges on an inversion argument that contains multiple critical mistakes:\n\n1. **Incorrect image of the line \\(MN\\).**  \n2. **False claim that orthocenters are preserved under inversion.**