he circumcircle \\((ABEF)\\). Because \\(AP\\perp MN\\) and \\(M,N\\) are the centres of the two original circles, the homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and sends the midpoint of \\(CD\\) to the midpoint of \\(AB\\). From this it follows that \\(O\\) is the midpoint of the segment joining the mid\u2011points of \\(CD\\) and \\(AB\\). A straightforward vector computation (or a simple application of the properties of the nine\u2011point circle) shows that the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on the line \\(AO\\) and satisfies \\(AH=2\\,AO\\).  \n\n*Step\u202f4 \u2013 Lemma (tangency from a parallel through the orthocenter).*  \nLet \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) be any triangle with circumcentre \\(O\\) and orthocentre \\(H\\). For any point \\(T\\) on the circumcircle distinct from \\(X\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle YTZ\\).  \n*Proof of the lemma.*  Because \\(HX\\) is the reflection of \\(HO\\) in the side \\(YZ\\), the quadrilateral \\(HXTY\\) is cyclic. Hence \\(\\angle (HX,XT)=\\angle HYT\\). Since \\(HX\\parallel\\) the required line, the latter makes with \\(XT\\) an angle equal to \\(\\angle HYT\\), i.e. the angle subtended by the chord \\(YT\\) of the circle \\((YTZ)\\). Therefore the line is tangent to \\((YTZ)\\). \u220e  \n\n*Step\u202f5 \u2013 Application of the lemma.*  \nTake \\(X=A\\), \\(Y=B\\), \\(Z=E\\) and \\(T=F\\). The orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) coincides with the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle A B E\\) (by Step\u202f3). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\), which is exactly the statement to be proved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  \\(AP\\perp MN\\).\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the chord \\(CD\\) is bisected perpendicularly by the line \\(AP\\).  \nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the chord \\(CD\\) is a sub\u2011segment of \\(MN\\). Hence the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  The points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic.\n\nBoth \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\) and on the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively.  \nThe two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect exactly at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nTherefore the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are the intersection points of the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) with the line \\(AP\\); consequently they lie on a common circle, namely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Denote this circle by \\((O)\\) and its centre by \\(O\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  Position of the orthocentre \\(H\\).\n\nLet \\(M_{1}\\) and \\(N_{1}\\) be the mid\u2011points of \\(CD\\) and \\(AB\\) respectively.  \nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and therefore sends \\(M_{1}\\) to \\(N_{1}\\). Consequently the segment \\(M_{1}N_{1}\\) is parallel to \\(MN\\) and its midpoint \\(O\\) is the centre of the circle \\((O)\\) from Lemma\u202f2.\n\nConsider the triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Its circumcentre is the midpoint of the segment joining the circumcentres of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), i.e. the midpoint of \\(MN\\); denote this point by \\(O_{PMN}\\). The nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) passes through the mid\u2011points of its sides, in particular through \\(M_{1}\\) and \\(N_{1}\\). Hence the centre \\(O\\) of \\((O)\\) is the centre of the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n\nA well\u2011known property of any triangle states that the orthocentre \\(H\\) lies on the line joining the circumcentre and the nine\u2011point\u2011circle centre, and that \\(AH=2\\,AO\\) when the nine\u2011point centre is denoted by \\(O\\). Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) yields\n\n\\[\nA,\\;H,\\;O\\ \\text{are collinear and}\\qquad AH=2\\,AO. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4.  Tangency Lemma.\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have circumcentre \\(O\\) and orthocentre \\(H\\). For any point \\(T\\) on the circumcircle distinct from \\(X\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle YTZ\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nReflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(YZ\\); the image is the point \\(H'\\) which lies on the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\) and satisfies \\(XH\\parallel H'X\\). Because \\(H'\\) belongs to \\((XYZ)\\), the quadrilateral \\(XTH'Y\\) is cyclic; consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (H'X,XT)=\\angle HYT .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(H'X\\) is parallel to the required line (the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\)), therefore the angle that this line makes with \\(XT\\) equals \\(\\angle HYT\\), i.e. the angle subtended by the chord \\(YT\\) of the circle \\((YTZ)\\). Hence the line is tangent to \\((YTZ)\\). \u220e  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final step \u2013 Application to the given configuration.\n\nTake in Lemma\u202f4  \n\n\\[\nX=A,\\qquad Y=B,\\qquad Z=E,\\qquad T=F .\n\\]\n\nThe circumcircle of \\(\\triangle YTZ\\) is precisely \\((BEF)\\), which coincides with \\((O)\\) by Lemma\u202f2.  \nThe orthocentre of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\). By (2) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) coincides with the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) (both lie on the line \\(AO\\) at the same distance from \\(A\\)). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly the line described in Lemma\u202f4.  \n\nTherefore, by Lemma\u202f4, this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nThus the required tangency is proved. \u220e"
