ne \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of chord \\(CD\\). Because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on line \\(MN\\), \\(CD\\subset MN\\); thus \\(AP\\perp MN\\). No gap.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma\u202f2.**  \n*Statement:* \u201cThe points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic.\u201d  \n*Analysis:*  \n- \\(A,B\\) are the two intersection points of circles \\(\\Omega\\) (center \\(M\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (center \\(N\\)).  \n- \\(E\\) is defined as the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\); therefore \\(E\\in\\Omega\\) but generally \\(E\\notin\\Gamma\\).  \n- \\(F\\) is defined as the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore \\(F\\in\\Gamma\\) but generally \\(F\\notin\\Omega\\).  \n\nThere is no reason for a single circle to pass through all four points. The argument given (\u201cthey are the intersection points of the two circles with the line \\(AP\\)\u201d) is incorrect: a line intersecting two circles yields at most two points per circle, not a common circle through all four. Hence **Critical Error** \u2013 the conclusion is false, and all later reasoning that depends on this circle is invalid.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma\u202f3.**  \n*Statement:* Various claims about a homothety centred at \\(A\\), the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(M_{1}N_{1}\\), and the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n\n*Analysis of first claim:*  \n- The existence of a homothety centred at \\(A\\) mapping \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) would require that the two circles be homothetic with centre at one of their intersection points. This holds only when the two circles are tangent at that point or when the line joining their centres passes through the intersection point. In the present configuration the centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on line \\(MN\\) which indeed passes through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); however, the ratio of radii is not forced to equal the ratio of distances \\(AM:AN\\). No proof is supplied that such a homothety exists; the claim is unjustified. Hence **Critical Error**.\n\n*Analysis of second claim:*  \n- \\(M_{1}\\) and \\(N_{1}\\) are defined as mid\u2011points of \\(CD\\) and \\(AB\\). These are not mid\u2011points of any side of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Therefore they are not automatically points on the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). The statement that the nine\u2011point circle passes through them is false, leading to another **Critical Error**.\n\n*Analysis of Euler\u2011line claim:*  \n- The relation \\(AH=2\\,AO\\) is a misstatement of Euler\u2019s theorem (which says \\(OH = 3 OG\\) where \\(O\\) is the circumcentre, \\(G\\) the centroid). The orthocentre, circumcentre, and nine\u2011point centre are collinear, but the distances involve the circumcentre, not the nine\u2011point centre. Moreover, there is no justification that point \\(A\\) lies on this line for triangle \\(PMN\\). This is a **Justification Gap** (and also factually incorrect).\n\nBecause Lemma\u202f2 is already false, any subsequent use of the circle \\((O)\\) is baseless. Consequently the remainder of the proof collapses.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma\u202f4.**  \n*Statement:* A general tangency lemma involving a triangle \\(XYZ\\) and a point \\(T\\) on its circumcircle.  \n\n*Verification:*  \n- The reflection of the orthocentre \\(H\\) across side \\(YZ\\) indeed lies on the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\); denote it \\(H'\\).  \n- However, the claim \u201c\\(XH\\parallel H'X\\)\u201d is incorrect. In general, \\(XH\\) and \\(H'X\\) are not parallel; they intersect at \\(X\\). The intended property is that \\(X, H, H'\\) are collinear, not parallel. The subsequent angle equality \\(\\angle (H'X,XT)=\\angle HYT\\) is therefore unjustified. This constitutes a **Justification Gap** that undermines the lemma\u2019s proof.\n\nEven if Lemma\u202f4 were correct (which it is not as stated), its application later depends on the false Lemma\u202f2 and the erroneous identification of orthocentres in Lemma\u202f3.\n\n---\n\n**Final Step (Application).** 