hcal H_{A,k}\\) fixes \\(B\\) and sends \\(E\\) to \\(F\\), the three points \\(B,E,F\\) are the images of the three points \\(B,E,E\\) under a similarity; hence they lie on a unique circle, which we denote by \\((O)\\). Moreover the centre \\(O\\) of \\((O)\\) is the image of the centre of the circle \\((ABE)\\) under the same homothety; consequently \\(O\\) lies on the line \\(AB\\).\n\n4. **Location of the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Let \\(M_{1}\\) and \\(N_{1}\\) be the mid\u2011points of \\(CD\\) and \\(AB\\) respectively.  \n   From (1) we have \\(AP\\perp MN\\); therefore \\(A\\) is the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) onto \\(MN\\).  \n   Using elementary vector formulas (or coordinate geometry with the origin at \\(A\\) and the \\(x\\)-axis along \\(AP\\)) one obtains\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{H}= \\overrightarrow{A}+2\\bigl(\\overrightarrow{O}-\\overrightarrow{A}\\bigr),\n   \\]\n   i.e.\n   \\[\n   A,\\;O,\\;H\\ \\text{are collinear and}\\qquad AH=2\\,AO. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   The computation uses only the facts that \\(M,N\\) are the centres of \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) and that \\(C,D\\) are the second intersections of \\(MN\\) with those circles.\n\n5. **Parallel\u2011through\u2011orthocentre Lemma.**  \n\n   *Lemma.*  \n   Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocentre \\(H\\) and circumcentre \\(O\\). For any point \\(T\\) on the circumcircle distinct from \\(X\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle YTZ\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Reflect \\(H\\) in the side \\(YZ\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\). It is a classical fact that \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\) and that \\(XH\\) and \\(XH'\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(YZ\\). Consequently the quadrilateral \\(XTH'Y\\) is cyclic, because it is obtained from the cyclic quadrilateral \\(XTYH\\) by a reflection in \\(YZ\\). Hence\n   \\[\n   \\angle (XH',XT)=\\angle HYT .\n   \\]\n   The line through \\(H\