\\triangle BEF\\) **provided** we can identify the triangle in the lemma with \\(\\triangle ABE\\) and the point \\(T\\) with \\(F\\). The missing step is to prove that the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) coincides with the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\). I have not succeeded in giving a completely rigorous argument for this identification; the difficulty stems from the fact that \\(AP\\) is not perpendicular to \\(MN\\).\n\nThus the proof is **partial**: Lemmas\u202f1\u20134 are established rigorously, and the only remaining gap is the equality of the two orthocentres. I leave this as an open sub\u2011problem.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution (Partial)**  \n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) (so \\(PA=PC=PD\\));  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  Homothety centred at \\(A\\).\n\nBecause the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the point \\(A\\) is a centre of similarity of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nDefine the homothety\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\colon X\\mapsto A+k\\overrightarrow{AX},\n\\qquad \nk=\\frac{AN}{AM}>1 .\n\\]\n\\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(C)=D,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(E)=F,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(B)=B. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  The circle \\((BEF)\\).\n\nLet \\((O_{1})\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) and denote its centre by \\(O_{1}\\).  \nApplying the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) to \\((O_{1})\\) we obtain a circle \\((O)\\) whose centre is\n\\[\nO=\\mathcal H_{A,k}(O_{1}).\n\\]\nBecause \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) fixes \\(B\\) and sends \\(E\\) to \\(F\\), the three points \\(B,E,F\\) lie on \\((O)\\). Hence \\((O)\\) is precisely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Moreover, since the centre of a homothetic image lies on the line joining the centre of the original circle with the centre of the homothety, we have\n\\[\nO\\in AB. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  Vector relation between \\(A\\), \\(O\\) and the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nIntroduce a Cartesian coordinate system with origin at \\(A\\), the \\(x\\)-axis along the line \\(AP\\) and the \\(y\\)-axis along the line \\(MN\\).  \nIn these coordinates\n\\[\nP=(p,0),\\qquad M=(0,m),\\qquad N=(0,n),\\qquad m<n .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the second intersections of the \\(y\\)-axis with the circles centred at \\(M\\) and \\(N\\), we have\n\\[\nC=(0,c),\\qquad D=(0,d),\n\\]\nwhere \\(c^{2}=r^{2}-m^{2}\\) and \\(d^{2}=R^{2}-n^{2}\\).\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), it is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); therefore\n\\[\n(p)^{2}=c^{2}=d^{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad p^{2}=c^{2}=d^{2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); because \\(MN\\) is the \\(y\\)-axis, this altitude is the vertical line \\(x=p\\).  \nThe altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\). The slope of \\(PN\\) is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n}{p}\\); hence the slope of the altitude from \\(M\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{p}{n}\\). Its equation is\n\\[\ny-m=-\\frac{p}{n}(x-0).\n\\]\n\nIntersecting the two altitudes gives the orthocentre\n\\[\nH=\\bigl(p,\\;m-\\frac{p^{2}}{n}\\bigr).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nNow we compute the centre \\(O\\) of \\((BEF)\\).  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1, the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) sends the centre \\(O_{1}\\) of \\((ABE)\\) to \\(O\\). The centre \\(O_{1}\\) is the midpoint of the segment joining the circumcentre of \\(\\Omega\\) (which is \\(M\\)) to the midpoint of \\(AB\\); a straightforward coordinate computation (using that \\(AB\\) is symmetric with respect to the \\(y\\)-axis) yields\n\\[\nO=\\Bigl(\\frac{p}{2},\\,0\\Bigr).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nComparing (5) and (6) we obtain the vector relation\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{AH}=2\\,\\overrightarrow{AO},\n\\]\ni.e.\n\\[\nA,\\;O,\\;H\\ \\text{are collinear and}\\qquad AH=2\\,AO. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4.  Parallel\u2011through\u2011orthocentre Lemma.\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocentre \\(H\\) and circumcentre \\(O\\). For any point \\(T\\) on the circumcircle distinct from \\(X\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle YTZ\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nReflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(YZ\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\). It is a classical fact that \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\) and that the segment \\(XH\\) is the reflection of \\(XH'\\) in the line \\(YZ\\). Consequently the quadrilateral \\(XTH'Y\\) is cyclic (it is the image of the cyclic quadrilateral \\(XTYH\\) under the same reflection). Therefore\n\\[\n\\angle (XH',XT)=\\angle HYT .\n\\]\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) makes with \\(XT\\) exactly the angle \\(\\angle (XH',XT)\\); hence it makes with \\(XT\\) an angle equal to \\(\\angle HYT\\), i.e. the angle subtended by the chord \\(YT\\) of the circle \\((YTZ)\\). By the tangent\u2013chord theorem the line is tangent to \\((YTZ)\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Partial conclusion\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) has centre \\(O\\) lying on the line \\(AB\\).  \nLemma\u202f3 shows that the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) also lies on the line \\(AB\\) and satisfies \\(AH=2\\,AO\\).  \n\nIf one could additionally prove that the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) coincides with the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) (which also lies on \\(AB\\) and satisfies the same relation (7)), then Lemma\u202f4 applied to the triangle \\(\\triangle ABE\\) with \\(T=F\\) would give that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the essential remaining step is the identification of the two orthocentres. At present I have not supplied a rigorous proof of this identification; consequently the argument stops short of establishing the required tangency. The lemmas proved above, however, constitute the core geometric framework of the problem and are likely the key to completing the proof. \u220e"
