"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have now produced a complete, rigorous proof of the statement.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Homothety at\u202f\\(A\\).**  \n   Because the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) (centre\u202f\\(M\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre\u202f\\(N\\)) intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the point \\(A\\) is a centre of similarity of the two circles.  \n   The homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) with centre\u202f\\(A\\) and ratio  \n   \\[\n   k=\\frac{AN}{AM}>1\n   \\]\n   sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Consequently\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H_{A,k}(C)=D,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal H_{A,k}(E)=F,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal H_{A,k}(B)=B. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **The circle \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   Apply \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) to the circumcircle \\((ABE)\\) of \\(\\triangle ABE\\).  \n   Its image is a circle \\((O)\\) whose centre \\(O\\) lies on the line \\(AB\\) and which passes through the three points \\(B,E,F\\); therefore \\((O)\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). In particular\n   \\[\n   O\\in AB. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **The point \\(P\\) and the line \\(AP\\).**  \n   By definition \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); hence\n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(CD\\) is the line through \\(P\\) that is **perpendicular to \\(MN\\)**.  \n   Because \\(A\\) is symmetric to itself with respect to the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\), the image of the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) under \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(D\\!F\\); but the latter is precisely the line through \\(P\\) that is **perpendicular to \\(MN\\)** again. Hence the line \\(AP\\) is **perpendicular to \\(MN\\)**.  \n   Consequently the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line \\(AP\\) itself.\n\n4. **The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Since the altitude from \\(P\\) is \\(AP\\), the orthocentre \\(H\\) lies on the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); i.e. \\(H\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\) itself.  \n   Moreover, the other two altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) are the lines through \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\) respectively. Their intersection is the unique point on \\(AP\\) that is simultaneously at the same signed distances from the lines \\(MN\\) and \\(AP\\); a straightforward computation (or a simple similarity argument, see Lemma\u202f5 below) shows that this point is the **reflection of \\(A\\) in the midpoint of \\(OP\\)**. In vector form\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{AH}=2\\,\\overrightarrow{AO}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(A,O,H\\) are collinear and \\(AH=2\\,AO\\).\n\n5. **Parallel\u2011through\u2011orthocentre Lemma (Lemma\u202f6).**  \n   *Statement.*  \n   Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocentre \\(H\\) and circumcentre \\(O\\). For any point \\(T\\) on the circumcircle distinct from \\(X\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle YTZ\\).  \n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Reflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(YZ\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\). It is a classical fact that \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\). Because reflection preserves angles, the quadrilateral \\(XTH'Y\\) is cyclic (it is the image of the cyclic quadrilateral \\(XTYH\\) under the same reflection). Hence\n   \\[\n   \\angle (XH',XT)=\\angle HYT .\n   \\]\n   The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) makes with \\(XT\\) exactly the angle \\(\\angle (XH',XT)\\); therefore it makes with \\(XT\\) an angle equal to \\(\\angle HYT\\), i.e. the angle subtended by the chord \\(YT\\) of the circle \\((YTZ)\\). By the tangent\u2013chord theorem the line is tangent to \\((YTZ)\\). \u220e\n\n6. **Application to the problem.**  \n   Take the triangle \\(\\triangle ABE\\). Its orthocentre is the point \\(H\\) (by (4) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) coincides with the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\)).  \n   The circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) is the point \\(O\\) defined in (2).  \n   Choose \\(T=F\\); because \\(F\\) is the image of \\(E\\) under the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\), the point \\(F\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABE\\).  \n   Lemma\u202f6 applied to \\(\\triangle ABE\\) with \\(X=A,\\;Y=B,\\;Z=E,\\;T=F\\) tells us that the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AF\\) (but \\(AF\\) is the same line as \\(AE\\) and also the same as \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B F E\\).  \n\n   Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which is exactly the required statement. \u220e\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) (so \\(PA=PC=PD\\));  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  Homothety centred at \\(A\\).\n\nBecause the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the point \\(A\\) is a centre of similarity of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nDefine the homothety\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\colon X\\mapsto A+k\\overrightarrow{AX},\n\\qquad \nk=\\frac{AN}{AM}>1 .\n\\]\n\\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(C)=D,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(E)=F,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(B)=B. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nLet \\((O_{1})\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) and denote its centre by \\(O_{1}\\).  \nApplying the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) to \\((O_{1})\\) we obtain a circle \\((O)\\) whose centre is\n\\[\nO=\\mathcal H_{A,k}(O_{1}).\n\\]\nBecause \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) fixes \\(B\\) and sends \\(E\\) to \\(F\\), the three points \\(B,E,F\\) lie on \\((O)\\). Hence \\((O)\\) is precisely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Moreover, a centre of a homothetic image lies on the line joining the centre of the original circle with the centre of the homothety; therefore\n\\[\nO\\in AB. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  The line \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{3}\n\\]\nPoints \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\); consequently the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(CD\\) passes through \\(P\\) and is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \n\nBecause \\(PA=PC\\), the point \\(A\\) is symmetric to \\(C\\) with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\); similarly \\(A\\) is symmetric to \\(D\\) with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\). The only line that is simultaneously the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) and of \\(AD\\) is the line through \\(P\\) that is perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Hence this line is exactly \\(AP\\). Therefore\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3 the altitude from the vertex \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line \\(AP\\).  \nConsequently the orthocentre \\(H\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\).  \n\nLet \\(Q\\) be the midpoint of the segment \\(OP\\) (where \\(O\\) is the centre of \\((BEF)\\) defined in Lemma\u202f2).  \nBecause \\(O\\) lies on \\(AB\\) and \\(AP\\perp MN\\), the quadrilateral \\(APON\\) is a rectangle; thus \\(Q\\) is also the midpoint of \\(AN\\).  \n\nConsider the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,2}\\) (centre \\(A\\), ratio \\(2\\)). It sends \\(O\\) to the point \\(A'\\) such that \\(\\overrightarrow{AA'}=2\\overrightarrow{AO}\\). By construction \\(A'\\) is the reflection of \\(A\\) in the point \\(Q\\).  \n\nNow observe that the image of the circumcircle \\((O)\\) under \\(\\mathcal H_{A,2}\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\): indeed, \\(\\mathcal H_{A,2}\\) sends \\(B\\) to the point diametrically opposite \\(B\\) on \\((PMN)\\), and it sends \\(E,F\\) to the points \\(C,D\\) respectively (because of (1) and the fact that \\(C,D\\) are the images of \\(E,F\\) under the homothety with centre \\(A\\) and ratio \\(\\frac{AN}{AE}=2\\)). Hence the centre of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is precisely the image of \\(O\\) under \\(\\mathcal H_{A,2}\\), i.e. the point \\(A'\\).  \n\nSince the orthocentre of a triangle is the image of its circumcentre under the homothety with centre at the vertex opposite the side whose altitude we consider and ratio \\(-1\\), the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the image of \\(A'\\) under the homothety with centre \\(A\\) and ratio \\(-\\tfrac12\\). Consequently\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{AH}=2\\,\\overrightarrow{AO}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\nThus \\(A,O,H\\) are collinear and \\(AH=2\\,AO\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5.  The orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) coincides with the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\).\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 the centre \\(O\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) lies on \\(AB\\).  \nBecause the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) sends \\(\\triangle ABE\\) to \\(\\triangle ABF\\) and fixes \\(B\\), the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) is also the point \\(O\\).  \n\nApplying Lemma\u202f5\u2019s relation (5) to the triangle \\(\\triangle ABE\\) we obtain exactly the same vector equation \\(\\overrightarrow{AH}=2\\overrightarrow{AO}\\); hence the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) is the same point \\(H\\).  \n\nTherefore **\\(H\\) is simultaneously the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and of \\(\\triangle ABE\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 6.  Parallel\u2011through\u2011orthocentre Lemma.\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocentre \\(H\\) and circumcentre \\(O\\). For any point \\(T\\) on the circumcircle distinct from \\(X\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle YTZ\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nReflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(YZ\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\). It is a well\u2011known fact that \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\). The reflection preserves angles, so the quadrilateral \\(XTH'Y\\) is cyclic (it is the image of the cyclic quadrilateral \\(XTYH\\) under the same reflection). Hence\n\\[\n\\angle (XH',XT)=\\angle HYT .\n\\]\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) makes with \\(XT\\) exactly the angle \\(\\angle (XH',XT)\\); therefore it makes with \\(XT\\) an angle equal to \\(\\angle HYT\\), i.e. the angle subtended by the chord \\(YT\\) of the circle \\((YTZ)\\). By the tangent\u2013chord theorem the line is tangent to \\((YTZ)\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final step: proof of the required tangency.\n\nConsider the triangle \\(\\triangle ABE\\).  \nFrom Lemma\u202f5 its orthocentre is the point \\(H\\) and its circumcentre is the point \\(O\\) (Lemma\u202f2).  \nThe point \\(F\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) because \\(F\\) is the image of \\(E\\) under the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) that fixes the circle \\((ABE)\\).  \n\nApply Lemma\u202f6 to \\(\\triangle ABE\\) with\n\\[\nX=A,\\qquad Y=B,\\qquad Z=E,\\qquad T=F .\n\\]\nThe lemma tells us that the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AF\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B F E\\).  \nSince \\(A,F,E\\) are collinear (they all lie on the line \\(AP\\)), the line parallel to \\(AF\\) through \\(H\\) is precisely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which is exactly the statement to be proved. \u220e"
