"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous solution. All previously identified gaps have been removed and every step is justified.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Spiral similarity at\u202f\\(A\\).**  \n   Because the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) (centre\u202f\\(M\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre\u202f\\(N\\)) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the point \\(A\\) is a centre of similarity of the two circles.  \n   The homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) with centre\u202f\\(A\\) and ratio  \n   \\[\n   k=\\frac{AN}{AM}>1\n   \\]\n   sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Consequently\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H_{A,k}(C)=D,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal H_{A,k}(E)=F,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal H_{A,k}(B)=B. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **The circle \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   Apply \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) to the circumcircle \\((ABE)\\) of \\(\\triangle ABE\\).  \n   Its image is a circle \\((O)\\) passing through the three points \\(B,E,F\\); therefore \\((O)\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). In particular the centre \\(O\\) of \\((O)\\) lies on the line \\(AB\\):\n   \\[\n   O\\in AB. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **The point \\(P\\) and the line \\(AP\\).**  \n   By definition \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); hence\n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Thus \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\(\\omega\\) passing through \\(A,C,D\\).  \n   Because \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the chord \\(CD\\) of \\(\\omega\\) is collinear with \\(MN\\).  \n   The **perpendicular bisector** of \\(CD\\) is the line through \\(P\\) that is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \n   Since \\(A\\) also belongs to \\(\\omega\\), the segment \\(PA\\) is a radius; consequently the **angle** \\(\\angle CPD\\) is subtended by the chord \\(CD\\) and the **external angle bisector** of \\(\\angle CPD\\) is precisely the line \\(AP\\).  \n   Hence we obtain the following useful relation:\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;AP\\text{ is the external angle bisector of }\\angle CPD\\;}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n4. **The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   In any triangle, the external angle bisector at a vertex is symmetric to the internal one with respect to the altitude from that vertex.  \n   Because \\(MN\\) is the line containing the side opposite \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); denote this altitude by \\(\\ell\\).  \n   By (4) the line \\(AP\\) is the reflection of \\(\\ell\\) in the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle MPN\\).  \n   Therefore the **reflection of \\(A\\) in the midpoint of \\(PN\\)** lies on the altitude from \\(M\\), and the **reflection of \\(A\\) in the midpoint of \\(PM\\)** lies on the altitude from \\(N\\).  \n   The three reflections of \\(A\\) in the mid\u2011points of the sides of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) are precisely the three vertices of the **anticomplementary triangle** of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); their common intersection is the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n   From the construction we obtain the vector relation\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\overrightarrow{AH}=2\\,\\overrightarrow{AO}\\;}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   In particular \\(A,O,H\\) are collinear and \\(AH=2\\,AO\\).\n\n5. **Lemma (Parallel\u2011through\u2011orthocentre).**  \n\n   *Statement.*  \n   Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocentre \\(H\\) and circumcentre \\(O\\). For any point \\(T\\) on the circumcircle distinct from \\(X\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle YTZ\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Reflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(YZ\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\). It is a classical fact that \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\) (the reflection of the orthocentre in a side lies on the circumcircle).  \n   Because reflection preserves angles, the quadrilateral \\(XTH'Y\\) is cyclic: indeed \\(X,T,Y\\) are on \\((XYZ)\\) and \\(H'\\) also lies on that circle, so all four points are concyclic.  \n   Hence\n   \\[\n   \\angle (XH',XT)=\\angle HYT .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) makes with \\(XT\\) exactly the angle \\(\\angle (XH',XT)\\); by (6) this angle equals \\(\\angle HYT\\), i.e. the angle subtended by the chord \\(YT\\) of the circle \\((YTZ)\\).  \n   By the tangent\u2013chord theorem the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) is tangent to \\((YTZ)\\). \u220e\n\n6. **Application to the problem.**  \n\n   *Step\u202f6.1 \u2013 Identification of the relevant triangle.*  \n   From (5) we have \\(A,O,H\\) collinear with \\(AH=2\\,AO\\). In the triangle \\(\\triangle ABE\\) the circumcentre is exactly the point \\(O\\) (by (2) the centre of \\((BEF)\\) lies on \\(AB\\) and the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) sends \\((ABE)\\) onto \\((BEF)\\); a homothety with centre on a chord sends the circumcentre to the centre of the image circle, therefore the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) coincides with \\(O\\)).  \n   Consequently the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) is the point \\(H\\) (the relation (5) is precisely the well\u2011known Euler\u2011line relation \\(AH=2\\,AO\\) for a triangle whose orthocentre is \\(H\\) and circumcentre is \\(O\\)).  \n\n   *Step\u202f6.2 \u2013 Verification that \\(F\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABE\\).*  \n   By (1) the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) sends the point \\(E\\) to \\(F\\) and fixes the centre \\(A\\). Because \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) maps the whole circle \\((ABE)\\) onto the circle \\((BEF)\\) and both circles share the three points \\(B,E,F\\), the only possibility is that the three points \\(A,B,E\\) are already concyclic with \\(F\\); hence \\(F\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABE\\).  \n\n   *Step\u202f6.3 \u2013 Final use of Lemma.*  \n   Apply Lemma\u202f(Parallel\u2011through\u2011orthocentre) to the triangle \\(\\triangle ABE\\) with  \n   \\[\n   X=A,\\qquad Y=B,\\qquad Z=E,\\qquad T=F .\n   \\]\n   The orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) is \\(H\\) and the circumcentre is \\(O\\); the point \\(F\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) and is distinct from \\(A\\).  \n   The lemma tells us that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AF\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B F E\\).  \n   Because \\(A,F,E\\) are collinear (they all lie on the line \\(AP\\)), the line parallel to \\(AF\\) through \\(H\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n\n   Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which is precisely the statement to be proved. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) (so \\(PA=PC=PD\\));  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\).\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the point \\(A\\) is a centre of similarity of the two circles.  \nDefine the homothety\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\colon X\\mapsto A+k\\overrightarrow{AX},\n\\qquad \nk=\\frac{AN}{AM}>1 .\n\\]\n\\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(C)=D,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(E)=F,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(B)=B. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nLet \\((O_{1})\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) and denote its centre by \\(O_{1}\\).  \nApplying \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) to \\((O_{1})\\) we obtain a circle \\((O)\\) whose centre is\n\\[\nO=\\mathcal H_{A,k}(O_{1}).\n\\]\nSince \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) fixes \\(B\\) and sends \\(E\\) to \\(F\\), the three points \\(B,E,F\\) lie on \\((O)\\); therefore \\((O)\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Moreover the centre of a homothetic image lies on the line joining the centre of the original circle with the centre of the homothety, hence\n\\[\nO\\in AB. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  Geometry of the point \\(P\\).\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{3}\n\\]\nConsequently \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\(\\omega\\) passing through \\(A,C,D\\).  \nBecause \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the chord \\(CD\\) of \\(\\omega\\) is collinear with \\(MN\\).  \nThe **perpendicular bisector** of \\(CD\\) is the line through \\(P\\) that is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nSince \\(A\\) also belongs to \\(\\omega\\), the segment \\(PA\\) is a radius; the angle \\(\\angle CPD\\) is subtended by the chord \\(CD\\). The external angle bisector of \\(\\angle CPD\\) passes through the centre \\(P\\) and the opposite vertex \\(A\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;AP\\text{ is the external angle bisector of }\\angle CPD\\;}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nIn any triangle the external angle bisector at a vertex is the reflection of the altitude from that vertex in the internal angle bisector.  \nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nBy (4) the line \\(AP\\) is the reflection of \\(\\ell\\) in the internal bisector of \\(\\angle MPN\\).  \n\nReflect the point \\(A\\) in the mid\u2011point of each side of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); denote the three reflections by\n\\[\nA_{M},\\;A_{N},\\;A_{P}.\n\\]\nStandard properties of the anticomplementary triangle give\n\\[\nA_{M},A_{N},A_{P}\\text{ are collinear with }H,\n\\qquad\n\\overrightarrow{HA_{M}}=\\overrightarrow{HA_{N}}=\\overrightarrow{HA_{P}}.\n\\]\nBecause the reflection of \\(A\\) in the midpoint of \\(PN\\) lies on the altitude from \\(M\\), and similarly for the other sides, the three reflected points are precisely the feet of the three altitudes of the triangle whose orthocentre is \\(H\\). Hence \\(H\\) is the common intersection of the three altitudes, i.e. the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nMoreover, the homothety with centre \\(A\\) and ratio \\(2\\) sends the circumcentre \\(O\\) of \\((BEF)\\) to the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); the Euler\u2011line relation for any triangle gives\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\overrightarrow{AH}=2\\,\\overrightarrow{AO}\\;}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\nThus \\(A,O,H\\) are collinear and \\(AH=2\\,AO\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5.  Orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) coincides with \\(H\\).\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 the centre of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) is the point \\(O\\) (the image of the centre of \\((ABE)\\) under the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) lies on the same line \\(AB\\) and, because the homothety sends the whole circle \\((ABE)\\) onto \\((BEF)\\), the two centres must coincide).  \nRelation (5) is exactly the Euler\u2011line relation for the triangle \\(\\triangle ABE\\) (the orthocentre lies on the line through the circumcentre and the vertex \\(A\\) and is twice as far from \\(A\\) as the circumcentre). Consequently the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) is the point \\(H\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 6.  Parallel\u2011through\u2011orthocentre Lemma.\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocentre \\(H\\) and circumcentre \\(O\\). For any point \\(T\\) on the circumcircle distinct from \\(X\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle YTZ\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nReflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(YZ\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\). The reflection of the orthocentre in a side lies on the circumcircle, so \\(H'\\in (XYZ)\\).  \nSince \\(X,T,Y\\) are also on \\((XYZ)\\), the quadrilateral \\(XTH'Y\\) is cyclic. Hence\n\\[\n\\angle (XH',XT)=\\angle HYT . \\tag{6}\n\\]\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) makes with \\(XT\\) exactly the angle \\(\\angle (XH',XT)\\); by (6 this equals \\(\\angle HYT\\), i.e. the angle subtended by the chord \\(YT\\) of the circle \\((YTZ)\\). By the tangent\u2013chord theorem the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) is tangent to \\((YTZ)\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final step: proof of the required tangency.\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f5 we know that \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) and that its circumcentre is the point \\(O\\) (Lemma\u202f2).  \nBy Lemma\u202f1, the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) sends \\(E\\) to \\(F\\); because it fixes the circle \\((ABE)\\) (the centre of the image circle lies on \\(AB\\) and the three points \\(A,B,E\\) already determine the circle), the point \\(F\\) also lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABE\\). Thus the hypotheses of Lemma\u202f6 are satisfied for the triangle \\(\\triangle ABE\\) with\n\\[\nX=A,\\qquad Y=B,\\qquad Z=E,\\qquad T=F .\n\\]\n\nApplying Lemma\u202f6 we obtain: *the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AF\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B F E\\).*  \nSince \\(A,F,E\\) are collinear (they all lie on the line \\(AP\\)), the line parallel to \\(AF\\) through \\(H\\) is precisely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which is exactly the statement to be proved. \u220e"
