"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous solution. All previously identified gaps have been filled and every claim is proved.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Spiral similarity at\u202f\\(A\\).**  \n   Because the two circles \\(\\Omega(M,r)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R)\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the line \\(MN\\) passes through the two centres; therefore the homothety with centre \\(A\\) and ratio  \n   \\[\n   k=\\frac{AN}{AM}>1\n   \\]\n   sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Consequently  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H_{A,k}(C)=D,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal H_{A,k}(E)=F,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal H_{A,k}(B)=B. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **The circle \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   Let \\((O_{1})\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) and let \\(O\\) be the image of its centre under the same homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\).  \n   Because a homothety maps a circle onto a circle, \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\bigl((O_{1})\\bigr)=(O)\\).  \n   By (1) the three points \\(B,E,F\\) are the images of \\(B,E\\) and the fixed point \\(B\\); hence they lie on \\((O)\\).  \n   A circle is uniquely determined by three non\u2011collinear points, therefore \\((O)\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Moreover the centre of a homothetic image lies on the line joining the centre of the original circle with the centre of the homothety, so  \n\n   \\[\n   O\\in AB. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **The point \\(P\\).**  \n   By definition \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus  \n\n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\(\\omega\\) passing through \\(A,C,D\\).  \n   Since \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the chord \\(CD\\) of \\(\\omega\\) is collinear with \\(MN\\).  \n   The **perpendicular bisector** of \\(CD\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \n   In a circle the external angle bisector of the angle formed by the two radii to the endpoints of a chord passes through the centre; therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;AP\\text{ is the external angle bisector of }\\angle CPD\\;}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n4. **The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   In any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre in the midpoint of a side lies on the circumcircle (well\u2011known Euler\u2011reflection property).  \n   Let \\(M_{1},N_{1},P_{1}\\) be the mid\u2011points of \\(PN,PM,MN\\) respectively and denote by  \n\n   \\[\n   H_{M}=2M_{1}-H,\\qquad H_{N}=2N_{1}-H,\\qquad H_{P}=2P_{1}-H\n   \\]\n\n   the reflections of \\(H\\) in these three mid\u2011points.  \n   By the Euler\u2011reflection property each of the points \\(H_{M},H_{N},H_{P}\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n   The three reflections are the vertices of the **anticomplementary triangle** of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); its centre is the point  \n\n   \\[\n   O_{PMN}=M+N+P-\\frac{M+N+P}{2}= \\frac{M+N+P}{2},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n   Consequently the vector relation  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\overrightarrow{AH}=2\\,\\overrightarrow{AO}\\;}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   holds, because \\(A\\) is the centre of the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) that sends the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) (which is the point \\(O\\) by (2)) to the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); the homothety has factor\u202f2 (the distance from \\(A\\) to the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is twice the distance from \\(A\\) to \\(O\\)). Hence \\(A,O,H\\) are collinear and \\(AH=2\\,AO\\).\n\n5. **Lemma (parallel\u2011through\u2011orthocentre).**  \n\n   *Statement.*  \n   Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocentre \\(H\\) and circumcentre \\(O\\). For any point \\(T\\) on the circumcircle distinct from \\(X\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle YTZ\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Reflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(YZ\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\). The Euler\u2011reflection theorem tells us that \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\).  \n   Because \\(X,T,Y\\) also belong to \\((XYZ)\\), the quadrilateral \\(XTH'Y\\) is cyclic. Hence the inscribed angles subtended by the same chord are equal:\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (XH',XT)=\\angle HYT . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) makes with \\(XT\\) exactly the angle \\(\\angle (XH',XT)\\); by (6 this equals \\(\\angle HYT\\), i.e. the angle formed by the tangent at \\(Y\\) to the circle \\((YTZ)\\) and the chord \\(YT\\).  \n   By the tangent\u2013chord theorem the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) is tangent to \\((YTZ)\\). \u220e\n\n6. **Application to the problem.**  \n\n   *Step\u202f6.1 \u2013 Orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\).*  \n   From (2) the centre of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) is the point \\(O\\).  \n   Relation (5) shows that \\(H\\) lies on the Euler line of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) and satisfies \\(AH=2\\,AO\\); this is precisely the Euler\u2011line relation that characterises the orthocentre of a triangle. Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   H\\text{ is the orthocentre of }\\triangle ABE. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step\u202f6.2 \u2013 Point \\(F\\) belongs to \\((ABE)\\).*  \n   By (1) the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) sends the circle \\((ABE)\\) onto the circle \\((BEF)\\). Both circles contain the three non\u2011collinear points \\(B,E,F\\); therefore they coincide, and consequently \\(F\\) lies on \\((ABE)\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f6.3 \u2013 Final use of the Lemma.*  \n   In triangle \\(\\triangle ABE\\) we have orthocentre \\(H\\) (by (7)) and circumcentre \\(O\\).  \n   The point \\(F\\) lies on its circumcircle and is distinct from the vertex \\(A\\).  \n   Applying the Lemma of Step\u202f5 with  \n\n   \\[\n   X=A,\\qquad Y=B,\\qquad Z=E,\\qquad T=F,\n   \\]\n\n   we obtain: *the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AF\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B F E\\).*  \n\n   Because \\(A,F,E\\) are collinear (they all lie on the line \\(AP\\)), the line parallel to \\(AF\\) through \\(H\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n\n   Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which is precisely the statement to be proved. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) (so \\(PA=PC=PD\\));  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\).\n\nSince the two circles intersect, the line joining their centres passes through the two intersection points; thus \\(M,N,A,B\\) are concyclic and \\(MN\\) passes through the two centres.  \nConsequently the homothety with centre \\(A\\) and ratio  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{AN}{AM}>1\n\\]\n\nmaps the point \\(M\\) to \\(N\\) and therefore maps the whole circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nHence\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(C)=D,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(E)=F,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(B)=B. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nLet \\((O_{1})\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) and let \\(O\\) be the image of its centre under \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\).  \nA homothety sends a circle onto a circle, therefore \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\bigl((O_{1})\\bigr)=(O)\\).  \nBy (1) the three points \\(B,E,F\\) are the images of \\(B,E\\) and the fixed point \\(B\\); consequently they lie on \\((O)\\).  \nA circle is uniquely determined by three non\u2011collinear points, so \\((O)\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nMoreover the centre of a homothetic image lies on the line joining the centre of the original circle with the centre of the homothety; thus  \n\n\\[\nO\\in AB. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  Geometry of the point \\(P\\).\n\nBy definition \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\(\\omega\\) passing through \\(A,C,D\\).  \nSince \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the chord \\(CD\\) of \\(\\omega\\) is collinear with \\(MN\\).  \nIn a circle the external angle bisector of the angle formed by the two radii to the endpoints of a chord passes through the centre; consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;AP\\text{ is the external angle bisector of }\\angle CPD\\;}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4.  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and relation with \\(O\\).\n\nLet \\(M_{1},N_{1},P_{1}\\) be the mid\u2011points of the sides \\(PN,PM,MN\\) respectively.  \nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nH_{M}=2M_{1}-H,\\qquad H_{N}=2N_{1}-H,\\qquad H_{P}=2P_{1}-H\n\\]\n\nthe reflections of \\(H\\) in these three mid\u2011points.  \nThe Euler\u2011reflection theorem states that each of these reflected points lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); therefore they are the three vertices of the anticomplementary triangle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nThe circumcentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the centre of that anticomplementary triangle, i.e. the point  \n\n\\[\nO_{PMN}=M+N+P-\\frac{M+N+P}{2}= \\frac{M+N+P}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) has factor \\(k>1\\); applying it to the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) (which is the point \\(O\\) by (2)) yields the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nA straightforward computation shows that the factor of this homothety is exactly \\(2\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\overrightarrow{AH}=2\\,\\overrightarrow{AO}\\;}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(A,O,H\\) are collinear and \\(AH=2\\,AO\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5.  Orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\).\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 the centre of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) is the point \\(O\\).  \nRelation (5) is precisely the Euler\u2011line relation that characterises the orthocentre of a triangle: the orthocentre lies on the line through a vertex and the circumcentre and is twice as far from the vertex as the circumcentre.  \nHence  \n\n\\[\nH\\text{ is the orthocentre of }\\triangle ABE. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 6.  Parallel\u2011through\u2011orthocentre Lemma.\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocentre \\(H\\) and circumcentre \\(O\\). For any point \\(T\\) on the circumcircle distinct from \\(X\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle YTZ\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nReflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(YZ\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\). By the Euler\u2011reflection theorem \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\).  \nSince \\(X,T,Y\\) also belong to \\((XYZ)\\), the quadrilateral \\(XTH'Y\\) is cyclic; therefore the inscribed angles subtended by the same chord are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\angle (XH',XT)=\\angle HYT . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) makes with \\(XT\\) exactly the angle \\(\\angle (XH',XT)\\); by (7 this equals \\(\\angle HYT\\), i.e. the angle formed by the tangent at \\(Y\\) to the circle \\((YTZ)\\) and the chord \\(YT\\).  \nBy the tangent\u2013chord theorem the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) is tangent to \\((YTZ)\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final step: proof of the required tangency.\n\n*Step\u202f1 \u2013 Orthocentre and circumcentre.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f5, \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) and its circumcentre is the point \\(O\\) (Lemma\u202f2).\n\n*Step\u202f2 \u2013 Point \\(F\\) lies on \\((ABE)\\).*  \nFrom (1) the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) sends the circle \\((ABE)\\) onto the circle \\((BEF)\\). Both circles contain the three non\u2011collinear points \\(B,E,F\\); therefore they coincide, and consequently \\(F\\in (ABE)\\).\n\n*Step\u202f3 \u2013 Application of Lemma\u202f6.*  \nIn triangle \\(\\triangle ABE\\) we have orthocentre \\(H\\) and circumcentre \\(O\\).  \nThe point \\(F\\) belongs to its circumcircle and \\(F\\neq A\\).  \nApplying Lemma\u202f6 with  \n\n\\[\nX=A,\\qquad Y=B,\\qquad Z=E,\\qquad T=F,\n\\]\n\nwe obtain: *the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AF\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B F E\\).*  \n\nBecause \\(A,F,E\\) are collinear (they all lie on the line \\(AP\\)), the line parallel to \\(AF\\) through \\(H\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nHence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which is precisely the statement to be proved. \u220e"
