ntres \\(MN\\) **does not** pass through the intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). Instead, \\(MN\\) is perpendicular to the common chord \\(AB\\) and passes through the midpoint of that chord. Therefore the statement \u201c\\(M,N,A,B\\) are concyclic\u201d is false (four points are not generally concyclic; the only guaranteed concyclicity is that \\(M,N\\) lie on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\)). Consequently there is **no homothety centred at \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\)**. The claimed homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) does not exist.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 This false geometric fact invalidates Lemma\u202f1 and all subsequent lemmas that rely on the homothety.\n\n**Consequence:** Because Lemma\u202f1 is false, the proof cannot proceed using the claimed images \\(C\\mapsto D\\), \\(E\\mapsto F\\), \\(B\\mapsto B\\). Any statements derived from (1) are unsupported.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nRelies on Lemma\u202f1\u2019s homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) to claim that the image of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Since the homothety does not exist, the reasoning is baseless.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** (dependent on Lemma\u202f1). No further justification is possible.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 Geometry of the point \\(P\\)\n\n> **Quoted text:** \u201cSince \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the chord \\(CD\\) of \\(\\omega\\) is collinear with \\(MN\\). In a circle the external angle bisector of the angle formed by the two radii to the endpoints of a chord passes through the centre; consequently \\(AP\\) is the external angle bisector of \\(\\angle CPD\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe first part (chord \\(CD\\) lies on \\(MN\\)) is correct because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are defined as the second intersections of line \\(MN\\) with the two circles. However, the claim that \u201cthe external angle bisector of the angle formed by the two radii to the endpoints of a chord passes through the centre\u201d is **incorrect**. In a circle, the **perpendicular bisector** of a chord passes through the centre; there is no notion of an \u201cexternal angle bisector of the angle formed by the two radii\u201d that yields the line \\(AP\\). The statement that \\(AP\\) is the external angle bisector of \\(\\angle CPD\\) does not follow from the definition of \\(P\\) as the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). In fact, \\(AP\\) is simply a radius of the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) (since \\(PA=PC=PD\\)), but it is not generally an angle bisector of \\(\\angle CPD\\).\n\n**Classification:** **Justification Gap** \u2013 The conclusion may be true in special configurations, but the provided argument is insufficient and, as written, false. Since this lemma is not used later (the final argument relies on Lemma\u202f5 and Lemma\u202f6), we note the gap but do not let it invalidate the entire proof beyond the earlier critical error.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f4 \u2013 Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and relation with \\(O\\)\n\nThis lemma builds on the (non\u2011existent) homothety from Lemma\u202f1 and on a claimed computation that the homothety factor is exactly \\(2\\). Because the homothety does not exist, the derivation of \\(\\overrightarrow{AH}=2\\overrightarrow{AO}\\) is unfounded.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** (depends on Lemma\u202f1). The statement \\(\\overrightarrow{AH}=2\\overrightarrow{AO}\\) is not justified.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f5 \u2013 Orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\)\n\nThe lemma asserts that the relation \\(\\overrightarrow{AH}=2\\overrightarrow{AO}\\) characterises the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\). Even if the relation were true, the proof that \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) would require showing that \\(H\\) lies on the altitudes of that triangle, which is not done. Moreover, because Lemma\u2