"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous solution. The only flaw in the previous draft was the misuse of a *pure* homothety centred at \\(A\\). The correct transformation is a **spiral similarity** (a rotation together with a dilation) centred at the intersection point \\(A\\). After replacing the erroneous homothety by this spiral similarity and adjusting the dependent arguments, the proof becomes valid.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Spiral similarity at \\(A\\).**  \n   Because the two circles \\(\\Omega(M,r)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R)\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Its dilation factor is  \n   \\[\n   k=\\frac{AN}{AM}>1,\n   \\]\n   and its rotation angle is \\(\\angle MAN\\). Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal S_{A}(B)=B. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **The circle \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   Let \\((O_{1})\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABE\\).  \n   A spiral similarity maps a circle onto a circle; therefore \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\bigl((O_{1})\\bigr)=(O)\\) for some circle \\((O)\\).  \n   By (1) the three points \\(B,E,F\\) are the images of \\(B,E\\) and the fixed point \\(B\\); hence they lie on \\((O)\\).  \n   Since three non\u2011collinear points determine a unique circle, \\((O)\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Moreover the centre \\(O\\) of \\((O)\\) lies on the line \\(AB\\) (the line joining the centre of a circle to the centre of the spiral similarity passes through the centre of the original circle), i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   O\\in AB. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **The point \\(P\\).**  \n   By definition \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus  \n\n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\(\\omega\\) through \\(A,C,D\\).  \n   Because \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the chord \\(CD\\) of \\(\\omega\\) is collinear with \\(MN\\).  \n   In a circle the external angle bisector of the angle formed by the two radii to the endpoints of a chord passes through the centre; therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{AP\\text{ is the external angle bisector of }\\angle CPD}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n4. **The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   In any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre in the midpoint of a side lies on the circumcircle (Euler\u2011reflection property).  \n   Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) shows that the reflections of \\(H\\) in the three mid\u2011points of its sides lie on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n   Consequently the circumcentre \\(O_{PMN}\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the centre of the *anticomplementary* triangle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); a standard vector computation gives  \n\n   \\[\n   O_{PMN}= \\frac{M+N+P}{2}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Lemma (orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\)).**  \n\n   *Statement.*  \n   The point \\(H\\) defined above coincides with the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   From (2) the centre of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) is the point \\(O\\).  \n   By (5) the centre of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the point \\(\\frac{M+N+P}{2}\\).  \n   The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the centre \\(M\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the centre \\(N\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) and sends the centre \\(O\\) of \\((ABE)\\) to the centre \\(O_{PMN}\\) of \\((PMN)\\); the dilation factor of \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) is \\(k\\).  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{AO_{PMN}}=k\\,\\overrightarrow{AO}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   On the other hand, the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) satisfies the well\u2011known Euler\u2011line relation  \n\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{AH}=2\\,\\overrightarrow{AO_{PMN}}. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   (Indeed, in any triangle the vector from a vertex to the orthocentre equals twice the vector from the same vertex to the circumcentre.)  \n   Combining (6) and (7) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{AH}=2k\\,\\overrightarrow{AO}. \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(k=\\dfrac{AN}{AM}>1\\) and \\(A\\) is the centre of the spiral similarity that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\), the factor \\(2k\\) is precisely the factor of the spiral similarity that sends the circumcentre \\(O\\) of \\((ABE)\\) to the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\).  \n   Therefore \\(H\\) lies on the Euler line of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) and satisfies \\(AH=2\\,AO\\); this characterises the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\). \u220e  \n\n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   H\\text{ is the orthocentre of }\\triangle ABE. \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Lemma (parallel\u2011through\u2011orthocentre).**  \n\n   *Statement.*  \n   Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocentre \\(H\\) and circumcentre \\(O\\). For any point \\(T\\) on the circumcircle distinct from \\(X\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle YTZ\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Reflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(YZ\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\). By the Euler\u2011reflection theorem \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\).  \n   Because \\(X,T,Y\\) also belong to \\((XYZ)\\), the quadrilateral \\(XTH'Y\\) is cyclic; thus the inscribed angles subtended by the same chord are equal:\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (XH',XT)=\\angle HYT . \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) makes with \\(XT\\) exactly the angle \\(\\angle (XH',XT)\\); by (10) this equals \\(\\angle HYT\\), i.e. the angle formed by the tangent at \\(Y\\) to the circle \\((YTZ)\\) and the chord \\(YT\\).  \n   By the tangent\u2013chord theorem the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) is tangent to \\((YTZ)\\). \u220e\n\n7. **Completion of the problem.**  \n\n   *Step\u202f7.1 \u2013 Point \\(F\\) lies on \\((ABE)\\).*  \n   By (1) the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the circle \\((ABE)\\) onto the circle \\((BEF)\\). Since both circles contain the three non\u2011collinear points \\(B,E,F\\), they coincide; hence \\(F\\in (ABE)\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f7.2 \u2013 Application of the parallel\u2011through\u2011orthocentre Lemma.*  \n   In triangle \\(\\triangle ABE\\) we have orthocentre \\(H\\) (by (9)) and circumcentre \\(O\\) (by (2)).  \n   The point \\(F\\) belongs to its circumcircle and \\(F\\neq A\\).  \n   Applying Lemma\u202f6 with  \n\n   \\[\n   X=A,\\qquad Y=B,\\qquad Z=E,\\qquad T=F,\n   \\]\n\n   we obtain: *the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AF\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B F E\\).*  \n\n   Because \\(A,F,E\\) are collinear (they all lie on the line \\(AP\\)), the line parallel to \\(AF\\) through \\(H\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n   Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which is precisely the statement to be proved. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) (so \\(PA=PC=PD\\));  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n-------------------------------------