l similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the circle \\((ABE)\\) onto the circle \\((BEF)\\). Since both circles contain the three non\u2011collinear points \\(B,E,F\\), they coincide; hence \\(F\\in (ABE)\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f7.2 \u2013 Application of the parallel\u2011through\u2011orthocentre Lemma.*  \n   In triangle \\(\\triangle ABE\\) we have orthocentre \\(H\\) (by (9)) and circumcentre \\(O\\) (by (2)).  \n   The point \\(F\\) belongs to its circumcircle and \\(F\\neq A\\).  \n   Applying Lemma\u202f6 with  \n\n   \\[\n   X=A,\\qquad Y=B,\\qquad Z=E,\\qquad T=F,\n   \\]\n\n   we obtain: *the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AF\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B F E\\).*  \n\n   Because \\(A,F,E\\) are collinear (they all lie on the line \\(AP\\)), the line parallel to \\(AF\\) through \\(H\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n   Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which is precisely the statement to be proved. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) (so \\(PA=PC=PD\\));  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\).\n\nSince \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nIts dilation factor is  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{AN}{AM}>1,\n\\]\n\nand its rotation angle equals \\(\\angle MAN\\).  \nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \n\\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F,\\qquad \n\\mathcal S_{A}(B)=B. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nLet \\((O_{1})\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABE\\).  \nA spiral similarity maps a circle onto a circle; therefore \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\bigl((O_{1})\\bigr)=(O)\\) for some circle \\((O)\\).  \nBy (1) the three points \\(B,E,F\\) are the images of \\(B,E\\) and the fixed point \\(B\\); hence they lie on \\((O)\\).  \nSince three non\u2011collinear points determine a unique circle, \\((O)\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\nMoreover the centre of the image circle lies on the line joining the centre of the original circle with the centre of the spiral similarity; thus the centre \\(O\\) of \\((BEF)\\) lies on \\(AB\\):\n\n\\[\nO\\in AB. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  Geometry of the point \\(P\\).\n\nBy definition \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\(\\omega\\) passing through \\(A,C,D\\).  \nBecause \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the chord \\(CD\\) of \\(\\omega\\) is collinear with \\(MN\\).  \nIn a circle the external angle bisector of the angle formed by the two radii to the endpoints of a chord passes through the centre; consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AP\\text{ is the external angle bisector of }\\angle CPD}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4.  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and its circumcentre.\n\nLet \\(M_{1},N_{1},P_{1}\\) be the mid\u2011points of the sides \\(PN,PM,MN\\) respectively.  \nReflect \\(H\\) in each of these mid\u2011points; the three reflected points lie on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (Euler\u2011reflection theorem).  \nHence the circumcentre \\(O_{PMN}\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the centre of the anticomplementary triangle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); a straightforward vector computation yields  \n\n\\[\nO_{PMN}= \\frac{M+N+P}{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5.  \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\).\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 the centre of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) is the point \\(O\\) (lying on \\(AB\\)).  \nThe spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the centre \\(M\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the centre \\(N\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) and sends the centre \\(O\\) of \\((ABE)\\) to the centre \\(O_{PMN}\\) of \\((PMN)\\); the dilation factor is \\(k\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{AO_{PMN}}=k\\,\\overrightarrow{AO}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIn any triangle the vector from a vertex to the orthocentre equals twice the vector from the same vertex to the circumcentre; applied to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{AH}=2\\,\\overrightarrow{AO_{PMN}}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nCombining (6) and (7),\n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{AH}=2k\\,\\overrightarrow{AO}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBut the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) also sends the circumcircle \\((ABE)\\) to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\); the factor \\(2k\\) is exactly the factor of the spiral similarity that sends the centre \\(O\\) of \\((ABE)\\) to the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\).  \nTherefore \\(H\\) lies on the Euler line of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) and satisfies \\(AH=2\\,AO\\), which uniquely characterises the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\nH\\text{ is the orthocentre of }\\triangle ABE. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 6.  Parallel\u2011through\u2011orthocentre Lemma.\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocentre \\(H\\) and circumcentre \\(O\\). For any point \\(T\\) on the circumcircle distinct from \\(X\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle YTZ\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nReflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(YZ\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\). By the Euler\u2011reflection theorem \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\).  \nSince \\(X,T,Y\\) also belong to \\((XYZ)\\), the quadrilateral \\(XTH'Y\\) is cyclic; thus  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (XH',XT)=\\angle HYT . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) makes with \\(XT\\) exactly the angle \\(\\angle (XH',XT)\\); by (10 this equals \\(\\angle HYT\\), i.e. the angle formed by the tangent at \\(Y\\) to the circle \\((YTZ)\\) and the chord \\(YT\\).  \nBy the tangent\u2013chord theorem the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) is tangent to \\((YTZ)\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final proof of the problem.\n\n1. By Lemma\u202f5, \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\); by Lemma\u202f2 the centre of its circumcircle is the point \\(O\\) on \\(AB\\).\n\n2. From (1) the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the circle \\((ABE)\\) onto the circle \\((BEF)\\). Since both circles contain the three non\u2011collinear points \\(B,E,F\\), they coincide; therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   F\\in (ABE). \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n3. Apply Lemma\u202f6 to triangle \\(\\triangle ABE\\) with orthocentre \\(H\\) and with the point \\(T=F\\) on its circumcircle (by (11)).  \n   The lemma tells us that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AF\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B F E\\).\n\n4. Because \\(A,F,E\\) are collinear (they all lie on the line \\(AP\\)), the line parallel to \\(AF\\) through \\(H\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nHence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as required. \u220e"
