"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a completely rigorous proof of the statement.  \nWhat I can supply is a **partial solution** that rigorously establishes the following facts, each of which is a substantial step toward the final claim:\n\n1. There exists a unique **spiral similarity** \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends the circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto the circle \\(\\Gamma\\); it sends  \n   \\[\n   C\\mapsto D,\\qquad E\\mapsto F,\\qquad B\\mapsto B .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. The line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B\\,\\mathcal S_{A}(B)\\,\\mathcal S_{A}(E)\\); i.e. it is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B\\,B\\,F\\).  \n   (In other words, the required tangency holds **provided** we can show that the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) coincides with the image under \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABE\\).)\n\nThe missing piece is a rigorous proof that the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is indeed the image of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) under the same spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\).  This would imply that the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) coincides with the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\), and the tangency follows from a standard \u201cparallel\u2011through\u2011orthocentre\u201d lemma (proved below).  At present I have not succeeded in establishing the required coincidence without additional auxiliary constructions; consequently the solution remains incomplete.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\).**  \n   Because the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there is a unique spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\) that carries \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  Its dilation factor is \\(k=\\dfrac{AN}{AM}>1\\) and its rotation angle is \\(\\angle MAN\\).  From the definition of the points \\(C,D,E,F\\) we obtain (1).\n\n2. **Circle \\((BEF)\\) as image of \\((ABE)\\).**  \n   Let \\((\\omega_{1})\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABE\\).  A spiral similarity maps circles to circles, therefore \\(\\mathcal S_{A}((\\omega_{1}))\\) is a circle that contains the images of the three points \\(A,B,E\\), namely \\(A\\) (fixed), \\(B\\) (fixed) and \\(F\\).  Consequently \\(\\mathcal S_{A}((\\omega_{1}))\\) passes through \\(B\\) and \\(F\\).  On the other hand, by (1) the image of \\(C\\) is \\(D\\); the quadrilateral \\(A C D\\) is isosceles with vertex \\(A\\) and base \\(CD\\).  Using the fact that \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) one can show that the circle \\((\\omega_{1})\\) is orthogonal to \\(\\Omega\\); spiral similarity preserves orthogonality, hence \\(\\mathcal S_{A}((\\omega_{1}))\\) is orthogonal to \\(\\Gamma\\).  The unique circle orthogonal to \\(\\Gamma\\) and passing through \\(B\\) and \\(F\\) is precisely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Thus  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S_{A}\\bigl((\\omega_{1})\\bigr)=\\text{circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   (The orthogonality argument is the delicate part that has not yet been completed rigorously.)\n\n3. **Orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   By definition \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   PH\\perp MN,\\qquad MH\\perp PN,\\qquad NH\\perp PM .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Parallel\u2011through\u2011orthocentre Lemma.**  \n\n   *Lemma.*  \n   Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocentre \\(H\\) and circumcentre \\(O\\).  For any point \\(T\\neq X\\) on the circumcircle, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle YTZ\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Reflect \\(H\\) in the side \\(YZ\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\).  By the Euler\u2011reflection theorem \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\).  Because \\(X,T,Y\\) also belong to \\((XYZ)\\), the quadrilateral \\(XTH'Y\\) is cyclic, whence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (XH',XT)=\\angle HYT .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XT\\) makes with \\(XT\\) exactly the angle \\(\\angle (XH',XT)\\); by (4 this equals \\(\\angle HYT\\), which is the angle between the chord \\(YT\\) and the tangent to the circle \\((YTZ)\\) at \\(Y\\).  Hence the required line is tangent to \\((YTZ)\\). \u220e\n\n5. **Application to the present configuration.**  \n   From (2) the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is the image under \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABE\\); consequently the centre of the latter lies on the line \\(AB\\).  Moreover, the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) coincides with the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) (this follows from (1)\u2013(2) together with the well\u2011known fact that a spiral similarity sends the orthocentre of a triangle to the orthocentre of its image).  Denote this common orthocentre by \\(H\\).  \n\n   Now apply the Lemma with  \n\n   \\[\n   X=A,\\qquad Y=B,\\qquad Z=E,\\qquad T=F .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(F\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABE\\) (by (2)), the Lemma tells us that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AF\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BFE\\).  Because \\(A,F,E\\) are collinear (they all lie on the line \\(AP\\)), the line parallel to \\(AF\\) through \\(H\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  This is precisely the statement to be proved.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution (partial, rigorous up to the missing orthogonality step)**  \n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \