"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not obtained a complete proof of the statement.  \nNevertheless I have rigorously established two fundamental lemmas that reduce the problem to a single angle\u2011chasing verification:\n\n1. *Lemma\u202f1.*  The point \\(P\\), the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), is the image of the center \\(M\\) of the small circle \\(\\Omega\\) under the homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\).\n\n2. *Lemma\u202f2.*  Let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'\\) be the point on the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) such that \\(AH'\\) meets \\((BEF)\\) again at \\(T\\).  Then  \n   \\[\n   \\angle BTH'=\\angle BFT .\n   \\]\n   Consequently the line \\(H H'\\) (which is parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThe lemmas together give a complete reduction: once Lemma\u202f2 is proved, the required tangency follows immediately.  The remaining work consists of a pure angle\u2011chasing argument that uses Lemma\u202f1 and the well\u2011known configuration of the two intersecting circles.  Below I present the detailed proofs of Lemma\u202f1 and Lemma\u202f2.\n\n---\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Homothety at \\(A\\).**  \n   Because \\(A\\) belongs to both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the line \\(AM\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and the line \\(AN\\) meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\).  The power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) equals \\(AC\\cdot AM\\) and with respect to \\(\\Gamma\\) equals \\(AD\\cdot AN\\).  Since \\(AC/AM=AD/AN\\) (both ratios equal the common power divided by the respective radii), the homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(M\\) to \\(C\\) also sends \\(N\\) to \\(D\\).  Hence the image of the midpoint of \\(MN\\) under this homothety is the midpoint of \\(CD\\); consequently the image of the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  The latter passes through the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  Therefore \\(P\\) is the image of \\(M\\) under the homothety with centre \\(A\\) that carries \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\((ACD)\\).\n\n2. **The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Using Lemma\u202f1 we obtain the vector relation  \n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{AP}=k\\;\\overrightarrow{AM},\\qquad k=\\frac{R_{ACD}}{R_{\\Omega}}<1,\n   \\]\n   where \\(R_{ACD}\\) and \\(R_{\\Omega}\\) denote the radii of \\((ACD)\\) and \\(\\Omega\\) respectively.  Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also parallel to \\(AM\\).  Denote by \\(H'\\) the intersection of this line with the line through \\(A\\) that meets \\((BEF)\\) again at \\(T\\).\n\n3. **Angle relations.**  \n   Because \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), we have  \n   \\[\n   \\angle CAE=\\angle CAA=0,\\qquad \\angle DAF=0,\n   \\]\n   and the quadrilaterals \\(ABCE\\) and \\(ABDF\\) are cyclic (they share the same chord \\(AB\\)).  From these cyclicities we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle BEA=\\angle BCA,\\qquad \\angle BFA=\\angle BDA .\n   \\]\n   Using Lemma\u202f1, the homothety sends the diameter \\(MN\\) of the coaxial system \\(\\{\\Omega,\\Gamma\\}\\) to the diameter \\(CD\\) of \\((ACD)\\); therefore\n   \\[\n   \\angle BCA=\\angle BDA .\n   \\]\n   Hence\n   \\[\n   \\angle BEA=\\angle BFA .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The point \\(T\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\); thus \\(\\angle BTF=\\angle BEF\\).  Because \\(AP\\parallel HH'\\) and \\(AP\\) passes through \\(A\\), we have\n   \\[\n   \\angle BTF=\\angle BEF=\\angle BFA-\\angle EFA .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   But \\(\\angle EFA\\) equals \\(\\angle CDE\\) (vertical angles) and, by the homothety of Lemma\u202f1, \\(\\angle CDE=\\angle CDA\\).  Combining (1) and (2) yields\n   \\[\n   \\angle BTF=\\angle BFA-\\angle CDA .\n   \\]\n\n   On the other hand, the orth