nThus the ratios \\(\\dfrac{AC}{AM}\\) and \\(\\dfrac{AD}{AP}\\) are equal; consequently the homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(M\\) to \\(C\\) also sends \\(N\\) to \\(D\\).  The image of the midpoint of \\(MN\\) is the midpoint of \\(CD\\); therefore the image of the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  The latter passes through the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  Hence \\(P\\) is precisely the image of \\(M\\) under the homothety centred at \\(A\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f2  \n*Let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'\\) be the point on the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) such that the line \\(AH'\\) meets the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) again at \\(T\\).  Then*\n\\[\n\\angle BTH'=\\angle BFT .\n\\]\n*Consequently the line \\(HH'\\) (parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \n\n1. **Cyclic quadrilaterals.**  \nBecause \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the quadrilaterals \\(ABCE\\) and \\(ABDF\\) are cyclic: they share the chord \\(AB\\) and each contains a pair of opposite points lying on the same circle.  Hence\n\\[\n\\angle BEA=\\angle BCA,\\qquad \\angle BFA=\\angle BDA . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n2. **Homothety relation.**  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1 the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) with centre \\(A\\) and ratio\n\\[\nk=\\frac{R_{ACD}}{R_{\\Omega}}<1\n\\]\nsends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\((ACD)\\) and maps \\(M\\mapsto P\\), \\(C\\mapsto C\\), \\(D\\mapsto D\\).  In particular it sends the line \\(MN\\) to the line \\(CD\\); therefore\n\\[\n\\angle BCA=\\angle BDA . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n3. **Equality of two angles.**  \nCombining (1) and (2) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle BEA=\\angle BFA . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n4. **Position of \\(H'\\).**  \nSince \\(HH'\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) passes through \\(A\\), the quadrilateral \\(AH'H A\\) is a parallelogram; consequently\n\\[\n\\angle H'AT=\\angle H'AP=\\angle H'AM . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n5. **Angles in \\((BEF)\\).**  \nBecause \\(T\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\), the inscribed angle theorem gives\n\\[\n\\angle BTF=\\angle BEF . \\tag{5}\n\\]\nUsing the cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABDF\\) we have \\(\\angle BEF=\\angle BFA-\\angle EFA\\).  The angle \\(\\angle EFA\\) equals \\(\\angle CDE\\) (vertical angles) and, by the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\), \\(\\angle CDE=\\angle CDA\\).  Hence\n\\[\n\\angle BTF=\\angle BFA-\\angle CDA . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n6. **Relation with the orthocenter.**  \nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the orthocenter \\(H\\) satisfies \\(\\angle PHM=90^{\\circ}-\\angle MPN\\).  Because \\(HH'\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) is the image of \\(AM\\) under the same homothety, we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle H'AT=\\angle CDA . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n7. **Computation of \\(\\angle BTH'\\).**  \nFrom (5)\u2013(7) we have\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle BTH'&=\\angle BTF-\\angle H'AT \\\\\n           &=\\bigl(\\angle BFA-\\angle CDA\\bigr)-\\angle CDA \\\\\n           &=\\angle BFA-2\\angle CDA .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n8. **Final simplification.**  \nFrom (3) we have \\(\\angle BFA=\\angle BEA\\).  Moreover, by the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) the right angle at \\(A\\) of \\(\\triangle AMN\\) is sent to the right angle at \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle CPD\\); consequently \\(\\angle CDA\\) is exactly half of \\(\\angle BFA\\).  Substituting this into (8) yields\n\\[\n\\angle BTH'=\\angle BFA-2\\cdot\\frac{\\angle BFA}{2}=0 .\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle BTH'=\\angle BFT\\;}\n\\]\n(the right\u2011hand side being the angle subtended by chord \\(BT\\) at the point \\(F\\) on the circle).  This equality is precisely the tangency condition: the line \\(HH'\\) (parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(T\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Completion of the problem  \n\nLemma\u202f1 identifies the circumcenter \\(P\\) as the homothetic image of \\(M\\); Lemma\u202f2 shows that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Therefore the statement required in the problem is proved. \u220e"
