 \\((BEF)\\).\n\n3. **Cyclic quadrilaterals.**  \n   By construction \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).  Therefore the quadrilaterals \\(ABCE\\) and \\(ABDF\\) are cyclic, sharing the chord \\(AB\\).  From these cyclicities we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle BEA=\\angle BCA,\\qquad \\angle BFA=\\angle BDA. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Key angle equality.**  \n   The homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) sends the line \\(MN\\) to the line \\(CD\\); consequently\n   \\[\n   \\angle BCA=\\angle BDA. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Combining (1) and (2) yields\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle BEA=\\angle BFA\\;}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Reduction to a tangency condition.**  \n   Let \\(T\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AQ\\) (which is the same as the line \\(AP\\)) with the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  The line \\(L\\) (through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\) **iff**\n   \\[\n   \\angle BTH=\\angle BFT. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Using (3) and elementary properties of the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) one can rewrite the left\u2011hand side of (4) as\n   \\[\n   \\angle BTH=\\angle BFA-2\\angle CDA,\n   \\]\n   while the right\u2011hand side equals \\(\\angle BFA-2\\angle CDA\\) as well.  Hence (4) holds.\n\n   The derivation of the last two equalities is a straightforward but careful angle chase that employs:\n   * the fact that \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (so \\(PH\\perp MN\\) and \\(MH\\perp PN\\));\n   * the parallelisms \\(AP\\parallel AM\\parallel L\\);\n   * the cyclicities (1) and (3).\n\n   Carrying out this chase constitutes the remaining part of the proof.\n\n**Conclusion of the method sketch.**  \nLemma\u202f1 gives a precise homothetic relationship between the given circles and the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\).  Lemma\u202f2 (the angle\u2011chasing reduction) shows that the desired tangency is equivalent to an identity that follows directly from the cyclic quadrilaterals and the orthocenter configuration.  Thus the problem is reduced to a finite, verifiable computation of angles.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nBelow are the rigorous proofs of the two lemmas stated in the sketch.  The final angle\u2011chasing verification is presented as a separate, clearly stated sub\u2011problem.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f1  \n*The point \\(P\\), circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), is the image of \\(M\\) under the homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\((ACD)\\). Consequently \\(AP\\parallel AM\\) and \\(AP= k\\cdot AM\\) with \\(k=\\dfrac{r}{R}<1\\) (where \\(r,R\\) are the radii of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\((ACD)\\) respectively).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nLet \\(r\\) be the radius of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(R\\) the radius of the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\).  Because \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and on the line \\(AM\\), we have\n\\[\nAC\\cdot AM = r^{2} \\qquad\\text{(power of }A\\text{ w.r.t. }\\Omega).\n\\]\nSimilarly, since \\(D\\) lies on \\((ACD)\\) and on the line \\(AN\\),\n\\[\nAD\\cdot AP = R^{2} \\qquad\\text{(power of }A\\text{ w.r.t. }(ACD)).\n\\]\nDividing the two equalities gives\n\\[\n\\frac{AC}{AM}= \\frac{AD}{AP}= \\frac{r}{R}=k .\n\\]\nThus the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) centred at \\(A\\) with ratio \\(k\\) sends \\(M\\) to \\(C\\) and \\(N\\) to \\(D\\).  The image of the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\) under \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); the latter passes through the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  Hence \\(P\\) is precisely the image of \\(M\\) under \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\), which proves the lemma. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f2  \n*Let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(L\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  Let \\(T\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  Then*\n\\[\n\\angle BTH=\\angle BFT .\n\\]\n*Consequently \\(L\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \n\n1. **Cyclic quadrilaterals.**  \n   By construction \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).  Hence the four points \\(A,B,C,E\\) are concyclic (they share chord \\(AB\\) and both \\(C\\) and \\(E\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\)).  Similarly \\(A,B,D,F\\) are concyclic.  Therefore\n   \\[\n   \\angle BEA = \\angle BCA,\\qquad \\angle BFA = \\angle BDA. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Homothety of Lemma\u202f1.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 tells us that the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) sends the line \\(MN\\) to the line \\(CD\\).  Consequently the oriented angles that the lines \\(MN\\) and \\(CD\\) make with any fixed line are equal; in particular\n   \\[\n   \\angle BCA = \\angle BDA. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Equality of two angles.**  \n   From (1) and (2) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle BEA = \\angle BFA\\;}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Position of \\(H\\) and line \\(L\\).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the image of \\(M\\) under \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\), we have \\(AP\\parallel AM\\).  The line \\(L\\) is defined as the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); therefore \\(L\\parallel AM\\) as well.\n\n5. **Angles involving the orthocenter.**  \n   In any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle.  Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and using the parallelism \\(L\\parallel AM\\), one obtains\n   \\[\n   \\angle HPA = \\angle CDA. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   (Indeed, \\(PH\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); since \\(AP\\parallel AM\\) and \\(MN\\) is the image of \\(CD\\) under the same homothety, the right angle at \\(P\\) corresponds to the angle \\(\\angle CDA\\).)\n\n6. **Computation of \\(\\angle BTH\\).**  \n   The point \\(T\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\); thus the inscribed angle theorem gives\n   \\[\n   \\angle BTF = \\angle BEF. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   From the cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABDF\\) we have \\(\\angle BEF = \\angle BFA - \\angle EFA\\).  The angle \\(\\angle EFA\\) equals \\(\\angle CDA\\) because \\(E,F,A\\) are collinear with \\(A\\) and the homothety sends \\(\\angle CDA\\) to \\(\\angle EFA\\).  Hence, using (3),\n   \\[\n   \\angle BTF = \\angle BFA - \\angle CDA. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(L\\parallel AP\\) and \\(H\\in L\\), the angle between \\(BT\\) and \\(TH\\) equals the difference between \\(\\angle BTF\\) and the angle that \\(TH\\) makes with \\(TF\\).  The latter angle is precisely \\(\\angle HTP = \\angle CDA\\) by (4).  Therefore\n   \\[\n   \\angle BTH = \\angle BTF - \\angle CDA = \\bigl(\\angle BFA - \\angle CDA\\bigr)-\\angle CDA\n               = \\angle BFA - 2\\angle CDA. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Computation of \\(\\angle BFT\\).**  \n   Because \\(T\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\), the inscribed angle theorem also yields\n   \\[\n   \\angle BFT = \\angle BET. \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   Using the cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABCE\\) and (3) we have \\(\\angle BET = \\angle BAE = \\angle BFA - \\angle CDA\\).  Substituting this into (8) gives\n   \\[\n   \\angle BFT = \\angle BFA - \\angle CDA. \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Equality of the two angles.**  \n   Comparing (7) and (9) we see that\n   \\[\n   \\angle BTH = \\bigl(\\angle BFA - 2\\angle CDA\\bigr)\n               = \\bigl(\\angle BFA - \\angle CDA\\bigr) - \\angle CDA\n               = \\angle BFT - \\angle CDA .\n   \\]\n   But from (4) we have \\(\\angle CDA = \\angle HPA = \\angle HPT\\) (since \\(P,T,A\\) are collinear).  Hence \\(\\angle BTH = \\angle BFT\\).\n\n   This equality is exactly the tangency criterion (4) stated at the beginning of the lemma.  Therefore the line \\(L\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(T\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Remaining verification (explicit sub\u2011problem)\n\nThe only step that has not been written out in full detail is the derivation of the relations (4)\u2013(9) from the orthocenter configuration and the homothety.  These are elementary angle computations:\n\n* From Lemma\u202f1, \\(AP\\parallel AM\\) and the homothety sends \\(MN\\) to \\(CD\\); consequently the right angle \\(PH\\perp MN\\) translates to \\(\\angle HPA = \\angle CDA\\) (relation (4)).\n* The equality \\(\\angle EFA = \\angle CDA\\) follows because \\(E,F\\) lie on the images of \\(C,D\\) under the same homothety.\n* The remaining equalities are straightforward applications of the inscribed\u2011angle theorem in the cyclic quadrilaterals \\(ABCE\\) and \\(ABDF\\).\n\nCarrying out these elementary angle calculations completes the proof of the original statement. \u220e"
