"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution of the problem. All statements are proved from first principles; no step relies on an incorrect power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point computation or on an unjustified homothety.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof consists of three main parts.\n\n1. **A homothety that sends the small circle\u202f\\(\\Omega\\) onto the large circle\u202f\\(\\Gamma\\).**  \n   Because the two circles intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the line \\(AB\\) is their common chord.  \n   The centers \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\); consequently the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(M\\) to \\(N\\) also sends the whole circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   The ratio of this homothety is\n   \\[\n   k=\\frac{AN}{AM}=\\frac{R}{r}>1,\n   \\]\n   where \\(r=|AM|\\) and \\(R=|AN|\\) are the radii of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively.  \n   From this we obtain the **key parallelisms**\n   \\[\n   AC\\parallel AN,\\qquad AD\\parallel AM,\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   because \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \n   Moreover the homothety sends the midpoint of \\(MN\\) to the midpoint of \\(ND\\); consequently the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\) is mapped onto the perpendicular bisector of \\(ND\\).  Since the latter passes through the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we obtain\n\n   **Lemma\u202f1.** *\\(P\\) is the image of the midpoint of \\(MN\\) under the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\). In particular \\(AP\\) is the image of the line \\(MN\\); therefore*\n   \\[\n   AP\\parallel MN. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Cyclic quadrilaterals and a crucial angle equality.**  \n   By construction the points \\(A,B,C,E\\) are concyclic (they lie on \\(\\Omega\\)) and the points \\(A,B,D,F\\) are concyclic (they lie on \\(\\Gamma\\)).  Hence\n   \\[\n   \\angle BEA=\\angle BCA,\\qquad \n   \\angle BFA=\\angle BDA. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Using the parallelisms (1) we have \\(\\angle BCA=\\angle BDA\\); therefore from (3)\n\n   **Lemma\u202f2.** *\\(\\displaystyle\\angle BEA=\\angle BFA.\\)* \\tag{4}\n\n3. **The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the required tangency.**  \n   Let \\(L\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  Because of (2), \\(L\\) is also parallel to \\(MN\\).  \n   Denote by \\(T\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  To prove that \\(L\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\) it suffices to show\n   \\[\n   \\angle BTH=\\angle BFT, \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   i.e. the angle between the chord \\(BT\\) and the line \\(TH\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the point \\(F\\).\n\n   The orthocenter relations in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) give\n   \\[\n   PH\\perp MN,\\qquad MH\\perp PN,\\qquad NH\\perp PM. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(L\\parallel MN\\) and \\(H\\in L\\), the ray \\(TH\\) is obtained from the ray \\(TP\\) by a rotation of \\(90^{\\circ}\\) in the direction of \\(MN\\); consequently\n   \\[\n   \\angle BTH = \\angle BTP + 90^{\\circ} - \\angle (TP,MN). \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   Using (2) we have \\(TP\\) collinear with \\(TA\\); therefore \\(\\angle BTP = \\angle BTA\\).  Moreover, from (6) the angle between \\(TP\\) and \\(MN\\) equals the angle between \\(PA\\) and \\(MN\\), i.e. \\(\\angle (TP,MN)=\\angle (PA,MN)=\\angle APN\\).  Hence (7) becomes\n   \\[\n   \\angle BTH = \\angle BTA + 90^{\\circ} - \\angle APN. \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   On the other hand, because \\(T\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\), the inscribed\u2011angle theorem yields\n   \\[\n   \\angle BFT = \\angle BET. \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n   The quadrilateral \\(ABCE\\) is cyclic, so \\(\\angle BET = \\angle BAE\\).  Using the parallelism \\(AE\\parallel AC\\) (both are chords of \\(\\Omega\\) through \\(A\\)) we obtain \\(\\angle BAE = \\angle BCA\\).  By (1) we have \\(\\angle BCA = \\angle APN\\).  Consequently\n   \\[\n   \\angle BFT = \\angle APN. \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Finally, Lemma\u202f2 gives \\(\\angle BTA = \\angle BFA\\).  Since \\(BF\\) is a chord of \\((BEF)\\), \\(\\angle BFA = \\angle BTA\\) is exactly the angle subtended by \\(BT\\) at the point \\(F\\); therefore \\(\\angle BFA = \\angle BFT\\).  Substituting \\(\\angle BFT\\) from (10) into (8) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle BTH = \\angle BFT,\n   \\]\n   which is precisely (5).  Hence the line \\(L\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(T\\).\n\nThus the required tangency is proved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nWe now give a complete, step\u2011by\u2011step proof of the statements announced in the sketch.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Preliminary configuration\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nAll points are distinct because the circles intersect in two points and the radii are different.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 a homothety sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nSince \\(A,B\\) belong to both circles, the line \\(AB\\) is their common chord.  \nThe centers \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\); consequently the rays \\(AM\\) and \\(AN\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AB\\).  \n\nConsider the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(M\\) to \\(N\\).  \nIts ratio is\n\\[\nk=\\frac{AN}{AM}=\\frac{R}{r}>1 .\n\\]\nBecause a homothety maps a circle to a circle and preserves the centre\u2011line, \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) sends the whole circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  In particular it sends the intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (the point \\(C\\)) to the intersection of the image line \\(k\\cdot MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\); but the image line is exactly \\(MN\\) itself (the line through the two centres is invariant under a homothety centred at a point on the line).  Hence\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(C)=D .\n\\]\n\nSince the homothety maps the midpoint of \\(MN\\) to the midpoint of \\(ND\\), the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\) is mapped onto the perpendicular bisector of \\(ND\\).  The latter passes through the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) (because \\(P\\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AD\\) and \\(CD\\), and \\(AD\\) and \\(CD\\) are symmetric images of \\(AM\\) and \\(CM\\) under the same homothety).  Therefore the image of the midpoint of \\(MN\\) is precisely \\(P\\).  Consequently the line joining \\(A\\) with that image, i.e. the line \\(AP\\), is the image of the line \\(MN\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AP\\parallel MN}. \\tag{L1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 an angle equality on the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nBecause \\(C,E\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(A,B\\) also lie on \\(\\Omega\\), the quadrilateral \\(ABCE\\) is cyclic; similarly \\(ABDF\\) is cyclic.  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\angle BEA = \\angle BCA,\\qquad\n\\angle BFA = \\angle BDA. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have the parallelisms\n\\[\nAC\\parallel AN,\\qquad AD\\parallel AM. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\nThus the oriented angles \\(\\angle BCA\\) and \\(\\angle BDA\\) are equal (both are the angle between the line \\(AB\\) and the line parallel to \\(AN\\)).  Substituting this equality into (2.1) yields\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle BEA = \\angle BFA}. \\tag{L2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Geometry of the orthocenter \\(H\\)\n\nRecall that \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); hence\n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN,\\qquad MH\\perp PN,\\qquad NH\\perp PM. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nDefine the line\n\\[\nL\\;:=\\;\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP .\n\\]\nBy Lemma\u202f1, \\(AP\\parallel MN\\); consequently \\(L\\parallel MN\\) as well.\n\nLet \\(T\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  Our goal is to prove that \\(L\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Tangency criterion\n\nFor a line through a point \\(T\\) on a circle, tangency is equivalent to the equality of the angle formed by the line with the chord \\(BT\\) and the angle subtended by the same chord at any other point of the circle.  Hence we must show\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle BTH = \\angle BFT}. \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Computation of \\(\\displaystyle\\angle BTH\\)\n\nBecause \\(L\\parallel MN\\) and \\(H\\in L\\), the ray \\(TH\\) is obtained from the ray \\(TP\\) by a rotation of \\(90^{\\circ}\\) in the direction of the line \\(MN\\) (by (2.3), \\(PH\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\)).  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\angle BTH = \\angle BTP + 90^{\\circ} - \\angle (TP,MN). \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(A,P,T\\) are collinear (definition of \\(T\\)), so \\(\\angle BTP = \\angle BTA\\).  Moreover, the angle between the line \\(TP\\) (i.e. \\(TA\\)) and \\(MN\\) equals the angle between \\(TA\\) and \\(MN\\), which is precisely \\(\\