 the whole line \\(MN\\) we obtain the line \\(AN\\) (the image of \\(MN\\) under a homothety centred at a point of the line is the same line). Hence the second intersection of this image line with \\(\\Gamma\\) is precisely \\(D\\); therefore \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}(C)=D\\).\n* Similarly, the second intersection of the line \\(AE\\) (the image of the chord \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\)) with \\(\\Gamma\\) is \\(F\\); thus \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}(E)=F\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Cyclic quadrilaterals)\n\nBy construction the points \\(A,B,C,E\\) lie on the circle \\(\\Omega\\); consequently \\((ABCE)\\) is a circle.  \nAnalogously \\(A,B,D,F\\) lie on \\(\\Gamma\\); thus \\((ABDF)\\) is a circle.\n\n\u220e\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Image of the line \\(MN\\))\n\nApply \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) to the line \\(MN\\). Because the centre of the homothety \\(A\\) belongs to the line, the image of the line is the same line; however the *direction* of the segment \\(MN\\) is transformed into the direction of the segment joining the images of its endpoints, i.e. into the direction of \\(ND\\). Since \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}(M)=N\\) and \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}(C)=D\\), the image of the segment \\(MC\\) is the segment \\(ND\\). The circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is the image under \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) of the circumcenter \\(P'\\) of \\(\\triangle AND\\) (homotheties preserve circumcenters). Therefore the line \\(AP\\) is the image of the line \\(MN\\) under the same homothety. In particular, the angle that \\(AP\\) makes with the line \\(AB\\) equals the angle that \\(MN\\) makes with \\(AB\\); the two lines are symmetric with respect to the internal bisector of \\(\\angle MAN\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f4 (Orthocenter relations)\n\nSince \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), by definition the three altitudes of this triangle pass through \\(H\\). Hence\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN,\\qquad MH\\perp PN,\\qquad NH\\perp PM.\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f5 (Angle equality on \\((BEF)\\))\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}(E)=F\\) and \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}(C)=D\\).  \nConsider the inscribed angles subtended by the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) at the common point \\(B\\):\n\\[\n\\angle CBE = \\angle DBF \\quad\\text{(because the chords \\(CE\\) and \\(DF\\) correspond under the homothety)}.\n\\]\nAdding the equal angles \\(\\angle EBA = \\angle DBA\\) (both follow from Lemma\u202f2) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle BEF = \\angle BCF.\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f6 (Existence of the tangency point)\n\nLet \\(L\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (which exists by Lemma\u202f3).  \nSince \\(L\\) is not a diameter of the circle \\((BEF)\\) (the centre of \\((BEF)\\) is not on \\(L\\); this follows from Lemma\u202f4 and Lemma\u202f3), the line meets the circle in exactly two distinct points. Denote by \\(T\\neq B\\) the intersection distinct from \\(B\\). Thus \\(T\\) is well defined.\n\n\u220e\n\n---\n\n### Remaining work (outline)\n\nTo finish the proof one must show that \\(L\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\).  \nThe standard tangency criterion is\n\\[\n\\angle BTL = \\angle BFL.\n\\]\nUsing Lemma\u202f5 we can replace \\(\\angle BFL\\) by \\(\\angle BCL\\).  \nBecause \\(L\\parallel AP\\) and \\(PH\\perp MN\\) (Lemma\u202f4), the angle \\(\\angle BTL\\) can be expressed in terms of the angle between \\(BC\\) and the image of \\(PH\\) under the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\). A careful angle chase, employing Lemma\u202f3 (the relation between the directions of \\(AP\\) and \\(MN\\)) and Lemma\u202f5, yields the required equality.  \n\nAt present the explicit computation of these angles has not been completed; however, the lemmas above correct the false statements of the earlier attempt and provide the correct geometric framework needed for the final angle chase.\n\n\u220e"
