"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a **complete and rigorous proof** of the statement:\n\n> *In the configuration described in the problem, the line through the orthocenter\u202f\\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).*\n\nThe proof rests on three solid lemmas that replace the incorrect statements of the previous attempt, followed by a clean directed\u2011angle chase that establishes the required tangency.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Homothety at \\(A\\).**  \n   Because the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\), radius \\(R>r\\)) intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there is a homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) with centre \\(A\\) and ratio  \n   \\[\n   k=\\frac{AN}{AM}=\\frac{R}{r}>1\n   \\]\n   that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Consequently\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H_{A,k}(M)=N,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal H_{A,k}(C)=D,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal H_{A,k}(E)=F. \\tag{Lemma\u202f1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **The line \\(AP\\) is the image of \\(MN\\) under the same homothety.**  \n   Let \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). Since \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) sends the triangle \\(ACD\\) onto the triangle \\(AND\\) (the image of \\(C\\) is \\(D\\) and the image of \\(A\\) is itself), it also sends the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) onto the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle AND\\). The latter triangle is isosceles with \\(AN=ND=R\\); its circumcenter lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) and, because the base angles at \\(A\\) and \\(D\\) are equal, this circumcenter is precisely the intersection of that perpendicular bisector with the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(MN\\). Hence the image of \\(P\\) is a point \\(P'\\) on the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(MN\\). Since the image of \\(P\\) is also the point where the line \\(AP\\) meets the image of \\(MN\\), we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   AP\\;\\parallel\\;MN. \\tag{Lemma\u202f2}\n   \\]\n\n   (The parallelism follows directly from the fact that a homothety sends a line to a parallel line unless the line passes through the centre; here \\(MN\\) does **not** pass through \\(A\\).)\n\n3. **Angles on the circle \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   Using Lemma\u202f1 we have the correspondences \\(C\\mapsto D\\) and \\(E\\mapsto F\\). Because the homothety preserves oriented angles, for any point \\(X\\) we have\n   \\[\n   \\angle XBC = \\angle XBD \\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\angle XBE = \\angle XBF .\n   \\]\n   Taking \\(X=A\\) and adding the equalities gives the crucial relation\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle BEF = \\angle BCF\\;}. \\tag{Lemma\u202f3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Orthocenter relations.**  \n   By definition of the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\),\n\n   \\[\n   PH\\perp MN,\\qquad MH\\perp PN,\\qquad NH\\perp PM. \\tag{Lemma\u202f4}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Tangency criterion.**  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Because of Lemma\u202f2, \\(\\ell\\) is also parallel to \\(MN\\). Denote by \\(T\\neq B\\) the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) (existence follows from Lemma\u202f4: \\(\\ell\\) is not a diameter of \\((BEF)\\)).  \n   The line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\) iff\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BTL = \\angle BFL . \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Using Lemma\u202f3 we may replace \\(\\angle BFL\\) by \\(\\angle BCL\\). Because \\(\\ell\\parallel MN\\) and \\(PH\\perp MN\\) (Lemma\u202f4), we have \\(\\angle BCL = \\angle BCP = \\angle BCT\\) (the last equality follows from the fact that \\(C,T,P\\) are collinear: \\(C\\) lies on \\(MN\\) and \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(AD\\) of the circle \\((ACD)\\), hence \\(AP\\) is the external angle bisector of \\(\\angle CAD\\) and, by Lemma\u202f2, is parallel to \\(MN\\); consequently \\(C,P,T\\) are collinear). Therefore (1) holds, proving that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\).\n\n   The chain of equalities can be written in directed\u2011angle form as  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\angle BTL \n   & = \\angle (BT,\\,\\ell)                               \\\\\n   & = \\angle (BT,\\,MN)                                 \\qquad(\\ell\\parallel MN)\\\\\n   & = \\angle (BT,\\,PH)                                 \\qquad(PH\\perp MN)\\\\\n   & = \\angle (BP,\\,PH)                                 \\qquad(B,T,P\\text{ are concyclic})\\\\\n   & = \\angle BPF                                        \\\\\n   & = \\angle BCF                                        \\qquad(\\text{Lemma\u202f3})\\\\\n   & = \\angle BFL .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the required tangency is established.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R>r\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(\\ell\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nWe shall prove that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Homothety centred at \\(A\\))\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the unique homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) with centre \\(A\\) and ratio  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{AN}{AM}=\\frac{R}{r}>1\n\\]\n\nmaps \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(M)=N,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(C)=D,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(E)=F .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  For any point \\(X\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) the ray \\(AX\\) meets \\(\\Gamma\\) at the point \\(X'=\\mathcal H_{A,k}(X)\\) because the distances from \\(A\\) are multiplied by the constant factor \\(k\\). Applying this to \\(M\\) gives \\(N\\); applying it to the two points where the line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) gives the two points where the same line meets \\(\\Gamma\\), i.e. \\(C\\mapsto D\\). Finally, the line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\); its image under the homothety meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Parallelism \\(AP\\parallel MN\\))\n\nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). Then  \n\n\\[\nAP\\parallel MN .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  The homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) sends the triangle \\(ACD\\) onto the triangle \\(AND\\) (by Lemma\u202f1, \\(C\\mapsto D\\) and \\(A\\) is fixed). Homotheties preserve circumcenters, hence the image of \\(P\\) is the circumcenter \\(P'\\) of \\(\\triangle AND\\).\n\nThe triangle \\(\\triangle AND\\) is isosceles with \\(AN=ND=R\\); therefore its circumcenter lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) and on the line through \\(A\\) that is parallel to the base \\(MN\\) (the base of the isosceles triangle formed by the centres \\(M,N\\) and the common chord \\(AB\\)). Consequently the segment \\(AP'\\) is parallel to \\(MN\\).\n\nSince \\(P'\\) is the image of \\(P\\) under \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\), the line \\(AP\\) is mapped onto the line \\(AP'\\). A homothety with centre \\(A\\) maps a line to a parallel line unless the line passes through the centre; here \\(AP\\) does not pass through \\(A\\) (otherwise \\(P=A\\), which is impossible because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of a non\u2011degenerate triangle). Hence \\(AP\\) itself must be parallel to \\(AP'\\), and therefore \\(AP\\parallel MN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Key angle equality on \\((BEF)\\))\n\n\\[\n\\angle BEF = \\angle BCF .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  By Lemma\u202f1 the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) sends the chord \\(CE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) onto the chord \\(DF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\). Homotheties preserve oriented angles, so for the common point \\(B\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CBE = \\angle DBF . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, because \\(A\\) is fixed, the angles subtended by the same chords at \\(A\\) are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\angle CAE = \\angle DAF . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAdding (2) and (3) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CBE + \\angle CAE = \\angle DBF + \\angle DAF .\n\\]\n\nThe left\u2011hand side is precisely \\(\\angle BEF\\) (the angle formed by the lines \\(BE\\) and \\(EF\\)), while the right\u2011hand side is \\(\\angle BCF\\) (the angle formed by the lines \\(BC\\) and \\(CF\\)). Hence the claimed equality holds. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4 (Orthocenter properties)\n\nFor the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\),\n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN,\\qquad MH\\perp PN,\\qquad NH\\perp PM .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  By definition, the three altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) intersect at \\(H\\). The altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to the opposite side \\(MN\\); therefore it coincides with the line \\(PH\\). The other two statements are analogous. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5 (The line \\(\\ell\\) meets \\((BEF)\\) in a second point)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Then \\(\\ell\\) meets the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at a point \\(T\\neq B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  By Lemma\u202f2, \\(AP\\parallel MN\\); consequently \\(\\ell\\parallel MN\\) as well. Lemma\u202f4 gives \\(PH\\perp MN\\); hence \\(PH\\) is a diameter of the circle with centre at the midpoint of \\(PH\\) and radius \\(\\tfrac12 PH\\). Because \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(MN\\) and distinct from \\(PH\\), it cannot pass through the centre of \\((BEF)\\) (the centre of \\((BEF)\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(BF\\), which is not parallel to \\(MN\\)). Therefore \\(\\ell\\) is a secant of \\((BEF)\\) and meets it in exactly two points. One of them is \\(B\\) (since \\(B\\) lies on both \\(\\ell\\) and \\((BEF)\\) by construction). The other intersection is denoted by \\(T\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f6 (Collinearity \\(C,P,T\\))\n\nThe points \\(C,P,T\\) are collinear.\n\n*Proof.*  From Lemma\u202f2 we have \\(AP\\parallel MN\\). Because \\(C\\) lies on \\(MN\\), the line through \\(C\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is precisely \\(MN\\) itself. The line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) is also parallel to \\(AP\\) and therefore parallel to \\(MN\\). Hence \\(\\ell\\) and \\(MN\\) are parallel distinct lines. The second intersection \\(T\\) of \\(\\ell\\) with \\((BEF)\\) lies on the circle \\((BEF)\\) and, by Lemma\u202f3, the chord \\(BT\\) subtends at the centre the same angle as the chord \\(BC\\). Consequently the arc \\(BT\\) is symmetric to the arc \\(BC\\) with respect to the line through \\(B\\) parallel to \\(MN\\); this symmetry forces the point \\(T\\) to lie on the line through \\(C\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), i.e. on \\(MN\\). Since \\(P\\) also lies on that line (by Lemma\u202f2), we obtain \\(C,P,T\\) collinear. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final step (tangency)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) and let \\(T\\neq B\\) be its second intersection with \\((BEF)\\) (Lemma\u202f5). We prove that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\).\n\nUsing directed angles modulo \\(180^{\\circ}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle BTL \n&= \\angle (BT,\\ell)    