. Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(M)=N,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(C)=D,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(E)=F .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  For any point \\(X\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) the ray \\(AX\\) meets \\(\\Gamma\\) at the point \\(X'=\\mathcal H_{A,k}(X)\\) because the distances from \\(A\\) are multiplied by the constant factor \\(k\\). Applying this to \\(M\\) gives \\(N\\); applying it to the two points where the line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) gives the two points where the same line meets \\(\\Gamma\\), i.e. \\(C\\mapsto D\\). Finally, the line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\); its image under the homothety meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Parallelism \\(AP\\parallel MN\\))\n\nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). Then  \n\n\\[\nAP\\parallel MN .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  The homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) sends the triangle \\(ACD\\) onto the triangle \\(AND\\) (by Lemma\u202f1, \\(C\\mapsto D\\) and \\(A\\) is fixed). Homotheties preserve circumcenters, hence the image of \\(P\\) is the circumcenter \\(P'\\) of \\(\\triangle AND\\).\n\nThe triangle \\(\\triangle AND\\) is isosceles with \\(AN=ND=R\\); therefore its circumcenter lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) and on the line through \\(A\\) that is parallel to the base \\(MN\\) (the base of the isosceles triangle formed by the centres \\(M,N\\) and the common chord \\(AB\\)). Consequently the segment \\(AP'\\) is parallel to \\(MN\\).\n\nSince \\(P'\\) is the image of \\(P\\) under \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\), the line \\(AP\\) is mapped onto the line \\(AP'\\). A homothety with centre \\(A\\) maps a line to a parallel line unless the line passes through the centre; here \\(AP\\) does not pass through \\(A\\) (otherwise \\(P=A\\), which is impossible because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of a non\u2011degenerate triangle). Hence \\(AP\\) itself must be parallel to \\(AP'\\)