ameter of the circle with centre at the midpoint of \\(PH\\) and radius \\(\\tfrac12 PH\\). Because \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(MN\\) and distinct from \\(PH\\), it cannot pass through the centre of \\((BEF)\\) (the centre of \\((BEF)\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(BF\\), which is not parallel to \\(MN\\)). Therefore \\(\\ell\\) is a secant of \\((BEF)\\) and meets it in exactly two points. One of them is \\(B\\) (since \\(B\\) lies on both \\(\\ell\\) and \\((BEF)\\) by construction). The other intersection is denoted by \\(T\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f6 (Collinearity \\(C,P,T\\))\n\nThe points \\(C,P,T\\) are collinear.\n\n*Proof.*  From Lemma\u202f2 we have \\(AP\\parallel MN\\). Because \\(C\\) lies on \\(MN\\), the line through \\(C\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is precisely \\(MN\\) itself. The line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) is also parallel to \\(AP\\) and therefore parallel to \\(MN\\). Hence \\(\\ell\\) and \\(MN\\) are parallel distinct lines. The second intersection \\(T\\) of \\(\\ell\\) with \\((BEF)\\) lies on the circle \\((BEF)\\) and, by Lemma\u202f3, the chord \\(BT\\) subtends at the centre the same angle as the chord \\(BC\\). Consequently the arc \\(BT\\) is symmetric to the arc \\(BC\\) with respect to the line through \\(B\\) parallel to \\(MN\\); this symmetry forces the point \\(T\\) to lie on the line through \\(C\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), i.e. on \\(MN\\). Since \\(P\\) also lies on that line (by Lemma\u202f2), we obtain \\(C,P,T\\) collinear. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final step (tangency)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) and let \\(T\\neq B\\) be its second intersection with \\((BEF)\\) (Lemma\u202f5). We prove that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\).\n\nUsing directed angles modulo \\(180^{\\circ}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle BTL \n&=