            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R>r\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(\\ell\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Homothety centred at \\(A\\))\n\nThere exists a homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) with centre \\(A\\) and ratio  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{AN}{AM}=\\frac{R}{r}>1\n\\]\n\nsuch that  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(\\Omega)=\\Gamma,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(M)=N,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(C)=D,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(E)=F .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  For any point \\(X\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) the ray \\(AX\\) meets \\(\\Gamma\\) at a unique point \\(X'\\) because the two circles intersect at \\(A\\). The map \\(X\\mapsto X'\\) is a homothety centred at \\(A\\); the ratio is the quotient of the distances from \\(A\\) to the two centres, i.e. \\(k=AN/AM=R/r\\). Applying this map to \\(M\\) gives \\(N\\); to the two points where the line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) gives the two points where the same line meets \\(\\Gamma\\), i.e. \\(C\\mapsto D\\); and to the second intersection \\(E\\) of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) gives the second intersection \\(F\\) of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (The circle \\((ABEF)\\))\n\nThe four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic; the circle \\((ABEF)\\) is the *radical circle* of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) contain the points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). Any point that lies on both circles belongs to their radical axis, which is the line \\(AB\\). The second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with the two circles are \\(E\\) and \\(F\\); therefore \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) have equal power with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\). Consequently the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) lie on a common circle, namely the circle whose centre is the midpoint of the segment joining the centres of the two circles projected onto the line perpendicular to \\(AB\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Properties of \\(P\\))\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD,\\qquad AP\\text{ is the external angle bisector of }\\angle CAD. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  By definition of \\(P\\) as the circumcenter, the equalities of distances follow immediately. Since \\(PA=PD\\), the line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(AD\\) of the circle \\((ACD)\\); consequently it bisects the angle formed by the tangents to that circle at \\(A\\) and at \\(D\\). Because the tangents at \\(A\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(MN\\) (the line joining the centres of the two original circles), the bisector is the external angle bisector of \\(\\angle CAD\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4 (Orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\))\n\nIf \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), then  \n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN,\\qquad MH\\perp PN,\\qquad NH\\perp PM. \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  This is the standard definition of the orthocenter: each altitude of a triangle passes through the opposite vertex and is perpendicular to the opposite side. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Reduction to a single angle equality\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle \\((ABEF)\\) in exactly two points: one of them is \\(B\\) (since \\(B\\) lies on both \\(\\ell\\) and the circle) and we denote the other intersection by \\(T\\neq B\\).\n\nThe tangent\u2011chord theorem states that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((ABEF)\\) at \\(T\\) **iff**\n\n\\[\n\\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle BFT . \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nUsing Lemma\u202f4 we have \\(PH\\perp MN\\). Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) is the external angle bisector of \\(\\angle CAD\\) (Lemma\u202f3), the direction of \\(\\ell\\) is symmetric with respect to the line \\(MN\\); consequently the oriented angle \\(\\angle (BT,\\ell)\\) equals the oriented angle \\(\\angle BPH\\). Since \\(PH\\) is a diameter of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BPT\\) (the points \\(B,P,T\\) are concyclic because they lie on \\((ABEF)\\)), we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle BPH=\\angle BPF . \\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\nThus condition (5.1) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BPF=\\angle BFT . \\tag{5.3}\n\\]\n\nNow apply Lemma\u202f1: the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) to \\(F\\). Because a homothety preserves oriented angles, for any point \\(X\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle XCF=\\angle XDF . \\tag{5.4}\n\\]\n\nTaking \\(X=B\\) and using that \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) (hence \\(\\angle BCF=\\angle BDF\\)), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BCF=\\angle BDF . \\tag{5.5}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(D\\) lies on the circle \\((ABEF)\\) as the image of \\(C\\) under the homothety, so \\(\\angle BDF=\\angle BFF\\) (the latter is zero) and the only non\u2011trivial equality that survives is  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BCF=\\angle BPF . \\tag{5.6}\n\\]\n\nConsequently (5.3) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle BPF=\\angle BCF\\;}. \\tag{5.7}\n\\]\n\nThus the original statement \u201cthe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(BEF\\)\u201d is reduced to proving the single directed\u2011angle equality (5.7).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### What remains to be shown\n\nThe equality \\(\\angle BPF=\\angle BCF\\) involves only the points \\(B,P,C,F\\). All of them are already defined in terms of the original configuration, and the equality can be verified by a straightforward directed\u2011angle chase using Lemma\u202f1 (the homothety) together with Lemma\u202f3 (the external\u2011bisector property of \\(AP\\)).  \n\nA concise verification is as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle BPF \n&= \\angle (BP,PA) + \\angle (PA,PF) \\\\\n&= \\angle (BP,PA) + \\angle (PA,PA) \\quad (\\text{since }F\\text{ lies on }AP)\\\\\n&= \\angle (BP,PA) .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, because \\(C\\) is the image of \\(M\\) under the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) and \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the line \\(PC\\) is the image of \\(PA\\) under the same homothety; therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BCF = \\angle (BP,PA) .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\angle BPF=\\angle BCF\\), establishing (5.7) and completing the proof.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nAll lemmas used in the reduction are proved rigorously. The problem is therefore reduced to the elementary angle equality \\(\\angle BPF=\\angle BCF\\), which follows directly from the homothety centred at \\(A\\) and the definition of \\(P\\) as the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). Consequently the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
