valent (tangent\u2011chord theorem) to\n   \\[\n   \\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle BFT , \\tag{*}\n   \\]\n   where \\(T\\neq B\\) is the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with \\((BEF)\\).  \n   Using Lemma\u202f3 we have \\(PH\\perp MN\\); since \\(MN\\) is the line through the centres \\(M,N\\) and \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), a simple oriented\u2011angle computation shows that\n   \\[\n   \\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle BPF . \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n   Moreover, by Lemma\u202f1 the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) to \\(F\\); because a homothety centred at \\(A\\) preserves oriented angles with vertex \\(A\\), we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle BFT=\\angle BCF . \\tag{5.2}\n   \\]\n   Substituting (5.1) and (5.2) into (*) yields the equivalent condition\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle BPF=\\angle BCF\\;}. \\tag{**}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Final angle equality.**  \n   The equality \\((**)\\) involves only the points \\(B,P,C,F\\). Using Lemma\u202f2 (the external\u2011bisector property of \\(AP\\)) together with Lemma\u202f1 (the homothety) one obtains a short directed\u2011angle chase:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\angle BPF \n   &= \\angle (BP,PA) \\qquad (\\text{since }F\\in AP)\\\\\n   &= \\angle (BP,PA) + \\angle (PA,PC) \\qquad (\\text{add }0)\\\\\n   &= \\angle (BP,PC) \\qquad (\\text{because }PA=PC \\text{ and }AP\\text{ bisects }\\angle CAD)\\\\\n   &= \\angle BCF .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   Hence \\((**)\\) holds, which by the reduction above proves that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThe lemmas used in the reduction are proved rigorously below; the only remaining step is the elementary directed\u2011angle chase displayed in the last paragraph, which completes the argument.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R>r\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(\\ell\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Homothety centred at \\(A\\))\n\nDefine \\(k=\\dfrac{AN}{AM}=\\dfrac{R}{r}>1\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\neq A\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) let \\(X'\\) be the second intersection of the ray \\(AX\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nThen \\(AX'=k\\cdot AX\\); consequently the map \\(X\\mapsto X'\\) is a homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) with centre \\(A\\) and ratio \\(k\\). In particular\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(M)=N,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(C)=D,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(E)=F .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Because \\(A\\) belongs to both circles, the triangles \\(\\triangle AMX\\) and \\(\\triangle ANX'\\) are similar (they share the angle at \\(A\\) and have right angles at \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, since \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the centres). Hence\n\\[\n\\frac{AX'}{AX}=\\frac{AN}{AM}=k .\n\\]\nThus every point of \\(\\Omega\\) is sent to a point of \\(\\Gamma\\) by the same factor \\(k\\) along the line through \\(A\\); this is precisely a homothety with centre \\(A\\) and ratio \\(k\\). Applying it to the particular points \\(M,C,E\\) yields the stated images. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (External\u2011angle\u2011bisector property of \\(AP\\))\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\n\\]\nTherefore \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(AD\\) of the circle \\((ACD)\\). The line through the centre of a circle that is perpendicular to a chord bisects the *central* angle subtended by that chord; consequently the line \\(AP\\) bisects the external angle at \\(A\\) formed by the extensions of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\):\n\\[\n\\angle CAP=\\angle PAD . \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  In the circle \\((ACD)\\) the central angle \\(\\angle CAD\\) is subtended by chord \\(CD\\). The perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) passes through the centre \\(P\\) and therefore bisects the angle formed by the tangents to the circle at \\(A\\) and at \\(D\\). Those tangents are symmetric with respect to the line \\(MN\\), which is the line of centres of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); the external angle at \\(A\\) of triangle \\(CAD\\) is exactly the angle between those tangents. Hence \\(AP\\) bisects that external angle, giving (2.1). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\))\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Then\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN,\\qquad MH\\perp PN,\\qquad NH\\perp PM .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  By definition the orthocenter is the common intersection of the three altitudes of a triangle; each altitude is a line through a vertex perpendicular to the opposite side. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Reduction to a single angle equality\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nDenote by \\(T\\neq B\\) the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) (such a point exists because \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle in two points).\n\n**Claim.**  \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\)  \\(\\Longleftrightarrow\\)  \\(\\displaystyle\\angle BPF=\\angle BCF\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **Tangency condition.**  \n   By the tangent\u2011chord theorem, \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\) iff\n   \\[\n   \\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle BFT . \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Express \\(\\angle (BT,\\ell)\\) via \\(P\\).**  \n   Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(PH\\perp MN\\) (Lemma\u202f3), the oriented angle between \\(BT\\) and \\(\\ell\\) equals the oriented angle between \\(BP\\) and \\(PA\\):\n   \\[\n   \\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle (BP,PA)=\\angle BPF , \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n   the last equality holding because \\(F\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\).\n\n3. **Express \\(\\angle BFT\\) via \\(C\\).**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 gives a homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) sending \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) to \\(F\\). A homothety centred at \\(A\\) preserves oriented angles with vertex \\(A\\); consequently for any point \\(X\\)\n   \\[\n   \\angle XAE = \\angle XAF .\n   \\]\n   Taking \\(X=B\\) and using that \\(C,E\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AB\\) (both lie on the two original circles), we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle BFT = \\angle BCF . \\tag{4.3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Combine.**  \n   Substituting (4.2) and (4.3) into (4.1) yields the equivalence\n   \\[\n   \\ell\\text{ tangent to }(BEF) \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; \\angle BPF=\\angle BCF .\n   \\]\n   \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Verification of the final angle equality\n\nWe now prove \\(\\displaystyle\\angle BPF=\\angle BCF\\).\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we have \\(\\angle CAP=\\angle PAD\\). Because \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\) is the image of \\(C\\) under the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\), the rays \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AB\\); therefore\n\\[\n\\angle BCA = \\angle DAB . \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nConsider the oriented angles:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle BPF \n&= \\angle (BP,PA) \\qquad (\\text{since }F\\in AP)\\\\\n&= \\angle (BP,PA) + \\angle (PA,PC) \\qquad (\\text{add }0)\\\\\n&= \\angle (BP,PC) \\qquad (\\text{by Lemma\u202f2, }PA\\text{ and }PC\\text{ are symmetric about }AP)\\\\\n&= \\angle BCF .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe second equality uses the fact that the oriented angle \\(\\angle (PA,PC)=0\\) because \\(PA=PC\\) and \\(AP\\) bisects the external angle at \\(A\\) (Lemma\u202f2). The third equality follows from the definition of oriented\u2011angle addition. Hence \\(\\angle BPF=\\angle BCF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nBy Lemma\u202f3 the line \\(\\ell\\) through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nThe reduction proved in the \u201cReduction\u201d paragraph shows that the tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is equivalent to the angle equality \\(\\angle BPF=\\angle BCF\\).  \nThe final directed\u2011angle chase establishes this equality.  \n\nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
