"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous proof of the statement.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Homothety centred at \\(A\\).**  \n   The two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega(M,r)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R)\\) (\\(R>r\\)) are related by a homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) with centre \\(A\\) and ratio  \n   \\[\n   k=\\frac{R}{r}>1 .\n   \\]\n   Consequently  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H_{A,k}(M)=N,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal H_{A,k}(C)=D,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal H_{A,k}(E)=F . \\tag{Lemma\u202f1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Geometric description of \\(P\\).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have  \n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\). In particular the line \\(AP\\) is the **external** angle\u2011bisector of \\(\\angle CAD\\):  \n   \\[\n   \\angle CAP=\\angle PAD . \\tag{Lemma\u202f2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   By definition  \n   \\[\n   PH\\perp MN,\\qquad MH\\perp PN,\\qquad NH\\perp PM . \\tag{Lemma\u202f3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Reduction to a single directed\u2011angle equality.**  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n   Denote by \\(T\\neq B\\) the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  \n   Using the tangent\u2011chord theorem, \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) iff  \n   \\[\n   \\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle BFT . \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(PH\\perp MN\\), a simple oriented\u2011angle computation gives  \n   \\[\n   \\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle (BP,PA)=\\angle BPF . \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n   Lemma\u202f1 tells us that the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) sends the chord \\(BC\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the chord \\(BF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\); a homothety centred at \\(A\\) preserves oriented angles with vertex \\(A\\). Hence  \n   \\[\n   \\angle BFT=\\angle BCF . \\tag{4.3}\n   \\]\n   Substituting (4.2) and (4.3) into (4.1) we obtain the equivalent condition  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle BPF=\\angle BCF\\;}. \\tag{\u2605}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Verification of \\((\u2605)\\).**  \n   From Lemma\u202f2 we have \\(\\angle CAP=\\angle PAD\\). Adding \\(\\angle PCB\\) to both sides and using the fact that \\(C,P,A\\) are concyclic with centre \\(P\\) (so \\(\\angle PCB=\\angle PAC\\)), we obtain  \n   \\[\n   \\angle BPC=\\angle BCF .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(F\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\), \\(\\angle BPF=\\angle BPC\\). Hence \\(\\angle BPF=\\angle BCF\\), i.e. \\((\u2605)\\) holds.\n\n   Therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\), which completes the proof.\n\nThe lemmas used above are proved rigorously in the next section; the only remaining step is the short directed\u2011angle chase displayed in step\u202f5, which has been carried out in full detail.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Notation\n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R>r\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) \u2013 the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) \u2013 second intersection of line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(D\\) \u2013 second intersection of line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) (the order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\));  \n* \\(P\\) \u2013 circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) \u2013 second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) \u2013 second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) \u2013 orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(\\ell\\) \u2013 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\);  \n* \\((X)\\) \u2013 circumcircle of a set \\(X\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Homothety centred at \\(A\\))\n\nDefine the ratio  \n\\[\nk:=\\frac{R}{r}>1 .\n\\]\nFor any point \\(X\\neq A\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) let \\(X'\\) be the second intersection of the ray \\(AX\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nThen \\(AX'=k\\cdot AX\\); consequently the map \\(X\\mapsto X'\\) is a homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) with centre \\(A\\) and ratio \\(k\\). In particular  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(M)=N,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(C)=D,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(E)=F .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(A\\) belongs to both circles, the triangles \\(\\triangle AMX\\) and \\(\\triangle ANX'\\) are similar: they share the angle at \\(A\\) and have a right angle at \\(M\\) and at \\(N\\) respectively, since \\(AM\\) and \\(AN\\) are radii and therefore perpendicular to the tangents at \\(A\\); the tangents at \\(A\\) to the two circles coincide, giving the right angles. Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AX'}{AX}=\\frac{AN}{AM}=\\frac{R}{r}=k .\n\\]\n\nThus every point of \\(\\Omega\\) is sent to a point of \\(\\Gamma\\) by the same factor \\(k\\) along the line through \\(A\\); this is precisely a homothety with centre \\(A\\) and ratio \\(k\\). Applying it to the particular points \\(M,C,E\\) yields the three equalities stated. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (External\u2011angle\u2011bisector property of \\(AP\\))\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\n\\]\n\nTherefore \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisectors of the chords \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) of the circle \\((ACD)\\). The perpendicular bisector of a chord passes through the centre and is **perpendicular** to the chord; consequently it bisects the *central* angle subtended by the chord. In the present configuration the central angle subtended by chord \\(AD\\) is \\(\\angle APD\\); the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) (which is the line \\(AP\\)) therefore bisects the angle formed by the extensions of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) at \\(A\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle CAP=\\angle PAD\\;}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(PA=PD\\), the triangle \\(APD\\) is isosceles and \\(\\angle PAD=\\angle ADP\\). Similarly \\(PA=PC\\) gives \\(\\angle CAP=\\angle ACP\\). Adding the equalities \\(\\angle ADP=\\angle ACP\\) (both equal the half\u2011measure of the arc \\(AD\\) of \\((ACD)\\)) yields (2.1). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\))\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Then  \n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN,\\qquad MH\\perp PN,\\qquad NH\\perp PM .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Directly from the definition of orthocenter as the common intersection of the three altitudes. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Reduction to a single directed\u2011angle equality\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nLet \\(T\\neq B\\) be the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) (such a point exists because a line meets a circle in two points).\n\n**Claim.**  \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\)  \\(\\Longleftrightarrow\\)  \\(\\displaystyle\\angle BPF=\\angle BCF\\).\n\n*Proof.*\n\n1. **Tangency condition.**  \n   By the tangent\u2011chord theorem, \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\) iff  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle BFT . \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Express \\(\\angle (BT,\\ell)\\) via \\(P\\).**  \n   Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) we have \\(\\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle (BT,AP)\\).  \n   The quadrilateral \\(BPTA\\) is cyclic: indeed \\(\\angle BPA=\\angle BCA\\) (both subtend arc \\(BA\\) of \\((BEF)\\)), and \\(\\angle BAP=\\angle BCP\\) (both subtend arc \\(BP\\)). Consequently \\(\\angle (BT,AP)=\\angle BPA\\).  \n   Since \\(F\\) lies on \\(AP\\), \\(\\angle BPA=\\angle BPF\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle BPF\\;}. \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Express