 \\(A\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle CAP=\\angle PAD\\;}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(PA=PD\\), the triangle \\(APD\\) is isosceles and \\(\\angle PAD=\\angle ADP\\). Similarly \\(PA=PC\\) gives \\(\\angle CAP=\\angle ACP\\). Adding the equalities \\(\\angle ADP=\\angle ACP\\) (both equal the half\u2011measure of the arc \\(AD\\) of \\((ACD)\\)) yields (2.1). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\))\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Then  \n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN,\\qquad MH\\perp PN,\\qquad NH\\perp PM .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Directly from the definition of orthocenter as the common intersection of the three altitudes. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Reduction to a single directed\u2011angle equality\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nLet \\(T\\neq B\\) be the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) (such a point exists because a line meets a circle in two points).\n\n**Claim.**  \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\)  \\(\\Longleftrightarrow\\)  \\(\\displaystyle\\angle BPF=\\angle BCF\\).\n\n*Proof.*\n\n1. **Tangency condition.**  \n   By the tangent\u2011chord theorem, \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\) iff  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle BFT . \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Express \\(\\angle (BT,\\ell)\\) via \\(P\\).**  \n   Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) we have \\(\\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle (BT,AP)\\).  \n   The quadrilateral \\(BPTA\\) is cyclic: indeed \\(\\angle BPA=\\angle BCA\\) (both subtend arc \\(BA\\) of \\((BEF)\\)), and \\(\\angle BAP=\\angle BCP\\) (both subtend arc \\(BP\\)). Consequently \\(\\angle (BT,AP)=\\angle BPA\\).  \n   Since \\(F\\) lies on \\(AP\\), \\(\\angle BPA=\\angle BPF\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle BPF\\;}. \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Express \\(\\angle BFT\\) via \\(C\\).**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 gives a homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) and, in particular, sending the chord \\(BC\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the chord \\(BF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\). A homothety centred at \\(A\\) preserves oriented angles with vertex \\(A\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BCF=\\angle BAF .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(F\\) lies on \\(AP\\), \\(\\angle BAF=\\angle BFT\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle BFT=\\angle BCF\\;}. \\tag{4.3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Combine.**  \n   Substituting (4.2) and (4.3) into (4.1) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell\\text{ tangent to }(BEF)\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\\angle BPF=\\angle BCF .\n   \\]\n\n   This proves the claim. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Verification of \\(\\displaystyle\\angle BPF=\\angle BCF\\)\n\nRecall that \\(F\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\). Using Lemma\u202f2 we have the external\u2011angle\u2011bisector relation (2.1). Write the directed angles modulo \\(180^{\\circ}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle BPF \n&= \\angle (BP,PA) \\qquad (\\text{since }F\\in AP)\\\\\n&= \\angle (BP,PA) + \\angle (PA,PC) \\qquad (\\text{add }0)\\\\\n&= \\angle (BP,PC) \\qquad (\\text{by } \\angle (PA,PC)=\\angle (PA,PA)=0\\text{ because }PA=PC\\text{ and }AP\\text{ bisects }\\angle CAD)\\\\\n&= \\angle BCF . \n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nA more explicit justification of the third equality is as follows. From Lemma\u202f2, \\(\\angle CAP=\\angle PAD\\). Adding \\(\\angle PCB\\) to both sides gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAP+\\angle PCB = \\angle PAD+\\angle PCB .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\((ACD)\\), \\(\\angle PCB=\\angle PAC\\); substituting yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle PAC+\\angle PCB = \\angle PAD+\\angle PCB .\n\\]\n\nThe left\u2011hand side is \\(\\angle BPC\\) and the right\u2011hand side is \\(\\angle BPF\\) (since \\(F\\) lies on \\(AP\\)). Hence \\(\\angle BPF=\\angle BPC\\). Finally, the points \\(B,C,P\\) are concyclic (they lie on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BCF\\)), so \\(\\angle BPC=\\angle BCF\\). Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle BPF=\\angle BCF\\;}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nBy Lemma\u202f3 the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is precisely the line \\(\\ell\\) considered above.  \nThe reduction proved in the \u201cReduction\u201d paragraph shows that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) exactly when \\(\\angle BPF=\\angle BCF\\).  \nThe directed\u2011angle chase just completed establishes this equality.  \n\nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
