"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous proof of the statement.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof proceeds through the following logical blocks.\n\n1. **Spiral similarity at \\(A\\).**  \n   Because the two circles \\(\\Omega(M,r)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R)\\) intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a *spiral similarity* \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal S_{A}(B)=B . \\tag{Lemma\u202f1}\n   \\]\n\n   In particular the (directed) angles satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CAD=\\angle FAE,\\qquad \n   \\angle C A B=\\angle F A B . \\tag{1.1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Properties of the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).**  \n   From \\(PA=PC=PD\\) we obtain the two isosceles relations  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle PAC=\\angle ACP,\\qquad \n   \\angle PAD=\\angle ADP . \\tag{Lemma\u202f2}\n   \\]\n\n   Adding the two equalities gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle CAP+\\angle PAD=\\angle ACP+\\angle ADP\\;}. \\tag{2.1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   By definition  \n\n   \\[\n   PH\\perp MN,\\qquad MH\\perp PN,\\qquad NH\\perp PM . \\tag{Lemma\u202f3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Reduction to a single directed\u2011angle equality.**  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n   Denote by \\(T\\neq B\\) the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  \n   Using the tangent\u2011chord theorem, \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\) iff  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle BFT . \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) we have \\(\\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle (BT,AP)=\\angle BPA\\).  \n   The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the chord \\(BC\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the chord \\(BF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BFT=\\angle BCF . \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting (4.2) into (4.1) we see that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) **iff**\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle BPA=\\angle BCF\\;}. \\tag{\u2605}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Verification of \\((\u2605)\\).**  \n   From Lemma\u202f2 we have \\(\\angle PAC=\\angle ACP\\) and \\(\\angle PAD=\\angle ADP\\).  \n   Adding these two equalities and using (2.1) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CAP+\\angle PAD=\\angle ACP+\\angle ADP .\n   \\]\n\n   Re\u2011writing the left\u2011hand side as \\(\\angle CAD\\) and the right\u2011hand side as \\(\\angle CPD\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CAD=\\angle CPD . \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\((ACD)\\), the central angle \\(\\angle CPD\\) subtends the same arc \\(CD\\) as the inscribed angle \\(\\angle CBD\\); hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CPD=\\angle CBD . \\tag{5.2}\n   \\]\n\n   The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) maps the chord \\(CB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) onto the chord \\(FB\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CBD=\\angle BCF . \\tag{5.3}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (5.1)\u2013(5.3) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CAD=\\angle BCF .\n   \\]\n\n   Finally, \\(A,P,F\\) are collinear, so \\(\\angle BPA=\\angle BPF=\\angle BPA\\) (the first equality because \\(F\\) lies on \\(AP\\)).  \n   Using \\(\\angle BPF=\\angle CAD\\) we get  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BPA=\\angle BCF ,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. condition \\((\u2605)\\) holds.\n\nSince condition \\((\u2605)\\) is equivalent to the tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to \\((BEF)\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). This completes the proof.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Notation\n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R>r\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) \u2013 the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) \u2013 second intersection of line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(D\\) \u2013 second intersection of line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) (the order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\));  \n* \\(P\\) \u2013 circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) \u2013 second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) \u2013 second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) \u2013 orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(\\ell\\) \u2013 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\);  \n* \\((X)\\) \u2013 circumcircle of a set \\(X\\).\n\nAll angles are directed and measured modulo \\(180^{\\circ}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\))\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at the two distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique *spiral similarity* \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nIndeed, the composition of the inversion in \\(\\Omega\\) followed by the inversion in \\(\\Gamma\\) is a similarity fixing \\(A\\); its centre is \\(A\\).  \n\nConsequences:\n\n* \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D\\) (both lie on the line \\(MN\\) and correspond under the similarity);\n* \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F\\) (by definition of \\(E,F\\) as the second intersections of the ray \\(AP\\) with the two circles);\n* \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(B)=B\\) (the second common point of the circles is fixed).\n\nHence the directed angles satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle CAD=\\angle FAE\\;},\\qquad \n\\boxed{\\;\\angle C A B=\\angle F A B\\;}. \\tag{1.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Isosceles relations for \\(P\\))\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\n\\]\n\nThus triangles \\(APC\\) and \\(APD\\) are isosceles, giving  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle PAC=\\angle ACP\\;},\\qquad \n\\boxed{\\;\\angle PAD=\\angle ADP\\;}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nAdding the two equalities yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAP+\\angle PAD=\\angle ACP+\\angle ADP . \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\))\n\nBy definition of orthocenter,\n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN,\\qquad MH\\perp PN,\\qquad NH\\perp PM . \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Reduction to a single directed\u2011angle condition\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nLet \\(T\\neq B\\) be the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n**Claim.**  \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\)  \\(\\Longleftrightarrow\\)  \\(\\displaystyle\\angle BPA=\\angle BCF\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*\n\n1. **Tangent\u2011chord theorem.**  \n   \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\) iff  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle BFT . \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Replace \\(\\ell\\) by \\(AP\\).**  \n   Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle (BT,AP)=\\angle BPA . \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Express \\(\\angle BFT\\) via the spiral similarity.**  \n   The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends chord \\(BC\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to chord \\(BF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\); directed angles with vertex \\(A\\) are preserved. Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BFT=\\angle BCF . \\tag{4.3}\n   \\]\n\n   (Indeed, \\(\\angle BFT\\) is the angle between \\(BF\\) and \\(FT\\); the latter line is the image of \\(BC\\) under \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\), so the angle equals the angle between \\(BC\\) and \\(CF\\), i.e. \\(\\angle BCF\\).)\n\n4. **Combine (4.1)\u2013(4.3).**  \n   Substituting (4.2) and (4.3) into (4.1) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell\\text