e \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle (BT,AP)=\\angle BPA . \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n\n   The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the chord \\(BC\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the chord \\(BD\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) (Lemma\u202f1).  \n   Since \\(F\\) is the image of \\(E\\) and \\(E\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\), the image of the line \\(BC\\) under \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) is the line \\(BF\\).  \n   Hence the directed angle between \\(BF\\) and the chord \\(FC\\) equals the directed angle between \\(BC\\) and the chord \\(CD\\); i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle BFT=\\angle BCD\\;}. \\tag{4.3}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting (4.2) and (4.3) into (4.1) we obtain the equivalent condition  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle BPA=\\angle BCD\\;}. \\tag{\u2605}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Verification of \\((\u2605)\\).**  \n\n   *Step\u202f5.1.* From (2.2) we have \\(\\angle CAD=\\angle CPD\\).  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\((ACD)\\), the central angle \\(\\angle CPD\\) subtends the same arc \\(CD\\) as the inscribed angle \\(\\angle CBD\\) (the latter is taken on the same circle \\((ACD)\\) with vertex \\(B\\) on the circle \u2013 this is legitimate because \\(B\\) lies on \\((ACD)\\); indeed \\(B\\) belongs to both original circles, and the three points \\(A,C,D\\) already determine the circle \\((ACD)\\), which therefore passes through \\(B\\)). Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CPD=\\angle CBD . \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step\u202f5.2.* Applying the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to the chord \\(CB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) we obtain the chord \\(DB\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CBD=\\angle BCD . \\tag{5.2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step\u202f5.3.* Combining (5.1) and (5.2) we get  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle CAD=\\angle BCD\\;}. \\tag{5.3}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step\u202f5.4.* Because \\(A,P,F\\) are collinear, the angle \\(\\angle BPA\\) equals the angle formed by the lines \\(BP\\) and \\(PF\\); i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n