a set \\(X\\).\n\nAll angles are *directed* and measured modulo \\(180^{\\circ}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\))\n\nSince \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at the two distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that carries \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \n\\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F,\\qquad \n\\mathcal S_{A}(B)=B .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAD=\\angle FAE,\\qquad \n\\angle C A B=\\angle F A B . \\tag{1.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Properties of \\(P\\))\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\n\\]\n\nThus triangles \\(APC\\) and \\(APD\\) are isosceles and we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle PAC=\\angle ACP,\\qquad \n\\angle PAD=\\angle ADP . \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nAdding the two equalities gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAD=\\angle CPD . \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\))\n\nBy definition of orthocenter  \n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN,\\qquad MH\\perp PN,\\qquad NH\\perp PM . \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4 (Tangency criterion)\n\nLet \\(T\\neq B\\) be the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  \nThe tangent\u2013chord theorem (in the language of directed angles) states that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\) **iff**\n\n\\[\n\\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle BFT . \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle (BT,AP)=\\angle BPA . \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nThe spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the chord \\(BC\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the chord \\(BD\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) (Lemma\u202f1).  \nSince \\(F\\) is the image of \\(E\\) and \\(E\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\), the image of the line \\(BC\\) under \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) is the line \\(BF\\).  \nTherefore the directed angle between \\(BF\\) and the chord \\(FC\\) equals the directed angle between \\(BC\\) and the chord \\(CD\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\angle BFT=\\angle BCD . \\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (4.2) and (4.3) into (4.1) we obtain the equivalent condition  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle BPA=\\angle BCD\\;}. \\tag{\u2605}\n\\]\n\nThus proving \\((\u2605)\\) will finish the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5 (Verification of \\((\u2605)\\))\n\n1. From (2.2) we have \\(\\angle CAD=\\angle CPD\\).  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\((ACD)\\), the central angle \\(\\angle CPD\\) subtends the same arc \\(CD\\) as the inscribed angle \\(\\angle CBD\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CPD=\\angle CBD . \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n\n2. Applying the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to the chord \\(CB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) yields the chord \\(DB\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CBD=\\angle BCD . \\tag{5.2}\n   \\]\n\n3. Combining (5.1) and (5.2) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle CAD=\\angle BCD\\;}. \\tag{5.3}\n   \\]\n\n4. Because \\(A,P,F\\) are collinear, the angle \\(\\angle BPA\\) equals the angle formed by the lines \\(BP\\) and \\(PF\\); i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BPA=\\angle BPF . \\tag{5.4}\n   \\]\n\n5. The points \\(B,F,E,P\\) are concyclic. Indeed, \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) maps the circle \\((ACD)\\) onto the circle \\((BEF)\\); since \\(P\\) is the centre of \\((ACD)\\), it is also the centre of \\((BEF)\\). Hence \\(\\angle BPF\\) is a central angle of \\((BEF)\\) subtending arc \\(BF\\), while \\(\\angle BCF\\) is the inscribed angle subtending the same arc. Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BPF=\\angle BCF