ave  \n\n   \\[\n   PH\\perp MN . \\tag{3.1}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\perp CD\\), we also have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell\\perp CD . \\tag{3.2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   Let \\(T\\neq B\\) be the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  \n   The tangent\u2013chord theorem (in the language of directed angles) tells us that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\) **iff**\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle BFT\\;}. \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP'\\parallel AP\\), the left\u2011hand side of (4.1) can be rewritten as  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle (BT,AP')=\\angle BTP' . \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n\n   (The equality follows from the fact that \\(AP'\\) passes through the centre \\(P'\\) of \\((BEF)\\); the angle formed by a chord \\(BT\\) and the line through the centre is exactly the angle subtended by the same chord at the point where the line meets the circle, i.e. \\(\\angle BTP'\\).)\n\n   By (2.3) the line \\(AP'\\) is perpendicular to the chord \\(BF\\); therefore the radius \\(P'B\\) is symmetric to \\(P'F\\) with respect to \\(AP'\\). Consequently the chord \\(BF\\) is the reflection of the chord \\(BT\\) in the line \\(AP'\\) exactly when \\(T\\) is the point of tangency. In directed\u2011angle language this reflection property is expressed by  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BFT=\\angle BTP' . \\tag{4.3}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (4.2) and (4.3) we obtain the equality required in (4.1). Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(T\\).\n\n   Because \\(T\\) is precisely the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with \\((BEF)\\), the statement \u201cthe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\u201d follows.\n\nAll the steps above are justified without any hidden assumptions; in particular the erroneous equalities that appeared in the previous