on of \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  \nThe tangent\u2013chord theorem (expressed with directed angles) states that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\) **iff**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle BFT\\;}. \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\parallel AP'\\) (Lemma\u202f2), the angle on the left\u2013hand side of (4.1) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle (BT,AP')=\\angle BTP' . \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\n(The last equality follows from the well\u2011known fact that, for a chord \\(BT\\) of a circle, the angle formed by the chord and the line through the centre equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the point where that line meets the circle again; here the line through the centre is \\(AP'\\) and it meets the circle again at \\(T\\).)\n\nFrom (2.4) we know that \\(AP'\\) is perpendicular to the chord \\(BF\\). Hence the reflection of the chord \\(BT\\) in the line \\(AP'\\) coincides with the chord \\(BF\\). In directed\u2011angle language this reflection property is expressed by  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle BFT=\\angle BTP'\\;}. \\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\nCombining (4.2) and (4.3) we obtain exactly the equality required in (4.1). Therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(T\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) that passes through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). This completes the proof. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
