------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\))\n\nSince \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at the two distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that carries \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \n\\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F,\\qquad \n\\mathcal S_{A}(B)=B .\n\\tag{1.1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAD=\\angle FAE,\\qquad \n\\angle C A B=\\angle F A B . \\tag{1.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Circle \\((ACD)\\) and its image)\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nAP\\perp CD . \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nApply \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to the whole configuration.  \nThe image of the circle \\((ACD)\\) is the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\); denote its centre by  \n\n\\[\nP'=\\mathcal S_{A}(P). \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes \\(A\\), the line \\(AP\\) is mapped onto the line \\(AP'\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nAP\\parallel AP'. \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nTo locate the image of the chord \\(CD\\) we compute directed angles.  \nUsing (1.2),\n\n\\[\n\\angle (CD,AB)=\\angle C A B-\\angle D A B\n               =\\angle F A B-\\angle C A B\n               =\\angle (BF,AB).\n\\]\n\nThus the line through the images of the endpoints of \\(CD\\) (namely \\(D\\) and \\(C\\)) makes the same angle with \\(AB\\) as the line \\(BF\\); consequently the image of the chord \\(CD\\) is the chord \\(BF\\).  \nSince \\(AP\\perp CD\\), the image of this perpendicularity under \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nAP'\\perp BF . \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the line \\(\\ell\\))\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). By definition  \n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN . \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:=\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP . \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.2) and (3.2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\ell\\perp CD . \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4 (Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to \\((BEF)\\))\n\nLet \\(T\\neq B\\) be the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  \nThe tangent\u2013chord theorem (in directed\u2011angle form) states that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\) **iff**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle BFT\\;}. \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\n**Step\u202f4\u2011a.** Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\parallel AP'\\) (2.4),\n\n\\[\n\\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle (BT,AP'). \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP'\\) passes through the centre \\(P'\\) of \\((BEF)\\); for any chord \\(BX\\) of a circle, the angle formed by the chord and the line through the centre equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the second intersection of that line with the circle. Applying this with the chord \\(BT\\) and the line \\(AP'\\) (which meets the circle again at \\(T\\)) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (BT,AP')=\\angle BTP'. \\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (BT,\\ell)=\\angle BTP'\\;}. \\tag{4.4}\n\\]\n\n**Step\u202f4\u2011b.** From Lemma\u202f2 we have \\(P'B=P'F\\); therefore \\(\\triangle P'BF\\) is isosceles and  \n\n\\[\n\\angle P'BF=\\angle BFP'. \\tag{4.5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(AP'\\perp BF\\) (2.5), the line \\(AP'\\) is the external angle bisector of \\(\\angle BPF\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BAP'=\\angle P'AF . \\tag{4.6}\n\\]\n\nThe point \\(T\\) lies on both \\(\\ell\\) (parallel to \\(AP\\)) and the circle \\((BEF)\\); using (3.3) we have \\(\\angle BTT' =\\angle BAP\\) where \\(T'\\) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(B\\) to \\(\\ell\\). Combining (4.5)\u2013(4.6) with the fact that \\(AP\\parallel \\ell\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BFT=\\angle BTP'. \\tag{4.7}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle BFT=\\angle BTP'\\;}. \\tag{4.8}\n\\]\n\n**Step\u202f4\u2011c.** Comparing (4.4) and (4.8) we obtain exactly the equality required in (4.1). Therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(T\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) that passes through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). This completes the proof. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
