E\\to F\\), \\(B\\to B\\) and the angle equalities.\n   - **Assessment:** These follow from the definition of the spiral similarity; acceptable.\n\n*No errors in Lemma\u202f1.*\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Circle \\((ACD)\\) and its image)\n\n3. **Statement (2.1):** \u201c\\(PA=PC=PD\\).\u201d\n   - **Assessment:** Correct, as \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).\n\n4. **Statement (2.2):** \u201cThus \\(AP\\perp CD\\).\u201d\n   - **Issue:** **Critical Error.** For a circle, the line from the centre to the **midpoint** of a chord is perpendicular to the chord. Here \\(AP\\) joins the centre \\(P\\) to a vertex \\(A\\) of the triangle, not to the midpoint of chord \\(CD\\). No reason is given that \\(A\\) is the midpoint of \\(CD\\); in fact it is generally not. Hence the perpendicularity claim is false. This invalidates any later argument that relies on \\(AP\\perp CD\\).\n\n   *Because this is a critical error, the logical chain from this point onward that depends on (2.2) is broken. Nevertheless, we continue scanning the rest of the solution for independent parts.*\n\n5. **Application of \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to the whole configuration.** The claim that the image of circle \\((ACD)\\) is \\((BEF)\\) and that its centre is \\(P'=\\mathcal S_{A}(P)\\) (2.3) is plausible: a spiral similarity maps circles to circles, preserving the centre\u2019s image. However, the justification that the image of \\((ACD)\\) is exactly \\((BEF)\\) is omitted; it is assumed without proof. This is a **Justification Gap**.\n\n6. **Parallelism (2.4):** \u201cBecause \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes \\(A\\), the line \\(AP\\) is mapped onto the line \\(AP'\\); hence \\(AP\\parallel AP'\\).\u201d\n   - **Assessment:** Correct: a similarity maps a line through the centre to a line through the image of the centre; the two lines share the point \\(A\\), so they are the same line, not merely parallel. Actually \\(AP\\) and \\(AP'\\) coincide, not just parallel. The statement \u201cpar