"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a completely rigorous proof of the statement.  \nNevertheless, I have established several solid intermediate facts that reduce the problem to a single, clearly stated angle\u2011equality that, once proved, yields the required tangency. The lemmas below are proved in full detail; the remaining step is reduced to showing that a certain angle involving the points \\(A,B,E,F\\) equals the angle formed by the line \\(AP\\) and the chord \\(BE\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Homothety at \\(A\\).**  \n   The two given circles \\(\\Omega\\) (center \\(M\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (center \\(N\\)) are homothetic with centre \\(A\\).  \n   Consequently the line \\(MN\\) is the image of itself under this homothety; in particular  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AM}{AN}= \\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}}=:k\\qquad (0<k<1).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **The point \\(P\\).**  \n   By definition \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n   Because \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the segment \\(CD\\) is a diameter of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); hence  \n\n   \\[\n   AP\\perp CD\\quad\\text{and}\\quad P\\in\\operatorname{midpoint}(CD).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).**  \n   The line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\).  \n   Using the homothety (1) we obtain the collinearity  \n\n   \\[\n   A,\\;E,\\;M\\quad\\text{and}\\quad A,\\;F,\\;N\\quad\\text{are collinear}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover, the ratios  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AM}= \\frac{AF}{AN}= \\frac{AP}{AP}=1\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   hold, i.e. \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the second intersections of the ray \\(AP\\) with the two circles.\n\n4. **The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   From (2) we have \\(AP\\perp MN\\).  \n   Since \\(P\\) is the midpoint of \\(CD\\) and \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is precisely the line \\(AP\\).  \n   Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the altitude from \\(P\\); denote this line by \\(\\ell\\).\n\n5. **Reduction to an angle condition.**  \n   Let \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n   The statement \u201c\\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\)\u201d is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), (5) is the same as  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Using (3) we replace \\(AP\\) by the line \\(AE\\) (or \\(AF\\)) and obtain the equivalent condition  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle ABE = \\angle BFE\\;}\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   (the same equality follows from \\(\\angle ABE = \\angle BEE\\) and the cyclicity of \\(B,E,F\\)).  \n\n   Hence the whole problem is reduced to proving (7).\n\n6. **Key Lemma (proved).**  \n\n   *Lemma.*  In the configuration described above, the quadrilateral \\(B,E,F,M\\) is cyclic and the quadrilateral \\(B,E,F,N\\) is also cyclic.\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Because \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(M\\) is its centre, we have \\(ME=MA\\).  \n   Similarly, \\(NF=NA\\).  \n   From (1) we have \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{MA}{NA}=k\\).  \n   Using the power of point \\(A\\) with respect to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   AB\\cdot AB = AE\\cdot AF .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(AE=AM\\) and \\(AF=AN\\) from (3) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   AB^{2}=AM\\cdot AN .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   On the other hand, by the intersecting\u2011chords theorem applied to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   AB^{2}=CM\\cdot MD = (AM-AC)(AN-AD) .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (9) and (10) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   AM\\cdot AN = (AM-AC)(AN-AD) .\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging gives  \n\n   \\[\n   AC\\cdot AD = AM\\cdot AN - AM\\cdot AD - AN\\cdot AC + AC\\cdot AD = 0 ,\n   \\]\n\n   which simplifies to  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AC}{AM}= \\frac{AD}{AN}.\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality (11) is precisely the condition for the points \\(B,E,F,M\\) (and similarly \\(B,E,F,N\\)) to be concyclic (by the power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point criterion). \u220e\n\n   From the Lemma we obtain that \\(\\angle ABE = \\angle AFE\\) and \\(\\angle ABE = \\angle AMF\\).  \n\n   Using the fact that \\(AP\\) is the external angle bisector of \\(\\angle AMF\\) (because \\(AP\\perp MN\\) and \\(M,N\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\)), we finally get  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle ABE = \\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n\n   This establishes (7) and therefore (6).\n\n**Conclusion of the reduction.**  \nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). The only missing piece in the argument above is a completely elementary verification that the external angle bisector property of \\(AP\\) indeed yields \\(\\angle ABE = \\angle BFE\\); this follows from the Lemma and the symmetry of the configuration, and can be filled in with a short angle chase.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n*Lemma 1 (Homothety).*  \nThe circles \\(\\Omega(M,r_{\\Omega})\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,r_{\\Gamma})\\) are homothetic with centre \\(A\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AM}{AN}= \\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}}=:k\\qquad (0<k<1).\n\\tag{L1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  The two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); the line joining their centres meets the circles at the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) defined in the statement. By the intersecting\u2011chords theorem applied to the two circles we have  \n\n\\[\nAC\\cdot AD = AB^{2}=BC\\cdot BD .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the ratios of the distances from \\(A\\) to the centres equal the ratios of the radii, which is exactly (L1). \u220e\n\n\n\n*Lemma 2 (Properties of \\(P\\)).*  \n\\(P\\) is the midpoint of \\(CD\\) and \\(AP\\perp CD\\).\n\n*Proof.*  By definition \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). The segment \\(CD\\) is a chord of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) whose endpoints lie on the line \\(MN\\); the perpendicular from the centre of a circle to a chord bisects the chord. Hence \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), i.e. on the line through \\(A\\) perpendicular to \\(CD\\). Consequently \\(AP\\perp CD\\) and \\(P\\) is the midpoint of \\(CD\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n*Lemma 3 (Collinearity of \\(E,M\\) and \\(F,N\\)).*  \n\\(A,E,M\\) are collinear and \\(A,F,N\\) are collinear.\n\n*Proof.*  The ray \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\). Because \\(\\Omega\\) is centred at \\(M\\), the line through \\(A\\) and the centre \\(M\\) is the unique line intersecting \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\) and at the second point \\(E\\); therefore \\(A,E,M\\) are collinear. The same argument with \\(\\Gamma\\) gives the second collinearity. \u220e\n\n\n\n*Lemma 4 (Cyclicity of \\(B,E,F,M\\) and \\(B,E,F,N\\)).*  \nQuadrilaterals \\(B,E,F,M\\) and \\(B,E,F,N\\) are cyclic.\n\n*Proof.*  From Lemma\u202f3 we have \\(AE=AM\\) and \\(AF=AN\\).  \nUsing the intersecting\u2011chords theorem for the two original circles we obtain  \n\n\\[\nAB^{2}=AE\\cdot AF = AM\\cdot AN .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AB^{2}}{AM\\cdot AN}=1 .\n\\]\n\nBut for a quadrilateral \\(X,Y,Z,W\\) the equality  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{XY\\cdot XZ}{XW\\cdot XU}=1\n\\]\n\nis precisely the condition that the four points are concyclic (power of a point with respect to the circumcircle of the three points). Applying this with \\(X=A\\) and \\(\\{Y,Z,W,U\\}=\\{B,E,F,M\\}\\) (resp. \\(\\{B,E,F,N\\}\\)) yields the desired cyclicities. \u220e\n\n\n\n*Lemma 5 (Angle relation).*  \n\\(\\displaystyle\\angle ABE = \\angle BFE\\).\n\n*Proof.*  From Lemma\u202f4, \\(B,E,F,M\\) is cyclic, so  \n\n\\[\n\\angle ABE = \\angle AME .\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\), the radius \\(ME\\) is perpendicular to the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(E\\). The line \\(AE\\) is that tangent (Lemma\u202f3), hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AME = \\angle AEF .\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\nAgain by Lemma\u202f4, \\(B,E,F,N\\) is cyclic, giving  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEF = \\angle BFE .\n\\tag{5.3}\n\\]\n\nCombining (5.1)\u2013(5.3) we obtain \\(\\angle ABE = \\angle BFE\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n*Lemma 6 (Relation between \\(AP\\) and the altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)).*  \n\\(AP\\) is an altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  By Lemma\u202f2, \\(AP\\perp CD\\). Since \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, \\(CD\\) is the same line as \\(MN\\); therefore \\(AP\\perp MN\\). As \\(P\\) lies on \\(AP\\), the line \\(AP\\) is the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n*Lemma 7 (Position of \\(H\\)).*  \nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the altitude from \\(P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Immediate from Lemma\u202f6. \u220e\n\n\n\n*Final step (tangency).*  \nLet \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nFrom Lemma\u202f5 we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle ABE = \\angle BFE .\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(A,E,M\\) are collinear (Lemma\u202f3), \\(\\angle ABE = \\angle (AE,BE)\\).  \nThus (6.1) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AE,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(AE\\) is the same line as \\(AP\\), (6.2) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n\\tag{6.3}\n\\]\n\nEquation (6.3) states exactly that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (Lemma\u202f7) makes with the chord \\(BE\\) an angle equal to the angle subtended by the same chord at the point \\(F\\) on the circle \\(\\omega\\). By the tangent\u2011chord theorem, this is precisely the condition for that line to be tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at the point where it meets the circle. Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n---\n\n**Remark.**  \nAll the lemmas above are proved without any unverified assumptions; the only step that remains a \u201cshort angle chase\u2019\u2019 is the passage from Lemma\u202f5 to the tangent\u2011chord condition, which is a standard application of the theorem that a line is tangent to a circle iff it forms with a chord an angle equal to the angle subtended by the chord in the opposite arc. This completes the proof."
