u2011circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and \\(PH'\\parallel AP\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line \\(HH'\\) and is parallel to the altitude from \\(P\\). We shall denote this line by  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell\\;:=\\;\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP . \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Reduction to an angle equality.**  \n   Let \\(\\omega\\) be the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n   By the tangent\u2013chord theorem, \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) iff  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) coincides with the line \\(AE\\) (by (3)), (8) is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(AE,BE)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Using the cyclicities (6) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(AE,BE)=\\angle ABE =\\angle AFE =\\angle BFE , \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   where the first equality follows from the fact that \\(A,E,M\\) are collinear, the second from the cyclic quadrilateral \\(B,E,F,M\\), and the third from the cyclic quadrilateral \\(B,E,F,N\\).  \n   Hence (9) holds, and by (8) the line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminary configuration\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{1}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{2}>r_{1}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the points where the line \\(MN\\) meets the two circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Homothety at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the centre\u2011line \\(MN\\) is the radical axis of the two circles. Hence the homothety with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps \\(M\\) to \\(N\\) and preserves the line \\(MN\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AM}{AN}= \\frac{r_{1}}{r_{2}}=:k,\\qquad 0<k<1. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The point \\(P\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), it lies on the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\). Because \\(C,D\\) are collinear with \\(M,N\\), the chord \\(CD\\) is a sub\u2011segment of the line \\(MN\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nP\\text{ is the midpoint of }CD,\\qquad AP\\perp CD\\;(=MN). \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\). In a circle the line through a point of the circle and the centre meets the circle at the antipodal point; hence  \n\n\\[\nA,E,M\\text{ are collinear and }AE=AM. \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nAnalogously  \n\n\\[\nA,F,N\\text{ are collinear and }AF=AN. \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Two cyclic quadrilaterals\n\nFrom (4.1)\u2013(4.2) we have  \n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AF = AM\\cdot AN . \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nThe intersecting\u2011chords theorem applied to the two original circles gives  \n\n\\[\nAB^{2}=AE\\cdot AF . \\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\nCombining (5.1) and (5.2) yields  \n\n\\[\nAB^{2}=AM\\cdot AN . \\tag{5.3}\n\\]\n\nEquation (5.3) is precisely the equality of powers of the point \\(A\\) with respect to the circles \\((BEM)\\) and \\((BEN)\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nB,E,F,M\\ \\text{and}\\ B,E,F,N\\ \\text{are cyclic}. \\tag{5.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nFrom (3.1) we have \\(AP\\perp MN\\); thus \\(AP\\) is the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). In any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circum\u2011circle of the triangle. Reflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); denote the reflected point by \\(H'\\). Then  \n\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN),\\qquad PH'\\parallel AP . \\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly the line \\(HH'\\); we shall denote it by  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:=\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP . \\tag{6.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Tangency condition\n\nLet \\(\\omega\\) be the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nBy the tangent\u2013chord theorem, \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) iff  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{7.1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and, by (4.1), the line \\(AP\\) coincides with the line \\(AE\\), (7.1) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(AE,BE)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{7.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Evaluation of the angles\n\nFrom (4.1) we have \\(A,E,M\\) collinear, hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(AE,BE)=\\angle(AM,BE)=\\angle ABE . \\tag{8.1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(B,E,F,M\\) is cyclic (5.4), the inscribed\u2011angle theorem gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle ABE = \\angle AFE . \\tag{8.2}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, because \\(B,E,F,N\\) is cyclic,  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AFE = \\angle BFE . \\tag{8.3}\n\\]\n\nCombining (8.1)\u2013(8.3) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(AE,BE)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{8.4}\n\\]\n\nThus (7.2) holds, and by (7.1) the line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9.  Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) (i.e. the line \\(\\ell\\) defined in (6.2)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\u220e"
