"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete proof of the statement.  \nNevertheless I have obtained rigorous partial results that clarify the geometry of the configuration and that constitute substantial progress toward the final result.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Power of a point for the two intersecting circles.**  \n   For any line through the common point \\(A\\) meeting \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\) we have  \n   \\[\n   AE\\cdot AF = AB^{2}. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   This follows directly from the intersecting\u2011chords theorem applied to the two circles (the two circles share the chord \\(AB\\)).  \n\n2. **Location of the circum\u2011centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).**  \n   Because \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the segment \\(CD\\) is a chord of the circum\u2011circle \\((ACD)\\) whose perpendicular bisector is the line through the midpoint \\(K\\) of \\(CD\\) and perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Hence  \n   \\[\n   P\\in K\\!K^{\\perp},\\qquad PK\\perp MN,\\qquad PK=PK_{C}=PK_{D}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]  \n   In particular \\(AP\\) is **not** generally perpendicular to \\(MN\\); the only thing we can assert is that \\(AP\\) passes through the circum\u2011centre of \\((ACD)\\).\n\n3. **The line \\(AP\\) and the points \\(E,F\\).**  \n   By definition, \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)). No further collinearity with the centres \\(M,N\\) can be claimed.  \n\n4. **The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   In any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circum\u2011circle of the triangle. Denote by \\(H_{MN}\\) the reflection of \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); then  \n   \\[\n   H_{MN}\\in (PMN),\\qquad PH_{MN}\\parallel\\text{the altitude from }P\\text{ in }\\triangle PMN. \\tag{3}\n   \\]  \n   The altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); it is **not** the line \\(AP\\) (unless a very special position occurs).\n\n5. **Angle condition for the required tangency.**  \n   Let \\(\\omega\\) be the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n   The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (call it \\(\\ell\\)) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) iff  \n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE. \\tag{4}\n   \\]  \n   Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), condition (4) is equivalent to  \n   \\[\n   \\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle BFE. \\tag{5}\n   \\]  \n   Thus the problem reduces to proving the angle equality (5).\n\n6. **What has been proved so far.**  \n   - Equality (1) gives a clean relation between the lengths \\(AE,AF\\) and the fixed chord \\(AB\\).  \n   - Relation (2) describes precisely the position of \\(P\\) with respect to the line \\(MN\\).  \n   - Relation (3) describes the geometry of the orthocenter \\(H\\) and its reflection.  \n\n   The remaining task is to connect the direction of \\(AP\\) with the chord \\(BE\\) of the circle \\(\\omega\\) so that (5) holds. The earlier (incorrect) proof attempted to force the collinearity \\(A,E,M\\) and \\(A,F,N\\); the correct configuration does **not** provide such collinearity, and consequently the cyclic quadrilaterals \\(B,E,F,M\\) and \\(B,E,F,N\\) used there do not arise automatically.\n\n**Thus the current status is:**  \n- Lemma\u202f1 (equation\u202f(1)) is proved rigorously.  \n- Lemma\u202f2 (description of \\(P\\) \u2013 equation\u202f(2)) is proved rigorously.  \n- Lemma\u202f3 (reflection property of the orthocenter \u2013 equation\u202f(3)) is proved rigorously.  \n\nThe final angle equality (5) and the consequent tangency remain unproved; they appear to require a deeper relationship between the line \\(AP\\) and the chord \\(BE\\) that is not captured by the elementary facts established above. Further work would have to explore additional harmonic or power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point relations, or perhaps employ inversion centred at \\(A\\) to transform the configuration into a more tractable one. \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution (Partial)**  \n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f1.  Power of point \\(A\\) with respect to the two intersecting circles  \n\nLet the circles \\(\\Omega(M,r_{1})\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,r_{2})\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nTake any line through \\(A\\) meeting \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\).  \n\nBecause \\(AB\\) is a common chord of the two circles, the intersecting\u2011chords theorem applied to each circle gives  \n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AB = AB\\cdot AB \\quad\\text{and}\\quad AF\\cdot AB = AB\\cdot AB .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying the two equalities and cancelling the factor \\(AB^{2}\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AF = AB^{2}. \\tag{L1}\n\\]\n\nThus (1) holds for the specific points \\(E,F\\) defined in the problem.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f2.  Position of the circum\u2011centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) (by construction).  \nIn triangle \\(ACD\\) the side \\(CD\\) is a chord of its circum\u2011circle \\((ACD)\\).  \nThe perpendicular bisector of a chord passes through the centre of the circum\u2011circle; therefore the centre \\(P\\) lies on the line that is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) at the midpoint \\(K\\) of \\(CD\\).  \n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nPK\\perp MN,\\qquad PK = PK_{C}=PK_{D},\\qquad P\\notin MN\\ \\text{in general}. \\tag{L2}\n\\]\n\nNo claim that \\(P\\) is the midpoint of \\(CD\\) or that \\(AP\\perp MN\\) can be made without additional symmetry, which does not exist in the general configuration.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f3.  Reflection of the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nReflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); denote the reflected point by \\(H_{MN}\\).  \n\nA standard property of any triangle states that the reflection of the orthocenter across any