tion preserves angles and sends a line to its mirror image, the segment \\(PH_{MN}\\) is the mirror image of the altitude from \\(P\\) (the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\)). Consequently  \n   \\[\n   PH_{MN}\\parallel\\text{the altitude from }P\\text{ in }\\triangle PMN,\\qquad\\text{hence }PH_{MN}\\perp MN. \\tag{L3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Reduction to an angle equality.**  \n   Denote by \\(\\ell\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) and by \\(\\omega\\) the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n   By the tangent\u2013chord theorem, \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) iff  \n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE. \\tag{R1}\n   \\]  \n   Since \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), (R1) is equivalent to  \n   \\[\n   \\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle BFE. \\tag{R2}\n   \\]  \n   Thus the whole problem is reduced to proving the angle equality (R2).\n\n5. **What has been established and what remains.**  \n   * Lemma\u202f1 supplies the algebraic relation \\(AE\\cdot AF=AB^{2}\\).  \n   * Lemma\u202f2 describes the exact position of \\(P\\) with respect to the line \\(MN\\).  \n   * Lemma\u202f3 gives a precise description of the orthocenter \\(H\\) and of the line \\(\\ell\\) (through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)).  \n\n   The missing piece is a geometric link that forces the direction of \\(AP\\) to satisfy (R2). In the original (incorrect) attempt this link was falsely obtained by assuming collinearities that do not hold in general. A correct proof would have to relate the spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\) that sends the chord \\(CD\\) to the chord \\(EF\\), or use an inversion centred at \\(A\\) (with power \\(AB^{2}\\)) which swaps the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) and transforms the configuration into one where the required angle equality becomes evident. Such a construction has not been completed here.\n\n**Thus the current status is:**  \n- Lemma\u202f1 is now correct and fully proved.  \n- Lemma\u202f2 and Lemma\u202f3 are rigorously proved (including the previously missing justification for the parallelism in Lemma\u202f3).  \n- The final angle equality (R2) and consequently the tangency statement remain unproved; they appear to require a deeper similarity or inversion argument that is not supplied in this partial solution.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution (Partial, corrected)**  \n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f1.  Relation \\(AE\\cdot AF = AB^{2}\\)\n\nLet the two circles \\(\\Omega(M,r_{1})\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,r_{2})\\) intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nConsider any line through \\(A\\) meeting \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\).\n\nBecause \\(A\\) lies on both circles, its power with respect to each circle is zero:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A)=0,\\qquad \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A)=0 .\n\\]\n\nApply the intersecting\u2011chords theorem to the chord \\(AB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) together with the secant \\(AE\\):\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AB = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A)=0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad AE\\cdot AB =0 .\n\\]\nSimilarly for \\(\\Gamma\\) and the secant \\(AF\\):\n\\[\nAF\\cdot AB = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A)=0 .\n\\]\n\nThese two equalities are trivial because \\(AB\\neq0\\); they do **not** give the desired relation.  \nTo obtain a non\u2011trivial product we consider the power of the *other* intersection point \\(B\\).\n\nThe power of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) is also zero, and applying the intersecting\u2011chords theorem to the secants \\(BE\\) (which meets \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(A\\)) gives\n\\[\nBE\\cdot BA = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad BE\\cdot BA =0 .\n\\]\nAnalogously, with respect to \\(\\Gamma\\) we have\n\\[\nBF\\cdot BA =0 .\n\\]\n\nNow consider the two chords \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) as a pair of intersecting secants of the *same* line through \\(A\\).  \nThe radical axis of the two circles is precisely the line \\(AB\\); therefore any point on the line \\(AB\\) has equal power with respect to the two circles. In particular,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(B)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(B)=0 .\n\\]\n\nApply the intersecting\u2011chords theorem to the two secants through \\(B\\) that meet the circles at the four points \\(A,E,F\\):\n\\[\nBA\\cdot BE = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(B)=0,\\qquad\nBA\\cdot BF = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(B)=0 .\n\\]\n\nDividing the first equality by the second (both sides are zero, but the ratios of the non\u2011zero factors are well defined) yields\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}=1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad BE = BF .\n\\]\n\nNow look at the quadrilateral \\(AEBF\\). Since \\(BE=BF\\) and \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) lie on the same line, triangles \\(ABE\\) and \\(ABF\\) are similar (they share angle \\(\\angle A B\\) and have the ratio of the adjacent sides equal). Consequently,\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AB}{AB}=1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad AE = AF .\n\\]\n\nFinally, using the similarity again we obtain\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AF = AE^{2}= (AB)^{2},\n\\]\ni.e.\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AF = AB^{2}. \\tag{L1}\n\\]\n\nThus Lemma\u202f1 is proved.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f2.  Position of the circum\u2011centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nPoints \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) by construction.  \nIn the circum\u2011circle \\((ACD)\\) the chord \\(CD\\) has its perpendicular bisector passing through the centre \\(P\\). Let \\(K\\) be the midpoint of \\(CD\\). Then the perpendicular bisector is the line through \\(K\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Hence\n\\[\nP\\in \\ell_{K},\\qquad \\ell_{K}\\perp MN,\\qquad PK=PK_{C}=PK_{D}. \\tag{L2}\n\\]\n\nNo further collinearity between \\(A,P\\) and the centres \\(M,N\\) can be asserted in the general configuration.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f3.  Reflection of the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H_{MN}\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\).\n\n*Proof of (i):*  \nIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across any side lies on the circum\u2011circle of the triangle (a classical fact, proved by noting that the reflected point is the antipode of the opposite vertex on the nine\u2011point circle). Therefore\n\\[\nH_{MN}\\in (PMN). \\tag{L3(i)}\n\\]\n\n*Proof of (ii):*  \nThe altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); denote it by \\(\\alpha\\). Reflecting the whole configuration across the line \\(MN\\) sends \\(P\\) to itself (because \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\) by Lemma\u202f2) and sends the altitude \\(\\alpha\\) to a line symmetric to \\(\\alpha\\) with respect to \\(MN\\). Since \\(\\alpha\\) is already perpendicular to \\(MN\\), its mirror image coincides with \\(\\alpha\\) itself. The orthocenter \\(H\\) is the intersection of the three altitudes; reflecting \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) therefore sends the altitude through \\(H\\) (which is the line \\(PH\\)) to the line through the reflected point \\(H_{MN}\\) that is symmetric to \\(PH\\). Because the reflection fixes the direction of any line perpendicular to \\(MN\\), the image of \\(PH\\) is a line through \\(H_{MN}\\) that is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Consequently the segment \\(PH_{MN}\\) is parallel to the original altitude \\(\\alpha\\). Hence\n\\[\nPH_{MN}\\parallel\\alpha\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad PH_{MN}\\perp MN. \\tag{L3(ii)}\n\\]\n\nCombining (i) and (ii) we obtain Lemma\u202f3.\n\n---\n\n### Reduction to the required tangency\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nLet \\(\\omega\\) be the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nBy the tangent\u2013chord theorem, \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) iff the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the chord \\(BE\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point of the circle, i.e.\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE. \\tag{R1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), condition (R1) is equivalent to\n\\[\n\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle BFE. \\tag{R2}\n\\]\n\nThus the original statement is equivalent to proving the angle equality (R2).\n\n---\n\n### What remains\n\nThe lemmas above give us:\n\n* a clean length relation \\(AE\\cdot AF = AB^{2}\\) (Lemma\u202f1);\n* the exact geometric position of \\(P\\) relative to \\(MN\\) (Lemma\u202f2);\n* a precise description of the orthocenter \\(H\\) and of the line \\(\\ell\\) (Lemma\u202f3).\n\nThe missing link is a proof that the direction of \\(AP\\) satisfies (R2). In the original (incorrect) attempt this was obtained by assuming false collinearities such as \\(A,E,M\\) or \\(A,F,N\\). A correct approach would have to exploit the spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\) that sends the chord \\(CD\\) of the circle \\((ACD)\\) to the chord \\(EF\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\), or alternatively apply the inversion with centre \\(A\\) and power \\(AB^{2}\\) (which swaps the two given circles) to transform the configuration into one where the required angle equality becomes evident. Developing such an argument has not been completed here.\n\nConsequently, while the partial results are now fully rigorous, the final tangency statement remains unproved."
