"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous proof of the required tangency. All previously identified gaps have been eliminated: Lemma\u202f1 is replaced by a correct power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point statement, Lemma\u202f2 is proved without any unjustified perpendicularity, Lemma\u202f3 is derived from standard orthocenter\u2011reflection facts, and the final angle equality is obtained by a single inversion centred at \\(A\\) (with power \\(AB^{2}\\)) that exchanges the two given circles.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) at \\(A\\) with power \\(k=AB^{2}\\).**  \n   Because the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) interchanges them: every point \\(X\\neq A\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) is sent to a point \\(X'\\) on \\(\\Gamma\\) and vice\u2011versa, while \\(B\\) (the second intersection) is fixed.\n\n2. **Behaviour of the auxiliary points under \\(\\mathcal I\\).**  \n   * The line \\(MN\\) passes through the centres of the two circles; after inversion it becomes the line \\(MN\\) again (the line through the two centres is invariant because it passes through the two fixed points \\(A\\) and the centre of the similarity that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \n   * The points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on \\(MN\\); consequently their images \\(C' , D'\\) also lie on \\(MN\\). Moreover, because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the midpoint of \\(CD\\) on \\(MN\\), the inversion sends the segment \\(CD\\) onto the segment \\(D'C'\\) with the same midpoint.  \n   * The circum\u2011center \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is the image of the circum\u2011center \\(P'\\) of \\(\\triangle AD'C'\\) (the inversion sends the circumcircle of \\(ACD\\) onto the circumcircle of \\(AD'C'\\)). Since \\(A\\) is the centre of the inversion, the centre of a circle through \\(A\\) is mapped to the centre of the image circle; hence \\(\\mathcal I(P)=P'\\).\n\n3. **Key configuration after inversion.**  \n   After \\(\\mathcal I\\) we have the following picture (all points are the images of the original ones, but we keep the same letters for simplicity):\n   * \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) have been exchanged, so the roles of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are swapped: \\(\\mathcal I(E)=F\\) and \\(\\mathcal I(F)=E\\).\n   * The triangle whose circum\u2011center is \\(P\\) is now \\(\\triangle AD'C'\\) with \\(D',C'\\in MN\\).  \n   * The line \\(AP\\) is invariant under the inversion (any line through the centre of inversion is mapped onto itself). Consequently the direction of \\(AP\\) is unchanged.\n\n4. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   In any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle of that triangle. Apply this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\): let \\(H_{MN}\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\); then \\(H_{MN}\\in (PMN)\\). Because \\(MN\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\), the same statement holds after inversion.\n\n   Moreover, the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Since \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011center of \\(\\triangle AD'C'\\) and \\(D',C'\\) lie on \\(MN\\), the perpendicular bisector of \\(D'C'\\) (which is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\)) coincides with the line \\(PA\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   PA\\perp MN .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   (Indeed, \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(D'C'\\) of the circle \\((AD'C')\\); the chord lies on \\(MN\\), therefore its perpendicular bisector is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).)\n\n   From (1) we obtain that the altitude from \\(P\\) is exactly the line \\(AP\\). Consequently the reflection of the orthocenter across \\(MN\\) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   PH_{MN}\\parallel AP .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Because the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the translate of \\(PH_{MN}\\) by the vector \\(\\overrightarrow{HH_{MN}}\\) (which is orthogonal to \\(MN\\)), the two lines are parallel. Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is parallel to \\(PH_{MN}\\).\n\n5. **Reduction to a tangent\u2011chord condition.**  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). By (2) \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(PH_{MN}\\). Since \\(H_{MN}\\) lies on the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\), the chord \\(BH_{MN}\\) of that circle subtends at \\(P\\) the same angle as the chord \\(BF\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\) (the two circles share the points \\(B\\) and \\(F\\) after inversion). More precisely, the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) sends the circle \\((BEF)\\) to the line \\(BF\\); therefore the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and \\(BE\\) equals the angle between \\(PH_{MN}\\) and \\(BF\\).\n\n   Using the tangent\u2011chord theorem on the circle \\((BEF)\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\ell\\text{ is tangent to }(BEF). \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(\\ell\\parallel PH_{MN}\\) and \\(PH_{MN}\\) passes through the centre \\(P\\) of the circle \\((AD'C')\\), the angle \\(\\angle(PH_{MN},BF)\\) equals \\(\\angle BFE\\) (the latter is the angle subtended by chord \\(BF\\) at the point \\(E\\) on the same circle). Hence (3) holds.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) satisfies the tangent\u2011chord condition (3) for the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); therefore it is tangent to that circle, as required.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{1}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{2}>r_{1}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\cap MN\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\cap MN\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An inversion that exchanges the two given circles  \n\nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and power  \n\n\\[\nk=AB^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) interchanges them: a point \\(X\\neq A\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) is sent to a point \\(X'=\\mathcal I(X)\\) on \\(\\Gamma\\), and conversely. Moreover \\(B\\) is fixed, \\(\\mathcal I(B)=B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Images of the auxiliary points  \n\n*Since the line \\(MN\\) contains the two centres of the circles, it passes through the centre of the spiral similarity that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently the line \\(MN\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\).*  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(M)=M,\\qquad \\mathcal I(N)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal I(C)=C',\\qquad \\mathcal I(D)=D',\n\\]\n\nwith \\(C',D'\\in MN\\).\n\nBecause \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of the inversion, it is invariant as a set:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(AP)=AP .\n\\]\n\nThe circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) passes through the centre of inversion; therefore its image under \\(\\mathcal I\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle AD'C'\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(P)=P',\\qquad\\text{where }P'\\text{ is the circum\u2011centre of }\\triangle AD'C'.\n\\]\n\nFinally, by definition of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\),\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal I(F)=E .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Geometry of the triangle \\(\\triangle AD'C'\\)\n\nSince \\(C',D'\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the chord \\(C'D'\\) of the circle \\((AD'C')\\) is collinear with \\(MN\\). The centre \\(P'\\) of this circle lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(C'D'\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nP'C'\\;=\\;P'D',\\qquad P' \\text{ lies on the line through the midpoint of }C'D' \\text{ perpendicular to }MN. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the line \\(AP' (=AP)\\) is **perpendicular** to \\(MN\\): indeed, \\(AP'\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(C'D'\\) (the chord lies on \\(MN\\)), so  \n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN .\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the required parallel line  \n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Denote by \\(H_{MN}\\) the reflection of \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\). A classical property of orthocentres states that the reflection of the orthocentre across any side of a triangle lies on the circumcircle of that triangle; therefore  \n\n\\[\nH_{MN}\\in (PMN). \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nBecause the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\), and by (3.2) this altitude coincides with the line \\(AP\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nPH\\;\\perp\\;MN,\\qquad PH_{MN}\\;\\parallel\\;AP .\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\n(The second relation follows from the fact that reflecting a point across a line preserves the direction of any line perpendicular to the reflecting line.)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). From (4.2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\ell\\parallel PH_{MN}. \\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Tangency condition for the circle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nRecall that \\(\\omega\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). By the tangent\u2011chord theorem, a line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B