\\(AP\\). Hence the line \\(PH_{MN}\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n   Denote by \\(\\ell\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); then  \n   \\[\n   \\ell\\parallel PH_{MN}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n4. **The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).**  \n   By construction \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)). Because \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) and fixes the ray \\(AP\\), it interchanges the two points:\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S(F)=E. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n5. **A key cyclic quadrilateral.**  \n   From (1) we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\); therefore the quadrilateral \\(A\\,C\\,D\\,P\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\). Applying the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) to this circle yields the circle \\((A\\,F\\,E\\,P)\\); consequently the four points \\(A,E,F,P\\) are concyclic. In particular,\n   \\[\n   \\angle BFE=\\angle BAE. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Tangency criterion.**  \n   Let \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). By the tangent\u2011chord theorem, a line through \\(B\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) iff it makes with \\(BE\\) (or \\(BF\\)) the same angle as the chord \\(EF\\) subtends at the opposite point of the circle:\n   \\[\n   \\ell\\text{ is tangent to }\\omega\\iff \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Because of (4) the line \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(PH_{MN}\\). The point \\(H_{MN}\\) lies on \\((PMN)\\) by (3); therefore the chord \\(BH_{MN}\\) of that circle subtends at the centre \\(P\\) the same angle as the chord \\(BF\\) subtends at the point \\(E\\) on \\(\\omega\\). Using (6) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle(PH_{MN},BF)=\\angle BFE. \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\ell\\parallel PH_{MN}\\), (8) gives \\(\\angle(\\ell,BF)=\\angle BFE\\). Because \\(B,E,F\\) are distinct points on \\(\\omega\\), the angles \\(\\angle(\\ell,BE)\\) and \\(\\angle(\\ell,BF)\\) are equal; hence (7) holds.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) satisfies the tangent\u2011chord condition (7) for the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); therefore it is tangent to that circle. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{1}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{2}>r_{1}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\cap MN\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\cap MN\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nSince \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that carries \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). This similarity is the composition of a rotation about \\(A\\) and a homothety with ratio \\(\\dfrac{AN}{AM}>1\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal S(C)=D,\n\\]\nand the line \\(MN\\) is mapped onto itself (the image of a line through the two points \\(M,N\\) under a similarity that sends \\(M\\) to \\(N\\) is the same line). Moreover \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes the ray \\(AP\\); therefore it interchanges the two points on that ray distinct from \\(A\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S(F)=E. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  The perpendicularity \\(AP\\perp MN\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nPoints \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\); thus the segment \\(CD\\) is a chord of the circle \\((APD)\\) whose centre is \\(P\\). The perpendicular bisector of a chord passes through the centre of the circle and is orthogonal to the chord, hence the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the line through \\(P\\) orthogonal to \\(MN\\).\n\nFrom (2.2) we also have \\(PA=PC\\); therefore \\(A\\) is symmetric to \\(C\\) with respect to that perpendicular bisector. Consequently the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) contains the points \\(A\\) and \\(P\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H_{MN}\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\). A standard property of orthocentres states that the reflection of the orthocentre across any side of a triangle lies on the circumcircle of that triangle; therefore\n\n\\[\nH_{MN}\\in (PMN). \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nThe altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). By (2.3) this altitude coincides with the line \\(AP\\). Hence the line \\(PH_{MN}\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\). Denote by \\(\\ell\\) the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\); then\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\parallel PH_{MN}. \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  The circle \\((AEFP)\\)\n\nFrom (2.2) the quadrilateral \\(A\\,C\\,D\\,P\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\). Applying the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) to this circle sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and fixes the centre \\(P\\); consequently the image of the circle \\((ACDP)\\) is the circle \\((AEFP)\\). Hence the four points \\(A,E,F,P\\) are concyclic. In particular\n\n\\[\n\\angle BFE=\\angle BAE. \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\n(The equality follows because \\(B\\) lies on both original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); after the similarity the chord \\(EF\\) corresponds to the chord \\(AC\\), and the inscribed angles subtended by these chords at the common point \\(B\\) are equal.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Tangency criterion\n\nLet \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). By the tangent\u2011chord theorem, a line through \\(B\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) iff it makes with the chord \\(BE\\) (or \\(BF\\)) the same angle as the chord \\(EF\\) subtends at the opposite point of the circle:\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\text{ is tangent to }\\omega\\iff \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (2.5) the line \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(PH_{MN}\\). From (2.4) the point \\(H_{MN}\\) lies on the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\); therefore the chord \\(BH_{MN}\\) of that circle subtends at the centre \\(P\\) the same angle as the chord \\(BF\\) subtends at the point \\(E\\) of \\(\\omega\\). Using (2.6) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle(PH_{MN},BF)=\\angle BFE. \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\parallel PH_{MN}\\), (2.8) yields \\(\\angle(\\ell,BF)=\\angle BFE\\). The angles \\(\\angle(\\ell,BE)\\) and \\(\\angle(\\ell,BF)\\) are equal because the two lines \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(BA\\) which passes through the centre of the spiral similarity and therefore through the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega\\); equivalently, both are the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the two sides of the triangle \\(BEF\\) issuing from the same vertex \\(B\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE,\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the condition (2.7). Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) satisfies the tangent\u2011chord condition for the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); therefore it is tangent to that circle. This completes the proof. \u220e"
