ns of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles) to each other.\n\n* **Cyclicity of \\(A,E,F,P\\).**  \n  By definition \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and the circle \\((A C D)\\) has centre \\(P\\). Applying the similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) to this circle maps it onto the circle \\((A E F)\\) (the image of a circle under a similarity is a circle). Since \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes the centre \\(A\\) and sends \\(C\\) to \\(E\\) and \\(D\\) to \\(F\\), the image circle has the same centre \\(P\\). Hence \\(A,E,F,P\\) are concyclic.\n\n* **Reflection of the orthocenter.**  \n  In any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle of that triangle. Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) gives that the point \\(H_{MN}\\) (the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\)) belongs to the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\).\n\n* **Remaining step (open).**  \n  The original claim is that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). The lemmas above provide the necessary configuration: the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also parallel to the line \\(PH_{MN}\\) (since the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\), and \\(AP\\) is known to be perpendicular to \\(MN\\) \u2013 a fact that, however, has not been proved rigorously here). With a correct proof of the perpendicularity \\(AP\\perp MN\\) one could identify \\(PH_{MN}\\) as the line through \\(P\\) parallel to the desired tangent, and then use the cyclic quadrilaterals \\((A E F P)\\) and \\((B E F)\\) together with angle chasing to obtain the tangency condition. This final angle\u2011chasing argument remains to be supplied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution (Partial \u2013 rigorous lemmas)**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{1}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{2}>r_{1}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\cap MN\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\cap MN\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 \u2013 Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at the two distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nIndeed, the composition of the homothety with centre \\(A\\) and ratio \\(\\dfrac{AN}{AM}>1\\) with the rotation that aligns the ray \\(AM\\) with the ray \\(AN\\) maps every point of \\(\\Omega\\) to the unique point of \\(\\Gamma\\) lying on the same ray from \\(A\\).  \n\nConsequences of the definition are:\n\n* \\(\\mathcal S(M)=N\\) (centres are mapped to each other);\n* \\(\\mathcal S(C)=D\\) (the two points of intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with the circles are mapped to each other);\n* For any ray \\(AX\\) meeting \\(\\Omega\\) again at a point \\(Y\\), the image \\(\\mathcal S(Y)\\) is the second intersection of the same ray \\(AX\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n  Applying this to the ray \\(AP\\) gives\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal S(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S(F)=E .\n  \\tag{2.1}\n  \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 \u2013 The quadrilateral \\(A,E,F,P\\) is cyclic\n\nBy definition of \\(P\\) we have\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\nHence the points \\(A,C,D\\) lie on the circle \\((A C D)\\) whose cent