ce from \\(A\\) equals \\(PA\\) is \\(P\\) itself\u201d is false because the ray \\(AP\\) contains infinitely many points at distance \\(PA\\) from \\(A\\) (the circle of radius \\(PA\\) intersects the ray at two points, \\(P\\) and the point symmetric to \\(P\\) with respect to \\(A\\)). Hence the conclusion \\(P'=P\\) is not justified.\n\n   **Classification:** **Justification Gap** \u2013 the proof does not rigorously establish that \\(P\\) is the centre of \\((AEF)\\); the equality \\(PA=PE=PF\\) remains unproven.\n\n4. **Conclusion of Lemma\u202f2:** The statement that \\(A,E,F,P\\) are concyclic with centre \\(P\\) is true (it follows from the fact that \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and the spiral similarity), but the provided justification is incomplete. This gap propagates to later lemmas that rely on the equality \\(PA=PE=PF\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 The line \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\)\n\n1. **Claim:** \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the line \\(MN\\) onto itself, and its restriction to \\(MN\\) is a half\u2011turn about the midpoint \\(K\\) of \\(CD\\).  \n   - *Issue:* While \\(\\mathcal S\\) swaps \\(M\\) and \\(N\\), it does **not** necessarily map the entire line \\(MN\\) onto itself; a spiral similarity with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(M\\) to \\(N\\) will map the line through \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) to the line through \\(N\\) and \\(\\mathcal S(N)=\\) (image of \\(N\\) under \\(\\mathcal S\\)), which is not guaranteed to be the same line. The assertion that the restriction is a half\u2011turn is therefore unsupported.\n\n2. **Derivation of perpendicularity:** The argument proceeds by stating that because \\(CD\\) is invariant as a set under \\(\\mathcal S\\) but its orientation is reversed, the line \\(CD\\) must be perpendicular to the line joining \\(A\\) and the centre of the half\u2011turn \\(K\\). This reasoning is not a standard property of spiral similarities and is not proved.\n\n3. **Use of the fact that \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector