educe \\(AP'=PC\\). Because \\(PC=PA\\), we have \\(AP'=PA\\). The only point on the ray \\(AP\\) whose distance from \\(A\\) equals \\(PA\\) is \\(P\\) itself; therefore \\(P'=P\\).\n\nConsequently the centre of \\((A E F)\\) coincides with \\(P\\), and from (3) we obtain\n\n\\[\nPA=PE=PF .\n\\]\n\nHence the four points \\(A,E,F,P\\) are concyclic with centre \\(P\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 The line \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\)\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we know that \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the line \\(MN\\) onto itself, because \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the images of each other and the line \\(MN\\) is the unique line joining them. Moreover \\(\\mathcal S\\) interchanges the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) on that line; therefore the restriction of \\(\\mathcal S\\) to \\(MN\\) is a half\u2011turn (rotation by \\(180^{\\circ}\\)) about the midpoint \\(K\\) of \\(CD\\). Hence the midpoint \\(K\\) is the centre of this half\u2011turn and consequently \\(K\\) is the midpoint of the segment joining the centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\); in particular \\(K\\) lies on \\(MN\\).\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal S\\) is a similarity with centre \\(A\\), the image of the segment \\(CD\\) is the segment \\(DC\\); thus \\(CD\\) is invariant as a set but its orientation is reversed. The only way a similarity with centre \\(A\\) can reverse a segment while fixing its endpoints is that the line \\(CD\\) be perpendicular to the line joining the centre of the similarity (\\(A\\)) with the centre of the half\u2011turn (\\(K\\)). Hence\n\n\\[\nAK\\perp CD . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\((A C D)\\), the radius \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nPA\\perp CD . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom (4) and (5) we obtain that the two lines \\(AK\\) and \\(PA\\) are parallel. Both pass through \\(A\\); consequently they coincide, i.e. \\(K\\) lies on \\(PA\\). Since \\(K\\) is the midpoint of \\(CD\\) and \\(K\\in MN\\), the line \\(PA\\) passes through the midpoint of \\(CD\\) and is perpendicular to \\(CD\\). Because \\(CD\\subset MN\\), we finally have\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(AP\\) is the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4 \u2013 The reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\)\n\nLet \\(H_{MN}\\) be the reflection of the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) across the side \\(MN\\).  \nA classical property of orthocentres states that the reflection of the orthocentre of a triangle across any side lies on the circum\u2011circle of that triangle. Applying it to side \\(MN\\) yields\n\n\\[\nH_{MN}\\in (PMN) . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, because the reflection across a line preserves the angle with that line, the segment \\(PH_{MN}\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Combining this with Lemma\u202f3 we obtain\n\n\\[\nPH_{MN}\\parallel AP . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5 \u2013 Tangency of the required line\n\nDenote by \\(\\omega\\) the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nFrom Lemma\u202f2 the quadrilateral \\((A,E,F,P)\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\); hence\n\n\\[\n\\angle BFE = \\angle BAE . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes the two intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) of the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore it sends the ray \\(AB\\) onto itself. Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE = \\angle BAP . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nUsing (8) we have \\(\\angle (PH_{MN},BE)=\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BAP\\).  \nFrom (9)\u2013(10) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle (PH_{MN},BE)=\\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nThus the angle formed by the line \\(PH_{MN}\\) with the chord \\(BE\\) of \\(\\omega\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point \\(F\\) of \\(\\omega\\). By the **tangent\u201