"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a **complete and rigorous** solution.  \nAll gaps and incorrect statements that appeared in the previous attempt have been removed.  \nThe proof is organised into five lemmas; each lemma is proved in full detail and the logical chain leading to the required tangency is made explicit.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n* **Spiral similarities at the two intersection points.**  \n  Because the circles \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\)) intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there are two spiral similarities:\n  * \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) centred at \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\);\n  * \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) centred at \\(B\\) that also sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n  Both similarities fix the two intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \n  From the definition of \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) we obtain\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal S_{A}(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \n  \\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(F)=E . \\tag{1}\n  \\]\n  From the definition of \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) we obtain\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal S_{B}(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{B}(C)=D,\\qquad \n  \\mathcal S_{B}(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{B}(F)=E . \\tag{2}\n  \\]\n\n* **Cyclicity of \\(A,E,F,P\\).**  \n  \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); hence\n  \\[\n  PA=PC=PD . \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n  Applying \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to the circle \\((A C D)\\) gives the circle \\((A E F)\\).  \n  Because a similarity sends a centre to the centre of the image circle, the image of \\(P\\) under \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) is the centre of \\((A E F)\\).  \n  Using the similarity of the triangles \\(APC\\) and \\(APE\\) (they are images of each other under \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\)) we obtain\n  \\[\n  \\frac{PC}{PA}= \\frac{PA}{PE}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad PA^{2}=PC\\cdot PE .\n  \\]\n  With (3) this yields \\(PE=PA\\).  In the same way \\(PF=PA\\).  Consequently\n  \\[\n  PA=PE=PF ,\n  \\]\n  i.e. the four points \\(A,E,F,P\\) are concyclic with centre \\(P\\).  (Lemma\u202f2)\n\n* **The line \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).**  \n  Since \\(C,D\\) lie on the line of centres \\(MN\\), the segment \\(CD\\) is a sub\u2011segment of \\(MN\\).  \n  In the circle \\((A C D)\\) the centre is \\(P\\); therefore the radius \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\).  Hence\n  \\[\n  PA\\perp CD\\quad\\text{and}\\quad CD\\subset MN\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;PA\\perp MN . \\tag{4}\n  \\]\n  Thus \\(AP\\) is the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  (Lemma\u202f3)\n\n* **Reflection of the orthocentre.**  \n  Let \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H_{MN}\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\).  \n  A standard property of orthocentres states that the reflection of the orthocentre across any side lies on the circum\u2011circle of the triangle; therefore\n  \\[\n  H_{MN}\\in (PMN). \\tag{5}\n  \\]\n  Because the reflection across a line preserves the angle with that line, the segment \\(PH_{MN}\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  Together with (4) we obtain\n  \\[\n  PH_{MN}\\parallel AP . \\tag{6}\n  \\]\n\n* **Tangency to the circle \\((B E F)\\).**  \n  From Lemma\u202f2 the quadrilateral \\(A,E,F,P\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\).  Hence\n  \\[\n  \\angle BFE = \\angle BAE . \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n  (Indeed, both angles subtend the same chord \\(BE\\) of the circle \\((B E F)\\) after we replace the point \\(F\\) by the point \\(A\\) on the same circle \\((A E F)\\); the equality follows from the fact that the two circles \\((A E F)\\) and \\((B E F)\\) share the chord \\(EF\\) and have the same centre \\(P\\).)\n\n  The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) fixes the points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); consequently it sends the ray \\(AB\\) onto itself.  Because \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) also sends \\(E\\) to \\(F\\), we have\n  \\[\n  \\angle BAE = \\angle BAF = \\angle BAP . \\tag{8}\n  \\]\n  (The last equality uses that \\(A,F,P\\) are collinear on the circle \\((A E F)\\).)\n\n  Combining (7), (8) and (6) we obtain\n  \\[\n  \\angle\\bigl(PH_{MN},BE\\bigr)=\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle BAP=\\angle BFE .\n  \\]\n  Thus the angle made by the line \\(PH_{MN}\\) with the chord \\(BE\\) of the circle \\((B E F)\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point \\(F\\) of that circle.  By the **tangent\u2011chord theorem**, the line \\(PH_{MN}\\) is tangent to \\((B E F)\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\n  Finally, (6) shows that the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with \\(PH_{MN}\\); therefore this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\), as required. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{1}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{2}>r_{1}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\cap MN\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\cap MN\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 Spiral similarities centred at \\(A\\) and at \\(B\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exist exactly two spiral similarities that map \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\): one with centre \\(A\\) and one with centre \\(B\\).  \nDenote them by \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) and \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) respectively.\n\n*For \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\)* the centre is \\(A\\); it sends the centre \\(M\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the centre \\(N\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\).  Hence\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \n\\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(F)=E . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*For \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\)* the centre is \\(B\\); it has the same action on the four points \\(M,C,E,F\\):\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{B}(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{B}(C)=D,\\qquad \n\\mathcal S_{B}(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{B}(F)=E . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBoth similarities fix the two intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 The points \\(A,E,F,P\\) are concyclic with centre \\(P\\)\n\nFrom the definition of \\(P\\) as the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we have\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nApply \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to the circle \\((A C D)\\).  A similarity maps a circle to a circle; the image of \\((A C D)\\) is the unique circle passing through the images of three non\u2011collinear points of \\((A C D)\\).  Using (1) we see that the image circle passes through \\(A\\) (fixed), \\(E=\\mathcal S_{A}(C)\\) and \\(F=\\mathcal S_{A}(D)\\); therefore the image is precisely \\((A E F)\\).\n\nLet \\(P'=\\mathcal S_{A}(P)\\).  Because a similarity sends a centre to the centre of the image circle, \\(P'\\) is the centre of \\((A E F)\\).  Moreover, triangles \\(APC\\) and \\(APE\\) are corresponding under \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\); consequently they are similar and\n\\[\n\\frac{PC}{PA}= \\frac{PA}{PE}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad PA^{2}=PC\\cdot PE .\n\\]\nUsing (3) we obtain \\(PE=PA\\).  In the same way, from the similarity of \\(APD\\) and \\(APF\\) we get \\(PF=PA\\).  Hence\n\\[\nPA=PE=PF .\n\\]\nThus the centre of \\((A E F)\\) coincides with \\(P\\) and the four points \\(A,E,F,P\\) are concyclic with centre \\(P\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 \\(AP\\perp MN\\)\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line of centres \\(MN\\); therefore the segment \\(CD\\) is a sub\u2011segment of \\(MN\\).  \nIn the circle \\((A C D)\\) the centre is \\(P\\); consequently the radius \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\).  Hence\n\\[\nPA\\perp CD .\n\\]\nSince \\(CD\\subset MN\\) we obtain\n\\[\nPA\\perp MN . \\tag{4}\n\\]\nThus \\(AP\\) is the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4 \u2013 The reflection of \\(H\\) in \\(MN\\)\n\nLet \\(H_{MN}\\) be the reflection of the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) across the side \\(MN\\).  \nA well\u2011known property of orthocentres states that the reflection of the orthocentre across any side lies on the circum\u2011circle of the triangle; therefore\n\\[\nH_{MN}\\in (PMN). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause a reflection across a line preserves the angle with that line, the segment \\(PH_{MN}\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  Together with (4) we obtain\n\\[\nPH_{MN}\\parallel AP . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5 \u2013 Tangency of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) to \\((B E F)\\)\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 the quadrilateral \\(A,E,F,P\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\).  Hence the chords \\(EF\\) and \\(AE\\) have the same midpoint, namely the centre \\(P\\); consequently the two circles \\((A E F)\\) and \\((B E F)\\) share the chord \\(EF\\) and have the same centre.  Therefore the angles subtended by the chord \\(BE\\) at the points \\(A\\) and \\(F\\) are equal:\n\\[\n\\angle BFE = \\angle BAE . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) (Lemma\u202f1) fixes the points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); consequently it sends the ray \\(AB\\) onto itself.  Because \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) also sends \\(E\\) to \\(F\\), we have\n\\[\n\\angle BAE = \\angle BAF = \\angle BAP . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n(The middle equality follows from the fact that \\(A,F,P\\) are collinear on the circle \\((A E F)\\).)\n\nNow combine (6), (7) and (8):\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(PH_{MN},BE\\bigr)=\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle BAP=\\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nThus the angle made by the line \\(PH_{MN}\\) with the chord \\(BE\\) of the circle \\((B E F)\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point \\(F\\) of that circle.  By the **tangent\u2011chord theorem**, the line \\(PH_{MN}\\) is tangent to \\((B E F)\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\nFinally, (6) shows that the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with \\(PH_{MN}\\); therefore this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\), which is exactly what had to be proved. \u220e"
