"**Final Verdict:** The solution is **invalid** because it contains several **Critical Errors** that break the logical chain of the proof.\n\n---\n\n### List of Findings\n* **Location:** Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 \u201cThus the centre of \\((A E F)\\) coincides with \\(P\\) and the four points \\(A,E,F,P\\) are concyclic with centre \\(P\\).\u201d\n  * **Issue:** Critical Error \u2013 the argument that the similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the centre \\(P\\) to the centre of \\((A E F)\\) is false; a spiral similarity does **not** preserve circle centres in general. Consequently the conclusion that \\(PA=PE=PF\\) is unjustified.\n\n* **Location:** Lemma\u202f4 \u2013 \u201cA well\u2011known property of orthocentres states that the reflection of the orthocentre across any side lies on the circum\u2011circle of the triangle; therefore \\(H_{MN}\\in (PMN).\\)\u201d\n  * **Issue:** Critical Error \u2013 the reflected point lies on the **circumcircle of the triangle formed by the original vertices**, i.e. the reflection of \\(H\\) across side \\(MN\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) **only if** the triangle is acute and the side is a side of the triangle. Here \\(MN\\) is a side of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), so the statement is true, but the proof later uses the fact that \\(PH_{MN}\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) (which follows from the reflection) **and** that it is parallel to \\(AP\\) (from Lemma\u202f3). The parallelism is correct, but the later use of \\(H_{MN}\\) as the point through which the tangent line passes is not justified because the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is **not** the same as the line \\(PH_{MN}\\) (the latter passes through \\(P\\), the former passes through \\(H\\)). This mismatch makes the subsequent tangent argument invalid.\n\n* **Location:** Lemma\u202f5 \u2013 \u201cthe two circles \\((A E F)\\) and \\((B E F)\\) share the chord \\(EF\\) and have the same centre.\u201d\n  * **Issue:** Critical Error \u2013 \\((B E F)\\) does **not** share the centre \\(P\\); the centre of \\((B E F)\\) is generally different from \\(P\\). The claim that both circles have the same centre is false, so the equal\u2011angle statements that follow (especially (7) and (8)) are unsupported.\n\n* **Location:** Lemma\u202f5 \u2013 \u201c\\(\\angle BFE = \\angle BAE\\)\u201d (Equation\u202f(7)).\n  * **Issue:** Critical Error \u2013 This equality would hold only if \\(A\\) and \\(F\\) subtend the same chord \\(BE\\) in the **same** circle. Since \\(A\\) is not on the circumcircle \\((B E F)\\), the equality is unjustified.\n\n* **Location:** Lemma\u202f5 \u2013 \u201c\\(\\angle BAE = \\angle BAF = \\angle BAP\\)\u201d (Equation\u202f(8)).\n  * **Issue:** Justification Gap \u2013 The step \u201c\\(\\angle BAE = \\angle BAF\\)\u201d is claimed to follow because \u201c\\(A,F,P\\) are collinear on the circle \\((A E F)\\)\u201d. However, collinearity of \\(A,F,P\\) does **not** imply that the angles with vertex at \\(A\\) are equal; a proper argument would need to show that \\(E,F,P\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(AP\\), which has not been established.\n\n* **Location:** Final step \u2013 \u201cthe line \\(PH_{MN}\\) is tangent to \\((B E F)\\) at the point \\(B\\).\u201d\n  * **Issue:** Critical Error \u2013 Even if the previous angle equalities were correct, the tangent\u2011chord theorem would give tangency at the point where the angle between the line and the chord equals the angle in the opposite segment; the proof incorrectly identifies the point of tangency as \\(B\\) without justification, and the line used (\\(PH_{MN}\\)) is not the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (as noted above).\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Verification Log\n\nBelow each quoted passage from the solution, I indicate whether the step is correct, a **Critical Error**, or a **Justification Gap**, and I give the reasoning.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 Spiral similarities centred at \\(A\\) and at \\(B\\)\n\n> \u201cBecause the two circles intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exist exactly two spiral similarities that map \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\): one with centre \\(A\\) and one with centre \\(B\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** This statement is correct. Two intersecting circles admit exactly two spiral similarities fixing the intersection points.\n\n> \u201cFor \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) \u2026 \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(M)=N,\\; \\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D,\\; \\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F,\\; \\mathcal S_{A}(F)=E\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** The mapping of centres \\(M\\to N\\) and of the points on the line \\(MN\\) is correct for the similarity centred at \\(A\\). The statements \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F\\) and \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(F)=E\\) follow from the definition of \\(E,F\\) as the second intersections of line \\(AP\\) with the two circles, so they are acceptable.\n\n> \u201cFor \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) \u2026 the same action on the four points \\(M,C,E,F\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** Correct; the spiral similarity centred at \\(B\\) also sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) and therefore maps the same four points accordingly.\n\n*Conclusion:* Lemma\u202f1 is sound.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 The points \\(A,E,F,P\\) are concyclic with centre \\(P\\)\n\n> \u201cFrom the definition of \\(P\\) as the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** Correct by definition of circum\u2011centre.\n\n> \u201cApply \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to the circle \\((A C D)\\). \u2026 the image circle passes through \\(A\\) (fixed), \\(E=\\mathcal S_{A}(C)\\) and \\(F=\\mathcal S_{A}(D)\\); therefore the image is precisely \\((A E F)\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** The image of a circle under a spiral similarity is indeed a circle passing through the images of three non\u2011collinear points. Since \\(A\\) is fixed, the image of \\((A C D)\\) is a circle through \\(A,E,F\\). This part is correct.\n\n> \u201cLet \\(P'=\\mathcal S_{A}(P)\\). Because a similarity sends a centre to the centre of the image circle, \\(P'\\) is the centre of \\((A E F)\\).\u201d\n\n**Critical Error:** A spiral similarity does **not** in general send the centre of a circle to the centre of its image. The centre of a circle is not preserved under a similarity unless the similarity is a *pure* similarity (i.e., a homothety combined with a rotation) **and** the centre lies on the line joining the two centres of the circles. Here \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) is a spiral similarity with centre \\(A\\); the centre \\(P\\) of \\((A C D)\\) is not generally mapped to the centre of \\((A E F)\\). Hence the claim that \\(P'\\) is the centre of \\((A E F)\\) is unfounded.\n\n> \u201cMoreover, triangles \\(APC\\) and \\(APE\\) are corresponding under \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\); consequently they are similar and \\(\\frac{PC}{PA}= \\frac{PA}{PE}\\) \u2026 Using (3) we obtain \\(PE=PA\\).\u201d\n\n**Critical Error:** Even if the triangles are similar, the ratio derived is \\(\\frac{PC}{PA}= \\frac{PA}{PE}\\) which gives \\(PA^{2}=PC\\cdot PE\\). Substituting \\(PC=PA\\) (from (3)) yields \\(PA^{2}=PA\\cdot PE\\) and thus \\(PA=PE\\) **provided** \\(PA\\neq0\\). Algebraically this is fine, but the similarity claim rests on the previous false statement that the similarity maps the centre to the centre; the similarity does map the *points* \\(C\\) and \\(E\\) correctly, but it does **not** guarantee that the triangle \\(APC\\) is mapped to \\(APE\\) with the same orientation needed for the ratio. The step is therefore not justified.\n\n> \u201cIn the same way \u2026 we get \\(PF=PA\\). Hence \\(PA=PE=PF\\). Thus the centre of \\((A E F)\\) coincides with \\(P\\) and the four points \\(A,E,F,P\\) are concyclic with centre \\(P\\).\u201d\n\n**Critical Error:** The conclusion that \\(P\\) is the centre of \\((A E F)\\) follows from the (false) premise that a similarity sends the centre to the centre. Even if we accept the equalities \\(PA=PE=PF\\), they only show that \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(A,E,F\\); this does imply that \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle AEF\\). However, the derivation of those equalities is flawed, so the result is not established rigorously. Moreover, the statement \u201cthe four points \\(A,E,F,P\\) are concyclic with centre \\(P\\)\u201d is redundant: a set of points cannot be \u201cconcyclic with centre \\(P\\)\u201d; rather, they are *on a circle whose centre is \\(P\\)*. The wording indicates a lack of precision.\n\n*Conclusion for Lemma\u202f2:* The lemma contains a **Critical Error** (incorrect use of properties of spiral similarity) that invalidates the claim that \\(P\\) is the centre of \\((A E F)\\). Consequently, any later argument that relies on this fact is unsound.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 \\(AP\\perp MN\\)\n\n> \u201cThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line of centres \\(MN\\); therefore the segment \\(CD\\) is a sub\u2011segment of \\(MN\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** Correct.\n\n> \u201cIn the circle \\((A C D)\\) the centre is \\(P\\); consequently the radius \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** In any circle, the line joining the centre to the midpoint of a chord is perpendicular to the chord. However, \\(PA\\) is a radius to point \\(A\\), not necessarily to the midpoint of \\(CD\\). The statement \u201cthe radius \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\)\u201d is **incorrect**; the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the line through \\(P\\) and the midpoint of \\(CD\\), which is not guaranteed to be \\(PA\\). The correct fact is that the line through \\(P\\) and the midpoint of \\(CD\\) is perpendicular to \\(CD\\). Since \\(A\\) is not generally the midpoint of \\(CD\\), the claim is false.\n\n**Critical Error:** The deduction that \\(PA\\perp CD\\) (and hence \\(PA\\perp MN\\)) is unfounded. The perpendicularity would hold only if \\(A\\) were the midpoint of arc \\(CD\\) in the circle \\((ACD)\\), which is not given.\n\n*Conclusion for Lemma\u202f3:* The lemma contains a **Critical Error**; the perpendicularity of \\(AP\\) to \\(MN\\) has not been proved.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f4 \u2013 The reflection of \\(H\\) in \\(MN\\)\n\n> \u201cA well\u2011known property of orthocentres states that the reflection of the orthocentre across any side lies on the circum\u2011circle of the triangle; therefore \\(H_{MN}\\in (PMN).\\)\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** This property is correct for a triangle: reflecting the orthocentre across a side yields a point on the circumcircle. Since \\(MN\\) is a side of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the statement is valid.\n\n> \u201cBecause a reflection across a line preserves the angle with that line, the segment \\(PH_{MN}\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** Reflection of a point across a line yields a segment that is perpendicular to the line of reflection **only** if the original point lies on the line perpendicular to the reflecting line through the original point. Here \\(P\\) is not generally symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\); the segment \\(PH_{MN}\\) is **not** guaranteed to be perpendicular to \\(MN\\). The correct fact is that the line joining a point and its reflection is perpendicular to the reflecting line, but the reflected point is \\(H\\), not \\(P\\). Thus the segment \\(HH_{MN}\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\), not \\(PH_{MN}\\).\n\n**Critical Error:** The claim that \\(PH_{MN}\\perp MN\\) is false.\n\n> \u201cTogether with (4) we obtain \\(PH_{MN}\\parallel AP\\).\u201d\n\n**Critical Error:** Since (4) itself is unproved (and actually false), and the perpendicularity of \\(PH_{MN}\\) to \\(MN\\) is false, the parallelism conclusion is baseless.\n\n*Conclusion for Lemma\u202f4:* The lemma contains **Critical Errors** concerning the geometry of the reflection; the derived parallelism is not established.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f5 \u2013 Tangency of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) to \\((B E F)\\)\n\n> 