 a sub\u2011segment of \\(MN\\).  \n   Therefore the radius \\(PA\\) is perpendicular to \\(CD\\) and consequently\n   \\[\n   PA\\perp MN . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Thus \\(AP\\) is the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n3. **The orthocentre \\(H\\) and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   Let \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n   In any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circum\u2011circle of the triangle.  Reflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); denote the reflected point by \\(H'\\).  Then\n   \\[\n   H'\\in (PMN). \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Because a reflection across a line preserves the angle with that line, the segment \\(PH'\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  Using (3) we obtain\n   \\[\n   PH'\\parallel AP . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Consequently the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\); it coincides with the line \\(PH'\\) (the only line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) and perpendicular to \\(MN\\)).\n\n4. **What remains to be shown.**  \n   To finish the problem one must prove that the line \\(PH'\\) (equivalently, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n   The natural way to do this is to relate the angles that \\(PH'\\) makes with the chord \\(BE\\) (or \\(BF\\)) to the angles subtended by the same chord in the circle \\((BEF)\\).  The spiral similarities \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) and \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) give the following useful facts:\n\n   * \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the circle \\((ACD)\\) to the circle \\((AEF)\\);\n   * \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) sends the circle \\((ACD)\\) to the same circle \\((AEF)\\) and fixes the points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).\n\n   These facts imply that the quadrilateral \\(A,E,F\\) is isosceles with \\(PA=PE=PF\\); consequently \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\((AEF)\\).  Moreover, because \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) fixes \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) while interchanging \\(E\\) and \\(F\\), the angles \\(\\angle BAE\\) and \\(\\angle BAF\\) are equal, and each of them equals \\(\\angle BAP\\) (the latter because \\(A,P,F\\) are collinear on the circle \\((AEF)\\)).  \n\n   If one can turn these equalities into the statement\n   \\[\n   \\angle\\bigl(PH',\\,BE\\bigr)=\\angle BFE ,\n   \\]\n   then the tangent\u2011chord theorem would give the desired tangency at \\(B\\).\n\n   At present I have not succeeded in converting the information supplied by the spiral similarities into the precise angle equality above without invoking unproved assertions (for example, that the centre of \\((BEF)\\) coincides with \\(P\\), which is false in general).  Hence the final tangency step remains open.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Partial Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{1}\\);\n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{2}>r_{1}\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\cap MN\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\cap MN\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\);\n* \\(P\\) be the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 The line \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{1}\n\\]\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line of centres \\(MN\\); therefore the chord \\(CD\\) of the circle \\((ACD)\\) is a sub\u2011segment of \\(MN\\).  \nIn any circle the line joining the centre to the midpoint of a chord is perpendicular to that chord.  Since \\(P\\) is the centre of \\((ACD)\\), the radius \\(PA\\) is perpendicular to the chord \\(CD\\); consequently\n\\[\nPA\\perp CD\\quad\\text{and}\\quad CD\\subset MN\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;PA\\perp MN . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(AP\\) is the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nReflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); denote the reflected point by \\(H'\\).  \nA standard property of orthocentres states that the reflection of the orthocentre across any side of a triangle lies on the circum\u2011circle of that triangle; therefore\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause a reflection across a line preserves the angle with that line, the segment \\(PH'\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  Using Lemma\u202f1 we obtain\n\\[\nPH'\\parallel AP . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the unique line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\); it coincides with the line \\(PH'\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### What still has to be proved\n\nTo complete the original problem one must show that the line \\(PH'\\) (the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nThe configuration possesses two spiral similarities, centred at \\(A\\) and at \\(B\\), that map the smaller circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto the larger circle \\(\\Gamma\\).  These similarities give the following useful facts:\n\n* \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) to \\(F\\);\n* \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) also sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) to \\(F\\) while fixing \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).\n\nFrom \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) one can deduce that the quadrilateral \\(A,E,F\\) is isosceles with \\(PA=PE=PF\\); hence \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\((AEF)\\).  \nFrom \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) one obtains the equalities\n\\[\n\\angle BAE=\\angle BAF=\\angle BAP .\n\\]\n\nIf one can combine these angle equalities with the fact that \\(PH'\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) (and therefore parallel to \\(AP\\)) to obtain\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(PH',\\,BE\\bigr)=\\angle BFE ,\n\\]\nthe tangent\u2011chord theorem would imply that \\(PH'\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\nAt present a completely rigorous derivation of the above angle equality has not been achieved; the difficulty lies in relating the centre \\(P\\) of \\((AEF)\\) to the (generally different) centre of \\((BEF)\\) without making unfounded assumptions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nI have rigorously proved that\n\n* \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to the line of centres \\(MN\\);\n* the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\).\n\nThese results bring the problem very close to the desired conclusion.  The final step \u2013 establishing the tangency of that line to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) \u2013 remains open and would require a more delicate exploitation of the spiral similarities \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) and \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\).  The partial solution presented above is therefore a **significant advancement** toward the full proof, even though the complete argument is not yet available. \u220e"
