f a chord is perpendicular to that chord.  Since \\(P\\) is the centre of \\((ACD)\\), the radius \\(PA\\) is perpendicular to the chord \\(CD\\); consequently\n\\[\nPA\\perp CD\\quad\\text{and}\\quad CD\\subset MN\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;PA\\perp MN . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(AP\\) is the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nReflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); denote the reflected point by \\(H'\\).  \nA standard property of orthocentres states that the reflection of the orthocentre across any side of a triangle lies on the circum\u2011circle of that triangle; therefore\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause a reflection across a line preserves the angle with that line, the segment \\(PH'\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  Using Lemma\u202f1 we obtain\n\\[\nPH'\\parallel AP . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the unique line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\); it coincides with the line \\(PH'\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### What still has to be proved\n\nTo complete the original problem one must show that the line \\(PH'\\) (the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nThe configuration possesses two spiral similarities, centred at \\(A\\) and at \\(B\\), that map the smaller circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto the larger circle \\(\\Gamma\\).  These similarities give the following useful facts:\n\n* \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) to \\(F\\);\n* \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) also sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) to \\(F\\) while fixing \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).\n\nFrom \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) one can deduce that the quad