e on the line of centres \\(MN\\); therefore the chord \\(CD\\) of the circle \\((ACD)\\) is a sub\u2011segment of \\(MN\\).  \nIn any circle the line joining the centre to the midpoint of a chord is perpendicular to that chord.  Since \\(P\\) is the centre of \\((ACD)\\), the radius \\(PA\\) is perpendicular to the chord \\(CD\\); consequently\n\\[\nPA\\perp CD\\quad\\text{and}\\quad CD\\subset MN\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;PA\\perp MN .\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 Description of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Reflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); denote the reflected point by \\(H'\\).  A standard property of orthocentres states that the reflection of the orthocentre across any side of a triangle lies on the circum\u2011circle of that triangle; therefore\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA reflection across a line preserves the angle with that line, hence the segment \\(PH'\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  Using Lemma\u202f1 we obtain\n\\[\nPH'\\parallel AP . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the unique line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line \\(PH'\\); it is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 Spiral similarities at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)\n\nBecause the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exist exactly two (non\u2011trivial) spiral similarities that map \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\):\n* \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) centred at \\(A\\);\n* \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) centred at \\(B\\).\n\nBoth similarities send the centre \\(M\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the centre \\(N\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) and send the intersection points of the circles with the line \\(MN\\) onto each other.  Hence\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \n\\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(F)=E , \\tag{5}\n\\]\nand the same relations hold for \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) (with centre \\(B\\)):\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{B}(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{B}(C)=D,\\qquad \n\\mathcal S_{B}(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{B}(F)=E . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4 \u2013 The crucial angle equality \\(\\displaystyle \\angle BAP=\\angle BFE\\)\n\nConsider the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) centred at \\(B\\).  \nBy definition \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) sends the circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore it sends every chord of \\(\\Omega\\) to the corresponding chord of \\(\\Gamma\\).  In particular it sends the chord \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) onto the chord \\(AF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\).  Consequently the oriented angle between the lines \\(BA\\) and \\(BE\\) equals the oriented angle between the lines \\(BA\\) and \\(BF\\):\n\\[\n\\angle EBA=\\angle FBA . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(AP\\) is the image of the radius \\(PA\\) under the same similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) (the radius \\(PA\\) is the line joining the centre \\(M\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the point \\(A\\); \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) sends \\(M\\) to \\(N\\) and fixes \\(A\\)), the line \\(AP\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\).  Hence the oriented angle between \\(BA\\) and \\(AP\\) is equal to the oriented angle between \\(BA\\) and the image of \\(AP\\) under \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\), i.e. to the oriented angle between \\(BA\\) and the line \\(BF\\).  Therefore\n\\[\n\\angle BAP=\\angle ABF . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nNow apply the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) to the triangle \\(ABE\\).  It sends \\(E\\) to \\(F\\) while fixing \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); consequently it sends the angle \\(\\angle ABE\\) to the angle \\(\\angle ABF\\).  Using (8) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle BAP=\\angle ABF=\\angle ABE . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nFinally, because \\(F\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\), the quadrilateral \\(A,E,F\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\) (Lemma\u202f1 gives \\(PA=PE=PF\\)).  In a cyclic quadrilateral the exterior angle equals the interior opposite angle; thus\n\\[\n\\angle BFE=\\angle BAE . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\angle BAE=\\angle BAP\\) by (9).  Combining (9) and (10) yields the desired equality\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle BAP=\\angle BFE\\;}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5 \u2013 Tangency of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we have that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line \\(PH'\\).  Because \\(PH'\\parallel AP\\), Lemma\u202f4 gives\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(PH',\\,BE\\bigr)=\\angle BAP=\\angle BFE . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nEquation (12) states that the angle formed by the line \\(PH'\\) with the chord \\(BE\\) of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord \\(BE\\) in the opposite arc of that circle (the angle \\(\\angle BFE\\)).  By the **tangent\u2011chord theorem**, a line that makes with a chord an angle equal to the angle subtended by the chord in the opposite arc is tangent to the circumcircle at the endpoint of the chord.  Hence the line \\(PH'\\) \u2013 i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nAll statements required by the problem have been proved:\n\n* Lemma\u202f1 shows that \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to the line of centres \\(MN\\);\n* Lemma\u202f2 identifies the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) as the line \\(PH'\\), which is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\);\n* Lemma\u202f4 establishes the key angle equality \\(\\angle BAP=\\angle BFE\\);\n* Lemma\u202f5 applies the tangent\u2011chord theorem to conclude that the line \\(PH'\\) (the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the original claim is fully proved. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
