al S_{B}\\), i.e. to the oriented angle between \\(BA\\) and the line \\(BF\\).  Therefore\n\\[\n\\angle BAP=\\angle ABF . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nNow apply the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) to the triangle \\(ABE\\).  It sends \\(E\\) to \\(F\\) while fixing \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); consequently it sends the angle \\(\\angle ABE\\) to the angle \\(\\angle ABF\\).  Using (8) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle BAP=\\angle ABF=\\angle ABE . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nFinally, because \\(F\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\), the quadrilateral \\(A,E,F\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\) (Lemma\u202f1 gives \\(PA=PE=PF\\)).  In a cyclic quadrilateral the exterior angle equals the interior opposite angle; thus\n\\[\n\\angle BFE=\\angle BAE . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\angle BAE=\\angle BAP\\) by (9).  Combining (9) and (10) yields the desired equality\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle BAP=\\angle BFE\\;}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5 \u2013 Tangency of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we have that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line \\(PH'\\).  Because \\(PH'\\parallel AP\\), Lemma\u202f4 gives\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(PH',\\,BE\\bigr)=\\angle BAP=\\angle BFE . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nEquation (12) states that the angle formed by the line \\(PH'\\) with the chord \\(BE\\) of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord \\(BE\\) in the opposite arc of that circle (the angle \\(\\angle BFE\\)).  By the **tangent\u2011chord theorem**, a line that makes with a chord an angle equal to the angle subtended by the chord in the opposite arc is tangent to the circumcircle at the endpoint of the chord.  Hence the line \\(PH'\\) \u2013 i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nAll s