g chord of \\(\\Gamma\\). In particular it sends the chord \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) onto the chord \\(AF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\). Therefore \\(\\angle EBA=\\angle FBA\\).\u201d\n  * **Issue:** Critical Error \u2013 Even if a spiral similarity centred at \\(B\\) maps \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\), it does **not** necessarily map the chord \\(AE\\) (which lies on \\(\\Omega\\) but is not determined by the intersection points) to the chord \\(AF\\). The correspondence of chords under a spiral similarity is only guaranteed for chords that are images of each other under the similarity; this has not been established for \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\). Hence the angle equality \\(\\angle EBA=\\angle FBA\\) is unsupported.\n* **Location:** Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 \u201cThe line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre \\(A\\) of the spiral similarity; consequently \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) leaves the line \\(AP\\) invariant (it sends the radius \\(PA\\) of \\((ACD)\\) to the radius \\(PA\\) of \\((AEF)\\)). Hence \\(\\angle BAP=\\angle FAB\\).\u201d\n  * **Issue:** Critical Error \u2013 A spiral similarity centred at \\(B\\) does **not** in general fix the line \\(AP\\). The claim that \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) leaves \\(AP\\) invariant is unjustified; the only lines guaranteed to be invariant are those through the centre of the similarity (here \\(B\\)). Therefore the derived angle equality \\(\\angle BAP=\\angle FAB\\) is not proven.\n* **Location:** Lemma\u202f4 \u2013 \u201cEquation (12) states that the angle formed by the line \\(\\ell\\) with the chord \\(BE\\) \u2026 equals the angle subtended by the same chord in the opposite arc \u2026 By the tangent\u2011chord theorem \u2026 \\(\\ell\\) is tangent \u2026 at the point \\(B\\).\u201d\n  * **Issue:** Justification Gap \u2013 Even assuming the previous angle equality were correct, the solution does not verify that \\(\\ell\\) indeed meets the circle \\((BEF)\\) at \\(B\\); it only shows that the angle with chord \\(BE\\) equals the angle in the opposite arc. A full tangent\u2011chord argument requires confirming that \\(\\ell\\) passes through the point of tangency, which is omitted.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Verification Log\n\n#### Notation\nThe notation introduced is clear and matches the problem statement. No issues here.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 Claim that \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle AEF\\)\n\n1. **Statement:** \u201cBecause \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\).\u201d\n   - *Correct*: By definition of circum\u2011centre.\n\n2. **Statement:** \u201cSince \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) (the spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\)) maps \\(C\\) to \\(D\\), it also maps the radius \\(PA\\) of \\((ACD)\\) onto the radius \\(PA\\) of \\((AEF)\\). Consequently the distances from \\(P\\) to \\(E\\) and to \\(F\\) equal the distance from \\(P\\) to \\(A\\): \\(PE=PA=PF\\).\u201d\n   - *Critical Error*:  \n     - A spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\) preserves the *ratio* of distances from \\(A\\), not absolute distances from an arbitrary point \\(P\\).  \n     - There is no justification that \\(P\\) lies on the circle of similarity, nor that the image of the segment \\(PA\\) under \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) is the same segment.  \n     - In fact, if \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are distinct points on the line \\(AP\\) (as defined), the only way \\(PA=PE=PF\\) could hold is if \\(P\\) is the midpoint of \\(AF\\) and \\(E\\) coincides with \\(A\\) or \\(F\\), contradicting the construction.  \n     - Hence the conclusion \\(PE=PA=PF\\) is unfounded, making the lemma false.\n\n3. **Consequence:** \u201cThus \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle AEF\\).\u201d\n   - *Invalid*: Since the preceding equality is not established, this conclusion does not follow.\n\n*Result:* Lemma\u202f1 fails; all later arguments that depend on \\(P\\) being the circum\u2011centre of \\(AEF\\) are unsupported.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 Description of spiral similarities \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) and \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\)\n\n1. **Existence of two non\u2011trivial spiral similarities:** Correct in principle; intersecting circles admit two spiral similarities fixing the intersection points.\n\n2. **Claim:** \u201cBoth send the centre \\(M\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the centre \\(N\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) and map the points on the common line \\(MN\\) onto each other. Hence \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(M)=N,\\; \\mathcal S_{A}(C)=D,\\; \\mathcal S_{A}(E)=F,\\; \\mathcal S_{A}(F)=E\\).\u201d\n   - *Critical Error*:  \n     - While a spiral similarity that maps one circle to the other must send the *set* of points of the first circle to the set of points of the second, it does **not** necessarily send the centre of one to the centre of the other. The centre is not a point on the circle, so there is no reason for the similarity to map \\(M\\) to \\(N\\).  \n     - The mapping of the specific points \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) to \\(F\\) is also unproved; these points are defined via the line \\(MN\\) and the line \\(AP\\), which are not shown to be invariant under the similarity.  \n     - Therefore the equalities (3) and (4) are asserted without justification and are generally false.\n\n*Result:* Lemma\u202f2 is unsupported; any later use of the claimed correspondences is invalid.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 Angle equality \\(\\angle BAP = \\angle BFE\\)\n\nThe lemma builds on the (incorrect) statements from Lemma\u202f2.\n\n1. **Step:** \u201cBecause \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\), it sends every chord of \\(\\Omega\\) to the corresponding chord of \\(\\Gamma\\). In particular it sends the chord \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) onto the chord \\(AF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\). Therefore \\(\\angle EBA = \\angle FBA\\).\u201d\n   - *Critical Error*: The correspondence of chords under a spiral similarity is not automatic; it requires that the endpoints of the chord be images of each other. No argument is given that \\(E\\) maps to \\(F\\) under \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\). Hence the angle equality is not justified.\n\n2. **Step:** \u201cThe line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre \\(A\\) of the spiral similarity; consequently \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) leaves the line \\(AP\\) invariant \u2026 Hence \\(\\angle BAP = \\angle FAB\\).\u201d\n   - *Critical Error*: The centre of \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\) is \\(B\\), not \\(A\\). A spiral similarity fixes only lines through its own centre. There is no reason for the line \\(AP\\) to be invariant under \\(\\mathcal S_{B}\\). The derived angle equality is therefore unfounded.\n\n3. **Step:** Uses cyclic quadrilateral \\(AEFP\\) (from Lemma\u202f1) to claim \\(\\angle FAB = \\angle FEB\\).\n   - *Justification Gap*: Lemma\u202f1 is already invalid, so the cyclicity of \\(AEFP\\) is not established. Even if we assumed cyclicity, the statement \\(\\angle FAB = \\angle FEB\\) follows from the inscribed angle theorem, but the reliance on a false premise makes the step moot.\n\n4. **Subsequent steps** combine the above unsupported equalities to reach \\(\\angle BAP = \\angle BFE\\).\n   - *Result*: Since the foundational equalities are not proven, the conclusion is not justified.\n\n*Result:* Lemma\u202f3 fails due to multiple critical errors.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f4 \u2013 Tangency of the line \\(\\ell\\)\n\n1. **Step:** \u201c\\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) \u21d2 \\(\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle BAP\\).\u201d\n   - *Correct*: Parallelism gives equal corresponding angles.\n\n2. **Step:** Uses equality \\(\\angle BAP = \\angle BFE\\) from Lemma\u202f3.\n   - *Critical Error*: Lemma\u202f3 is invalid; the equality cannot be used.\n\n3. **Step:** Invokes the tangent\u2011chord theorem to claim \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(B\\).\n   - *Justification Gap*: Even if the angle equality held, the argument does not verify that \\(\\ell\\) passes through the point of tangency \\(B\\); it only shows the angle con