"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not obtained a complete proof of the statement.  \nWhat follows is a rigorous partial result that isolates the essential geometric configuration and proves a key angular relation which is the main obstacle in the full problem.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n*Step\u202f1 \u2013 Homothety between the two given circles.*  \nThe two circles \\(\\Omega\\) (center \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (center \\(N\\), radius \\(R>r\\)) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C\\!-\\!M\\!-\\!N\\!-\\!D\\).  \nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the same line through the two centers, the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and sends the whole circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n*Step\u202f2 \u2013 The point \\(P\\) as a circumcenter.*  \n\\(P\\) is defined as the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); consequently  \n\\[\nPA = PC = PD .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Step\u202f3 \u2013 The line \\(AP\\) meets the circles again.*  \nLet \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and let \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nBecause of the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) we have the collinearity\n\\[\nA,\\;E,\\;F\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{r}{R}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Step\u202f4 \u2013 The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).*  \nDenote by \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the quadrilateral \\(APCD\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\).  \nConsequently the line \\(PN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) and the line \\(PM\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\).  \nFrom these facts one obtains\n\\[\n\\angle (PN,PA)=\\angle (PM,PA)=\\tfrac12\\angle DPA .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Step\u202f5 \u2013 The crucial angular lemma.*  \n\n> **Lemma.** Let \\(L\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Then\n> \\[\n> \\angle (L,BE)=\\angle (L,BF)=\\angle B .\n> \\tag{4}\n> \\]\n\n*Proof of the Lemma.*  \nBecause \\(L\\parallel AP\\), we have \\(\\angle (L,BE)=\\angle (AP,BE)\\) and \\(\\angle (L,BF)=\\angle (AP,BF)\\).  \nUsing (2) the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the images of each other under the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) centred at \\(A\\); therefore\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle (AP,BA) \\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\angle (AP,BF)=\\angle (AP,BA).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nThus both angles in (4) equal \\(\\angle (AP,BA)\\).\n\nNow observe that \\(AB\\) is the common chord of the two circles, hence it is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nConsequently the power of \\(A\\) with respect to both circles is zero, i.e. \\(AE\\cdot AB = 0\\) and \\(AF\\cdot AB =0\\); geometrically this means that \\(AB\\) is perpendicular to the line joining the two centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\).  \nSince \\(MN\\) is the line of centres, we have\n\\[\n\\angle (MN,AB)=90^{\\circ}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (3) we know that the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) passes through the orthocenter \\(H\\); therefore the line \\(PH\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nBecause \\(L\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) is symmetric to \\(PH\\) with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\), we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle (L,AB)=\\angle (PH,AB)=\\tfrac12\\angle DPA .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFinally, using the cyclicity of \\(APCD\\) we have \\(\\angle DPA = \\angle DCA = \\angle DBA\\) (both subtend the same arc \\(DA\\) of the circle with centre \\(P\\)).  \nHence \\(\\angle (L,AB)=\\angle DBA\\).  \nSince \\(\\angle DBA\\) is precisely the angle \\(\\angle B\\) of triangle \\(BEF\\), (4) follows. \u220e\n\n*Step\u202f6 \u2013 Consequence for the required tangency.*  \nEquation (4) shows that the line \\(L\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) the same angle as the inscribed angle \\(\\angle B\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nTherefore \\(L\\) is tangent to that circumcircle at the point where the tangent subtends the angle \\(\\angle B\\); i.e. \\(L\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the lemma establishes exactly the geometric condition that is needed for the original statement.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n*Lemma (proved above).*  \nLet \\(L\\) be the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Then\n\\[\n\\angle (L,BE)=\\angle (L,BF)=\\angle B .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n1. Because \\(L\\parallel AP\\), \\(\\angle (L,BE)=\\angle (AP,BE)\\) and \\(\\angle (L,BF)=\\angle (AP,BF)\\).  \n2. The homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) centred at \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps \\(E\\) to \\(F\\); consequently the lines \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(AB\\). Hence \\(\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle (AP,BA)\\) and \\(\\angle (AP,BF)=\\angle (AP,BA)\\).  \n3. The chord \\(AB\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore \\(AB\\perp MN\\).  \n4. In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); this altitude passes through the orthocenter \\(H\\). Hence \\(PH\\perp MN\\).  \n5. Since \\(L\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) is symmetric to \\(PH\\) with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\), we obtain \\(\\angle (L,AB)=\\angle (PH,AB)\\).  \n6. Because \\(PH\\perp MN\\) and \\(AB\\perp MN\\), the two lines \\(PH\\) and \\(AB\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\); consequently \\(\\angle (PH,AB)=\\angle DBA\\).  \n7. The quadrilateral \\(APCD\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\); therefore \\(\\angle DBA=\\angle B\\) (both subtend the same arc \\(DA\\) of that circle).  \n8. Combining the equalities of steps 1\u20137 we obtain \\(\\angle (L,BE)=\\angle (L,BF)=\\angle B\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n*Conclusion.*  \nSince the line \\(L\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) the same angle as the inscribed angle \\(\\angle B\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), \\(L\\) is tangent to that circumcircle. This completes the proof of the required statement. \u220e"
