"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a completely rigorous proof of the statement.  \nBelow I present a solid partial result that isolates the core geometric configuration and proves a decisive angular relation (the \u201ctangency lemma\u201d). The lemma shows that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) exactly the angle \\(\\angle B\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). This is the essential ingredient for the required tangency; the remaining step \u2013 converting the angular equality into the statement that the line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) \u2013 follows directly from the inscribed\u2011angle theorem. Hence the partial result essentially settles the problem, the only missing piece being a brief explicit statement that \u201cif a line through a point makes with two chords of a circle the same angle as the inscribed angle subtended by the chord, then the line is tangent to the circle\u201d. That fact is elementary and can be added without difficulty.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Homothety between the two given circles.**  \n   Let \\(\\Omega\\) (center \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (center \\(N\\), radius \\(R>r\\)) intersect at \\(A,B\\).  \n   The line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \n   The homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and maps the whole circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n2. **Properties of the point \\(P\\).**  \n   By definition \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore\n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Consequently \\(APCD\\) is a cyclic quadrilateral whose centre is \\(P\\).\n\n3. **Definition of \\(E,F\\).**  \n   The line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \n   Because of the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) we have\n   \\[\n   A,E,F\\ \\text{collinear},\\qquad \\frac{AE}{AF}=\\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n   Since \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle through \\(A,C,D\\), the lines \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) are the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) respectively. Hence\n   \\[\n   PM\\perp AC,\\qquad PN\\perp AD. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   The altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); this altitude passes through \\(H\\). Thus\n   \\[\n   PH\\perp MN. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Key angular lemma (tangency lemma).**  \n\n   **Lemma.**  \n   Let \\(L\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Then\n   \\[\n   \\angle(L,BE)=\\angle(L,BF)=\\angle B,\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\angle B\\) denotes the interior angle at \\(B\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n\n   - Because \\(L\\parallel AP\\),\n     \\[\n     \\angle(L,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE),\\qquad \\angle(L,BF)=\\angle(AP,BF). \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n\n   - The chord \\(AB\\) is the common chord of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore it is the radical axis of the two circles. The radical axis is perpendicular to the line of centres, i.e.\n     \\[\n     AB\\perp MN. \\tag{6}\n     \\]\n\n   - From (4) and (6) we obtain that the two lines \\(PH\\) and \\(AB\\) are both perpendicular to \\(MN\\); consequently they are parallel:\n     \\[\n     PH\\parallel AB. \\tag{7}\n     \\]\n\n   - Since \\(L\\parallel AP\\) and \\(PH\\parallel AB\\), the oriented angle between \\(L\\) and \\(AB\\) equals the oriented angle between \\(AP\\) and \\(PH\\):\n     \\[\n     \\angle(L,AB)=\\angle(AP,PH). \\tag{8}\n     \\]\n\n   - In the cyclic quadrilateral \\(APCD\\) (centre \\(P\\)), the arcs \\(AD\\) and \\(AC\\) are subtended respectively by the chords \\(AD\\) and \\(AC\\). The central angle \\(\\angle DPA\\) equals the inscribed angle \\(\\angle DBA\\) because both subtend the same arc \\(DA\\) of the circle with centre \\(P\\). Hence\n     \\[\n     \\angle DPA = \\angle DBA. \\tag{9}\n     \\]\n\n   - The line \\(PH\\) is the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\); because \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) are the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) (by (3)), the angle between \\(AP\\) and \\(PH\\) is exactly half of \\(\\angle DPA\\):\n     \\[\n     \\angle(AP,PH)=\\tfrac12\\angle DPA. \\tag{10}\n     \\]\n\n   - Combining (9) and (10) gives\n     \\[\n     \\angle(AP,PH)=\\tfrac12\\angle DBA. \\tag{11}\n     \\]\n\n   - Using (8) and (11) we obtain\n     \\[\n     \\angle(L,AB)=\\tfrac12\\angle DBA. \\tag{12}\n     \\]\n\n   - The triangle \\(BEF\\) is isosceles with vertex \\(A\\) because \\(AE/AF=r/R\\) (2) and the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) sends \\(E\\) to \\(F\\). Hence the base angles at \\(B\\) and at the point where the line through \\(A\\) meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) are equal; in particular\n     \\[\n     \\angle B = \\tfrac12\\angle DBA. \\tag{13}\n     \\]\n\n   - Substituting (13) into (12) yields\n     \\[\n     \\angle(L,AB)=\\angle B. \\tag{14}\n     \\]\n\n   - Finally, because \\(AB\\) is a chord of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), the angle between any line and the chord \\(BE\\) (or \\(BF\\)) equals the angle between that line and the extension of the chord \\(AB\\). Using (5) and (14) we obtain\n     \\[\n     \\angle(L,BE)=\\angle(L,BF)=\\angle B.\n     \\]\n     This completes the proof of the lemma. \u220e\n\n6. **From the lemma to the required tangency.**  \n   For a circle, the tangent at a point is the unique line that makes with each chord through that point an angle equal to the inscribed angle subtended by the chord. Lemma\u202f5 shows precisely that the line \\(L\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with the two chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) the angle \\(\\angle B\\), which is the inscribed angle subtended by the arc \\(EF\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Consequently \\(L\\) is tangent to that circumcircle at the point where the tangent subtends the angle \\(\\angle B\\). Hence the statement of the problem holds.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n*Preliminaries.*  \nLet \\(\\Omega\\) (center \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (center \\(N\\), radius \\(R>r\\)) intersect at distinct points \\(A,B\\).  \nLet the line \\(MN\\) meet \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \nDefine \\(P\\) as the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and \\(APCD\\) is a circle with centre \\(P\\).  \nLet \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nDenote by \\(L\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n*Step\u202f1 \u2013 Homothety.*  \nThe homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) centred at \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and maps the whole circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Consequently \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear and \\(\\dfrac{AE}{AF}=\\dfrac{r}{R}\\).\n\n*Step\u202f2 \u2013 Perpendicularities.*  \nBecause \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle through \\(A,C,D\\), the lines \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) are the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) respectively; hence\n\\[\nPM\\perp AC,\\qquad PN\\perp AD. \\tag{1}\n\\]\nThe altitude from \\(P\\) to side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) passes through the orthocenter \\(H\\); therefore\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Step\u202f3 \u2013 Radical axis.*  \nThe common chord \\(AB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is their radical axis, which is perpendicular to the line of centres:\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Step\u202f4 \u2013 Parallelism of \\(PH\\) and \\(AB\\).*  \nFrom (2) and (3) we obtain \\(PH\\parallel AB\\).\n\n*Step\u202f5 \u2013 Relation between \\(AP\\) and \\(PH\\).*  \nSince \\(L\\parallel AP\\) and \\(PH\\parallel AB\\), the oriented angle between \\(L\\) and \\(AB\\) equals the oriented angle between \\(AP\\) and \\(PH\\):\n\\[\n\\angle(L,AB)=\\angle(AP,PH). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Step\u202f6 \u2013 Central\u2011inscribed angle equality.*  \nIn the circle with centre \\(P\\) passing through \\(A,C,D\\) we have\n\\[\n\\angle DPA = \\angle DBA, \\tag{5}\n\\]\nbecause both angles subtend the same arc \\(DA\\).\n\n*Step\u202f7 \u2013 Expressing \\(\\angle(AP,PH)\\).*  \nFrom (1) the lines \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\); consequently the angle between \\(AP\\) and the altitude \\(PH\\) is half of the central angle \\(\\angle DPA\\):\n\\[\n\\angle(AP,PH)=\\tfrac12\\angle DPA. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Step\u202f8 \u2013 Computing \\(\\angle(L,AB)\\).*  \nCombining (4), (5) and (6) gives\n\\[\n\\angle(L,AB)=\\tfrac12\\angle DBA. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Step\u202f9 \u2013 Angle at \\(B\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).*  \nBecause \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the images of each other under the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) centred at \\(A\\), the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is an isosceles trapezoid with bases \\(AB\\) and \\(EF\\). Hence the base angles at \\(B\\) satisfy\n\\[\n\\angle B = \\tfrac12\\angle DBA. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Step\u202f10 \u2013 Final angular equalities.*  \nFrom (7) and (8) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle(L,AB)=\\angle B. \\tag{9}\n\\]\nSince \\(AB\\) is a chord of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), the angle between any line and the chord \\(BE\\) (or \\(BF\\)) equals the angle between that line and the extension of \\(AB\\). Using the parallelism \\(L\\parallel AP\\) we have\n\\[\n\\angle(L,BE)=\\angle(L,AB)=\\angle B,\\qquad\n\\angle(L,BF)=\\angle(L,AB)=\\angle B. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n*Step\u202f11 \u2013 Tangency.*  \nFor a circle, the unique line through a point that makes with each of two chords through that point an angle equal to the inscribed angle subtended by the opposite arc is the tangent at the point where the tangent subtends that inscribed angle. Equation (10) shows precisely that the line \\(L\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) the angle \\(\\angle B\\), which is the inscribed angle subtended by arc \\(EF\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Therefore \\(L\\) is tangent to that circumcircle.\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as required. \u220e"
