            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n*Preliminaries.*  \nLet \\(\\Omega\\) (center \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (center \\(N\\), radius \\(R>r\\)) intersect at distinct points \\(A,B\\).  \nLet the line \\(MN\\) meet \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \nDefine \\(P\\) as the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and \\(APCD\\) is a circle with centre \\(P\\).  \nLet \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nDenote by \\(L\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n*Step\u202f1 \u2013 Homothety.*  \nThe homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) centred at \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and maps the whole circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Consequently \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear and \\(\\dfrac{AE}{AF}=\\dfrac{r}{R}\\).\n\n*Step\u202f2 \u2013 Perpendicularities.*  \nBecause \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle through \\(A,C,D\\), the lines \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) are the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) respectively; hence\n\\[\nPM\\perp AC,\\qquad PN\\perp AD. \\tag{1}\n\\]\nThe altitude from \\(P\\) to side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) passes through the orthocenter \\(H\\); therefore\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Step\u202f3 \u2013 Radical axis.*  \nThe common chord \\(AB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is their radical axis, which is perpendicular to the line of centres:\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Step\u202f4 \u2013 Parallelism of \\(PH\\) and \\(AB\\).*  \nFrom (2) and (3) we obtain \\(PH\\parallel AB\\).\n\n*Step\u202f5 \u2013 Relation between \\(AP\\) and \\(PH\\).*  \nSince \\(L\\parallel AP\\) and \\(PH\\parallel AB\\), the oriented angle between \\(L\\) and \\(AB\\) equals the oriented angle between \\(AP\\) and \\(PH\\):\n\\[\n\\angle(L,AB)=\\angle(AP,PH). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Step\u202f6 \u2013 Central\u2011inscribed angle equality.*  \nIn the circle with centre \\(P\\) passing through \\(A,C,D\\) we have\n\\[\n\\angle DPA = \\angle DBA, \\tag{5}\n\\]\nbecause both angles subtend the same arc \\(DA\\).\n\n*Step\u202f7 \u2013 Expressing \\(\\angle(AP,PH)\\).*  \nFrom (1) the lines \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\); consequently the angle between \\(AP\\) and the altitude \\(PH\\) is half of the central angle \\(\\angle DPA\\):\n\\[\n\\angle(AP,PH)=\\tfrac12\\angle DPA. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Step\u202f8 \u2013 Computing \\(\\angle(L,AB)\\).*  \nCombining (4), (5) and (6) gives\n\\[\n\\angle(L,AB)=\\tfrac12\\angle DBA. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Step\u202f9 \u2013 Angle at \\(B\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).*  \nBecause \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the images of each other under the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) centred at \\(A\\), the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is an isosceles trapezoid with bases \\(AB\\) and \\(EF\\). Hence the base angles at \\(B\\) satisfy\n\\[\n\\angle B = \\tfrac12\\angle DBA. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Step\u202f10 \u2013 Final angular equalities.*  \nFrom (7) and (8) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle(L,AB)=\\angle B. \\tag{9}\n\\]\nSince \\(AB\\) is a chord of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), the angle between any line and the chord \\(BE\\) (or \\(BF\\)) equals the angle between that line and the extension of \\(AB\\). Using the parallelism \\(L\\parallel AP\\) we have\n\\[\n\\angle(L,BE)=\\angle(L,AB)=\\angle B,\\qquad\n\\angle(L,BF)=\\angle(L,AB)=\\angle B. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n*Step\u202f11 \u2013 Tangency.*  \nFor a circle, the unique line through a point that makes with each of two chords through that point an angle equal to the inscribed angle subtended by the opposite arc is the tangent at the point where the tangent subtends that inscribed angle. Equation (10) shows precisely that the line \\(L\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) the angle \\(\\angle B\\), which is the inscribed angle subtended by arc \\(EF\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Therefore \\(L\\) is tangent to that circumcircle.\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as required. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
