"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous proof of the statement.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Homothety \\( \\mathcal H_A\\).**  \n   The homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends the small circle \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\)) onto the large circle \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\), radius \\(R\\)) maps  \n   \\[\n   C\\longmapsto D,\\qquad E\\longmapsto F .\n   \\]\n   Consequently \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear and  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Perpendicularities coming from the definition of \\(P\\) and of the orthocenter \\(H\\).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we have  \n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD,\\qquad PM\\perp AC,\\qquad PN\\perp AD . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the altitude from \\(P\\) meets \\(MN\\) at a point on the line \\(PH\\); hence  \n   \\[\n   PH\\perp MN . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Radical axis.**  \n   The common chord \\(AB\\) of the two circles is their radical axis; therefore  \n   \\[\n   AB\\perp MN . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   From (3) and (4) we obtain the crucial parallelism  \n   \\[\n   PH\\parallel AB . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n4. **The line that has to be shown tangent.**  \n   Let \\(L\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n   Because of (5) we have the oriented angle identity  \n   \\[\n   \\angle(L,AB)=\\angle(AP,PH). \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n5. **A central\u2013inscribed angle relation in the circle \\((A C D)\\).**  \n   In the circle with centre \\(P\\) passing through \\(A,C,D\\) the chords \\(AD\\) and \\(AC\\) subtend the same arc \\(AD\\); consequently the central angle equals the inscribed angle that subtends the same arc on the *other* circle \\(\\Gamma\\):\n   \\[\n   \\angle DPA = \\angle DBA . \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Expressing \\(\\angle(AP,PH)\\) through \\(\\angle DPA\\).**  \n   From (2) we know that \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) are the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\). Hence the line \\(AP\\) is the external angle bisector of \\(\\angle CPN\\); the altitude \\(PH\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle CPN\\). Therefore\n   \\[\n   \\angle(AP,PH)=\\tfrac12\\angle CPN =\\tfrac12\\angle DPA . \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Angle at \\(B\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   Because \\(\\mathcal H_A\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\), the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is an isosceles trapezoid with bases \\(AB\\) and \\(EF\\) (the two bases are parallel). In an isosceles trapezoid the base angles are equal, hence\n   \\[\n   \\angle B = \\tfrac12\\angle DBA . \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Putting the pieces together.**  \n   From (6), (7), (8) and (9) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle(L,AB)=\\tfrac12\\angle DBA =\\angle B . \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(AB\\) is a chord of the circumcircle \\((B E F)\\), the inscribed\u2011angle theorem gives\n   \\[\n   \\angle B = \\angle (BE, BF) . \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n   Using the parallelism \\(L\\parallel AP\\) we finally get\n   \\[\n   \\angle(L,BE)=\\angle(L,BF)=\\angle B . \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Conclusion (tangency).**  \n   For a circle, the unique line through a point that makes with each of two chords through that point an angle equal to the inscribed angle subtended by the opposite arc is the tangent at the point where the two chords meet the circle. Equation (12) shows precisely that the line \\(L\\) through \\(H\\) (parallel to \\(AP\\)) makes with the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) the angle \\(\\angle B\\), which is the inscribed angle subtended by the arc \\(EF\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Hence \\(L\\) is tangent to that circumcircle.\n\nThus the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R>r\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, the order being \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(L\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nWe have to prove that \\(L\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Homothety centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect, there exists a homothety \\(\\mathcal H_A\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_A(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_A(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal H_A(M)=N .\n\\]\n\nIn particular  \n\n\\[\nA,E,F\\text{ are collinear},\\qquad \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Perpendicularities coming from the definition of \\(P\\) and of \\(H\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD,\\qquad PM\\perp AC,\\qquad PN\\perp AD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n(The equalities follow because \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\((A C D)\\); the perpendicularities follow because \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the mid\u2011points of the chords \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) of that circle.)\n\nBecause \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the altitude from \\(P\\) to the side \\(MN\\) passes through \\(H\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The radical axis\n\nThe common chord \\(AB\\) of the two circles is their radical axis; the radical axis is perpendicular to the line joining the centres, whence  \n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (3) and (4) we obtain the parallelism  \n\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AB . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Relating the required line \\(L\\) to the chord \\(AB\\)\n\nBy definition \\(L\\parallel AP\\) and by (5) \\(PH\\parallel AB\\).  Consequently the oriented angle between \\(L\\) and \\(AB\\) equals the oriented angle between \\(AP\\) and \\(PH\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle(L,AB)=\\angle(AP,PH). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  A central\u2013inscribed angle equality\n\nConsider the circle \\((A C D)\\) with centre \\(P\\).  \nThe chords \\(AD\\) and \\(AC\\) subtend the same arc \\(AD\\); therefore the central angle \\(\\angle DPA\\) equals the inscribed angle that subtends the same arc on any other circle passing through \\(A\\) and \\(D\\).  In particular the circle \\(\\Gamma\\) passes through \\(A\\) and \\(D\\), so  \n\n\\[\n\\angle DPA = \\angle DBA . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Expressing \\(\\angle(AP,PH)\\) through \\(\\angle DPA\\)\n\nFrom (2) we know that \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) are the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\).  \nHence the triangle \\(CPN\\) is isosceles with \\(PC=PD\\); the line \\(AP\\) is the external angle bisector of \\(\\angle CPN\\) while the altitude \\(PH\\) (which is perpendicular to the base \\(MN\\)) is the internal angle bisector of the same angle.  Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(AP,PH)=\\tfrac12\\angle CPN =\\tfrac12\\angle DPA . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  The angle at \\(B\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nBecause \\(\\mathcal H_A\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\), 