\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\), the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is an isosceles trapezoid: the two bases \\(AB\\) and \\(EF\\) are parallel (they are images of each other under the homothety) and the non\u2011parallel sides \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are equal in length.  In an isosceles trapezoid the base angles are equal, whence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle B = \\tfrac12\\angle DBA . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Angle between \\(L\\) and the chords \\(BE, BF\\)\n\nCombining (6), (7), (8) and (9) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(L,AB)=\\tfrac12\\angle DBA =\\angle B . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nIn the circumcircle \\((B E F)\\) the inscribed\u2011angle theorem gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle B = \\angle (BE, BF) . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(L\\parallel AP\\), the angle that \\(L\\) makes with each of the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) equals the angle that \\(AP\\) makes with the same chords; using (10) and (11) we get  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(L,BE)=\\angle(L,BF)=\\angle B . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9.  Tangency\n\nFor a circle, the tangent at a point is characterised by the *tangent\u2011chord theorem*: a line is tangent to the circle at a point \\(X\\) iff the angle it makes with any chord through \\(X\\) equals the inscribed angle subtended by the opposite arc.  \nEquation (12) shows that the line \\(L\\) through \\(H\\) (parallel to \\(AP\\)) makes with the two chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) of the circumcircle \\((B E F)\\) exactly the inscribed angle \\(\\angle B\\) subtended by the opposite arc \\(EF\\).  Hence \\(L\\) is tangent to that circumcircle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nThus the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
