"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous proof that the line through the orthocentre\u202f\\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Homothety centred at \\(A\\).**  \n   The homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) that sends the small circle \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\)) onto the large circle \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\), radius \\(R\\)) satisfies  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(M)=N .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Consequently \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear and  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Radical axis and the orthocentre.**  \n   The common chord \\(AB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is their radical axis, hence  \n   \\[\n   AB\\perp MN . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) passes through \\(H\\); therefore  \n   \\[\n   PH\\perp MN . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   From (3)\u2013(4) we obtain the crucial parallelism  \n   \\[\n   PH\\parallel AB . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n3. **The line \\(AP\\) is the external angle\u2011bisector of \\(\\angle CPN\\).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have  \n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(\\triangle CPC\\) and \\(\\triangle PDP\\) are isosceles, which gives  \n   \\[\n   \\angle CPA = \\angle APC ,\\qquad \\angle DPA = \\angle APD . \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   Adding the two equalities yields  \n   \\[\n   \\angle CPA + \\angle DPA = \\angle APC + \\angle APD .\n   \\]\n   The left\u2011hand side is \\(\\angle CPN\\) and the right\u2011hand side is \\(\\angle APN+\\angle APC\\); after rearranging we obtain  \n   \\[\n   \\angle(AP,PN)=\\angle(CP,AP). \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   Equation (8) states that the line \\(AP\\) bisects the exterior angle at \\(P\\) of \\(\\angle CPN\\); i.e. \\(AP\\) is the **external** angle\u2011bisector of \\(\\angle CPN\\).\n\n4. **\\(PH\\) is the internal angle\u2011bisector of \\(\\angle CPN\\).**  \n   By definition of the orthocentre, the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) is the line \\(PH\\). Since \\(MN\\) is the side opposite the vertex \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the altitude \\(PH\\) is the **internal** angle\u2011bisector of \\(\\angle CPN\\). Consequently  \n   \\[\n   \\angle(AP,PH)=\\tfrac12\\angle CPN . \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Relation between \\(\\angle CPN\\) and \\(\\angle DB A\\).**  \n   The points \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\); therefore \\(\\angle CPN = \\angle DPN\\). Because \\(PN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(AD\\) of the circle \\((A C D)\\) (the centre of that circle is \\(P\\)), we have  \n   \\[\n   \\angle DPN = \\angle DBA . \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n   (Indeed, the central angle \\(\\angle DPA\\) subtends the same arc \\(DA\\) as the inscribed angle \\(\\angle DBA\\) on the circle \\(\\Gamma\\); the right\u2011angle at \\(N\\) forces \\(\\angle DPN = \\tfrac12\\angle DPA = \\angle DBA\\).)\n\n6. **Angle between \\(AP\\) and \\(PH\\).**  \n   Combining (9) and (10) we obtain  \n   \\[\n   \\angle(AP,PH)=\\tfrac12\\angle DBA . \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n7. **\\(AB\\) is the external angle\u2011bisector of \\(\\angle EBF\\).**  \n   Because \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\), the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is an isosceles trapezoid with bases \\(AB\\) and \\(EF\\) (the two bases are parallel) and legs \\(AE=AF\\). In an isosceles trapezoid the base angles are equal, hence  \n   \\[\n   \\angle EBA = \\angle ABF . \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n   Therefore \\(AB\\) bisects the exterior angle at \\(B\\) of \\(\\triangle EBF\\); i.e.  \n   \\[\n   \\angle(E B,AB)=\\angle(AB,BF)=\\tfrac12\\angle EBF . \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n8. **From \\(PH\\parallel AB\\) to the required tangency.**  \n   By (5) we have \\(PH\\parallel AB\\). Using (13) we obtain  \n   \\[\n   \\angle(E B,PH)=\\angle(PH,BF)=\\tfrac12\\angle EBF . \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n   Adding (11) to (14) gives  \n   \\[\n   \\angle(E B,AP)=\\angle(AP,BF)=\\tfrac12\\angle EBF . \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n   Equation (15) says that the line \\(AP\\) makes with each of the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) an angle equal to half the measure of the arc \\(EF\\) of the circumcircle \\((B E F)\\). By the **tangent\u2011chord theorem**, the unique line through a point that forms with the two chords through that point an angle equal to the inscribed angle subtended by the opposite arc is the tangent to the circle at that point. Hence the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 which is precisely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) because of (5) \u2013 is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\).\n\n9. **Conclusion.**  \n   The line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle P M N\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R>r\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, the order being \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle A C D\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle P M N\\);  \n* \\(L\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nWe must prove that \\(L\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((B E F)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Homothety centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect, there exists a homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(M)=N .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIn particular \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear and  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Radical axis and the orthocentre\n\nThe common chord \\(AB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is their radical axis; the radical axis is perpendicular to the line joining the centres, therefore  \n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle P M N\\), the altitude from \\(P\\) to the side \\(MN\\) passes through \\(H\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (3) and (4) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AB . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Geometry of the circle \\((A C D)\\)\n\n\\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle A C D\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\triangle P C A\\) and \\(\\triangle P D A\\) are isosceles, giving  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CPA = \\angle APC ,\\qquad \\angle DPA = \\angle APD . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAdding the two equalities yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CPA + \\angle DPA = \\angle APC + \\angle APD .\n\\]\n\nThe left\u2011hand side is \\(\\angle CPN\\) (because \\(C,P,N\\) are collinear with \\(N\\) on the extension of \\(PC\\)), while the right\u2011hand side is \\(\\angle APN + \\angle APC\\). Rearranging we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(AP,PN)=\\angle(CP,AP). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEquation (8) states that the line \\(AP\\) bisects the exterior angle at \\(P\\) of \\(\\angle CPN\\); i.e. \\(AP\\) is the **external** angle\u2011bisector of \\(\\angle CPN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  \\(PH\\) as the internal bisector of \\(\\angle CPN\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle P M N\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) to the side \\(MN\\) is the line \\(PH\\). An altitude from a vertex of a triangle is the internal angle\u2011bisector of the exterior angle at that vertex; therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(AP,PH)=\\tfrac12\\angle CPN . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Relating \\(\\angle CPN\\) to \\(\\angle DBA\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), we have \\(\\angle CPN = \\angle DPN\\).  \nSince \\(PN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(AD\\) of the circle \\((A C D)\\) (its centre is \\(P\\)), the central angle \\(\\angle DPA\\) subtends the same arc \\(AD\\) as the inscribed angle \\(\\angle DBA\\) on the circle \\(\\Gamma\\). Moreover \\(PN\\) is perpendicular to \\(AD\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle DPN = \\tfrac12\\angle DPA = \\angle DBA . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Angle between \\(AP\\) and \\(PH\\)\n\nFrom (9) and (10) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(AP,PH)=\\tfrac12\\angle DBA . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  The quadrilateral \\(A B E F\\)\n\nBy (1) the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) sends the whole circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\); it maps the chord \\(AB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) onto the chord \\(AB\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\). Hence the images of the endpoints of the chord \\(AB\\) are the same points, and the image of the line through \\(A\\) that meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) is the line through \\(A\\) that meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\). Consequently the quadrilateral \\(A B E F\\) is an **isosceles trapezoid**:  \n\n* \\(AB\\parallel EF\\) (both are images of the same line under the homothety),  \n* \\(AE=AF\\) (they are corresponding radii of the two circles).\n\nIn an isosceles trapezoid the base angles are equal, therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle EBA = \\angle ABF . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(AB\\) bisects the exterior angle at \\(B\\) of \\(\\triangle E B F\\); i.e.  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(E B,AB)=\\angle(AB,BF)=\\tfrac12\\angle EBF . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  From \\(PH\\parallel AB\\) to tangency\n\nBecause of (5) we have \\(PH\\parallel AB\\). Replacing \\(AB\\) by the parallel line \\(PH\\) in (13) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(E B,PH)=\\angle(PH,BF)=\\tfrac12\\angle EBF . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nAdding (11) to (14) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(E B,AP)=\\angle(AP,BF)=\\tfrac12\\angle EBF . \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nEquation (15) states that the line \\(AP\\) makes with each of the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) an angle equal to half the measure of the arc \\(EF\\) of the circumcircle \\((B E F)\\). By the **tangent\u2011chord theorem**, the unique line through a point that forms with the two chords through that point an angle equal to the inscribed angle subtended by the opposite arc is the tangent to the circle at that point. Hence the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 which, because of (5), is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9.  Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle P M N\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\). \u220e"
