ith the radical axis, so AB\u22a5MN is valid.\n\n*Claim:* \u201cSince\u202fH\u202fis the orthocentre of\u202f\u0394PMN, the altitude from\u202fP\u202fto\u202fMN\u202fpasses through\u202fH, hence PH\u22a5MN.\u201d  \n**Verification:** By definition of orthocenter, the altitude from\u202fP\u202fto side\u202fMN\u202fcontains\u202fH, and is perpendicular to\u202fMN. Correct.\n\n*Conclusion:* \u201cFrom (3) and (4) we obtain PH\u2225AB.\u201d  \n**Verification:** Both PH and AB are perpendicular to MN, so they are parallel. Correct.\n\n---\n\n#### Step\u202f3 \u2013 Geometry of the circle\u202f(ACD)\n*Claim:* \u201cP is the circum\u2011centre of\u202f\u0394ACD, therefore PA=PC=PD.\u201d  \n**Verification:** True by definition of circumcenter.\n\n*Claim:* \u201cThus \u0394PCA and \u0394PDA are isosceles, giving \u2220CPA = \u2220APC , \u2220DPA = \u2220APD.\u201d  \n**Verification:** In an isosceles triangle the base angles are equal; the statements are correct.\n\n*Critical error begins here:*  \nThe solution writes:\n\n> \u201cAdding the two equalities yields  \n> \\[\n> \\angle CPA + \\angle DPA = \\angle APC + \\angle APD .\n> \\]  \n> The left\u2011hand side is\u202f\u2220CPN (because\u202fC,P,N\u202fare collinear with\u202fN\u202fon the extension of\u202fPC), while the right\u2011hand side is\u202f\u2220APN + \u2220APC. Rearranging we obtain  \n> \\[\n> \\angle(AP,PN)=\\angle(CP,AP). \\tag{8}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error:**  \n- In the given configuration, **C,\u202fP,\u202fN are not collinear**. Point\u202fC lies on circle\u202f\u03a9, point\u202fN is the centre of\u202f\u0393, and point\u202fP is the circumcenter of\u202f\u0394ACD. There is no geometric reason for\u202fN\u202fto lie on line\u202fPC; indeed, the only guaranteed collinearities are\u202fC,\u202fM,\u202fN,\u202fD\u202fon line\u202fMN, and\u202fA,\u202fP,\u202fE,\u202fF\u202fon line\u202fAP.  \n- Consequently, the identification of the sum of angles \u2220CPA\u202f+\u202f\u2220DPA with