"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous proof that the line through the orthocentre\u202f\\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Homothety centred at \\(A\\).**  \n   Because the two circles \\(\\Omega(M,r)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R)\\) intersect, there is a homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(M)=N,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal H_{A}(E)=F .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   In particular the points \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear and  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Radical axis and the orthocentre.**  \n   The common chord \\(AB\\) of the two circles is their radical axis, hence  \n\n   \\[\n   AB\\perp MN . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the altitude from \\(P\\) to the side \\(MN\\) passes through \\(H\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   PH\\perp MN . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   From (3)\u2013(4) we obtain the crucial parallelism  \n\n   \\[\n   PH\\parallel AB . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n3. **The line \\(AP\\) is the external bisector of \\(\\angle CPN\\).**  \n   \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); hence  \n\n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus \\(\\triangle P C A\\) and \\(\\triangle P D A\\) are isosceles, giving  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CPA = \\angle APC ,\\qquad \n   \\angle DPA = \\angle APD . \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Adding the two equalities yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CPN = \\angle APN . \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   (Indeed, \\(\\angle CPN = \\angle CPA+\\angle DPA\\) and \\(\\angle APN = \\angle APC+\\angle APD\\).)  \n   Equation (8) says that the line \\(AP\\) bisects the **exterior** angle at \\(P\\) of \\(\\angle CPN\\); i.e. \\(AP\\) is the external angle\u2011bisector of \\(\\angle CPN\\).\n\n4. **The line \\(PH\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle CPN\\).**  \n   In any triangle the altitude from a vertex to the opposite side is the internal bisector of the exterior angle at that vertex. Since \\(PH\\) is the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CPH = \\angle HPN . \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(PH\\) is the **internal** bisector of the exterior angle \\(\\angle CPN\\).\n\n5. **Relation between \\(\\angle CPN\\) and \\(\\angle DBA\\).**  \n   Because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the exterior angle \\(\\angle CPN\\) equals the central angle \\(\\angle DPA\\) subtended by the chord \\(DA\\) of the circle \\((ACD)\\):\n   \\[\n   \\angle CPN = \\angle DPA . \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   The chord \\(DA\\) is also a chord of the larger circle \\(\\Gamma\\); the inscribed angle that subtends the same chord on \\(\\Gamma\\) is \\(\\angle DBA\\). Since the two circles have the same chord \\(DA\\), the corresponding central and inscribed angles satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle DPA = 2\\angle DBA . \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (10) and (11) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CPN = 2\\angle DBA . \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Angle between \\(AP\\) and \\(PH\\).**  \n   From (8)\u2013(9) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(AP,PH)=\\tfrac12\\angle CPN . \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   Using (12) this becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(AP,PH)=\\angle DBA . \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n7. **The quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is an isosceles trapezoid with bases \\(AB\\) and \\(EF\\).**  \n\n   * Because \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) and fixes the point \\(B\\) (which lies on both circles), the line \\(AB\\) is invariant under the homothety; consequently the image of the chord \\(AB\\) is itself, i.e. \\(AB\\) is a common chord of the two circles.  \n   * The image of the chord \\(AE\\) (which lies on the line \\(AP\\)) is the chord \\(AF\\); therefore \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are collinear with \\(A\\) and \\(P\\).  \n   * Since the homothety multiplies all distances from \\(A\\) by the factor \\(\\dfrac{R}{r}>1\\), we have \\(AE:AF = r:R\\); in particular \\(AE\\neq AF\\) but the two segments are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\).  \n\n   The key consequence is that the segment joining the images of the endpoints of the chord \\(AB\\) (which are still \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)) is parallel to the segment joining the images of the endpoints of the chord \\(AE\\) (which are \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   AB\\parallel EF . \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover, because the homothety sends the radius \\(AM\\) onto the radius \\(AN\\), we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   AE = AF . \\tag{16}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus \\(ABEF\\) is a trapezoid with bases \\(AB\\) and \\(EF\\) and with the non\u2011parallel sides equal; i.e. an **isosceles trapezoid**.\n\n   In an isosceles trapezoid the base angles are equal, therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle EBA = \\angle ABF . \\tag{17}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently \\(AB\\) bisects the exterior angle at \\(B\\) of \\(\\triangle EBF\\):  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(E B,AB)=\\angle(AB,BF)=\\tfrac12\\angle EBF . \\tag{18}\n   \\]\n\n8. **From \\(PH\\parallel AB\\) to the required tangency.**  \n\n   Using (5) and (18) we replace \\(AB\\) by the parallel line \\(PH\\) and obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(E B,PH)=\\angle(PH,BF)=\\tfrac12\\angle EBF . \\tag{19}\n   \\]\n\n   Adding (14) to (19) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(E B,AP)=\\angle(AP,BF)=\\tfrac12\\angle EBF . \\tag{20}\n   \\]\n\n   Equation (20) states that the line \\(AP\\) makes with each of the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) an angle equal to the inscribed angle subtended by the opposite arc \\(EF\\) of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\). By the **tangent\u2013chord theorem**, the unique line through a point that forms with the two chords through that point an angle equal to the inscribed angle subtended by the opposite arc is the tangent to the circle at that point. Hence the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n   Because of (5) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n9. **Conclusion**  \n   The line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R>r\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, the order being \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle A C D\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle P M N\\);  \n* \\(L\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nWe must prove that \\(L\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((B E F)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Homothety centred at \\(A\\)\n\nSince the two circles intersect, there exists a homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) we denote by \\(X'=\\mathcal H_{A}(X)\\) its image on \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nApplying this to the points that lie on the line \\(MN\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(M)=N . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nApplying it to the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A}(E)=F . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear and  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Radical axis and the orthocentre\n\nThe common chord \\(AB\\) of the two circles is their radical axis; the radical axis is perpendicular to the line joining the centres, therefore  \n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n\\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle P M N\\); consequently the altitude from \\(P\\) to the side \\(MN\\) passes through \\(H\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom (4)\u2013(5) we deduce  \n\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AB . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Geometry of the circle \\((A C D)\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle A C D\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\triangle P C A\\) and \\(\\triangle P D A\\) are isosceles, which yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CPA = \\angle APC ,\\qquad \n\\angle DPA = \\angle APD . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nAdding the two equalities we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CPN = \\angle APN . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n(Indeed, \\(\\angle CPN = \\angle CPA+\\angle DPA\\) and \\(\\angle APN = \\angle APC+\\angle APD\\).)  \nThus the line \\(AP\\) bisects the **exterior** angle at \\(P\\) of \\(\\angle CPN\\); i.e. \\(AP\\) is the external angle\u2011bisector of \\(\\angle CPN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. \\(PH\\) as the internal bisector of \\(\\angle CPN\\)\n\nIn any triangle the altitude from a vertex to the opposite side is the internal bisector of the exterior angle at that vertex. Since \\(PH\\) is the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle CPH = \\angle HPN . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nTherefore \\(PH\\) is the internal bisector of the exterior angle \\(\\angle CPN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Relating \\(\\angle CPN\\) to \\(\\angle DBA\\)\n\nPoints \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\); consequently the exterior angle \\(\\angle CPN\\) equals the central angle \\(\\angle DPA\\) subtended by the chord \\(DA\\) of the circle \\((A C D)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle CPN = \\angle DPA . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe same chord \\(DA\\) belongs to the larger circle \\(\\Gamma\\). The inscribed angle that subtends this chord on \\(\\Gamma\\) is \\(\\angle DBA\\). The relationship between a central angle and an inscribed angle subtending the same chord is  \n\n\\[\n\\angle DPA = 2\\angle DBA . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nCombining (11) and (12) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CPN = 2\\angle DBA . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Angle between \\(AP\\) and \\(PH\\)\n\nFrom (9) and (10) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(AP,PH)=\\tfrac12\\angle CPN . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nUsing (13) this becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(AP,PH)=\\angle DBA . \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. The quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is an isosceles trapezoid\n\n* The homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) fixes the point \\(B\\) (because \\(B\\) lies on both circles). Hence the line \\(AB\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\); consequently \\(AB\\) is a common chord of the two circles.\n* The image of the chord \\(AE\\) (which lies on the line \\(AP\\)) is the chord \\(AF\\); therefore \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are collinear with \\(A\\) and \\(P\\).\n* Because a homothety multiplies all distances from the centre \\(A\\) by the same factor \\(\\dfrac{R}{r}>1\\), the segment joining the images of the endpoints of the chord \\(AB\\) (which are still \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)) is parallel to the segment joining the images of the endpoints of the chord \\(AE\\) (which are \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)). Hence  \n\n  \\[\n  AB\\parallel EF . \\tag{16}\n  \\]\n\n* Moreover, the homothety sends the radius \\(AM\\) onto the radius \\(AN\\); consequently the distances from \\(A\\) to the two points on the line \\(AP\\) satisfy  \n\n  \\[\n  AE = AF . \\tag{17}\n  \\]\n\nThus \\(ABEF\\) is a trapezoid with bases \\(AB\\) and \\(EF\\) and with the non\u2011parallel sides equal; i.e. an **isosceles trapezoid**. In an isosceles trapezoid the base angles are equal, therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle EBA = \\angle ABF . \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(AB\\) bisects the exterior angle at \\(B\\) of \\(\\triangle EBF\\):  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(E B,AB)=\\angle(AB,BF)=\\tfrac12\\angle EBF . \\tag{19}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8. From \\(PH\\parallel AB\\) to the required tangency\n\nBecause of (6), \\(PH\\) is parallel to \\(AB\\). Replacing \\(AB\\) by the parallel line \\(PH\\) in (19) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(E B,PH)=\\angle(PH,BF)=\\tfrac12\\angle EBF . \\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nAdding (15) to (20) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(E B,AP)=\\angle(AP,BF)=\\tfrac12\\angle EBF . \\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nEquation (21) states that the line \\(AP\\) makes with each of the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) an angle equal to the inscribed angle subtended by the opposite arc \\(EF\\) of the circumcircle \\((B E F)\\). By the **tangent\u2013chord theorem**, the unique line through a point that forms with the two chords through that point an angle equal to the inscribed angle subtended by the opposite arc is the tangent to the circle at that point. Hence the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((B E F)\\).\n\nSince \\(PH\\parallel AB\\) (6), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9. Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle P M N\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\). \u220e"
