gle the altitude from a vertex to the opposite side is the internal bisector of the exterior angle at that vertex. Since \\(PH\\) is the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle CPH = \\angle HPN . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nTherefore \\(PH\\) is the internal bisector of the exterior angle \\(\\angle CPN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Relating \\(\\angle CPN\\) to \\(\\angle DBA\\)\n\nPoints \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\); consequently the exterior angle \\(\\angle CPN\\) equals the central angle \\(\\angle DPA\\) subtended by the chord \\(DA\\) of the circle \\((A C D)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle CPN = \\angle DPA . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe same chord \\(DA\\) belongs to the larger circle \\(\\Gamma\\). The inscribed angle that subtends this chord on \\(\\Gamma\\) is \\(\\angle DBA\\). The relationship between a central angle and an inscribed angle subtending the same chord is  \n\n\\[\n\\angle DPA = 2\\angle DBA . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nCombining (11) and (12) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CPN = 2\\angle DBA . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Angle between \\(AP\\) and \\(PH\\)\n\nFrom (9) and (10) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(AP,PH)=\\tfrac12\\angle CPN . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nUsing (13) this becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(AP,PH)=\\angle DBA . \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. The quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is an isosceles trapezoid\n\n* The homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) fixes the point \\(B\\) (because \\(B\\) lies on both circles). Hence the line \\(AB\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\); consequently \\(AB\\) is a common chord of the two circles.\n* The image of the chord \\(AE\\) (which lies on the line \\(AP\\)) is the chord \\(AF\\); therefore \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are collinear with \\(A\\) and \\(P\\).\n* Because a homothety multiplies all distances from the centre 