\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A}(E)=F . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear and  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Radical axis and the orthocentre\n\nThe common chord \\(AB\\) of the two circles is their radical axis; the radical axis is perpendicular to the line joining the centres, therefore  \n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n\\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle P M N\\); consequently the altitude from \\(P\\) to the side \\(MN\\) passes through \\(H\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom (4)\u2013(5) we deduce  \n\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AB . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Geometry of the circle \\((A C D)\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle A C D\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\triangle P C A\\) and \\(\\triangle P D A\\) are isosceles, which yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CPA = \\angle APC ,\\qquad \n\\angle DPA = \\angle APD . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nAdding the two equalities we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CPN = \\angle APN . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n(Indeed, \\(\\angle CPN = \\angle CPA+\\angle DPA\\) and \\(\\angle APN = \\angle APC+\\angle APD\\).)  \nThus the line \\(AP\\) bisects the **exterior** angle at \\(P\\) of \\(\\angle CPN\\); i.e. \\(AP\\) is the external angle\u2011bisector of \\(\\angle CPN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. \\(PH\\) as the internal bisector of \\(\\angle CPN\\)\n\nIn any triangle the altitude from a vertex to the opposite side is the internal bisector of the exterior angle at that vertex. Since \\(PH\\) is the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle CPH = \\angle HPN . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nTherefore \\(PH\\) is the internal bisector of the exterior angle \\(\\angle CPN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Relating \\(\\angle CPN\\) to \\(\\angle DBA\\)\n\nPoints \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\); consequently the exterior angle \\(\\angle CPN\\) equals the central angle \\(\\angle DPA\\) subtended by the chord \\(DA\\) of the circle \\((A C D)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle CPN = \\angle DPA . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe same chord \\(DA\\) belongs to the larger circle \\(\\Gamma\\). The inscribed angle that subtends this chord on \\(\\Gamma\\) is \\(\\angle DBA\\). The relationship between a central angle and an inscribed angle subtending the same chord is  \n\n\\[\n\\angle DPA = 2\\angle DBA . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nCombining (11) and (12) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CPN = 2\\angle DBA . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Angle between \\(AP\\) and \\(PH\\)\n\nFrom (9) and (10) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(AP,PH)=\\tfrac12\\angle CPN . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nUsing (13) this becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(AP,PH)=\\angle DBA . \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. The quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is an isosceles trapezoid\n\n* The homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) fixes the point \\(B\\) (because \\(B\\) lies on both circles). Hence the line \\(AB\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\); consequently \\(AB\\) is a common chord of the two circles.\n* The image of the chord \\(AE\\) (which lies on the line \\(AP\\)) is the chord \\(AF\\); therefore \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are collinear with \\(A\\) and \\(P\\).\n* Because a homothety multiplies all distances from the centre \\(A\\) by the same factor \\(\\dfrac{R}{r}>1\\), the segment joining the images of the endpoints of the chord \\(AB\\) (which are still \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)) is parallel to the segment joining the images of the endpoints of the chord \\(AE\\) (which are \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)). Hence  \n\n  \\[\n  AB\\parallel EF . \\tag{16}\n  \\]\n\n* Moreover, the homothety sends the radius \\(AM\\) onto the radius \\(AN\\); consequently the distances from \\(A\\) to the two points on the line \\(AP\\) satisfy  \n\n  \\[\n  AE = AF . \\tag{17}\n  \\]\n\nThus \\(ABEF\\) is a trapezoid with bases \\(AB\\) and \\(EF\\) and with the non\u2011parallel sides equal; i.e. an **isosceles trapezoid**. In an isosceles trapezoid the base angles are equal, therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle EBA = \\angle ABF . \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(AB\\) bisects the exterior angle at \\(B\\) of \\(\\triangle EBF\\):  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(E B,AB)=\\angle(AB,BF)=\\tfrac12\\angle EBF . \\tag{19}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8. From \\(PH\\parallel AB\\) to the required tangency\n\nBecause of (6), \\(PH\\) is parallel to \\(AB\\). Replacing \\(AB\\) by the parallel line \\(PH\\) in (19) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(E B,PH)=\\angle(PH,BF)=\\tfrac12\\angle EBF . \\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nAdding (15) to (20) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(E B,AP)=\\angle(AP,BF)=\\tfrac12\\angle EBF . \\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nEquation (21) states that the line \\(AP\\) makes with each of the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) an angle equal to the inscribed angle subtended by the opposite arc \\(EF\\) of the circumcircle \\((B E F)\\). By the **tangent\u2013chord theorem**, the unique line through a point that forms with the two chords through that point an angle equal to the inscribed angle subtended by the opposite arc is the tangent to the circle at that point. Hence the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((B E F)\\).\n\nSince \\(PH\\parallel AB\\) (6), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9. Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle P M N\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\). \u220e"
