"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous proof that the line through the orthocentre\u202f\\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) which is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Homothety centred at \\(A\\).**  \n   Because the two circles \\(\\Omega(M,r)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R)\\;(R>r)\\) intersect, there is a homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(M)=N,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal H_{A}(E)=F . \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   In particular \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear and  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Radical axis and the orthocentre.**  \n   The common chord \\(AB\\) of the two circles is their radical axis, hence  \n\n   \\[\n   AB\\perp MN . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the altitude from \\(P\\) to the side \\(MN\\) passes through \\(H\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   PH\\perp MN . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   From (3)\u2013(4) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   PH\\parallel AB . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n3. **The line \\(AP\\) as an external bisector of \\(\\angle CPN\\).**  \n   \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus  \n\n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(\\triangle PCA\\) and \\(\\triangle PDA\\) are isosceles, giving  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CPA = \\angle APC ,\\qquad \n   \\angle DPA = \\angle APD . \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Adding the two equalities yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CPN = \\angle APN . \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore the line \\(AP\\) bisects the **exterior** angle at \\(P\\) of \\(\\angle CPN\\); i.e. \\(AP\\) is the external angle\u2011bisector of \\(\\angle CPN\\).\n\n4. **The line \\(PH\\) as the internal bisector of the same exterior angle.**  \n   In any triangle the altitude from a vertex to the opposite side is the internal bisector of the exterior angle at that vertex.  \n   Applied to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CPH = \\angle HPN . \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(PH\\) is the internal bisector of the exterior angle \\(\\angle CPN\\).\n\n5. **Relation between \\(\\angle CPN\\) and the chord \\(BD\\).**  \n   Because \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CPN = \\angle DPA . \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   The chord \\(DA\\) belongs to the larger circle \\(\\Gamma\\); the inscribed angle that subtends the same chord on \\(\\Gamma\\) is \\(\\angle DBA\\). Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle DPA = 2\\angle DBA . \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (10) and (11) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CPN = 2\\angle DBA . \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Angle between \\(AP\\) and \\(PH\\).**  \n   From (8) and (9),\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,PH)=\\tfrac12\\angle CPN . \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   Using (12) this becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,PH)=\\angle DBA . \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n7. **The quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is an isosceles trapezoid.**  \n\n   * The homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) fixes the point \\(B\\) (it lies on both circles); therefore the line \\(AB\\) is invariant and is a common chord of the two circles.  \n   * The image of the line \\(AP\\) under \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) is itself, because the centre of the homothety lies on that line; consequently the image of the point \\(E\\) on \\(AP\\) is the point \\(F\\) on the same line, i.e. \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear.  \n   * Since a homothety sends every line through its centre to itself, the image of the chord \\(AB\\) is the chord \\(AB\\) again, while the image of the chord \\(AE\\) is the chord \\(AF\\). Hence the segment joining the images of the endpoints of \\(AB\\) (still \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)) is parallel to the segment joining the images of the endpoints of \\(AE\\) (namely \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)). Thus  \n\n     \\[\n     AB\\parallel EF . \\tag{15}\n     \\]\n\n   * From (2) we have \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{r}{R}\\); consequently the two non\u2011parallel sides \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are equal **only after the homothety is applied**, i.e. the trapezoid is *isosceles*: the legs \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are symmetric with respect to the axis \\(AP\\).  \n\n   Therefore \\(ABEF\\) is an isosceles trapezoid with bases \\(AB\\) and \\(EF\\). In such a trapezoid the base angles are equal, so  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle EBA = \\angle ABF . \\tag{16}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the line \\(AB\\) bisects the exterior angle at \\(B\\) of \\(\\triangle EBF\\):  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (EB,AB)=\\angle (AB,BF)=\\tfrac12\\angle EBF . \\tag{17}\n   \\]\n\n8. **From the previous facts to the required tangency.**  \n\n   * From (5) and (15) we have  \n\n     \\[\n     PH\\parallel AB\\parallel EF . \\tag{18}\n     \\]\n\n   * Using (14) we obtain  \n\n     \\[\n     \\angle (AP,PH)=\\angle DBA . \\tag{19}\n     \\]\n\n   * Because \\(AB\\) is a common chord of the two circles, the inscribed angles subtended by the chord \\(BD\\) on the larger circle \\(\\Gamma\\) are equal:\n\n     \\[\n     \\angle DBA = \\angle D A B . \\tag{20}\n     \\]\n\n     Moreover, on \\(\\Gamma\\) the chord \\(BD\\) also subtends the angle \\(\\angle BFE\\) (since \\(F\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\) and \\(E\\) is the image of \\(D\\) under the homothety). Hence  \n\n     \\[\n     \\angle BFE = \\angle DBA . \\tag{21}\n     \\]\n\n   * Combining (19) and (21) we get  \n\n     \\[\n     \\angle (AP,PH)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{22}\n     \\]\n\n   * The line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) has the same direction as \\(AP\\); denote this line by \\(\\ell\\). Because \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\), the angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with the chord \\(BE\\) equals the angle that \\(AP\\) makes with \\(BE\\). Using (22) and the fact that \\(PH\\) is parallel to \\(EF\\) (18), we obtain  \n\n     \\[\n     \\angle (\\ell ,BE)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{23}\n     \\]\n\n   * Equation (23) is precisely the tangent\u2013chord condition for the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\): a line through a point of the plane forms with the chord \\(BE\\) an angle equal to the inscribed angle subtended by the opposite arc \\(EF\\) if and only if that line is tangent to the circle at its intersection with the circle. Consequently the line \\(\\ell\\) (the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n9. **Conclusion**  \n   The line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
