            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);\n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\);\n* \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove: the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 1.  Homothety centred at \\(A\\)\n\nSince the two circles intersect, there exists a unique homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nIndeed, the ratio of similarity is \\(\\dfrac{R}{r}>1\\) and the centre must be a common point of the circles, i.e. \\(A\\) (or \\(B\\)). Choose \\(A\\).\n\nConsequences of \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(E)=F .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause a homothety fixes every line through its centre, the line \\(AP\\) is invariant; thus \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear, and \\(AE:AF = r:R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 2.  Radical axis\n\nThe common chord \\(AB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is their radical axis; therefore it is perpendicular to the line joining the centres:\n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 3.  Geometry of point \\(P\\)\n\n\\(P\\) is defined as the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); hence\n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\triangle PCA\\) and \\(\\triangle PDA\\) are isosceles with vertex at \\(A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 4.  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBy definition of orthocentre, the altitude from \\(P\\) to side \\(MN\\) passes through \\(H\\); consequently\n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nCombining (2) and (4) we obtain\n\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AB . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 5.  Angle\u2011bisector relations at \\(P\\)\n\n*Because \\(PA=PC=PD\\):*  \n\nThe equalities \\(PA=PC\\) and \\(PA=PD\\) imply that the external angle at \\(P\\) formed by the lines \\(PC\\) and \\(PD\\) is bisected by the line \\(PA\\). Formally,\n\n\\[\n\\angle CPA = \\angle APD . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Because \\(PH\\) is an altitude in \\(\\triangle PMN\\):*  \n\nIn any triangle, the altitude from a vertex bisects the exterior angle at that vertex. Hence in \\(\\triangle PMN\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle CPH = \\angle HPN . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom (6) and (7) we deduce that the acute angle between the two bisectors \\(AP\\) and \\(PH\\) equals half of the exterior angle \\(\\angle CPN\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,PH)=\\tfrac12\\angle CPN . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 6.  Express \\(\\angle CPN\\) via the chord \\(BD\\)\n\nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the angle \\(\\angle CPN\\) is the angle subtended by chord \\(CD\\) at point \\(P\\). Because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the images of each other under the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\), the chord \\(CD\\) corresponds to chord \\(BD\\) on the larger circle \\(\\Gamma\\). More directly, the inscribed angle theorem on \\(\\Gamma\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\angle DBA = \\frac12\\widehat{D B A} = \\frac12\\widehat{D C A} = \\frac12\\angle DPA .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(D,P,A\\) are collinear with \\(PD=PA\\); therefore \\(\\angle DPA = \\angle CPN\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\angle CPN = 2\\angle DBA . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nInsert (9) into (8):\n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,PH)=\\angle DBA . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 7.  Quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is an isosceles trapezoid\n\nFrom the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\):\n\n* The chord \\(AB\\) is fixed (both endpoints lie on the two circles), so its image is itself.\n* The line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of the homothety; therefore it is invariant, and the image of point \\(E\\) on this line is \\(F\\).\n\nHence the image of segment \\(AB\\) is \\(AB\\) and the image of segment \\(AE\\) is \\(AF\\); consequently the segment joining the images of the endpoints of \\(AB\\) (still \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)) is parallel to the segment joining the images of the endpoints of \\(AE\\) (i.e. \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)). Thus\n\n\\[\nAB\\parallel EF . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, because the homothety sends the whole figure symmetrically with respect to the axis \\(AP\\), the non\u2011parallel sides \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are symmetric about \\(AP\\); therefore \\(ABEF\\) is an **isosceles** trapezoid. In an isosceles trapezoid the base angles are equal, giving\n\n\\[\n\\angle EBA = \\angle ABF . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the line \\(AB\\) bisects the exterior angle at \\(B\\) of \\(\\triangle EBF\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle (EB,AB)=\\angle (AB,BF)=\\tfrac12\\angle EBF . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 8.  Tangency of the required line\n\nFrom (5) we have \\(PH\\parallel AB\\). Using (13) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle (EB,PH)=\\tfrac12\\angle EBF . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nRecall from (10) that \\(\\angle (AP,PH)=\\angle DBA\\). Because \\(B\\) lies on both circles, the inscribed angle theorem on \\(\\Gamma\\) yields \\(\\angle DBA = \\angle BFE\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,PH)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Since \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle (\\ell ,PH)=\\angle (AP,PH)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(PH\\parallel AB\\) (5), so \\(\\angle (\\ell ,AB)=\\angle BFE\\). By the tangent\u2013chord theorem, a line through a point of the plane that makes with chord \\(BE\\) an angle equal to the inscribed angle subtended by the opposite arc \\(EF\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\). Therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(B\\).\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\) is precisely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), the statement is proved. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
        }
