int of tangency**. The solution does not establish that the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle at \\(B\\); it only shows an angle equality, which is insufficient without proving that \\(\\ell\\) actually passes through \\(B\\). This step is therefore incomplete.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Verification Log\n\n#### Step\u202f1 \u2013 Homothety centred at \\(A\\)\n\n- **Claim:** There is a homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) with centre \\(A\\), and it maps \\(C\\mapsto D\\), \\(M\\mapsto N\\), \\(E\\mapsto F\\).\n- **Analysis:**  \n  - A homothety centred at a common point of two intersecting circles indeed maps one circle onto the other, sending the centre \\(M\\) to \\(N\\). This part is correct.  \n  - However, the image of a point on \\(\\Omega\\) under this homothety must lie on the ray from \\(A\\) through that point. Since \\(C\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\) (the line of centres) and not on the ray \\(AC\\), its image cannot be \\(D\\) (which also lies on \\(MN\\)). Therefore \\(\\mathcal H_{A}(C)=D\\) is false.  \n  - Consequently the subsequent statements that rely on this mapping (e.g., the parallelism \\(AB\\parallel EF\\) and many angle relations) have no foundation.  \n- **Conclusion:** Critical Error. The proof\u2019s foundation collapses here.\n\n#### Step\u202f2 \u2013 Radical axis\n\n- **Claim:** \\(AB\\) is the radical axis, so \\(AB\\perp MN\\).\n- **Analysis:** For two intersecting circles, the line through the two intersection points is indeed the radical axis, and it is perpendicular to the line joining the centres. This is a standard fact.\n- **Conclusion:** Correct (provided the circles intersect, which is given).\n\n#### Step\u202f3 \u2013 Geometry of point \\(P\\)\n\n- **Claim:** \\(PA=PC=PD\\) because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).\n- **Analysis:** By definition of circumcenter, it is equidistant from all vertices of the triangle. Hence the claim is valid.\n- **Conclusion:** Correct.\n\n#### 