e segment joining the images of the endpoints of \\(AB\\) (still \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)) is parallel to the segment joining the images of the endpoints of \\(AE\\) (i.e. \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)). Thus\n\n\\[\nAB\\parallel EF . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, because the homothety sends the whole figure symmetrically with respect to the axis \\(AP\\), the non\u2011parallel sides \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are symmetric about \\(AP\\); therefore \\(ABEF\\) is an **isosceles** trapezoid. In an isosceles trapezoid the base angles are equal, giving\n\n\\[\n\\angle EBA = \\angle ABF . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the line \\(AB\\) bisects the exterior angle at \\(B\\) of \\(\\triangle EBF\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle (EB,AB)=\\angle (AB,BF)=\\tfrac12\\angle EBF . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 8.  Tangency of the required line\n\nFrom (5) we have \\(PH\\parallel AB\\). Using (13) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle (EB,PH)=\\tfrac12\\angle EBF . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nRecall from (10) that \\(\\angle (AP,PH)=\\angle DBA\\). Because \\(B\\) lies on both circles, the inscribed angle theorem on \\(\\Gamma\\) yields \\(\\angle DBA = \\angle BFE\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,PH)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Since \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle (\\ell ,PH)=\\angle (AP,PH)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(PH\\parallel AB\\) (5), so \\(\\angle (\\ell ,AB)=\\angle BFE\\). By the tangent\u2013chord theorem, a line through a point of the plane that makes with chord \\(BE\\) an angle equal to the inscribed angle subtended by the opposite arc \\(EF\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\). Therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(B\\).\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\) is precisely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), the statement is proved. \u220e"
