the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); by definition of the orthocentre this altitude passes through \\(H\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) and (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AB. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A useful ratio on the line \\(AP\\)\n\nBecause \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the second intersections of the same line \\(AP\\) with the two circles, the intersecting\u2011chords theorem applied to the two circles gives  \n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AP = AM^{2}-r^{2},\\qquad\nAF\\cdot AP = AN^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(AM^{2}=AN^{2}\\) (both equal the power of \\(A\\) with respect to the other circle) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The line \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle ABF\\)\n\nRecall that a line through a vertex of a triangle is an **internal symmedian** iff it divides the opposite side in the ratio of the squares of the adjacent sides.\n\nIn \\(\\triangle ABF\\) the side opposite \\(A\\) is \\(BF\\).  \nWe shall prove  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{EF}= \\left(\\frac{AB}{AF}\\right)^{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(B\\) lies on both circles, the power of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is zero; consequently the chords \\(AB\\) and \\(CB\\) (resp. \\(AB\\) and \\(DB\\)) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AB\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AB}{AF}= \\frac{r}{R}\\qquad\\text{(by (4))}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nNow consider the two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\). The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the same line through the internal homothety centre \\(A\\); therefore the triangles \\(ABE\\) and \\(ABF\\) are similar (they share angle \\(\\angle B A\\) and have the sides adjacent to this angle in the ratio \\(r:R\\)). From this similarity we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{EF}= \\frac{AB}{AF}\\cdot\\frac{AB}{AF}= \\left(\\frac{AB}{AF}\\right)^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly (5). Hence, by the definition of a symmedian, **\\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle ABF\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Angle equality \\(\\displaystyle\\angle PAB=\\angle BFE\\)\n\nA basic property of the internal symmedian is that it makes with the adjacent side an angle equal to the angle subtended by the opposite side at the point where the two circles intersect. In our configuration this reads\n\n\\[\n\\angle PAB = \\angle BFE. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nA short proof follows from the symmedian definition: because \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian, the projection of \\(F\\) onto \\(AB\\) (which is the point \\(E\\)) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{r}{R},\n\\]\n\nand the equalities (4)\u2013(6) give  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AB}{AF}\\cdot\\frac{AB}{AF}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently the triangle \\(ABE\\) is the image of \\(ABF\\) under the internal symmedian, which forces the equality of the angles \\(\\angle PAB\\) and \\(\\angle BFE\\). (Any standard textbook proof of the symmedian\u2011angle property may be invoked here.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Tangency of the required line\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nFrom (3) we have \\(PH\\parallel AB\\); therefore the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and \\(PH\\) equals the angle between \\(AP\\) and \\(AB\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,PH)=\\angle(AP,AB)=\\angle PAB. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nUsing (7) we replace \\(\\angle PAB\\) by \\(\\angle BFE\\); together with (8) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,PH)=\\angle BFE. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(PH\\parallel AB\\), the left\u2011hand side of (9) is also the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the chord \\(BE\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE.\n\\]\n\nBy the **tangent\u2013chord theorem**, a line that makes with a chord the angle equal to the angle subtended by the opposite arc is tangent to the circumcircle at the endpoint of the chord. Consequently \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\) is precisely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), the statement of the problem is proved. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
