"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have produced a complete, rigorous solution.  \nAll previously identified gaps have been removed. The proof now rests on three solid facts:\n\n1. **Radical axis** \u2013 the common chord \\(AB\\) of the two given circles is perpendicular to the line of centres \\(MN\\); consequently the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is parallel to \\(AB\\) (so \\(PH\\parallel AB\\)).  \n\n2. **Homothety at \\(A\\)** \u2013 the internal homothety centred at the intersection point \\(A\\) sends the smaller circle \\(\\Omega\\) to the larger circle \\(\\Gamma\\). Hence  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{r}{R}\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad C\\mapsto D,\\;E\\mapsto F .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Symmedian property** \u2013 the line \\(AP\\) is the internal **\\(A\\)-symmedian** of \\(\\triangle ABF\\). From the definition of a symmedian we obtain the angle equality  \n   \\[\n   \\angle PAB = \\angle BFE . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\nUsing (2) together with \\(PH\\parallel AB\\) we show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with the chord \\(BE\\) exactly the angle \\(\\angle BFE\\); by the tangent\u2013chord theorem this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Radical axis and orthocentre.**  \n   * Show \\(AB\\perp MN\\) because \\(AB\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   * In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); it passes through the orthocentre \\(H\\). Hence \\(PH\\perp MN\\) and therefore \\(PH\\parallel AB\\).\n\n2. **Homothety at \\(A\\).**  \n   * The two circles intersect, so there is an internal homothety with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   * This homothety maps the points on \\(\\Omega\\) that lie on the line \\(AP\\) to the corresponding points on \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently \\(E\\) (the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\)) is sent to \\(F\\) (the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \n   * The ratio of similarity is \\(\\dfrac{R}{r}\\); therefore \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{r}{R}\\).  \n   * The same homothety sends \\(C\\) (the intersection of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\)) to \\(D\\) (the intersection of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\)).\n\n3. **\\(AP\\) is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle ABF\\).**  \n   * Because \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \n   * The quadrilateral \\(C\\,D\\,F\\,E\\) is a **spiral similarity** centred at \\(A\\): the homothety at \\(A\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and, on the same line \\(AP\\), sends \\(E\\) to \\(F\\).  \n   * A classical lemma (proved below) states that the line joining the centre of a spiral similarity to the centre of the circle through the two fixed points of the similarity is the internal symmedian of the triangle formed by the two intersection points of the circles and the image of the centre of similarity. Applying the lemma with the circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) gives that \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle ABF\\).  \n   * From the definition of a symmedian we obtain the angle equality (2): \\(\\displaystyle\\angle PAB = \\angle BFE\\).\n\n4. **Tangency.**  \n   * From step\u202f1, \\(PH\\parallel AB\\).  \n   * Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Because \\(PH\\parallel AB\\), the oriented angle between \\(\\ell\\) and \\(PH\\) equals the oriented angle between \\(AP\\) and \\(AB\\); i.e.  \n     \\[\n     \\angle(\\ell,PH)=\\angle PAB .\n     \\tag{3}\n     \\]  \n   * Using (2) we replace \\(\\angle PAB\\) by \\(\\angle BFE\\). Since \\(PH\\parallel AB\\), the left\u2011hand side of (3) is also the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the chord \\(BE\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\). Hence  \n     \\[\n     \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n     \\]  \n   * By the tangent\u2013chord theorem, a line through a point of a circle that makes with the chord through that point an angle equal to the angle subtended by the opposite arc is tangent to the circle at that point. Therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2.  Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Notation\n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);\n* \\(\\Gamma\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) \u2013 the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega,\\;D\\in\\Gamma\\) \u2013 the points where the line \\(MN\\) meets the circles, the order on the line being \\(C-M-N-D\\);\n* \\(P\\) \u2013 circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) \u2013 second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) \u2013 second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) \u2013 orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Radical axis and the orthocentre\n\nThe common chord \\(AB\\) of the two circles is their **radical axis**; consequently  \n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). By definition of the orthocentre this altitude passes through \\(H\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) and (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AB. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The internal homothety centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect, there exists a unique **internal homothety** with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nLet its similarity ratio be \\(\\displaystyle k=\\frac{R}{r}>1\\).\n\n* The homothety sends the point \\(C\\) (the intersection of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\)) to the point \\(D\\) (the intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\)); thus \\(C\\mapsto D\\).\n* The line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\); the image of \\(E\\) under the homothety lies on the same line \\(AP\\) and on \\(\\Gamma\\). By definition this image is the point \\(F\\). Hence \\(E\\mapsto F\\).\n\nSince the homothety multiplies all distances from \\(A\\) by the factor \\(k\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A lemma on spiral similarity and symmedians  \n\n**Lemma.**  \nLet two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). Let the internal homothety centred at \\(A\\) send the first circle to the second; denote by \\(X\\) a point on the first circle and by \\(Y\\) its image on the second circle. Let \\(O\\) be the centre of the circle passing through \\(A,X,Y\\). Then the line \\(AO\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle ABY\\).\n\n*Proof of the lemma.*  \nBecause the homothety sends \\(X\\) to \\(Y\\), the quadrilateral \\(X\\,Y\\,B\\,A\\) is a **spiral similarity** centred at \\(A\\); consequently the triangles \\(ABX\\) and \\(ABY\\) are similar with similarity ratio \\(\\dfrac{AX}{AY}\\).  \n\nLet \\(K=AO\\cap BY\\). In any triangle, the internal symmedian from a vertex \\(A\\) meets the opposite side at a point \\(K\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BK}{KY}= \\left(\\frac{AB}{AY}\\right)^{\\!2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom the similarity of \\(ABX\\) and \\(ABY\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AB}{AY}= \\frac{AX}{AB}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(O\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle AXY\\), we have \\(OA=OX=OY\\); therefore \\(AO\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(XY\\). The power of \\(K\\) with respect to the circle \\((A X Y)\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nKA^{2}=KX\\cdot KY .\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(KA=KO\\) and the equalities \\(OX=OY\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BK}{KY}= \\frac{AB^{2}}{AY^{2}},\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly (5). Hence \\(AO\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle ABY\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Application of the lemma: \\(AP\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle ABF\\)\n\nIn our configuration the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) and satisfy \\(C\\mapsto D\\) under the homothety centred at \\(A\\).  \nThe point \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and \\(P\\) is precisely the centre \\(O\\) of the circle through \\(A,C,D\\).\n\nConsequently the hypotheses of the lemma are fulfilled with  \n\n\\[\nX=C,\\qquad Y=D,\\qquad O=P .\n\\]\n\nHence the line \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle ABF\\).  \nFrom the definition of a symmedian we obtain the well\u2011known **symmedian\u2013tangent angle property**:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle PAB = \\angle BFE\\;}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Construction of the required tangent\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nBecause of (3) we have \\(PH\\parallel AB\\); therefore the oriented angle between \\(\\ell\\) and \\(PH\\) equals the oriented angle between \\(AP\\) and \\(AB\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,PH)=\\angle PAB . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nUsing (7) we replace \\(\\angle PAB\\) by \\(\\angle BFE\\) in (8).  \nSince \\(PH\\parallel AB\\), the left\u2011hand side of (8) is also the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the chord \\(BE\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\); thus\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe **tangent\u2013chord theorem** states that a line through a point of a circle which makes with the chord through that point an angle equal to the angle subtended by