H\\parallel AB\\).\n\n2. **Homothety at \\(A\\).**  \n   * The two circles intersect, so there is a unique internal homothety with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   * Because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the homothety maps \\(C\\) to \\(D\\).  \n   * The line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\); the image of \\(E\\) under the same homothety lies on the same line \\(AP\\) and on \\(\\Gamma\\). By definition this image is the point \\(F\\).  \n   * The similarity ratio of the homothety is \\(\\displaystyle k=\\frac{R}{r}>1\\); consequently (1) holds.\n\n3. **Symmedian Lemma (proved below).**  \n   * The quadrilateral \\(A C D P\\) is cyclic because \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle A C D\\).  \n   * From (1) we have \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}\\); therefore the triangles \\(\\triangle A E C\\) and \\(\\triangle A F D\\) are similar (SAS).  \n   * Hence the directed angles satisfy  \n     \\[\n     \\angle EAC = \\angle FAD . \\tag{3}\n     \\]  \n   * The similarity also gives \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{r}{R}\\).  \n   * Because \\(\\triangle A C D\\) is isosceles with vertex \\(A\\) (its circum\u2011centre is \\(P\\)), the line \\(AP\\) is the **center of the spiral similarity** that sends segment \\(CD\\) to segment \\(EF\\).  \n   * A classical fact (proved in the Lemma below) states that the line joining the centre of such a spiral similarity to the common point \\(A\\) is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of the triangle formed by the two intersection points of the circles (\\(A\\) and \\(B\\)) and the image of the second circle\u2019s point (\\(F\\)). Consequently \\(AP\\) is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle ABF\\).  \n   * By the definition of a symmedian, the symmedian through a vertex makes with the adjacent side the same angle as the chord opposite that side subtends at the circum\u2011circle; therefore we obtain (2).\n\n4. **Tangency.**  \n 