---------------\n### 1.  Radical axis and the orthocentre\n\nThe common chord \\(AB\\) of the two circles is their **radical axis**; therefore  \n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). By definition of the orthocentre this altitude passes through \\(H\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) and (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AB. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The internal homothety centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the circles intersect, there exists a unique **internal homothety** with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nLet its similarity ratio be  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{R}{r}>1 .\n\\]\n\n* The homothety maps the point \\(C\\) (the intersection of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\)) to the point \\(D\\) (the intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\)); thus \\(C\\mapsto D\\).  \n* The line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\). The image of \\(E\\) under the homothety lies on the same line \\(AP\\) and on \\(\\Gamma\\); by definition this image is the point \\(F\\). Hence \\(E\\mapsto F\\).  \n\nSince every distance from \\(A\\) is multiplied by \\(k\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A symmedian lemma\n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> Let two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). Let the internal homothety centred at \\(A\\) send the first circle to the second; denote by \\(X\\) a point on the first circle and by \\(Y\\) its image on the second circle. Let \\(O\\) be the centre of the circle through \\(A,X,Y\\). Then the line \\(AO\\) is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle ABY\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause the homothety sends \\(X\\) to \\(Y\\), the quadrilateral \\(A X Y B\\) is a **spiral similarity** centred at \\(A\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AB}{AY}= \\frac{AX}{AB}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(A,X,Y\\) lie on a circle with centre \\(O\\); therefore \\(OA=OX=OY\\). Hence \\(O\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(XY\\).  \n\nLet \\(K=AO\\cap BY\\). In any triangle, the internal symmedian from a vertex \\(A\\) meets the opposite side at a point \\(K\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BK}{KY}= \\left(\\frac{AB}{AY}\\right)^{2}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom the similarity of triangles \\(ABX\\) and \\(ABY\\) (which follows from (5)) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AB}{AY}= \\frac{AX}{AB}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nUsing the power of the point \\(K\\) with respect to the circle \\((A X Y)\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nKA^{2}=KX\\cdot KY .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(KA=KO\\) and \\(OX=OY\\), the equality above gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BK}{KY}= \\frac{AB^{2}}{AY^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nComparing with (6) we see that the point \\(K\\) is exactly the intersection of the \\(A\\)-symmedian with \\(BY\\). Hence \\(AO\\) is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle ABY\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Application of the lemma: \\(AP\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle ABF\\)\n\nIn our configuration the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the images of each other under the homothety centred at \\(A\\); the point \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle A C D\\). Therefore \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle through \\(A,C,D\\).  \n\nFrom (4) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}.\n\\]\n\nThus the triangles \\(\\triangle A E C\\) and \\(\\triangle A F D\\) are similar (SAS), and consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle EAC = \\angle FAD . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nHence the line \\(AP\\) is the centre of the spiral similarity that sends segment \\(CD\\) to segment \\(EF\\).  \nApplying the Lemma with  \n\n\\[\nX=C,\\qquad Y=F,\\qquad O=P,\n\\]\n\nwe conclude that **\\(AP\\) is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle ABF\\)**.\n\nA basic property 