allel AB\\).\n\n2. **Homothety at \\(A\\).**  \n   * The two intersecting circles admit a unique internal homothety with centre \\(A\\); its ratio is \\(k=R/r>1\\).  \n   * This homothety sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) (both lie on the line of centres) and sends the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\), namely \\(E\\), to the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\), namely \\(F\\).  \n   * Consequently (1) holds.\n\n3. **Symmedian Lemma.**  \n   * Prove that for any points \\(X\\in\\Omega,\\;Y\\in\\Gamma\\) with \\(AX:AY = AC:AD\\) (i.e. \\(AX/AY = AC/AD\\)), the centre \\(P\\) of the circle \\((A X Y)\\) lies on the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle ABY\\).  \n   * The proof uses the law of sines in triangles \\(ABY\\) and \\(ABX\\) together with the similarity \\(\\triangle A X C\\sim\\triangle A Y D\\) that follows from (1). From these relations we obtain the defining ratio of the \\(A\\)-symmedian,\n     \\[\n     \\frac{BK}{KY}= \\left(\\frac{AB}{AY}\\right)^{2},\n     \\]\n     where \\(K=AP\\cap BY\\). Hence \\(AP\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian.  \n   * Applying the lemma with \\(X=C,\\;Y=F\\) yields (2).\n\n4. **Tangency.**  \n   * Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Because \\(PH\\parallel AB\\) (step\u202f1), the oriented angle between \\(\\ell\\) and \\(PH\\) equals the oriented angle between \\(AP\\) and \\(AB\\):\n     \\[\n     \\angle(\\ell,PH)=\\angle PAB . \\tag{3}\n     \\]  \n   * By (2) we may replace \\(\\angle PAB\\) with \\(\\angle BFE\\). Since \\(PH\\parallel AB\\), the left\u2011hand side of (3) is also the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the chord \\(BE\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\); therefore\n     \\[\n     \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{4}\n     \\]  \n   * By the tangent\u2013chord theorem, a line through a point of a circle that makes with the chord through that point an angle equal to the angle subtended by the opposite arc is tangent to the circle at that point. Equation (4