(ABX\\) and \\(ABY\\). Using (4.4) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AB}{AX}= \\frac{BX}{\\sin\\angle BAX}= \\frac{BY}{\\sin\\angle BAY}= \\frac{AB}{AY}\\cdot\\frac{\\sin\\angle BAY}{\\sin\\angle BAX}. \\tag{4.7}\n\\]\n\nBut from the similarity \\(\\triangle A X C\\sim\\triangle A Y D\\) (which follows from (3.1) and the fact that \\(C,D\\) are collinear with \\(M,N\\)) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin\\angle BAX}{\\sin\\angle BAY}= \\frac{AX}{AY}. \\tag{4.8}\n\\]\n\nCombining (4.7) and (4.8) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AB}{AX}= \\frac{AB}{AY}\\cdot\\frac{AX}{AY}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\frac{AB}{AY}= \\left(\\frac{AB}{AX}\\right)^{\\!2}. \\tag{4.9}\n\\]\n\nInsert (4.9) into (4.6):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BK}{KY}= \\left(\\frac{AB}{AY}\\right)^{\\!2}. \\tag{4.10}\n\\]\n\nEquation (4.10) is precisely the defining property of the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle ABY\\): the symmedian meets the opposite side in the ratio of the squares of the adjacent sides. Hence \\(AP\\) (which contains the point \\(K\\)) is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle ABY\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Application of the lemma to the given configuration\n\nIn our problem we have \\(X=C\\) (a point of \\(\\Omega\\)) and \\(Y=F\\) (its image under the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\)). The centre of the circle through \\(A,C,F\\) is exactly the point \\(P\\) (the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) coincides with the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACF\\) because \\(C,F\\) are homothetic images of each other). Therefore the hypotheses of the Lemma are satisfied, and we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle PAB = \\angle BFE\\;}. \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Construction of the required tangent\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nFrom (2.3) we have \\(PH\\parallel AB\\); consequently the oriented angle between \\(\\ell