\ell\\) does not necessarily intersect the circle \\((BEF)\\) at \\(B\\). This undermines the final conclusion.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Verification Log\n\n#### Section\u202f1 \u2013 Notation  \n- The notation is clear and consistent. No issues.\n\n#### Section\u202f2 \u2013 \u201cThe radical axis gives \\(PH\\parallel AB\\)\u201d\n\n1. **Claim:** \u201cThe common chord \\(AB\\) of the two circles is their radical axis; therefore \\(AB\\perp MN\\).\u201d  \n   - Correct: For two intersecting circles, the line joining their centers is perpendicular to the common chord. No error.\n\n2. **Claim:** \u201cIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). By definition of the orthocentre this altitude passes through \\(H\\); hence \\(PH\\perp MN\\).\u201d  \n   - Correct reasoning, assuming \\(P\\) is not collinear with \\(M,N\\). The solution does not explicitly argue that \\(P\\) is not on \\(MN\\); this is a **justification gap** (the altitude is well\u2011defined only if the vertex is not on the opposite side). The gap is minor and can be filled by noting that \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(A\\neq C\\neq D\\), so \\(P\\) cannot lie on \\(MN\\).\n\n3. **Conclusion:** From the two perpendicularities, \\(PH\\parallel AB\\).  \n   - Follows correctly from the previous statements. No further issue.\n\n#### Section\u202f3 \u2013 \u201cThe internal homothety centred at \\(A\\)\u201d\n\n- The existence of a unique internal homothety sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) is correct.  \n- The ratio \\(k=R/r\\) and the mapping \\(\\mathcal H(C)=D,\\ \\mathcal H(E)=F\\) are correct.  \n- Equation (3.1) \\(\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{r}{R}\\) follows from the homothety. No error.\n\n#### Section\u202f4 \u2013 \u201cA symmedian lemma\u201d\n\n**Lemma statement:**  \n- The lemma claims that for any point \\(X\\) on the first circle, with image \\(Y\\) on the second circle under the internal homothety at \\(A\\), the line \\(AP\\) (where \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle AXY\\)) is the internal \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle ABY\\).\n\n**Proof analysis:**\n\n1. **Equation (4.1):** \u201cBecause \\(P\\) is the centre of the circumcircle of \\(A,X,Y\\), \\(PA=PX=PY\\).\u201d  \n   - Correct: the circum\u2011centre is equidistant from the three vertices.\n\n2. **Equation (4.2):** \u201cFrom the definition of the homothety we have \\(\\frac{AX}{AY}= \\frac{r}{R}=k^{-1}\\).\u201d  \n   - Correct: homothety with centre \\(A\\) scales distances from \\(A\\) by factor \\(k\\).\n\n3. **Definition of \\(K\\):** \u201cLet \\(K=AP\\cap BY\\).\u201d  \n   - Fine.\n\n4. **Criterion (4.3):** \u201cIn \\(\\triangle ABY\\) the cevian \\(AK\\) is a symmedian iff \\(\\frac{BK}{KY}= \\left(\\frac{AB}{AY}\\right)^{2}\\).\u201d  \n   - This is a standard symmedian property (ratio of segments on the opposite side equals the square of the adjacent side ratios). Correct.\n\n5. **Law of sines in triangles \\(ABX\\) and \\(ABY\\) (4.4):**  \n   - The expressions are correct.\n\n6. **Claim:** \u201cSince \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) lie on the two circles that intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the oriented angles \\(\\angle AXB\\) and \\(\\angle AYB\\) are equal (they are both the measure of the arc \\(AB\\) of the respective circles).\u201d  \n   - **Critical Error.** The arcs \\(AB\\) on the two *different* circles generally have different measures; consequently \\(\\angle AXB\\) and \\(\\angle AYB\\) are not equal in general. This invalidates the subsequent equality \\(\\sin\\angle AXB=\\sin\\angle AYB\\).\n\n7. **Derivation of (4.5):** Uses the (false) equality of sines to obtain \\(\\frac{AX}{AY}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle ABX}{\\sin\\angle ABY}\\).  \n   - Because the previous step is false, (4.5) is not justified.\n\n8. **Step (4.6):** \u201cBecause \\(\\mathcal H\\) sends the whole first circle onto the second, the chords through \\(A\\) are proportional; in particular \\(\\frac{AB}{AB}=1,\\ \\frac{AX}{AY}=k^{-1}\\).\u201d  \n   - The first equality is trivial; the second repeats (4.2). No new information.\n\n9. **From (4.5) and (4.6) to (4.7):** \u201c\\(\\frac{\\sin\\angle ABX}{\\sin\\angle ABY}=k^{-1}\\).\u201d  \n   - This follows only if (4.5) were valid, which it is not.\n\n10. **Law of sines in triangle \\(ABY\\) for cevian \\(AK\\) (4.8):** The formula \\(\\frac{BK}{KY}= \\frac{AB}{AY}\\cdot\\frac{\\sin\\angle BAY}{\\sin\\angle ABK}\\) is correct.\n\n11. **Claim:** \u201cBecause \\(K\\) lies on \\(AP\\), the angles \\(\\angle BAY\\) and \\(\\angle ABK\\) are equal; denote their common value by \\(\\phi\\).\u201d  \n    - **Critical Error.** There is no reason for \\(\\angle BAY\\) (angle between \\(BA\\) and \\(AY\\)) to equal \\(\\angle ABK\\) (angle between \\(AB\\) and \\(BK\\)). The fact that \\(K\\) lies on \\(AP\\) does not relate these two angles. Hence (4.9) does not follow.\n\n12. **From (4.8) to (4.9):** Uses the false equality of angles, yielding \\(\\frac{BK}{KY}= \\frac{AB}{AY}\\). This is not justified.\n\n13. **Final step:** \u201cUsing (4.7) to replace \\(\\frac{AB}{AY}\\) by \\(\\left(\\frac{AB}{AY}\\right)^{2}\\) \u2026 we obtain (4.3).\u201d  \n    - **Critical Error.** No algebraic or geometric justification is given for squaring the ratio; the statement \u201creplace \u2026 by its square\u201d is nonsensical. The conclusion (4.3) is therefore unsupported.\n\n**Overall assessment of Lemma:** The proof contains multiple false statements and unjustified manipulations. Consequently the lemma is **not proven**; in fact the lemma is false in general. This constitutes a **critical error** that invalidates every later step that relies on the lemma (Sections\u202f5 and\u202f6).\n\n#### Section\u202f5 \u2013 \u201cApplication of the lemma\u201d\n\n- The argument assumes the lemma is true and then states that the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACF\\) coincides with that of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) because the homothety sends the circle \\((ACD)\\) onto \\((AEF)\\).  \n- **Critical Error:** The homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) maps point \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) to \\(F\\); it does **not** map the circle through \\(A,C,D\\) to the circle through \\(A,E,F\\). The circles \\((ACD)\\) and \\((AEF)\\) generally have different centres. Hence the claim that both circles share the centre \\(P\\) is false. Consequently the derived angle equality \\(\\angle PAB = \\angle BFE\\) (5.1) lacks justification.\n\nSince this step depends on the false lemma, it is also invalid.\n\n#### Section\u202f6 \u2013 \u201cConstruction of the required tangent\u201d\n\n1. **Definition of \\(\\ell\\):** \u201cLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\u201d  \n   - Correct.\n\n2. **Use of (2.3):** \u201cFrom (2.3) we have \\(PH\\parallel AB\\); consequently the oriented angle between \\(\\ell\\) and \\(PH\\) equals the oriented angle between \\(AP\\) and \\(AB\\): \\(\\angle(\\ell,PH)=\\angle PAB\\).\u201d  \n   - The equality of oriented angles follows from parallelism, so this step is fine **provided** \\(\\angle PAB\\) is known, which depends on the invalid Lemma.\n\n3. **Substitution using (5.1):** Replaces \\(\\angle PAB\\) with \\(\\angle BFE\\).  \n   - Invalid because (5.1) is unproved.\n\n4. **Claim:** \u201cBecause \\(PH\\parallel AB\\), the left\u2011hand side of (6.1) is also the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the chord \\(BE\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\); therefore \\(\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE\\).\u201d  \n   - **Critical Error.** The line \\(PH\\) is parallel to \\(AB\\), but there is no reason that the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and \\(PH\\) equals the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the chord \\(BE\\). The chord \\(BE\\) is not generally parallel to \\(PH\\). This step conflates different directions and is unjustified.\n\n5. **Application of tangent\u2013chord theorem:** The theorem requires the line to pass through the point of tangency (here \\(B\\)). 