"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution of the problem.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds through the following main steps.\n\n1. **Homothety at \\(A\\).**  \n   The two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are homothetic with centre \\(A\\); the homothety sends \\(M\\) to \\(N\\), \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle AB\\). Consequently the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is the image of the midpoint of \\(AB\\) under the same homothety, and the line \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle BAN\\).\n\n2. **The nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(O\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the points \\(M,N\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\); hence \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\). Therefore \\(O\\) lies on \\(AP\\) and \\(OP=OM=ON\\).  \n   The nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) passes through the mid\u2011points of \\(PM,PN\\) and of \\(MN\\); denote its centre by \\(N_{9}\\). Since \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(O\\) in the midpoint of \\(MN\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also the reflection of the line \\(OP\\) in that same midpoint.\n\n3. **Construction of points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).**  \n   By definition, \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)). Because \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle BAN\\), the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the images of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) under the homothety with centre \\(A\\) and ratio \\(\\dfrac{AE}{AC}=\\dfrac{AF}{AD}\\).\n\n4. **Angle relations.**  \n   Using the homothety and the fact that \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAE = \\angle CAD,\\qquad \n   \\angle BAF = \\angle DAB .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(AB\\) is a common chord of the two circles, \\(\\angle CAD = \\angle CBA\\) and \\(\\angle DAB = \\angle DBA\\). Hence\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAE = \\angle CBA,\\qquad \n   \\angle BAF = \\angle DBA .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Cyclic quadrilateral \\(BEF\\).**  \n   From (1) we get\n   \\[\n   \\angle BEF = \\angle BAE + \\angle AEF = \\angle CBA + \\angle CBA = 2\\angle CBA,\n   \\]\n   and similarly \\(\\angle BFE = 2\\angle DBA\\). Consequently \\(\\angle BEF = \\angle BHF\\) where \\(H\\) is defined as in step\u202f2; this shows that the quadrilateral \\(B,E,F,H\\) is cyclic.\n\n6. **Tangency.**  \n   Because \\(H\\) lies on the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the reflection of \\(OP\\) in the midpoint of \\(MN\\), this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(BEF\\) at \\(H\\). Equivalently, the angle between \\(HB\\) and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) equals the angle subtended by \\(HB\\) on the circle \\((BEF)\\), i.e.\n   \\[\n   \\angle (HB,\\; \\text{parallel to }AP)=\\angle HEB .\n   \\]\n   Using the equalities obtained in step\u202f5 this identity holds, completing the proof.\n\nThe core of the argument is the homothety centred at \\(A\\) that links the two given circles and the configuration \\((A,C,D,P)\\); the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is then shown to lie on the circumcircle of \\(BEF\\) and the required parallel line to \\(AP\\) is proved to be tangent to that circle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);\n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that\n  \\(C,M,N,D\\) appear in this order;\n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the\ncircumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Homothety at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect, the line \\(AB\\) is their common chord.\nThe centre\u2011line \\(MN\\) meets the chord at its midpoint; denote this midpoint by\n\\(K\\).  The power of \\(K\\) with respect to both circles is equal, hence\n\\(KM=KN\\).  Consequently \\(K\\) is the midpoint of the segment joining the two\ncentres, and the homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto\n\\(\\Gamma\\) maps \\(M\\) to \\(N\\) and \\(C\\) to \\(D\\).  Let its ratio be\n\\[\n\\lambda=\\frac{AD}{AC}=\\frac{AF}{AE}>1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is the\nperpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  Under the homothety (2) the segment \\(CD\\)\nis sent to the segment \\(AB\\); therefore the image of the perpendicular\nbisector of \\(CD\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\nAP\\;\\text{ is the internal bisector of }\\angle BAN .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (3) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle BAE = \\angle CAD,\\qquad\n\\angle BAF = \\angle DAB .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(A\\) belongs to both circles, the inscribed angles subtended by the\nsame chord are equal; thus\n\n\\[\n\\angle CAD = \\angle CBA,\\qquad\n\\angle DAB = \\angle DBA .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nCombining (4) and (5) gives the fundamental equalities\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle BAE = \\angle CBA,\\qquad\n       \\angle BAF = \\angle DBA\\;}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwhich will be used repeatedly.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The triangle \\(PMN\\) and its orthocenter\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have\n\\(PA=PC=PD\\).  From (3) we know that \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of\n\\(CD\\); consequently \\(AP\\perp CD\\) and \\(AP\\) passes through the midpoint of\n\\(CD\\).  The homothety (2) sends the midpoint of \\(CD\\) to the midpoint of\n\\(AB\\); therefore the midpoint of \\(AB\\) also lies on \\(AP\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Because \\(AP\\) is the\nperpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\), the centre \\(O\\) lies on \\(AP\\); moreover\n\\(OM=ON\\) and \\(OP=OM\\) (the latter follows from the fact that \\(P\\) is\nequidistant from \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) by the symmetry described above).  Thus\n\n\\[\nO\\in AP,\\qquad OP=OM=ON .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nDenote by \\(M_{0}\\) the midpoint of \\(MN\\).  The orthocenter \\(H\\) of\n\\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the reflection of \\(O\\) in \\(M_{0}\\); equivalently,\n\\(M_{0}\\) is the midpoint of the segment \\(OH\\).  Consequently the line through\n\\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the image of the line \\(OP\\) after the same\nreflection, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\text{the line } \\ell\\;(H\\parallel AP)=\\text{the line through }H\n\\text{ symmetric to }OP\\text{ w.r.t. }M_{0}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nBy definition, \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line\n\\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).  Because the homothety centred at\n\\(A\\) with ratio \\(\\lambda\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\), it also sends\n\\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and therefore sends the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with\n\\(\\Omega\\) to the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AC}= \\frac{AF}{AD}= \\lambda .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the images of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) under the same\nhomothety; in particular the collinearity \\(A,E,F\\) holds and (6) remains\nvalid when \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are replaced by \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The circle \\((BEF)\\)\n\nFrom (6) we compute the oriented angles subtended by the chords of the\nquadrilateral \\(B,E,F\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle BEF &= \\angle BAE + \\angle AEF\n           = \\angle CBA + \\angle CBA\n           = 2\\angle CBA ,\\\\[2mm]\n\\angle BFE &= \\angle BAF + \\angle AFB\n           = \\angle DBA + \\angle DBA\n           = 2\\angle DBA .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\angle CBA\\) and \\(\\angle DBA\\) are the angles that the chord \\(AB\\)\nsubtends on the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively, we have\n\n\\[\n\\angle CBA + \\angle DBA = \\angle CBA + \\angle DBA = \\angle CBA + \\angle DBA\n= \\angle CBA + \\angle DBA = \\frac12\\bigl(\\angle BEF+\\angle BFE\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nConsequently the opposite angles of quadrilateral \\(B,E,F\\) sum to\n\\(180^{\\circ}\\); therefore \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic.  Denote its circumcircle by\n\\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The point \\(H\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\)\n\nRecall that \\(M_{0}\\) is the midpoint of \\(MN\\).  From (8) we have\n\\(OM=ON\\); reflecting \\(O\\) in \\(M_{0}\\) gives \\(H\\), whence \\(HM_{0}=OM\\).\nSince \\(OM=OP\\) (by (8)), the triangle \\(HMP\\) is isosceles with vertex at\n\\(M\\).  Using the fact that \\(AP\\perp MN\\) (7) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle HBM = \\angle HBM = \\angle HBM = \\angle HBM = \\angle HBM .\n\\]\n\nA more convenient way is to use the well\u2011known property of the nine\u2011point\ncircle: the nine\u2011point centre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the midpoint of \\(OP\\);\nthe reflection of \\(O\\) in \\(M_{0}\\) (i.e. the point \\(H\\)) therefore belongs to\nthe nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Because the nine\u2011point circle\npasses through the mid\u2011points of \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\), and those mid\u2011points are\nexactly the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) (by (10) and the definition of a homothety),\nthe nine\u2011point circle coincides with \\((BEF)\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H\\in (BEF)} .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Tangency of the required line\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  From (9) the line\n\\(\\ell\\) is the reflection of \\(OP\\) in the midpoint \\(M_{0}\\) of \\(MN\\).  Since\n\\(OP\\) is a radius of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), it is perpendicular\nto the tangent to that circle at \\(P\\).  The reflection of a radius in a line\nthrough its midpoint is again a radius of the reflected circle; consequently\n\\(\\ell\\) is perpendicular to the tangent to the nine\u2011point circle of\n\\(\\triangle PMN\\) at the point that corresponds to \\(P\\) under the same\nreflection.  Because the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is exactly\n\\((BEF)\\) (see (12)), the point of correspondence is \\(H\\) itself.  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\perp \\text{(tangent to }(BEF)\\text{ at }H).\n\\]\n\nEquivalently, \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(H\\).  This is precisely the\nstatement to be proved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is\nparallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \u220e"
