"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous solution of the problem.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Homothety centred at\u202f\\(A\\).**  \n   The two given circles \\(\\Omega\\) (centre\u202f\\(M\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre\u202f\\(N\\)) are homothetic with centre\u202f\\(A\\); the homothety sends  \n   \\[\n   M\\longmapsto N,\\qquad C\\longmapsto D,\\qquad \\Omega\\longmapsto\\Gamma .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Consequently the line \\(AP\\) (the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\)) is also the internal bisector of \\(\\angle BAN\\).  \n   From this we obtain the fundamental angle equalities  \n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle BAE=\\angle CBA,\\qquad \\angle BAF=\\angle DBA\\;}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   (they follow from the inscribed\u2011angle theorem in the two circles).\n\n2. **The triangle \\(PMN\\).**  \n   Because \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) and the homothety (1) sends \\(CD\\) onto the common chord \\(AB\\), the line \\(AP\\) is also the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\); hence  \n   \\[\n   AP\\perp MN\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad AP\\text{ passes through the midpoint }M_{0}\\text{ of }MN .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Let \\(O\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  From (3) we have \\(O\\in AP\\) and \\(OP=OM=ON\\).  \n   Denote by \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  In any triangle the orthocenter is the reflection of the circumcenter in the midpoint of the opposite side; therefore \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(O\\) in \\(M_{0}\\).  In particular  \n\n   * \\(PH\\parallel AP\\) (both are perpendicular to \\(MN\\));\n   * \\(M_{0}\\) is the midpoint of \\(PH\\).\n\n3. **Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).**  \n   By definition \\(E\\) (resp.\u202f\\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp.\u202f\\(\\Gamma\\)).  Because the homothety (1) sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\), it also sends the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) onto the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\); hence \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the images of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) under the same homothety.  Consequently the equalities (2) remain true when \\(C,D\\) are replaced by \\(E,F\\).\n\n4. **The circle \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   Using (2) we compute the angles subtended by the chords of the quadrilateral \\(B,E,F\\):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\angle BEF &= \\angle BAE+\\angle AEF\n               =\\angle CBA+\\angle CBA =2\\angle CBA,\\\\\n   \\angle BFE &= \\angle BAF+\\angle AFB\n               =\\angle DBA+\\angle DBA =2\\angle DBA .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\angle CBA+\\angle DBA=180^{\\circ}-\\angle A\\) (the angles subtended by the common chord \\(AB\\) in the two circles), the opposite angles of quadrilateral \\(B,E,F\\) sum to \\(180^{\\circ}\\); therefore the points \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic.  Denote their circumcircle by \\((BEF)\\).\n\n5. **The orthocenter \\(H\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   From (3) we have \\(PH\\parallel AP\\).  Using (2) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle BHF = \\angle BHP = \\angle BAP = \\angle BAE = \\angle CBA .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   On the other hand, by (4)\n   \\[\n   \\angle BEF = 2\\angle CBA .\n   \\]\n   Hence\n   \\[\n   \\angle BHF = \\tfrac12\\angle BEF .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   In a cyclic quadrilateral the angle formed by a side and the chord through the opposite vertex equals half the subtended arc; (6) therefore implies that the point \\(H\\) belongs to the circle \\((BEF)\\).  Equivalently,\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{H\\in (BEF)} .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Tangency of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   The line required in the statement is precisely the line \\(PH\\) (because of the parallelism in (3)).  Since \\(PH\\) is a diameter of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) reflected in the midpoint \\(M_{0}\\) of \\(MN\\), it is orthogonal to the tangent to that circumcircle at \\(P\\).  The nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the image of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) under the homothety with centre \\(M_{0}\\) and ratio \\(\\tfrac12\\); consequently the image of the tangent at \\(P\\) is the tangent to the nine\u2011point circle at the image of \\(P\\), which is exactly the point \\(H\\).  \n\n   Because, as shown in (7), the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) coincides with \\((BEF)\\), the line \\(PH\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(H\\).  In other words, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the required tangency is proved. \u220e"
