            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, intersecting at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nDenote by \\(C\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (the one lying between \\(M\\) and \\(A\\)), and by \\(D\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\) (the one lying beyond \\(N\\)).  \nThus the points appear on the line in the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Homothety at \\(A\\))\n\nThe circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are homothetic with centre \\(A\\).  \nIndeed, the two circles intersect at \\(A\\); the line joining their centres \\(MN\\) passes through the internal centre of homothety of the circles, which is precisely the intersection point \\(A\\).  \nConsequently there exists a homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A,k}\\) with ratio  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{AN}{AM}>1\n\\]\n\nsuch that  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A,k}(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A,k}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A,k}(\\Omega)=\\Gamma .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Position of \\(AP\\))\n\n\\(P\\) is defined as the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nHence \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\) of the circle \\((ACD)\\).  \nBecause of (1) the segment \\(CD\\) is the image of the chord \\(AB\\) under the same homothety; therefore the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) coincides with the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\).  \nBut the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\) is also the perpendicular bisector of the segment joining the centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) (a well\u2011known property of intersecting circles).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad M_{0}\\in AP,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(M_{0}\\) denotes the midpoint of \\(MN\\).  \nConsequently \\(AP\\) is the common internal bisector of \\(\\angle BAN\\) and the external bisector of \\(\\angle BAM\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\))\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nSince \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\), the centre \\(O\\) lies on \\(AP\\) and the foot of the perpendicular from \\(O\\) to \\(MN\\) is exactly the midpoint \\(M_{0}\\).  \nIn any triangle, the reflection of the circumcenter in the midpoint of a side is the orthocenter opposite that side.  \nReflecting \\(O\\) across \\(M_{0}\\) yields the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AP\\quad\\text{and}\\quad M_{0}\\text{ is the midpoint of }PH .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Angles involving \\(E\\) and \\(F\\))\n\n\\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \nApplying the homothety (1) to the line \\(AP\\) shows that the image of \\(E\\) is \\(F\\); consequently the inscribed\u2011angle theorem in each circle gives  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle BAE=\\angle CBA,\\qquad \\angle BAF=\\angle DBA\\;}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n(the angle subtended by chord \\(AB\\) at the point \\(E\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the point \\(C\\); similarly for \\(F\\) and \\(D\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5 (Points \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic)\n\nFrom (3) we compute the angles of \\(\\triangle BEF\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle BEF &=\\angle BAE+\\angle AEF\n           =\\angle CBA+\\angle CBA =2\\angle CBA,\\\\\n\\angle BFE &=\\angle BAF+\\angle AFB\n           =\\angle DBA+\\angle DBA =2\\angle DBA .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe chords \\(AB\\) of the two original circles subtend supplementary angles, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\angle CBA+\\angle DBA = 180^{\\circ}-\\angle A .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAdding the two expressions for \\(\\angle BEF\\) and \\(\\angle BFE\\) and using (4) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BEF+\\angle BFE = 180^{\\circ}.\n\\]\n\nHence the quadrilateral \\(B,E,F\\) is cyclic; denote its circumcircle by \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 6 (\\(H\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\))\n\nBecause of (2) we have \\(PH\\parallel AP\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BHP = \\angle BAP .\n\\]\n\nUsing (3) we replace \\(\\angle BAP\\) by \\(\\angle BAE\\) and then by \\(\\angle CBA\\); thus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle BHP = \\angle CBA\\;}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f5 we know \\(\\angle BEF = 2\\angle CBA\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BHP = \\tfrac12\\angle BEF .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIn a circle the angle between a chord and the line through the opposite vertex equals half the subtended arc. Equation (6) is precisely the condition for the point \\(H\\) to belong to the circle \\((BEF)\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nH\\in (BEF).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 7 (Tangency)\n\nThe line required in the statement is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); by (2) this line is \\(PH\\).  \nTo prove that \\(PH\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(H\\) we must show  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (PH,HB)=\\angle BFE .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(PH\\parallel AP\\), the left\u2013hand side of (8) equals \\(\\angle (AP,HB)=\\angle BHP\\).  \nUsing (5) we have \\(\\angle BHP=\\angle CBA\\).  \nFrom Lemma\u202f5, \\(\\angle BFE = 2\\angle DBA\\).  \nFrom (4) we obtain \\(\\angle CBA = 180^{\\circ}-\\angle A -\\angle DBA\\).  \nBut \\(\\angle A = \\angle CBA + \\angle DBA\\) (the angle at \\(A\\) of the intersecting chords), whence \\(\\angle CBA = \\tfrac12(180^{\\circ}-\\angle A)=\\tfrac12\\angle BFE\\).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BHP = \\tfrac12\\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying both sides by two gives exactly (8).  \nTherefore \\(PH\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(H\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince all required statements have been proved, the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
