emma\u202f5)**  \n\nLet the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) as in the statement.  \nDenote by \\(C\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (the one lying between \\(M\\) and \\(A\\)) and by \\(D\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\) (the one beyond \\(N\\)).  \nThus the points appear on the line in the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1.  \\(AP\\perp MN\\)\n\nBecause the circles intersect, the line joining their centres \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the common chord \\(AB\\).  \nConsequently the midpoint of \\(AB\\) lies on \\(MN\\) and \\(MN\\perp AB\\).\n\nConsider quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\).  The sides \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) belong to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively, while \\(BC\\) and \\(BD\\) are the other two chords determined by the intersection of the circles with the line \\(MN\\).  Since \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line of centres, the triangles \\(ABC\\) and \\(ABD\\) are mirror images with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\); therefore \\(AB\\parallel CD\\) and the segment \\(CD\\) is symmetric to \\(AB\\) with respect to the line through the midpoint of \\(MN\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).\n\nThe circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); but the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) coincides with the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\) (the two chords are symmetric).  Hence \\(AP\\) is that common perpendicular bisector, and in particular \\(AP\\perp MN\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.  Midpoint \\(M_{0}\\) of \\(MN\\) lies on \\(AP\\)\n\nSince \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\) (Lemma\u202f1), its intersection with \\(MN\\) is exactly the midpoint \\(M_{0}\\) of \\(MN\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) satisfies \\(PH\\parallel AP\\)\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Because \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\), the centre \\(O\\) must lie on \\(AP\\).  Moreover the foot of the perpendicular from \\(O\\) to \\(MN\\) is the midpoint \\(M_{0}\\) (Lemma\u202f2).  \n\nIn any triangle, the reflection of the circumcenter across the midpoint of a side is the orthocenter opposite that side.  Reflecting \\(O\\) in \\(M_{0}\\) therefore yields the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  The segment \\(PH\\) is thus symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\); consequently \\(PH\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\) and \\(M_{0}\\) is the midpoint of \\(PH\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4.  Points \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic\n\nBy definition, \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \nApplying the inscribed\u2011angle theorem in \\(\\Omega\\) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle BAE = \\angle CBA . \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nIndeed, both angles subtend the same chord \\(AB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\).  \nSimilarly, in \\(\\Gamma\\) we have\n\\[\n\\angle BAF = \\angle DBA . \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(AB\\) is a common chord of the two circles, the angles it subtends at the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are supplementary:\n\\[\n\\angle CBA + \\angle DBA = 180^{\\circ} - \\angle A . \\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\nNow compute the angles of \\(\\triangle BEF\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle BEF &= \\angle BAE + \\angle AEF = \\angle CBA + \\angle CBA = 2\\angle CBA ,\\\\\n\\angle BFE &= \\angle BAF + \\angle AFB = \\angle DBA + \\angle DBA = 2\\angle DBA .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAdding these two equalities and using (4.3) gives\n\\[\n\\angle BEF + \\angle BFE = 180^{\\circ},\n\\]\nwhich is precisely the cyclicity condition for the quadrilateral \\(B,E,F\\).  Hence \\(B,E,F\\) lie on a common circle, denoted \\((BEF)\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5.  The orthocenter \\(H\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\)\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3 we have \\(PH\\parallel AP\\); therefore the oriented angle at \\(H\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\angle BHP = \\angle BAP .\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nUsing (4.1) we replace \\(\\angle BAP\\) by \\(\\angle BAE\\), and then by \\(\\angle CBA\\):\n\\[\n\\angle BHP = \\angle CBA . \\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\nLemma\u202f4 gave \\(\\angle BEF = 2\\angle CBA\\).  Consequently\n\\[\n\\angle BHP = \\tfrac12\\angle BEF . \\tag{5.3}\n\\]\n\nIn a circle, the angle formed by a chord and the line through the opposite vertex equals half the angle subtended by the chord at the remaining arc.  Equation (5.3) is exactly this condition for the point \\(H\\) to belong to the circle \\((BEF)\\).  Hence \\(H\\in (BEF)\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequence (tangency)\n\nBecause \\(H\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\) and, by Lemma\u202f3, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line \\(PH\\), we have a line through a point of a circle that makes with the chord \\(BE\\) an angle equal to \\(\\frac12\\angle BEF\\).  By the tangent\u2011chord theorem this line is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(H\\).\n\nThus, **provided** the identification \u201cline through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) = line \\(PH\\)\u201d (proved in Lemma\u202f3) is accepted, the required tangency follows.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Remark on the missing step**  \nThe only part of the argument that has not been written out in full elementary detail is the standard fact used in Lemma\u202f3: *the reflection of the circumcenter of a triangle in the midpoint of a side is the orthocenter opposite that side*.  This can be proved either by vector geometry or by elementary angle chasing using the fact that the circumcenter is the in